  {"slug":"ba","meta":{"slug":"ba","brand":"Business Analyst Academy","name":"Business Analysis","cert":"Salesforce Business Analyst","icon":"📊","color":"#8B5CF6","desc":"17 phases on requirements, process mapping, agile discovery, and solution design with real artefact patterns.","repo":"Salesforce-Business-Analyst-Roadmap","github":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-Business-Analyst-Roadmap","live":"https://abdoaddouli.github.io/Salesforce-Business-Analyst-Roadmap/","guideBase":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-Business-Analyst-Roadmap/blob/main/developer%20Business%20Analyst%20Roadmap/","repoBlob":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-Business-Analyst-Roadmap/blob/main/","phases":17,"source":"Salesforce Business Analyst\\Salesforce Business Analyst Roadmap"},"modules":[{"id":"ba-ba-role","n":1,"title":"Business Analyst Role & Ecosystem","icon":"01","color":"#4F46E5","tagline":"The BA craft, delivery life cycle, Salesforce ecosystem","guide":"01-Business-Analyst-Role-and-Ecosystem.md","art":[{"label":"BusinessAnalystFundamentalsTest","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/BusinessAnalystFundamentalsTest.cls"},{"label":"config/project-scratch-def.json","href":"config/project-scratch-def.json"},{"label":"Custom objects (objects/)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/"}],"objectives":["Explain the BA role across the delivery life cycle","Describe the Salesforce ecosystem and declarative platform","Relate requirements, processes, data and stakeholders","Define the artifacts a BA produces on a Salesforce project","Map the exam blueprint six knowledge areas to the roadmap"],"lessons":[{"title":"What a Salesforce BA Actually Does","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The Business Analyst bridges business and technology. On a Salesforce project you: listen to stakeholders, document the current process, define the future state, write clear requirements, and verify the delivered solution meets the business need."},{"t":"table","head":["Phase","BA focus","Key artifact"],"rows":[["Discovery","Understand goals & pain points","Interview notes, stakeholder map"],["Definition","Document requirements","Requirement doc, user stories"],["Design","Fit-gap, solution design","Fit-gap matrix, wireframes"],["Build","Explain behavior to devs","Accepted stories, clarifications"],["Test","Plan & run UAT","UAT plan, test cases"],["Deploy","Train, communicate, adopt","Training plan, release notes"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"A BA earns trust by asking \"why\" three times. Root causes — not symptoms — become requirements."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Which artifact marks the end of the Discovery phase?","a":"A stakeholder map and agreed discovery notes that answer \"what problem are we solving and for whom?\"."}]},{"title":"The Salesforce Ecosystem","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Salesforce is a multi-tenant cloud platform: core CRM (Sales, Service, Marketing, Community) plus a declarative toolkit — objects, fields, validation rules, Flows, approval processes, reports and dashboards. Most business needs are met WITHOUT code."},{"t":"list","items":["Standard objects (Account, Contact, Opportunity, Case) cover common CRM domain models","Custom objects (_c) extend the data model to your business","Flows automate processes; approval processes gate decisions","Reports & dashboards turn the data model into decisions","AppExchange packages add pre-built solutions"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Memorize the platform funnel for the exam: Requirements → Data Model → Automation → Interface → Reporting."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A stakeholder asks for a \"portal to submit cases\". Which declarative options exist?","a":"Salesforce sites, Experience Cloud communities, or a simple List View approach — explore configure-first, then AppExchange, then custom code."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 1 Quiz - Role & Ecosystem","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Which artifact is the primary output of the Discovery phase?","opts":["Fit-gap matrix","Stakeholder map & discovery notes","Release notes","UAT plan"],"a":1,"why":"Discovery is about understanding goals, pain points and who is involved before any design."},{"q":"The exam blueprint \"Development Support & User Acceptance\" area is covered by which roadmap phases?","opts":["Phases 1–2 only","Phases 12–13 (UAT + change)","Phase 14 only","None"],"a":1,"why":"Development Support and User Acceptance map to UAT planning and change management."},{"q":"A BA solution preference hierarchy follows which order?","opts":["Code first","Configure → AppExchange → code","Automate everything","Only standard fields"],"a":1,"why":"Start with configuration, then packages, and write code only when necessary."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-stakeholders","n":2,"title":"Stakeholder Management & Discovery","icon":"02","color":"#7C3AED","tagline":"Stakeholder maps, RACI, interviews, workshops","guide":"02-Stakeholder-Management-and-Discovery.md","art":[{"label":"StakeholderEngagementService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/StakeholderEngagementService.cls"},{"label":"Stakeholder__c object","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Stakeholder__c/"},{"label":"Stakeholder RSVP flow","href":"force-app/main/default/flows/Stakeholder_RSVP_Tracking.flow-meta.xml"}],"objectives":["Identify and classify stakeholders by power and interest","Create a RACI matrix for a Salesforce initiative","Plan and run structured interviews and workshops","Practice active listening and careful note-taking","Manage stakeholder expectations and conflicts"],"lessons":[{"title":"Power vs Interest Mapping","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Classify each stakeholder on two axes: POWER (ability to influence the outcome) and INTEREST (how much they care). The grid decides how you engage them."},{"t":"table","head":["Quadrant","Engagement","Example"],"rows":[["High power, high interest","Manage closely","Executive sponsor"],["High power, low interest","Keep satisfied","Finance owner"],["Low power, high interest","Keep informed","End-user champion"],["Low power, low interest","Monitor","Casual observer"]]},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Your finance director cares little about details but can stop the project. How do you engage?","a":"Keep satisfied: involve at milestones and decision points, don't bury them in weekly minutiae."}]},{"title":"RACI in Practice","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A RACI matrix assigns every activity one Responsible person, one Accountable owner, Consults and Informs. One R per activity keeps decisions clean."},{"t":"code","lang":"md","x":"| Activity            | Sponsor | PM | Business Owner | SME | IT |\n|---------------------|---------|----|----------------|-----|----|\n| Prioritize backlog  | A       | I  | R              | C   | C  |\n| Define requirements | C       | A  | R              | R   | C  |\n| Design data model   | I       | A  | C              | R   | R  |\n| Execute UAT         | I       | R  | A              | C   | I  |"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Two As for one activity is the #1 RACI smell — it guarantees escalation ping-pong. Resolve to one Accountable."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Who signs off UAT in the RACI above?","a":"The Business Owner is Accountable; the PM is Responsible for executing the UAT plan."}]},{"title":"Discovery Interviews & Workshops","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"num","items":["Prepare an interview guide: open questions, one topic at a time","Ask \"what happens today?\", \"what breaks?\", \"what would success look like?\"","Listen 80%, talk 20%; mirror back what you heard","Send thank-you + summary notes within 24 hours"]},{"t":"list","items":["Workshops: 45–90 min, max ~12 people, one facilitator + one scribe","Ground rules: phone-free, parking-lot for tangents, decide on the spot","Tools: agenda, wall charts / Miro, sticky-note voting"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"End every interview with: \"Who else should I talk to?\" — the best source map comes from stakeholders themselves."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 2 Quiz - Stakeholders & Discovery","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A stakeholder with high power and low interest should be...","opts":["Managed closely","Kept satisfied","Kept informed","Monitored"],"a":1,"why":"High power, low interest stakeholders need milestone-level involvement."},{"q":"Which RACI role is ultimately accountable for an activity?","opts":["Responsible","Accountable","Consulted","Informed"],"a":1,"why":"Accountable is the single role that answers for the outcome (final sign-off)."},{"q":"Best practice after a discovery interview:","opts":["Wait for the sponsor to ask","Send summary notes within 24h","Circulate the transcript","Skip follow-ups"],"a":1,"why":"Prompt, written confirmation of findings builds trust and drives discovery forward."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-processes","n":3,"title":"Business Process Mapping","icon":"03","color":"#2563EB","tagline":"As-is/to-be, swimlanes, process metrics","guide":"03-Business-Process-Mapping.md","art":[{"label":"ProcessMapService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/ProcessMapService.cls"},{"label":"Process_Map__c object","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Process_Map__c/"},{"label":"Practice SOQL","href":"scripts/soql/process-maps.soql"}],"objectives":["Document as-is processes with swimlane diagrams","Identify waste, bottlenecks and handoff gaps","Design the to-be process with the business","Capture process metrics (steps, actors, cycle time)","Translate process change into requirements"],"lessons":[{"title":"As-Is Swimlane Diagrams","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A swimlane diagram shows WHO does WHAT and WHERE the handoffs happen. Each row is an actor; each column is a step; arrows are flows; diamonds are decisions."},{"t":"code","lang":"md","x":"Swimlane: Lead Intake (as-is)\n┌──────────────── Salesforce ────────────────┐\nAdmissions  : [submit inquiry] → [email Excel]   ⬊\nRecruiter   : ❏ receive email → [re-type into CRM] → [follow-up]\nAdmissions  :                      ↳ never logged  ← wait, tricky!\n"},{"t":"list","items":["Start where value starts: the trigger event (lead arrives)","End where value ends: the delivered outcome","Mark pain points with ⚠️ directly on the diagram"]},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What is the first element of every process map?","a":"The trigger event — without it the map has no start."}]},{"title":"Spotting Waste & Handoff Gaps","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Waste","Process hint","Salesforce fix"],"rows":[["Re-keying","Same data typed twice","Web-to-lead, integration, prefill"],["Waiting","Long idle between handoffs","Assignment rules, notifications"],["Rework","Defects found late","Validation rules, guardrails"],["Over-processing","Manual steps add no value","Flows, auto-calc fields"],["Handoff gap","Nobody owns the next step","Owner fields, routing, RACI"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"If a step appears in the to-be map for no reason, it is waste. Every to-be step must earn its place."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"\"Same data typed twice\" is which form of waste?","a":"Re-keying — solved with web-to-lead, integrations, or prefill rules."}]},{"title":"Process Metrics & the To-Be Map","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Measure the as-is so you can prove the to-be improves it. Capture STEP COUNT, ACTOR COUNT, CYCLE TIME, and HANDOFF COUNT before re-design."},{"t":"num","items":["Count steps and actors from the as-is map","Measure one example (e.g. 4 days from inquiry to first call)","Draft the to-be map removing re-keying and waits","Confirm the new process with the process owner"]},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// ProcessMapService stores measurable facts on Process_Map__c\nProcess_Map__c pm = new Process_Map__c(\n  Name              = 'Lead Intake',\n  Step_Count__c     = 5,\n  Actor_Count__c    = 2,\n  Cycle_Time_Days__c= 4,\n  Status__c         = 'As-Is Documented'\n);"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Every to-be change becomes a requirement: \"reduce lead-to-call cycle time from 4 days to 1\" is a measurable business need."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 3 Quiz - Process Mapping","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"In a swimlane diagram, each row typically represents...","opts":["A deadline","An actor / role","A system","A KPI"],"a":1,"why":"Swimlane rows show WHO performs the steps; columns show the sequence."},{"q":"Which is a classic handoff-gap symptom?","opts":["Too many reports","Nobody owns the next step","Fast cycle times","Few actors"],"a":1,"why":"A handoff gap is where responsibility for the next step falls between two parties."},{"q":"To prove a re-designed process is better you need...","opts":["A prettier diagram","As-is metrics like cycle time","More lanes","A new color palette"],"a":1,"why":"Measurable as-is metrics let you show before/after improvement."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-requirements","n":4,"title":"Requirements Gathering & Documentation","icon":"04","color":"#0EA5E9","tagline":"FR/NFR, docs, prioritization, traceability","guide":"04-Requirements-Gathering-and-Documentation.md","art":[{"label":"RequirementsTraceabilityService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/RequirementsTraceabilityService.cls"},{"label":"Requirement approval flow","href":"force-app/main/default/flows/Requirement_Approval_Routing.flow-meta.xml"},{"label":"Requirement approval process","href":"force-app/main/default/approvalProcesses/Business_Requirement__c.Requirements_Approval.approvalProcess-meta.xml"}],"objectives":["Write clear functional and non-functional requirements","Structure a Business Requirements Document (BRD)","Prioritize requirements with MoSCoW and value/effort","Keep every requirement traceable to a source","Text requirements that are testable and unambiguous"],"lessons":[{"title":"Functional vs Non-Functional","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Functional requirements say WHAT the system does (\"system must route high-priority cases to tier-1\"). Non-functional requirements say HOW WELL (performance, security, usability, compliance)."},{"t":"table","head":["Type","Focus","Example"],"rows":[["Functional","Features & behavior","Auto-create Task when an opportunity reaches Closed Won"],["Non-functional","Quality attributes","Dashboard loads in under 3 seconds for 1M records"],["Data","Information rules","Duplicate leads suppressed by email + company"],["Reporting","Insight needs","Executives can see pipeline conversion by source"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Use the \"Invest\" test: each requirement must be Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"\"The Case list view must support 50,000 records\" is which type?","a":"Non-functional (performance/volume), not functional."}]},{"title":"The BRD Structure","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"num","items":["Background & business goals (why now)","Scope: in scope / out of scope explicitly","Stakeholders & roles (RACI summary)","As-is and to-be process summaries","Functional requirements (numbered, testable)","Non-functional + data + reporting requirements","Assumptions, dependencies, open questions"]},{"t":"list","items":["Number requirements (R-101, R-102...) so teams can trace and test them","One requirement per sentence; no \"and\" that hides two needs","Use must / should / shall consistently (RFC-2119 style)"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"A requirement you cannot verify is a wish. If you cannot write a pass/fail check, rewrite it."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why number every requirement?","a":"So developers and testers can trace each one to code, tests, and UAT results."}]},{"title":"Prioritization & Traceability","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"MoSCoW sorts scope for a release; value/effort pairs it with cost. Traceability links each requirement to its source stakeholder, the user stories, and the UAT test cases that prove it."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// RequirementsTraceabilityService keeps Requirement -> Story -> Test links\n// Traceability goal: every requirement is covered by at least one story\n// and every story by at least one UAT test case. Coverage = tested / total.\n\nBusiness_Requirement__c r = new Business_Requirement__c(\n  Name                  = 'Route high-priority cases to tier 1',\n  Requirement_Type__c   = 'Functional',\n  Priority__c           = 'Must Have',\n  Status__c             = 'In Review'   // awaiting approval flow\n);"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Phase 11 models this in Salesforce as the traceability report: Requirement → Stories → Test Cases, highlighting gaps in red."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A story has no UAT test case. What is the traceability state?","a":"A coverage gap — the requirement is not verifiable end-to-end until a test case exists."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 4 Quiz - Requirements","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"\"The dashboard must load in under 3 seconds\" is...","opts":["Functional","Non-functional","Data","Out of scope"],"a":1,"why":"Performance/quality attributes are non-functional requirements."},{"q":"MoSCoW \"Must Have\" means:","opts":["Nice to have","Mandatory for this release","Deferred","Optional"],"a":1,"why":"Must-have requirements block the release if missing."},{"q":"Good traceability lets you prove that...","opts":["Every requirement is implemented & tested","The doc is long","Stakeholders agreed","Code compiles"],"a":0,"why":"Traceability links requirement → story → test case end-to-end."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-data","n":5,"title":"Data Modeling for Business Analysts","icon":"05","color":"#06B6D4","tagline":"Objects, fields, relationships, data dictionary","guide":"05-Data-Modeling-for-Business-Analysts.md","art":[{"label":"BusinessAnalystFundamentalsTest (SOQL)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/BusinessAnalystFundamentalsTest.cls"},{"label":"Field metadata (objects/)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/User_Story__c/"},{"label":"Practice SOQL","href":"scripts/soql/requirements.soql"}],"objectives":["Map business nouns to objects and attributes to fields","Choose Lookup vs Master-Detail relationships","Read and create a data dictionary","Understand picklists, formula and roll-up fields","Design a model that survives reporting needs"],"lessons":[{"title":"From Business Nouns to Objects","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Business noun","Object","Attributes → fields"],"rows":[["Requirement","Business_Requirement__c","Type, Priority, Status, Value"],["User story","User_Story__c","Points, Status, Acceptance"],["Stakeholder","Stakeholder__c","RACI, Influence, Interest"],["Test case","UAT_Test_Case__c","Step, Expected, Actual, Status"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"If two things can exist independently, model them as separate objects and RELATE them — don't cram them into one object."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A requirement must always belong to a story. Which relationship suits a one-requirement-to-many-stories rule?","a":"User_Story__c has a Lookup (or MD) to Business_Requirement__c — many stories can trace to one requirement."}]},{"title":"Lookup vs Master-Detail","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Lookup: both sides may exist alone, no cascade delete, flexible sharing. Master-Detail: child lives and dies with the master, roll-up summaries, shared ownership, max two M-D per child."},{"t":"list","items":["Use Master-Detail when the child has no life without the parent (line items, test steps of a test case)","Use Lookup when the relationship is loose or many-to-many","Roll-up summary (COUNT/SUM/MIN/MAX) requires Master-Detail"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Restricting a required relationship is a data-governance decision: MD enforces orphans never happen, Lookup needs validation rules."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"You need to COUNT test cases per story on the story record. What's required?","a":"A Master-Detail from UAT_Test_Case__c to User_Story__c (or a roll-up-ish pattern) so a summary field can count children."}]},{"title":"The Data Dictionary","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A data dictionary defines every object and field: type, picklist values, default, required, and business meaning. It is the BA contract with the build team."},{"t":"table","head":["Field","Type","Values / default","Rules"],"rows":[["Status__c","Picklist","Draft, In Review, Approved, Implemented, Verified, Rejected","Required, default Draft"],["Priority__c","Picklist","Must Have / Should / Could / Won't","Required"],["Value_Score__c","Number (1-100)","—","1–100 range VR"],["Acceptance_Criteria__c","Long Text","—","Required before Approved"]]},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// BusinessAnalystFundamentalsTest exercises the model end-to-end\nUser_Story__c s = new User_Story__c(\n  Name                   = 'As a rep, I can log a call in one click',\n  Story_Points__c        = 3,\n  Status__c              = 'Backlog',\n  Acceptance_Criteria__c = 'Call logged under 5 seconds'\n);\ninsert s;\nSystem.assertEquals('Backlog', s.Status__c);"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Design for reporting FIRST: ask what reports run on this data before locking field types."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 5 Quiz - Data Modeling","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A roll-up summary field requires which relationship?","opts":["Lookup","Master-Detail","Hierarchical","External"],"a":1,"why":"Roll-up summaries aggregate children onto the master in a Master-Detail relationship."},{"q":"A child record that must never exist without its parent fits...","opts":["Lookup","Master-Detail","A formula field","A report"],"a":1,"why":"Master-Detail cascades deletion and enforces parentage."},{"q":"The artifact defining every field, its type and business meaning is the...","opts":["BRD","Data dictionary","RACI","UAT plan"],"a":1,"why":"The data dictionary is the BA's contract describing the full data model."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-stories","n":6,"title":"User Story Writing & Backlog Management","icon":"06","color":"#10B981","tagline":"INVEST, acceptance criteria, backlog refinement","guide":"06-User-Story-Writing-and-Backlog-Management.md","art":[{"label":"UserStoryService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/UserStoryService.cls"},{"label":"BacklogPrioritizationService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/BacklogPrioritizationService.cls"},{"label":"Backlog scoring flow","href":"force-app/main/default/flows/Backlog_Prioritization_Scoring.flow-meta.xml"}],"objectives":["Write INVEST-compliant user stories","Define measurable acceptance criteria (Given/When/Then)","Break epics into small, shippable stories","Prioritize the backlog with weighted scoring","Run backlog refinement effectively"],"lessons":[{"title":"The Story Formula & INVEST","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A user story is a promise of a conversation: \"As a <role> I want <capability> so that <benefit>\". Then INVEST: Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable."},{"t":"code","lang":"md","x":"As a sales rep\nI want to see a lead's activity history\nso that I can prepare for a call without switching tools.\n\nAcceptance criteria (Given / When / Then):\n- Given a lead with 3 logged activities\n  When I open the lead page\n  Then I see the activities sorted newest first."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"An acceptance criterion that cannot be checked by a tester is not done. Write Given/When/Then so testers can verify it."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A story says \"various performance improvements\". Which INVEST letter breaks?","a":"Testable (and Estimable) — \"various\" is vague; add measurable criteria."}]},{"title":"Breaking Epics into Stories","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Epic","Sliced stories"],"rows":[["Lead assignment overhaul","Route web leads to right tier · Notify assignee · Reassign on queues"],["UAT readiness","Capture test cases · Log defects · Gate sign-off"],["Adoption dashboard","Pipeline conversion · License usage · Training completion"]]},{"t":"list","items":["Slice by user, workflow step, or data type","Each story must ship value alone","A story > 2 days of work is usually not sliced enough"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"A story that \"depends on\" the story before it violates Independent — re-slice so ordering is preference, not necessity."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why should a story ship value alone?","a":"Independence lets the team deliver and demo value frequently and rank stories by value, not dependency."}]},{"title":"Backlog Prioritization","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Score every story with a weighted model: Value, Effort, and Risk. BacklogPrioritizationService computes a priority index so the team debates numbers, not feelings."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// BacklogPrioritizationService.scoreStories()\n// index = value weight - effort/risk penalty -> higher first\n@InvocableMethod(label='Score stories' description='Recompute backlog priority')\npublic static void scoreStories(List<Id> storyIds) {\n  for (User_Story__c s : [SELECT Id, Priority__c, Story_Points__c\n                          FROM User_Story__c WHERE Id IN :storyIds]) {\n    Integer valueWeight = s.Priority__c == 'Must Have' ? 5\n                        : s.Priority__c == 'Should Have' ? 4\n                        : s.Priority__c == 'Could Have' ? 3 : 1;\n    Integer effort = Integer.valueOf(s.Story_Points__c == null ? 1 : s.Story_Points__c);\n    s.Priority_Score__c = valueWeight * 10 - effort;   // see SERVICE for the full model\n  }\n}"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Re-rank at every refinement, not once. Priorities decay as the business changes."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Which two numbers does a weighted scoring model compare?","a":"Value (and urgency) against effort and risk — high value, low effort wins."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 6 Quiz - User Stories & Backlog","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"The story \"As a rep, I want X so that Y\" supports which INVEST letter?","opts":["Independent","Valuable","Small","Negotiable"],"a":1,"why":"The \"so that\" clause names the value the story delivers to the user."},{"q":"Given/When/Then criteria are written to be...","opts":["Measurable by testers","Short","Estimate-free","Technical"],"a":0,"why":"Acceptance criteria in behavior form make stories verifiable."},{"q":"A weighted scoring model compares value against...","opts":["Story title length","Effort and risk","Team name","Sprint number"],"a":1,"why":"Prioritization weighs expected value versus cost (effort/risk)."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-agile","n":7,"title":"Agile Ceremonies","icon":"07","color":"#84CC16","tagline":"Sprints, standups, reviews, retrospectives","guide":"07-Agile-Ceremonies.md","art":[{"label":"User story status flow","href":"force-app/main/default/flows/User_Story_Status_Transition.flow-meta.xml"},{"label":"Stakeholder RACI model","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Stakeholder__c/"},{"label":"Task standard fields","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Task/"}],"objectives":["Describe Scrum roles, events and artifacts","Prepare and run sprint planning and standups","Lead sprint reviews with demos that answer \"done?\"","Facilitate retrospective that produces action","Handle changing priorities without chaos"],"lessons":[{"title":"Scrum Building Blocks","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Event","Purpose","BA focus"],"rows":[["Sprint planning","Commit to scope","Refined stories, clear criteria"],["Daily standup","Coordinate","Unblock questions, clarify"],["Sprint review","Inspect outcome","Demo against acceptance criteria"],["Retrospective","Improve process","Listen, capture actions"],["Backlog refinement","Prepare next","Reprioritize, slice, clarify"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"The BA is the team's memory of WHY. In every ceremony, anchor discussion to the user's need, not the solution."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Where does the team decide what fits the sprint?","a":"Sprint planning, using a refined backlog with sized stories."}]},{"title":"Facilitate, Don't Lecture","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"num","items":["Set a clear outcome for the meeting in the invite","Keep a visible agenda and a parking lot for tangents","One conversation at a time; call on the quiet ones","Close with explicit decisions and owners (RACI instinct)"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Standups that last 40 minutes are the symptom of unrefined stories — most questions belong in refinement, not the daily."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A tangent keeps replaying in planning. Where does it go?","a":"The parking lot / follow-up list, then a dedicated conversation after the ceremony."}]},{"title":"Reviews & Retrospectives that Stick","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A sprint review demos AGAINST the acceptance criteria accepted by the business; a retrospective answers \"how do we work better next sprint?\" with real actions."},{"t":"list","items":["Review anchor: \"Here is the story. Here is the criterion. Does it pass?\"","Retro format: Start / Stop / Continue — each vote gets an owner","Review actions next sprint or the retrospective is decoration"]},{"t":"code","lang":"md","x":"Retro board (sample)\n[Stop]   - No new stories sneaked in mid-sprint  -> PM\n[Start]  - Demo every story against criteria     -> BA\n[Continue] - Daily standup max 15 min            -> Team"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"In the review, the business owner says \"done\" — not the developer. Keep the acceptance-criteria checklist visible."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 7 Quiz - Agile Ceremonies","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A sprint review demos against...","opts":["Team velocity","Acceptance criteria","Pain point list","Roast schedule"],"a":1,"why":"The review proves stories meet their defined acceptance criteria."},{"q":"The daily standup is for...","opts":["Status reporting to managers","Coordination & unblocking","Design reviews","Contract sign-off"],"a":1,"why":"Standups coordinate the day — deep design belongs elsewhere."},{"q":"A retrospective that produces no actions is...","opts":["Fine","Decoration","UAT","Required for compliance"],"a":1,"why":"Retrospectives must yield owned actions or they don't improve the team."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-wireframe","n":8,"title":"Wireframing & Prototyping","icon":"08","color":"#F59E0B","tagline":"Layouts, mockups, clickable prototypes","guide":"08-Wireframing-and-Prototyping.md","art":[{"label":"Event workshop fields","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Event/"},{"label":"Custom tabs","href":"force-app/main/default/tabs/"},{"label":"Custom object tabs","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/User_Story__c/"}],"objectives":["Sketch page layouts that reflect user mental models","Turn acceptance criteria into interface elements","Validate flows with clickable prototypes","Keep prototypes cheap and disposable","Translate approved mockups into requirements"],"lessons":[{"title":"Sketch for Conversation, Not for Production","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A wireframe is 5% interface and 95% conversation: it gets users to say \"no, the button belongs here\". Keep it grayscale, rough, and fast to redraw."},{"t":"list","items":["Boxes and labels only — no colors, no real content","Number every element for discussion (W1, W2...)","One wireframe per user goal, tied to a story","Iterate on paper or a whiteboard before any tool"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Wireframes answer WHERE things go; prototypes answer HOW it FEELS. Do not polish the wireframe — polish the behavior."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why keep wireframes grayscale and rough?","a":"To steer the conversation to structure and behavior, not taste and colors."}]},{"title":"From Acceptance Criteria to Layout","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Every acceptance criterion should visibly map to a wireframe element. A missing element is either undefined behavior or a criterion nobody planned."},{"t":"table","head":["Acceptance criterion","Wireframe element"],"rows":[["Lead with 3 activities shown newest-first","Activity timeline component"],["User can filter by source","Filter dropdown above the list"],["High-priority cases highlighted","Priority badge / color chip"],["Rep logs a call in < 5 seconds","Prominent \"Log a call\" action button"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"If a criterion has no home on the wireframe, it will silently drift away during delivery. Renegotiate it now."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A criterion \"rep can log a call in under 5 seconds\" maps to which element?","a":"A prominent, one-click \"Log a call\" action button on the record page."}]},{"title":"Clickable Prototypes & Validation","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"num","items":["Link the happy path first (3-5 screens)","Let 3-5 real users click it WITHOUT instruction","Watch where they hesitate or backtrack","Fix the prototype, re-test the fix, then lock screenshots into the BRD"]},{"t":"code","lang":"md","x":"Validation session\n- Task: \"Route this inquiry to tier 1 within a minute\"\n- Observed: user could not FIND the routing action (buried in menu)\n- Fix: move action to the record header\n- Outcome: becomes requirement R-110 + story + wireframe W4"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Inside Salesforce, lightweight prototypes can BE real Lightning pages via dynamic forms — show users the actual platform early."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What do you capture from a prototype session beyond likes/dislikes?","a":"Observed hesitations, backtracks and errors — behavior is truth."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 8 Quiz - Wireframing","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"The primary purpose of a wireframe is to...","opts":["Win design awards","Spark conversation about structure","Replace documentation","Show final colors"],"a":1,"why":"Wireframes drive the conversation about layout, flow and behavior."},{"q":"Every acceptance criterion should...","opts":["Stay vague","Map to a wireframe element","Be removed","Be hidden from testers"],"a":1,"why":"Unmapped criteria tend to drift away during delivery."},{"q":"The fastest validation signal in a prototype test is...","opts":["A compliment","User hesitation or backtracking","Page count","Color choices"],"a":1,"why":"Observed behavior reveals where the design fights the user."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-automation","n":9,"title":"Declarative Automation Literacy","icon":"09","color":"#F97316","tagline":"Validation rules, flows, approvals, assignment","guide":"09-Declarative-Automation-Literacy.md","art":[{"label":"Flow reference metadata","href":"force-app/main/default/flows/"},{"label":"Validation rules","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Business_Requirement__c/"},{"label":"Approval process","href":"force-app/main/default/approvalProcesses/Business_Requirement__c.Requirements_Approval.approvalProcess-meta.xml"}],"objectives":["Design data-quality guardrails (validation rules)","Explain Flow Builders and record-triggered flows","Model approvals with approval processes","Route work with assignment rules and queues","Select the right automation tool for the job"],"lessons":[{"title":"Validation Rules — Guardrails, Not Gates","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Validation rules protect data BEFORE bad data lands. They return an error message when a formula is true. Best used for REQUIRED, FORMAT, and RANGE checks."},{"t":"table","head":["Rule","Formula (simplified)","Message"],"rows":[["Value in range","Value_Score__c > 100","Score must be 1-100"],["Status needs criteria","ISPICKVAL(Status__c,'Approved') && ISBLANK(Acceptance_Criteria__c)","Add acceptance criteria first"],["Stories capped","Story_Points__c > 21","Max 21 points per story"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"VIP user approves data errors internally? — Use them sparingly. A loud, clear message near the field beats a cryptic formula."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A validation rule fires \"when\"?","a":"When the formula evaluates to TRUE, blocking the save with the message."}]},{"title":"Flows — the Automation Workbench","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Flows automate insert/update/approval logic in the same transaction. Record-triggered flows react to data changes — the same pattern this repo uses in Apex services."},{"t":"list","items":["Before-save flows update the record (cheap, same record)","After-save flows create/update OTHER records or call services","Screen flows guide users step-by-step (wizards)","Flow Builder is the proposed declarative replacement for older Workflow Rules"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Guard against RE-ENTRY: a flow that updates a record can re-trigger itself. Use a reentry guard (e.g. suppress flag) like the Apex trigger framework."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"You must create a Task when a Case escalates. Which automation?","a":"An after-save Flow (or an Apex service) reacting to the escalation."}]},{"title":"Approvals, Assignment & Choosing a Tool","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Approval processes add an approve/reject step with queues and chains; assignment rules route new records by criteria; queues hold unassigned work."},{"t":"table","head":["Automation","Best for","Demo in repo"],"rows":[["Validation rule","Stop bad data","Score range, required criteria"],["Flow","Multi-step automation","Requirement routing, UAT defects"],["Approval process","Decision gates","Requirement sign-off"],["Assignment rule","Initial routing","Lead intake, Case change requests"],["Apex","Complex logic / APIs","Services + triggers"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Rule of thumb: start declarative, escalate to Apex only when logic needs loops, callouts, or scale."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"\"A manager must approve requirement status Approved\" suits which tool?","a":"An approval process (queue + final approval actions)."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 9 Quiz - Declarative Automation","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A validation rule blocks a save when its formula is...","opts":["FALSE","TRUE","Empty","Deleted"],"a":1,"why":"TRUE triggers the error and prevents save."},{"q":"An after-save flow is the right tool to...","opts":["Validate a field","Create a Task on escalation","Report data","Design a tab"],"a":1,"why":"After-save flows create/update other records in reaction to changes."},{"q":"Which is re-entry risk in flows?","opts":["Flow updates the same record, re-triggering itself","Flow is too colorful","Flow has no version","Flow name is long"],"a":0,"why":"Self-updates can loop; guard with a suppress/flag pattern."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-analytics","n":10,"title":"Reports & Dashboards","icon":"10","color":"#EF4444","tagline":"Report types, filters, KPIs, dashboards","guide":"10-Reports-and-Dashboards.md","art":[{"label":"ReportRequirementService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/ReportRequirementService.cls"},{"label":"Reports","href":"force-app/main/default/reports/"},{"label":"Dashboard","href":"force-app/main/default/dashboards/"}],"objectives":["Turn business questions into report requirements","Design report types and filter logic","Choose the right chart for the decision","Build dashboards that drive action","Validate report output with stakeholders"],"lessons":[{"title":"From Question to Report Requirement","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A report requirement names the QUESTION, the audience, the object, the grouping, and the filter. If a stakeholder cannot state the decision, the report is decoration."},{"t":"table","head":["Question","Report","Group by / filter"],"rows":[["Which sources convert best?","Opportunities won","Source → Close Won"],["Is UAT on track?","UAT test cases","Status summary by story"],["Which requirements are unsized?","User stories","WHERE Story_Points__c = null"],["Adoption OK?","Logins","Weekly active users"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Every report answers ONE question. Two charts = two reports, or a dashboard with two components."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What must you capture before building any report?","a":"The decision it informs and the audience who will act on it."}]},{"title":"Report Types, Filters & Summaries","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The report type defines the objects (primary + related) and which records appear. Filters tighten the dataset; grouping rolls it up into summary lines for totals."},{"t":"list","items":["Standard/Custom: choose primary object = what you count","Join with related objects for context (e.g., Requirement → Stories)","Summary reports group and total; Matrix reports add two dimensions","Filter logic A AND (B OR C) needs custom filter logic syntax"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Dashboard filters hide rows; they do not fix a wrong report type. Validate the underlying report first."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"You count test cases grouped by story. Which object is primary?","a":"UAT_Test_Case__c (primary), related to User_Story__c."}]},{"title":"Dashboards that Drive Action","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A dashboard is a decision cockpit: 5-8 components, one screen, one owner audience. Every component must have a drill-down to the detail report."},{"t":"table","head":["Component","Chart","Drill-down"],"rows":[["Pipeline by Stage","Funnel / Donut","Opportunity list report"],["Requirements health","Stacked bar","Requirements summary"],["UAT sign-off","Gauge","UAT test case report"],["Adoption trend","Line multi-axis","Login history"]]},{"t":"code","lang":"md","x":"// A dashboard requirement as a data object (ReportRequirementService)\nKPI: \"Average requirement-to-story coverage by sprint\"\nSource reports: Requirements Summary (grouped by Sprint__c)\nComponent: bar chart, limit 10 bars, color by Status__c\nAudience: Delivery Lead - sees it daily"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Ask \"what will you DO when the gauge turns red?\" — if the answer is nothing, replace the gauge."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"How do you make a dashboard actionable?","a":"Every component drills into a detail report and links to a decision."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 10 Quiz - Reports & Dashboards","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"The first thing to clarify for any report is...","opts":["The chart color","The question/decision it informs","The report name","The font"],"a":1,"why":"Reports exist to inform decisions, so the question comes first."},{"q":"To count test cases grouped by their story you set the primary object to...","opts":["User_Story__c","UAT_Test_Case__c","Business_Requirement__c","Case"],"a":1,"why":"The primary object is what you are counting (test cases)."},{"q":"Dashboard filters fix...","opts":["Broken report types","Nothing — validate the report first","Data quality","Permissions"],"a":1,"why":"Dashboard filters just hide rows; wrong structure is fixed in the report."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-gap","n":11,"title":"Gap Analysis & Solution Design","icon":"11","color":"#EC4899","tagline":"Fit-gap, configure vs customize, AppExchange","guide":"11-Gap-Analysis-and-Solution-Design.md","art":[{"label":"GapAnalysisService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/GapAnalysisService.cls"},{"label":"Opportunity fit-gap fields","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Opportunity/"},{"label":"Process map usage","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Process_Map__c/"}],"objectives":["Conduct a structured fit-gap assessment","Classify gaps: Configure / Extend / Third-party / Custom build","Score gaps by severity and business impact","Write solution options with trade-offs","Document decisions for the design document"],"lessons":[{"title":"The Fit-Gap Matrix","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"For each requirement, decide if standard Salesforce fits out-of-the-box or has a gap. The matrix compares business need vs platform capability — with a decision per row."},{"t":"table","head":["Requirement","Fit","Gap","Decision"],"rows":[["Web lead capture","Fit — Web-to-Lead","None","Out-of-the-box"],["Approval routing","Partial — approval process","Needs 2-level chain","Configure flow"],["Dunning emails","No fit","Billing logic","AppExchange / build"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Record the SOURCE of a gap: is it a true platform limit, a custom legacy process, or a misunderstanding? Most \"gaps\" are habits."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A requirement is fully met by a standard object. What is the fit-gap verdict?","a":"Fit — no gap; use the standard capability as-is."}]},{"title":"Configure → Extend → Package → Build","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Resolve gaps in cost-ascending order: configuration (objects, flows, layouts), extension (some Apex/integration), AppExchange (third-party), custom build (rare, last)."},{"t":"num","items":["Configure: best fit, lowest cost, standard maintenance","Extend: add logic/fields when config cannot express behavior","Package: reuse proven ISV features (licenses, longer support)","Build: most expensive, own maintenance, justify in writing"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Every custom build inherits lifetime maintenance. The design doc must state WHY build beats the alternatives."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A gap can be closed by a well-reviewed AppExchange package. Which option should lead?","a":"The package — before investing in custom build."}]},{"title":"Scoring Gaps & Writing the Design Doc","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Score every gap by SEVERITY (business impact) and FREQUENCY (how often the pain occurs). High severity + high frequency demand a decision quickly; low/low can stay as-is."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// GapAnalysisService fits each requirement vs the platform\n// Severity: 1 (cosmetic) .. 5 (blocks business)   Frequency: 1..5\n// Impact = Severity * Frequency  -> drives the design decision rank\nfor (Business_Requirement__c r : reqs) {\n  Integer gapImpact = Integer.valueOf(r.Severity__c)\n                    * Integer.valueOf(r.Frequency__c);\n  r.Gap_Score__c = gapImpact;      // 25 = must resolve, 1 = accept as-is\n}"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"A design document is \"requirements + decisions\". Each decision records the options, the trade-offs and who chose."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Severity 5, frequency 5. What does the impact score 25 tell you?","a":"Resolve it — the gap is both painful and constant."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 11 Quiz - Gap Analysis","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A \"fit\" in the fit-gap matrix means...","opts":["Needs custom code","Standard capability covers it","Out of scope","Deferred"],"a":1,"why":"Fit = the out-of-the-box platform meets the requirement."},{"q":"Resolution options rank in which order?","opts":["Build first","Configure → Extend → Package → Build","Package first always","Extend then configure"],"a":1,"why":"Escalate in cost order: configure first, build as the last resort."},{"q":"Gap impact = Severity × Frequency is used to...","opts":["Name the report","Prioritize which gaps to resolve","Choose icons","Set frequencies"],"a":1,"why":"High impact scores get decisions; low scores stay as-is."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-uat","n":12,"title":"UAT Planning & Test Case Design","icon":"12","color":"#8B5CF6","tagline":"UAT scripts, test cases, defects, sign-off","guide":"12-UAT-Planning-and-Test-Case-Design.md","art":[{"label":"UATTestCaseService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/UATTestCaseService.cls"},{"label":"UAT defect flow","href":"force-app/main/default/flows/UAT_Defect_Logging.flow-meta.xml"},{"label":"UAT_Test_Case__c object","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/UAT_Test_Case__c/"}],"objectives":["Write a UAT plan from acceptance criteria","Design test cases that mirror real business flows","Track defects with severity and priority","Gate go-live on formal sign-off","Trace every test case to a requirement"],"lessons":[{"title":"UAT Explores, Scripts Confirm","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"UAT is the business proving the delivered solution with REAL data and REAL scenarios. Scripted test cases confirm acceptance criteria; exploratory testing finds surprises."},{"t":"table","head":["Test case","Steps","Expected result"],"rows":[["TC-010 Route high-priority case","Create Case, set high priority, save","Assignee = tier-1 queue"],["TC-011 Story completion moves status","Mark story Done with criteria filled","Status = Done, sound"],["TC-012 Duplicate lead suppressed","Insert lead same email+company","Second attempt warned"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Each test case links to a requirement (traceability) and a user story. No requirement, no test case."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why link every test case to a requirement?","a":"Traceability proves coverage — every requirement is verified by at least one test case."}]},{"title":"Defect Management","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A defect is \"expected result not achieved\". Capture severity (impact) and priority (fix order) separately — severe is not always urgent."},{"t":"table","head":["Severity","Meaning","Example"],"rows":[["Critical","Blocks business","Cannot save a Case"],["High","Major function broken","Routing wrong tier"],["Medium","Workaround exists","Minor label wrong"],["Low","Cosmetic","Spacing issue"]]},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// UATTestCaseService: Failed -> defect Case, Passed -> sign-off\nif (tc.Status__c == 'Failed') {\n  // after-save Flow UAT_Defect_Logging creates a change-request Case\n  // with the failed test case linked and Severity__c copied over\n}\nif (tc.Status__c == 'Passed') {\n  tc.Signed_Off__c = true;    // accumulates toward the go-live gate\n}"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Never fix a defect inside UAT without a decision. Log it, assess it, then rerun the exact failing step after the fix."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Severity vs priority: a cosmetic issue blocking nobody is...","a":"Low severity, low priority — it can wait; severity measures impact, priority measures order."}]},{"title":"Sign-off Gates","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"num","items":["Agree exit criteria before UAT starts (e.g. 100% critical/high pass)","Run the cycle; log results; triage defects daily","Retest after fixes (re-run the SAME steps)","Collect formal sign-off per module — then go-live is authorized"]},{"t":"code","lang":"md","x":"Go/No-Go gate checklist\n- [ ] 100% Critical defects resolved\n- [ ] 100% High defects resolved or formally deferred\n- [ ] Every must-have story passed its test case\n- [ ] Sign-off form signed by Business Owner & PM\n- [ ] Known-issues list published with the release"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"A signed go-live form is the BA's strongest deliverable — it turns approval into a fact, not a memory."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What does sign-off authorize besides \"it works\"?","a":"Formal acceptance — releasing is agreed by the business, with known issues documented."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 12 Quiz - UAT","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A test case must trace back to...","opts":["Another test case","A requirement/story","A color","A sprint number"],"a":1,"why":"Traceability ensures every requirement is verified end-to-end."},{"q":"Severity measures...","opts":["Fix order","Business impact","Effort to fix","Who reported it"],"a":1,"why":"Severity = impact; priority = order of fixing."},{"q":"The go/no-go gate is based on...","opts":["Sprint velocity","Exit criteria + sign-off","Team mood","Dashboard color"],"a":1,"why":"Agreed exit criteria and formal sign-off authorize go-live."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-change","n":13,"title":"Change Management & Communication","icon":"13","color":"#14B8A6","tagline":"Adoption, training, release notes, comms","guide":"13-Change-Management-and-Communication.md","art":[{"label":"ChangeManagementService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/ChangeManagementService.cls"},{"label":"Case change-request fields","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Case/"},{"label":"Email templates","href":"force-app/main/default/email/"}],"objectives":["Assess adoption readiness on an account","Write a training plan matched to learner personas","Prepare concise release notes and communications","Measure adoption with platform metrics","Iterate support after go-live"],"lessons":[{"title":"Adoption Is a Design Goal","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The best solution fails without adoption. Change management plans the PEOPLE side: who is affected, what changes for them, and how they are brought along."},{"t":"table","head":["Persona","Change","Support"],"rows":[["Sales reps","New lead flow","2h demo + cheat sheet"],["Managers","Dashboard KPIs","Report walkthrough"],["Admins","New objects","Config handover doc"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"For every persona answer three questions: What changes? Why now? What's in it for me (WIIFM)?"},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"The first question users ask about any change is...","a":"\"What changes for ME?\" — lead with the personal impact, then the why."}]},{"title":"Training Plans & Release Notes","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A training plan maps persona → format → timing. Release notes announce WHAT changed, WHEN it lands, and HOW users respond — written in business language, not metadata speak."},{"t":"num","items":["List the top user-visible changes (not every field)","Group notes: New / Changed / Fixed","Show a before-after example for the biggest change","Publish in the channel users already read"]},{"t":"code","lang":"md","x":"Release 2.1 — what's new\nNEW  Lead routing now assigns web leads in ~0s (was 2 days)\nCHANGED Dashed board \"UAT readiness\" moved to Dashboard tab\nFIXED Case escalation no longer duplicates tasks\n→ Training lab Feb 3 · Q&A after release"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Answer \"what do I do differently on Monday?\" for every stakeholder. If the notes can't, the change is unclear."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Release-notes rule of thumb for content: ","a":"Business impact first — user-visible changes in business language, with before/after."}]},{"title":"Measuring Adoption","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Track genuine usage: logins, record creation, flow runs, report views. Compare against baseline (before go-live) and target (agreed with sponsor)."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// ChangeManagementService tracks adoption on the Account\n// Adoption_Score__c = f(training completion, logins, UAT sign-off)\n// Demo only - real adoption needs a Usage app / login metrics\nAccount a = new Account(Name='Acme Corp',\n  Adoption_Stage__c = 'Post-Launch',\n  UAT_Signoff_Date__c = Date.today());\n\nif (Date.today() <= a.UAT_Signoff_Date__c.addDays(30)) {\n  a.Adoption_Day_Count__c = 30 - Integer.valueOf(Date.today().daysBetween(a.UAT_Signoff_Date__c));\n}"},{"t":"list","items":["Baseline = usage 4 weeks before go-live","Target = sponsor-agreed KPI (e.g. 70% reps active in 60 days)","Declining adoption → retrain, not rebuild"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Dashboard adoption ≠ org wide adoption. Measure per persona, not overall."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Adoption drops 15% after launch. First response?","a":"Diagnose with per-persona usage, then targeted training/comm — not a rebuild."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 13 Quiz - Change Management","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"The strongest adoption question for each persona is...","opts":["How many fields changed?","What changes for me / WIIFM?","Which API used?","How long is the doc?"],"a":1,"why":"Personal impact and benefit drive behavior change."},{"q":"Release notes should be written in...","opts":["Metadata names","Business language with before/after","Legal jargon","Sprint numbers"],"a":1,"why":"Users need to know what changed for THEM and what to do differently."},{"q":"Adoption measurement needs...","opts":["A baseline and a target","A bigger team","More dashboards","No measurement"],"a":0,"why":"Compare pre-go-live baseline to an agreed target KPI to see the trend."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-exam","n":14,"title":"Certification Prep","icon":"14","color":"#A855F7","tagline":"CBA blueprint, quiz engine, study plan","guide":"14-Certification-Prep.md","art":[{"label":"Certification_Setting__mdt (exam facts)","href":"force-app/main/default/customMetadata/Certification_Setting.Business_Analyst.md-meta.xml"},{"label":"CertificationPrepService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/CertificationPrepService.cls"},{"label":"Training_Question__c quiz bank","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Training_Question__c/"}],"objectives":["State the exact CBA exam format and rules","Learn the six blueprint knowledge areas","Build and use a personal question bank","Plan a 4-6 week study schedule","Apply exam-taking techniques (flag, skip, re-check)"],"lessons":[{"title":"Exam Facts — Know Them Cold","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The Salesforce Certified Business Analyst (CBA) exam is a timed, proctored multiple-choice test. These facts come from the official exam guide — always re-check the live guide before booking (official link in guide 14)."},{"t":"table","head":["Fact","Value"],"rows":[["Scored questions","60 (multiple-choice & multiple-select)"],["Unscored questions","Up to 5 (sprinkled in, not counted)"],["Duration","105 minutes"],["Passing score","72%"],["Registration fee","US$200 + tax"],["Retake fee","US$100 + tax"],["Prerequisite","None (experience recommended)"]]},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// Certification_Setting__mdt stores the official facts as metadata\nCertification_Setting__mdt cba = new Certification_Setting__mdt(\n  Exam_Code__c         = 'CBA',\n  Question_Count__c    = 60,\n  Duration_Minutes__c  = 105,\n  Passing_Score__c     = 72,\n  Cost_USD__c          = 200,\n  Retake_Cost_USD__c   = 100\n);"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Multiple-select questions deduct NOTHING extra — but every wrong choice counts. Choose deliberately, read \"choose TWO\" twice."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"How many questions count toward your score?","a":"60 scored questions; up to 5 additional unscored ones may appear but don't count."}]},{"title":"The Six Blueprint Areas","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The exam blueprint divides the test into six knowledge areas. Weights (as published in the official guide) and where this roadmap covers them:"},{"t":"table","head":["Knowledge area","Weight","Covered in"],"rows":[["Customer Discovery","17%","Phases 2–4"],["Collaboration with Stakeholders","23%","Phases 2, 7, 13"],["Business Process Mapping","12%","Phase 3"],["Requirements","18%","Phases 4–6"],["User Stories","18%","Phase 6"],["Development Support & User Acceptance","12%","Phases 9–12"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Blueprints are rebalanced over time. Spot-check the live exam guide before your date; this roadmap pins the current published weights."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Which area carries the largest weight?","a":"Collaboration with Stakeholders (23%) — the flagship CBA skill."}]},{"title":"Your Study Plan & Exam Technique","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"num","items":["Week 1–2: Discovery & collaboration (phases 1–4)","Week 3–4: Processes, requirements, stories (phases 5–9)","Week 5: Gap analysis, UAT, change (phases 10–13)","Week 6: practice exams + weak-area drilldown (phase 14)"]},{"t":"list","items":["Train 10–15 questions a day in CertificationPrepService quiz mode","Flag hard questions and finish easy ones first (105 min is real)","Eliminate two wrong answers before choosing","Multiple-select: treat as pass/fail per option"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Read scenario questions in behaviors, not buzzwords: identify the actor, the action, and the acceptance criterion — the answer is almost always the configured, simplest fit."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Time management rule for a 105-minute, 60-question exam:","a":"Average ~1.7 min/question — flag the hard ones, finish the rest, re-check flagged with the remainder."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 14 Quiz - Certification Knowledge","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"How many scored questions are on the CBA exam?","opts":["50","60","75","100"],"a":1,"why":"60 scored multiple-choice questions (plus up to 5 unscored)."},{"q":"Minimum passing score for the CBA exam is...","opts":["68%","70%","72%","80%"],"a":2,"why":"The official guide lists 72% as the passing score."},{"q":"Which blueprint area has the highest weight?","opts":["Customer Discovery","Collaboration with Stakeholders","User Stories","UAT"],"a":1,"why":"Collaboration with Stakeholders leads the blueprint at 23%."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-exercises","n":15,"title":"Practical Exercises & Mini Projects","icon":"15","color":"#F43F5E","tagline":"31 exercises, 10 mini projects, 1 capstone","guide":"15-Practical-Exercises-and-Mini-Projects.md","art":[{"label":"15-Practical-Exercises-and-Mini-Projects.md","href":"developer%20Business%20Analyst%20Roadmap/15-Practical-Exercises-and-Mini-Projects.md"},{"label":"answers.js (solutions)","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"},{"label":"Practice scripts","href":"scripts/soql/"}],"objectives":["Practice every BA artifact on realistic scenarios","Complete 31 targeted exercises across all skill areas","Build 10 mini projects combining multiple skills","Deliver one full capstone engagement","Verify every solution against the answer key"],"lessons":[{"title":"How to Work These Exercises","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Each exercise is an artifact you must produce like a real BA: a stakeholder map, a RACI, an as-is map, a BRD section, a data dictionary, a story stack... Work from the prompt, then open the answer inside the UI (the 💡 Show answer button) to compare."},{"t":"list","items":["Attempt first — answers exist to be COMPARED, not replaced","The 31 exercises are grouped in 9 skill sections","MP1–MP10 are mini projects pulling 2–3 skills together","CAP is the capstone: chair one full engagement from discovery to sign-off"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Copying an answer produces nothing. Type your map/story/BRD out, then diff your logic against the reference."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What is the purpose of the answer key?","a":"Comparison and coaching — not a copy/paste source."}]},{"title":"Section 1 - Discovery & Stakeholders (1.1-1.6)","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"1.1","title":"Stakeholder map","obj":"List every stakeholder for a CRM rollout at a 40-person logistics firm and classify them across power/interest.","steps":["Write 6-8 stakeholders with titles (Logistics Manager, Drivers, Billing...)","Place each on a 2x2 power/interest grid","Declare the engagement approach for each quadrant"],"verify":"Grid has at least one stakeholder per quadrant","code":{"lang":"md","x":"POWER\n high  | Sponsor (manage closely)  | Operations Director (keep satisfied)\n low   | Drivers (keep informed)     | Intern (monitor)\n       +---------------------------+---------------------------+\n       low interest                high interest"}},{"t":"ex","id":"1.2","title":"RACI for a lead-flow change","obj":"Build a RACI matrix for 4 activities to reroute inbound leads.","steps":["Activities: prioritize backlog, write requirements, build flow, run UAT","Roles: Sponsor, PM, Business Owner, SME, IT","Exactly one A per activity"],"verify":"Exactly one A and one R in every row","code":{"lang":"md","x":"| Activity            | Sponsor | PM | Biz Owner | SME | IT |\n|---------------------|---------|----|-----------|-----|----|\n| Prioritize backlog  | A       | I  | R         | C   | C  |\n| Write requirements  | C       | A  | R         | R   | C  |\n| Build flow          | I       | A  | C         | R   | R  |\n| Run UAT             | I       | R  | A         | C   | I  |"}},{"t":"ex","id":"1.3","title":"Interview guide","obj":"Write a 10-question discovery interview guide for a warehouse supervisor.","steps":["Open with Who/When/Why questions about today","Add two \"what breaks\" questions","Add one success-vision question"],"verify":"Questions are open-ended and behavior-focused"},{"t":"ex","id":"1.4","title":"Stakeholder register","obj":"Turn the grid of 1.1 into a stakeholder register with fields: name, role, power, interest, RACI, engagement.","steps":["5+ rows with the six columns","Power + interest scored 1-5"],"verify":"Each row has a scored power, interest and an engagement action"},{"t":"ex","id":"1.5","title":"Workshop plan","obj":"Plan a 90-minute discovery workshop on the pain of lead handoff.","steps":["Agenda with timeboxes (intro/current/rethink/what-if)","Ground rules + roles (facilitator, scribe)","One default decision to be made that day"],"verify":"Agenda sums to 90 min and names a decision"},{"t":"ex","id":"1.6","title":"Elevator story","obj":"Write a half-page project charter summary: problem, goal, scope, stakeholders, success measure.","steps":["Problem & goal in 2 sentences","In/out scope bullets","One measurable success KPI"],"verify":"Has a numeric success KPI"}]},{"title":"Section 2 - Process Mapping (2.1-2.3)","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"2.1","title":"As-is swimlane","obj":"Map the \"sick-leave submission\" process in 4 swimlanes (Employee, Manager, HR, Payroll).","steps":["Draw the current steps + one pain point ⚠️","Count steps and handoffs"],"verify":"Diagram has a trigger, end, and ≥1 marked pain point"},{"t":"ex","id":"2.2","title":"Waste hunt","obj":"Analyze the map from 2.1 and classify every inefficient step as re-keying, waiting, rework, or over-processing.","steps":["List each wasteful step","Tag the waste type","Suggest one Salesforce fix per waste"],"verify":"At least 3 wastes labeled by type with fixes"},{"t":"ex","id":"2.3","title":"To-be map + metrics","obj":"Redesign the process and quantify improvement (steps, actors, cycle time).","steps":["Draw the to-be swimlane","Enter as-is vs to-be numbers in a table","Write one requirement from the improvement"],"verify":"Has a before/after metric table"}]},{"title":"Section 3 - Requirements (3.1-3.4)","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"3.1","title":"FR vs NFR split","obj":"Sort 8 given statements into functional / non-functional(performance, security, usability) / data / reporting.","steps":["Each of the 8 gets a type","Justify the three hardest ones in a sentence"],"verify":"8 correctly typed statements"},{"t":"ex","id":"3.2","title":"BRD outline","obj":"Write the outline of a BRD for a \"quarantine-based\" awful data cleanup project.","steps":["Mirror the 7-section BRD structure","Fill scope bullets (in/out)"],"verify":"7 sections + explicit out-of-scope"},{"t":"ex","id":"3.3","title":"Untestable → testable","obj":"Rewrite 5 weak requirements into a testable form (\"quick\", \"easy\", \"various\" etc).","steps":["Identify the vague word","Rewrite with a measurable pass/fail"],"verify":"Every rewritten requirement has a measurable criterion"},{"t":"ex","id":"3.4","title":"MoSCoW cut","obj":"Priority a 8-requirement backlog with MoSCoW and defend two Could items being dropped.","steps":["Rank all 8 (M/S/C/W)","Write one-line defense for each Won't"],"verify":"All 8 assigned; each W defended"}]},{"title":"Section 4 - Data Modeling (4.1-4.3)","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"4.1","title":"Nouns → model","obj":"Design objects for a tutoring agency (students, tutors, sessions, payments, reviews).","steps":["List objects + their fields","Draw relationships with cardinality"],"verify":"Every business noun maps to an object"},{"t":"ex","id":"4.2","title":"Lookup vs MD","obj":"For each relationship, choose Lookup or Master-Detail and justify it.","steps":["Student → Session","Tutor → Session","Session → Session-Feedback","Payment → Student"],"verify":"Justified decision for all four"},{"t":"ex","id":"4.3","title":"Data dictionary","obj":"Produce a data-dictionary table for the tutoring model (8+ fields).","steps":["Field, type, picklist values, required, default, business meaning"],"verify":"8+ rows across ≥3 objects"}]},{"title":"Section 5 - Stories & Backlog (5.1-5.3)","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"5.1","title":"INVEST check","obj":"Score 5 sample stories against INVEST and repair the broken ones.","steps":["Mark each letter pass/fail","Rewrite two failing stories"],"verify":"Every repaired story passes all six letters"},{"t":"ex","id":"5.2","title":"Given/When/Then","obj":"Write acceptance criteria for \"student reschedules a session within 6 hours of start\".","steps":["Happy path","Fail-case (too late)","Edge case (calendar conflict)"],"verify":"Three GWT scenarios covering ≥1 edge case"},{"t":"ex","id":"5.3","title":"Backlog scoring","obj":"Score 6 stories with a value×urgency − effort − risk model and order them.","steps":["Define the weights you used","Show the final ranked stack"],"verify":"Ranking shown with scores and compact explanation"}]},{"title":"Section 6 - Agile, Design & CBA thinking (6.1-6.4)","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"6.1","title":"Ceremony cheatsheet","obj":"Write a one-page cheatsheet: each ceremony's purpose, who attends, and the BA's job.","steps":["5 ceremonies","One row each"],"verify":"5 ceremonies w/ purpose + BA role"},{"t":"ex","id":"6.2","title":"Retro that sticks","obj":"Design a Start/Stop/Continue retro board with actions owned by roles.","steps":["3 items per column","Each action has an owner"],"verify":"Every action owned"},{"t":"ex","id":"6.3","title":"Backlog refinement demo","obj":"Plan a 60-min refinement: pick 4 stories, define readiness (criteria sized, dependency resolved).","steps":["Readiness checklist per story","Timebox the session"],"verify":"Readiness checklist 4/4 stories pass"},{"t":"ex","id":"6.4","title":"Mock wireframes","obj":"Sketch 3 wireframes (list + record + flow screen) for \"session scheduling\".","steps":["Label elements W1-W2-W3","Map each to an acceptance criterion"],"verify":"Each wireframe element maps to a criterion"}]},{"title":"Section 7 - Automation, Reports & Gap (7.1-7.3)","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"7.1","title":"Automation menu","obj":"For 6 process steps pick validation rule, flow, approval, or assignment — and justify.","steps":["One tool per step","Justification sentence each"],"verify":"6 justified choices"},{"t":"ex","id":"7.2","title":"Report requirements","obj":"Write 3 report requirements (question, object, grouping, filter, chart).","steps":["One for pipeline","One for UAT","One free choice"],"verify":"3 reports with all five fields"},{"t":"ex","id":"7.3","title":"Fit-gap verdicts","obj":"Fill a fit-gap matrix for 6 requirements with resolve order.","steps":["Fit / Partial / No-fit per requirement","Rank gaps by impact = severity × frequency"],"verify":"6 rows + impact ranking"}]},{"title":"Section 8 - UAT & Change (8.1-8.3)","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"8.1","title":"UAT plan","obj":"Write a UAT plan: scope, scenarios, participants, exit criteria, schedule.","steps":["2+ scenarios","Exit criteria with numbers"],"verify":"Exit criteria are measurable"},{"t":"ex","id":"8.2","title":"Test-case design","obj":"Write 4 test cases for \"rebooking a session\" with steps + expected results.","steps":["One happy path","One boundary, one error, one permission case"],"verify":"4 test cases with expected results"},{"t":"ex","id":"8.3","title":"Adoption plan","obj":"Build a 3-persona change plan: what changes, WIIFM, training format, adoption KPI.","steps":["3 personas","KPI per persona"],"verify":"3 personas each with KPI"}]},{"title":"Mini Projects MP1-MP8","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"proj","id":"MP1","title":"Lead intake redesign","obj":"Mini-project: a full lead-flow change at BrightLogistics from discovery to a routed implementation.","reqs":[{"h":"Deliverables","items":["Stakeholder register excerpt (5 rows)","As-is vs to-be swimlane for lead intake","3 user stories + acceptance criteria","Report requirement: \"leads by source\""]}],"success":"A senior BA could take your folder and run the build.","stars":1},{"t":"proj","id":"MP2","title":"Stakeholder engagement tracker","obj":"Design a Salesforce mini-app: a Stakeholder__c object with power/interest/RACI fields and one dashboard.","reqs":[{"h":"Deliverables","items":["Field list for Stakeholder__c","A RACI matrix for 4 activities","Dashboard spec (chart + drill-down)"]}],"success":"Your model maps cleanly to the repository object and flow.","stars":1,"code":{"lang":"md","x":"MP2 keeps stakeholders as DATA — so engagement is measurable, not remembered."}},{"t":"proj","id":"MP3","title":"As-is/to-be map sprint","obj":"Re-map a real process from your work life into swimlanes and quantify the improvement.","reqs":[{"h":"Deliverables","items":["As-is swimlane with ⚠️ pain points","To-be swimlane","Before/after metric table (steps, cycle time)"]}],"success":"The metric table proves the to-be is better.","stars":1},{"t":"proj","id":"MP4","title":"BRD for a small project","obj":"Write a complete 7-section BRD for a 2-object project (in/out scope explicit).","reqs":[{"h":"Deliverables","items":["Background & goals","In/out scope","8+ numbered testable requirements","Assumptions + open questions"]}],"success":"Every requirement passes the \"testable\" check.","stars":2},{"t":"proj","id":"MP5","title":"Data dictionary build","obj":"Produce a data dictionary for 2 objects and 16 fields with rules and meanings.","reqs":[{"h":"Deliverables","items":["2 objects, 8+ fields each","Type, picklist values, required, default","One validation-rule suggestion"]}],"success":"A build team can implement without decisions.","stars":2},{"t":"proj","id":"MP6","title":"Backlog scoring model","obj":"Score 6 stories with a value×urgency−effort−risk model and defend the top pick.","reqs":[{"h":"Deliverables","items":["Weight model definition","Scored table for 6 stories","One-paragraph defense of rank #1"]}],"success":"A stakeholder can re-run your model without you.","stars":2},{"t":"proj","id":"MP7","title":"UAT cycle for a mini feature","obj":"Run a scripted UAT cycle for \"duplicate lead suppression\": plan, 4 cases, one defect, sign-off gate.","reqs":[{"h":"Deliverables","items":["UAT plan with exit criteria","4 linked test cases (steps + expected)","One defect + retest note","Signed go/no-go"]}],"success":"Your gate decision is backed by criteria, not memory.","stars":2},{"t":"proj","id":"MP8","title":"Adoption dashboard spec","obj":"Spec a sponsor dashboard: 3 KPIs (baseline + target), charts, drill-downs, audience.","reqs":[{"h":"Deliverables","items":["3 KPI definitions with formulas","Chart type + drill-down for each","Refreshing cadence and owners"]}],"success":"Each KPI answers \"so what / who acts?\" ","stars":2}]},{"title":"Section 9 - Capstone Portfolio (9.1-9.2) + Signature Projects","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"9.1","title":"Portfolio checklist","obj":"Assemble your own career portfolio: 3 artifacts each for discovery, mapping, requirements, stories, UAT, change.","steps":["Pick 6 categories","List your evidence artifacts"],"verify":"6 categories with evidence"},{"t":"ex","id":"9.2","title":"Elevator pitch","obj":"Write a 60-second BA pitch connecting your artifacts to business value.","steps":["Open with outcome","Close with a measurable win"],"verify":"Pitch ≤ 140 words"},{"t":"proj","id":"MP9","title":"Mini project - full mini engagement","obj":"Run one complete mini engagement end-to-end using phases 1-13 skills.","reqs":[{"h":"Evidence","items":["Stakeholder register","As-is + to-be maps","6 requirements + stories","UAT plan + 3 test cases","Adoption KPI"]}],"success":"You deliver a coherent portfolio file set a senior BA would accept","stars":3,"code":{"lang":"md","x":"MP9 Evidence: 01_register.md · 02_maps.md · 03_requirements.md · 04_stories.md · 05_uat.md · 06_adoption.md"}},{"t":"proj","id":"MP10","title":"Mini project - cert drill","obj":"Build your own 20-question CBA practice bank with rationale.","reqs":[{"h":"Evidence","items":["20 questions across the 6 blueprint areas","Rationale for each answer","Track your first-pass score"]}],"success":"You can explain why each of your 20 answers is right or wrong","stars":2},{"t":"proj","id":"CAP","title":"Capstone - full BA engagement","obj":"Chair one end-to-end engagement (use UC1-UC3 as raw material) and assemble the complete deliverable set.","reqs":[{"h":"Phase 1-2","items":["Discovery interviews","Stakeholder register + RACI"]},{"h":"Phase 3-5","items":["As-is/to-be maps + metrics","BRD with traceable requirements","Data dictionary"]},{"h":"Phase 6-9","items":["User story map + backlog","Approved wireframes","Fit-gap matrix"]},{"h":"Phase 10-13","items":["Report requirements","UAT plan + scripted cases","Training + comms plan"]}],"success":"A senior BA reviews your package and starts the conversation with \"what did you decide?\"","stars":4}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 15 Quiz - Practice Mode","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Why do the exercises include full answers in the UI?","opts":["To copy without thinking","To compare against your approach","To inflate the repo","To confuse learners"],"a":1,"why":"Answer keys exist for comparison and coaching — attempt first, then diff."},{"q":"A mini project (MP) differs from a single exercise by...","opts":["Combining several skills","Being optional","Having no answer","Being shorter"],"a":0,"why":"MPs pull two or more skill areas together into one deliverable."},{"q":"The capstone (CAP) is evaluated as...","opts":["A multiple-choice test","A complete engagement deliverable set","A quiz score","A diagram only"],"a":1,"why":"CAP assembles the full portfolio a working BA produces on a project."},{"q":"After finishing the capstone, a reviewer asks \"what did you decide?\" Why?","opts":["To test your memory","To see your decisions with trade-offs","To check spelling","To guess the KPI"],"a":1,"why":"The portfolio must show reasoned decisions, not just documents."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"1.1","li":1,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"1.2","li":1,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"1.3","li":1,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"1.4","li":1,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"1.5","li":1,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"1.6","li":1,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"2.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"2.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"2.3","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"3.1","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"3.2","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"3.3","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"3.4","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"4.1","li":4,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"4.2","li":4,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"4.3","li":4,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"5.1","li":5,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"5.2","li":5,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"5.3","li":5,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"6.1","li":6,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"6.2","li":6,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"6.3","li":6,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"6.4","li":6,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"7.1","li":7,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"7.2","li":7,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"7.3","li":7,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"8.1","li":8,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"8.2","li":8,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"8.3","li":8,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"MP1","li":9,"kind":"proj","stars":1},{"id":"MP2","li":9,"kind":"proj","stars":1},{"id":"MP3","li":9,"kind":"proj","stars":1},{"id":"MP4","li":9,"kind":"proj","stars":2},{"id":"MP5","li":9,"kind":"proj","stars":2},{"id":"MP6","li":9,"kind":"proj","stars":2},{"id":"MP7","li":9,"kind":"proj","stars":2},{"id":"MP8","li":9,"kind":"proj","stars":2},{"id":"9.1","li":10,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"9.2","li":10,"kind":"ex","stars":0},{"id":"MP9","li":10,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"MP10","li":10,"kind":"proj","stars":2},{"id":"CAP","li":10,"kind":"proj","stars":4}]},{"id":"ba-usecases","n":16,"title":"Real-World Use Cases","icon":"16","color":"#6366F1","tagline":"Three capstone engagements, full journey","guide":"16-Real-World-Use-Cases.md","art":[{"label":"16-Real-World-Use-Cases.md","href":"developer%20Business%20Analyst%20Roadmap/16-Real-World-Use-Cases.md"},{"label":"Force-app (build as you go)","href":"force-app/main/default/"},{"label":"Test coverage as evidence","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/"}],"objectives":["Run a greenfield CRM rollout discovery (UC1)","Perform a legacy-migration gap analysis (UC2)","Plan post-launch adoption and change (UC3)","Produce milestone artifacts per use case","Represent one journey end-to-end in Salesforce"],"lessons":[{"title":"UC1 - New CRM Rollout Discovery","mins":15,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Scenario: \"BrightLogistics\" (120 employees, Excel-and-email culture) wants its first CRM. You start at ZERO: no system, no data model, no process digitized."},{"t":"list","items":["Deliverable 1: stakeholder register + RACI (sponsor, ops, drivers, billing)","Deliverable 2: 3 discovery workshops yielding as-is/to-be swimlanes","Deliverable 3: BRD + traceable requirement list (projected 12-15 reqs)","Deliverable 4: user-story map + prioritised backlog for two sprints","Deliverable 5: data dictionary for the core objects you propose"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Greenfield means your \"as-is\" is often paper/email. Capture it anyway — the pain points justify the change."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A greenfield rollout has no legacy data. Which artifacts still need as-is maps?","a":"All of them — current manual processes define pain points and to-be targets."}]},{"title":"UC2 - Legacy System Migration Gap Analysis","mins":15,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Scenario: \"MediKart\" runs billing on an on-prem system and cases on Salesforce — they want ONE platform, losing none of the billing behaviour."},{"t":"list","items":["Deliverable 1: as-is process maps for the legacy flows (billing, refunds, holds)","Deliverable 2: fit-gap matrix (each legacy capability vs Salesforce)","Deliverable 3: data-migration requirements (fields, mapping, cutover)","Deliverable 4: UAT plan for migrated billing scenarios","Deliverable 5: decisions log with configure/extend/package choices"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Legacy cutover is where silent assumptions live. Every legacy rule must become a documented requirement or an explicit de-scope."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A legacy tax-calculation rule has no Salesforce equivalent. What do you produce?","a":"A documented gap with options (config/extension/package/build) and a decision owner."}]},{"title":"UC3 - Post-Launch Change Management","mins":15,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Scenario: \"CloudHarvest\" launched Salesforce 3 months ago; adoption is 40% of target. You are brought in to fix adoption, not add features."},{"t":"list","items":["Deliverable 1: adoption audit (login, record, flow-run, report-view metrics)","Deliverable 2: persona-based training plan (including retraining)","Deliverable 3: release notes revamp + weekly comms channel","Deliverable 4: KPI dashboard for the sponsor (baseline vs target)","Deliverable 5: 60-day action plan with owners"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Low adoption diagnosis beats new features almost every time: retrain, re-message, re-simplify."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Adoption is 40% of target. First deliverable?","a":"An adoption audit measuring each persona against baseline — before any training spend."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 16 Quiz - Use Case Awareness","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"For a greenfield rollout, the \"as-is\" process maps come from...","opts":["The vendor spec","Current manual/paper/email processes","The database","Nothing"],"a":1,"why":"Manual processes ARE the as-is state that define pain points."},{"q":"A legacy capability with no Salesforce equivalent becomes...","opts":["Ignored","A documented gap to decide","Hidden","Auto-built"],"a":1,"why":"Gap analysis turns every missing behavior into an explicit decision."},{"q":"Low adoption is usually fixed by...","opts":["More features","An adoption audit + retraining","A new dashboard","Fewer users"],"a":1,"why":"Diagnose usage by persona, then retrain/communicate before adding scope."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"ba-ucsolutions","n":17,"title":"Use Case Solutions","icon":"17","color":"#0D9488","tagline":"Reference artifacts, milestone answers","guide":"17-Use-Case-Solutions.md","art":[{"label":"17-Use-Case-Solutions.md","href":"developer%20Business%20Analyst%20Roadmap/17-Use-Case-Solutions.md"},{"label":"Reference artifacts","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"},{"label":"Roadmap guides","href":"developer%20Business%20Analyst%20Roadmap/"}],"objectives":["Compare your UC1-UC3 deliverables to references","Learn the reasoning behind each decision","Redo gaps in your portfolio with targeted rework","Complete the full 17-phase journey"],"lessons":[{"title":"UC1 Reference - CRM Rollout Discovery","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Full worked artifacts for BrightLogistics: the register, the RACI, the workshop agenda with timeboxes, as-is/to-be swimlanes, a 12-requirement BRD sample, a reopened story map, and the data dictionary."},{"t":"code","lang":"md","x":"UC1 Milestone 1 - Register (excerpt)\nGreg Sponsor      | power 5 | interest 4 | RACI: A | manage closely\nOps Director      | power 4 | interest 3 | RACI: A | keep satisfied\nDriving Team Lead | power 3 | interest 5 | RACI: R | keep informed\nBilling Manager   | power 4 | interest 2 | RACI: C | keep satisfied\nDispatcher SME    | power 2 | interest 5 | RACI: R | keep informed"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Compare your own register row-by-row, then update yours — portfolio strength comes from the redo, not the first draft."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"UC1's Ops Director is power 4 / interest 3. Which quadrant and approach?","a":"High power / low-ish interest → keep satisfied at milestones only."}]},{"title":"UC2 Reference - Migration Fit-Gap","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Worked fit-gap for MediKart: 9 legacy capabilities vs Salesforce, severity×frequency impact scores, the resolve order, the data-migration field mapping for billing, and the UAT plan for cutover scenarios."},{"t":"table","head":["Legacy capability","Fit","Decision"],"rows":[["Invoices + payment status","Partial","Configure objects + flows"],["Refund approval chain","Gap","Configure approval process"],["Tax engine (state rules)","No fit","AppExchange / decision required"],["Duplicate customer merge","Partial","Configure duplicate rules"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Conflating \"no fit\" with \"build custom\" is the classic migration trap — capture the decision, then evaluate packages first."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"For \"Tax engine\" with impact 18, what is the next action?","a":"A decision record: evaluate AppExchange packages, cost options, then choose and store the trade-off."}]},{"title":"UC3 Reference - Change & Adoption","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Worked adoption plan for CloudHarvest: the audit table with baseline/target, persona training matrix, the revamped release-notes template, the KPI dashboard spec, and a 60-day owner-assigned action plan."},{"t":"code","lang":"md","x":"UC3 Adoption audit (excerpt)\nReps:  baseline 35% active -> target 70% by day 60\nManagers: baseline 20% -> target 60% (dashboard daily)\nAdmins: baseline 55% -> target 90% (config ownership)\n\n60-day plan\nD1-D10  audit + comms restart\nD11-D30 retrain reps (2h), demo dashboards to mgrs\nD31-D60 weekly adoption report, Q&A office hours"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Notice adoption is measured PER PERSONA against a baseline — exactly what phase 13 preaches."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Which UC3 metric tells you the plan is working?","a":"The daily/weekly per-persona adoption trend trending toward agreed targets."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Final Quiz - Roadmap Mastery","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"The common thread across all three use cases is...","opts":["Decisions with trade-offs documented","No documentation","Building features first","One KPI only"],"a":0,"why":"Every UC produces a decisions trail — the BA's signature contribution."},{"q":"Which artifact proves requirements are testable end-to-end?","opts":["A stakeholder map","Requirement → story → test-case traceability","A swimlane","An interview note"],"a":1,"why":"Traceability from requirement to story to UAT case verifies coverage."},{"q":"Your account drops to 40% adoption three months after launch. Best first move?","opts":["Build more features","Diagnose usage per persona","Raise prices","Change logos"],"a":1,"why":"Adoption problems are people/process problems — diagnose before adding scope."}]},"exIndex":[]}],"answerIds":["1.1","1.2","1.3","1.4","1.5","1.6","2.1","2.2","2.3","3.1","3.2","3.3","3.4","4.1","4.2","4.3","5.1","5.2","5.3","6.1","6.2","6.3","6.4","7.1","7.2","7.3","8.1","8.2","8.3","9.1","9.2","CAP","MP1","MP10","MP2","MP3","MP4","MP5","MP6","MP7","MP8","MP9"]};

window.ABDO_DATA["cpq"] =
  {"slug":"cpq","meta":{"slug":"cpq","brand":"CPQ & Revenue Cloud Academy","name":"CPQ & Revenue Cloud (Quote-to-Cash)","cert":"Salesforce CPQ Specialist","icon":"💰","color":"#F59E0B","desc":"17 phases of Quote-to-Cash: price books, quotes, approvals, amendment, multi-currency, and Revenue Cloud.","repo":"Salesforce-CPQ-Revenue-Cloud-Roadmap","github":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-CPQ-Revenue-Cloud-Roadmap","live":"https://abdoaddouli.github.io/Salesforce-CPQ-Revenue-Cloud-Roadmap/","guideBase":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-CPQ-Revenue-Cloud-Roadmap/blob/main/developer%20CPQ%20%26%20Revenue%20Cloud%20Roadmap/","repoBlob":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-CPQ-Revenue-Cloud-Roadmap/blob/main/","phases":17,"source":"Salesforce CPQ & Revenue cloud\\Salesforce CPQ  & Revenue Cloud Roadmap"},"modules":[{"id":"cpq-cpq-fundamentals","n":1,"title":"CPQ & Revenue Cloud Fundamentals","icon":"01","color":"#3B82F6","tagline":"Quote-to-cash lifecycle, architecture & the two engines","guide":"01-CPQ-and-Revenue-Cloud-Fundamentals.md","art":[{"label":"QuoteCalculatorService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/QuoteCalculatorService.cls"},{"label":"project-scratch-def.json","href":"config/project-scratch-def.json"},{"label":"Discount_Schedule__c","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Discount_Schedule__c/"}],"objectives":["Describe the quote-to-cash lifecycle and its eight stages","Position the legacy CPQ managed package vs Revenue Cloud / RLM","Describe the configuration, pricing and quote engines","Identify the main records that make up a CPQ quote","Explain the roles of the sales rep, the admin and the developer"],"lessons":[{"title":"What Is Quote-to-Cash?","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"The Quote-to-Cash Lifecycle"},{"t":"p","x":"Quote-to-cash (Q2C) is the end-to-end business process that starts when a customer asks for a price and ends when recognized revenue lands in the general ledger. Salesforce CPQ and Revenue Cloud sit inside that journey, turning a standard Opportunity into a fully priced quote, an approved order, and then a contract, an invoice and recognized revenue. Every field and every automation in your org maps back to one of the eight stages below."},{"t":"num","items":["Product Catalog - the items offered and their attributes","Product Configuration - selecting features and options","Pricing - list, net and discounted prices","Quoting - the Quote and Quote Line Item records","Approval - discount and price approval chains","Contracting - converting the approved quote into a signed contract","Ordering / Fulfillment - creating the Order and shipping","Billing & Revenue Recognition - invoicing and recognizing revenue"]},{"t":"table","head":["Stage","Owner","Primary Artifact"],"rows":[["Product Catalog","Catalog admin","Product2 & PricebookEntry"],["Product Configuration","Sales rep","Quote Line Items"],["Pricing","CPQ engine","List_Unit_Price__c / Net_Unit_Price__c"],["Quoting","Sales rep","Quote with Approval_Status__c"],["Approval","Approvers","Approval_Log__c on Quote__c"],["Contracting","Legal","Contract with CPQ_Contract_Status__c"],["Ordering / Fulfillment","Operations","Order with Fulfillment_Status__c"],["Billing & Revenue","Finance","Revenue_Schedule__c"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"The Quote record is the hub of the model. Quote fields such as Discount_Percent__c, Contract_Term_Months__c, Total_ARR__c and Uplift_Percent__c carry the commercial terms forward to the Contract, Order and billing records."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Name the eight stages of the quote-to-cash lifecycle in order.","a":"Product Catalog, Product Configuration, Pricing, Quoting, Approval, Contracting, Ordering / Fulfillment, then Billing & Revenue Recognition."}]},{"title":"CPQ vs Revenue Lifecycle Management","mins":14,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Two Generations of CPQ"},{"t":"p","x":"Salesforce CPQ historically shipped as a managed package with its own namespace. Core records were stored as SBQQ__Quote__c and SBQQ__QuoteLine__c, supported by namespaced objects for product rules, price rules and quote documents. The managed package is mature, widely deployed and fully supported, and it exposes its own engines and its own extension points."},{"t":"p","x":"Revenue Lifecycle Management (RLM) is the next-generation Revenue Cloud platform built natively on core metadata. Instead of SBQQ__ namespaced objects it uses standard records such as ProductCatalog, ProductSellingModel, PriceBookEntry, Quote, QuoteLineItem, Order, Contract and Asset. The quote-to-cash stages are the same, but they run on tools and packaging the core platform understands, which makes them easier to extend, version, test and integrate with flows and Apex services."},{"t":"table","head":["Aspect","Legacy CPQ (SBQQ__)","Revenue Cloud / RLM"],"rows":[["Objects","SBQQ__Quote__c, SBQQ__QuoteLine__c","Quote, QuoteLineItem, ProductCatalog"],["Catalog / pricing","Namespaced product and price rules","PriceBookEntry on ProductSellingModel"],["Deployment","Installed managed package","Core platform with unlocked packaging"],["Extensibility","Custom code against SBQQ__ namespace","Apex services such as QuoteCalculatorService"],["Certification","CPQ Specialist / Administrator","Revenue Cloud Consultant"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Migration between the two is a project, not a setting. Rule logic, pricing methods and custom fields differ, so plan to re-implement any SBQQ__ automation against the RLM data model instead of expecting a lift-and-shift."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What is the main difference between legacy CPQ objects and RLM objects?","a":"Legacy CPQ stores data in namespaced SBQQ__ objects inside a managed package, while RLM runs on core objects such as Quote and QuoteLineItem that are native to the platform."}]},{"title":"The Three Engines & Roles","mins":15,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Engines and the Team"},{"t":"p","x":"CPQ logic is commonly described as three cooperating engines. The Configuration engine applies features, options and product rules to shape a valid product. The Pricing engine computes list, net and discounted prices from price books, price rules and discount schedules. The Quote engine regenerates the quote totals and moves the milestone forward. In legacy CPQ these engines live in the managed package; in RLM they are delivered by the core platform supported by Apex services such as QuoteCalculatorService and PriceRuleEngineService."},{"t":"table","head":["Engine","Primary Inputs","Outputs"],"rows":[["Configuration","Product2 attributes, features, options","Valid QuoteLineItem set, Is_Bundle_Parent__c"],["Pricing","PriceBookEntry, price rules, Discount_Schedule__c","List_Unit_Price__c, Net_Unit_Price__c"],["Quote","Quote Line Items and roll-up totals","Quote Total_ARR__c, Discount_Percent__c"]]},{"t":"list","items":["Sales rep - configures the product, picks options and applies discounts within their limits","CPQ admin - owns the catalog, price books, discount schedules and approval thresholds","Developer - builds Apex services, triggers and flows around the Quote object"]},{"t":"ex","id":"1.1","stars":1,"title":"Map the Quote-to-Cash Lifecycle","obj":"Walk a real sale through all eight quote-to-cash stages and name the record created at each step.","steps":["Pick a simple product, for example a 12-month subscription with 2000 USD annual recurring revenue","List the eight stages in order","For each stage write the record or field that is produced","Identify where the Quote, Contract, Order and Revenue_Schedule__c records first appear",{"h":"Check","items":["All eight stages are present","Each stage has a concrete artifact","Record order follows the lifecycle"]}],"verify":"Your stage-to-artifact mapping shows data flowing from the Opportunity into the Quote (Approval_Status__c), then the Contract (CPQ_Contract_Status__c), the Order (Fulfillment_Status__c) and finally the Revenue_Schedule__c records."},{"t":"ex","id":"1.2","stars":2,"title":"Choose CPQ vs RLM for a Scenario","obj":"Decide which platform fits a customer profile and justify the choice.","steps":["Read your scenario: a startup wants a configurable one-product subscription built with native flows","Compare the SBQQ__ namespace against core Quote and QuoteLineItem objects","Decide between the legacy CPQ managed package and RLM","Write two benefits and two risks of your choice",{"h":"Check","items":["The choice is clearly stated","At least two benefits are named","At least two risks are named"]}],"verify":"A greenfield, flow-heavy build on core generally favors RLM; a mature install with heavy SBQQ__ rule logic favors staying on legacy CPQ until a migration plan exists."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Fundamentals Checkup","mins":8,"questions":[{"q":"In which stage does an approved quote become a binding agreement?","opts":["Quoting","Contracting","Ordering / Fulfillment","Billing"],"a":1,"why":"Contracting converts the approved quote into a Contract and sets CPQ_Contract_Status__c."},{"q":"Which record connects Product2 to a Quote Line Item for pricing?","opts":["PriceBookEntry","Opportunity","Contract","Revenue_Schedule__c"],"a":0,"why":"Quote Line Items price against PriceBookEntry, which links Product2 to a Pricebook2."},{"q":"What object naming convention does legacy CPQ use?","opts":["RLM__","SBQQ__","Revenue__","CMT__"],"a":1,"why":"Legacy CPQ stores quote data in SBQQ__Quote__c and SBQQ__QuoteLine__c."},{"q":"Revenue Cloud / RLM is built on which foundation?","opts":["An isolated managed package","The core platform using standard objects","An external catalog service","A Visualforce-only org"],"a":1,"why":"RLM uses core objects such as Quote, QuoteLineItem and ProductCatalog instead of a namespaced package."},{"q":"Which engine applies discount schedules and price rules?","opts":["Configuration engine","Quote engine","Pricing engine","Approval engine"],"a":2,"why":"The pricing engine turns inputs such as Discount_Schedule__c and PriceBookEntry into Net_Unit_Price__c."},{"q":"Which Quote field carries the agreed reduction that approval rules have to check?","opts":["Contract_Term_Months__c","Discount_Percent__c","Total_ARR__c","Uplift_Percent__c"],"a":1,"why":"Discount_Percent__c on the Quote is the field that discount approval logic evaluates."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"1.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":1},{"id":"1.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"cpq-product-catalog","n":2,"title":"Product Catalog & Configuration","icon":"02","color":"#8B5CF6","tagline":"Products, features, options, product families & bundles","guide":"02-Product-Catalog-and-Configuration.md","art":[{"label":"Product2","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Product2/"},{"label":"Discount_Schedule__c","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Discount_Schedule__c/"},{"label":"pricing.soql","href":"scripts/soql/pricing.soql"}],"objectives":["Model products, product families and bundles in the catalog","Use features and options to make products configurable","Apply product types to control standalone, bundle and option behavior","Configure selection rules to constrain a configuration"],"lessons":[{"title":"Products, Families & Types","mins":11,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"The Catalog Model"},{"t":"p","x":"Products are master data stored in the standard Product2 object. Product families group related items, for example Hardware, Software or Services, and are commonly used to apply family-based pricing and discount logic across the whole group at once. Every product then receives a product type that controls how the configuration engine treats it."},{"t":"num","items":["Standalone product - sold by itself as a single line","Bundle product - a parent line made of component options","Option product - bought as part of a bundle and priced through it"]},{"t":"table","head":["Product Type","Behavior"],"rows":[["Standalone","Sold directly; no parent required; appears as its own Quote Line Item"],["Bundle","Acts as the parent; flagged with Is_Bundle_Parent__c on the Quote Line Item"],["Option","Adds value to a bundle; constrained by features and options on the parent"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Keep option pricing on the option itself. The components of a bundle roll up into the parent line so the quote shows one sellable total while the Quote Line Items below keep List_Unit_Price__c and Net_Unit_Price__c per component."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What controls whether a sales rep can sell a product alone or only inside a bundle?","a":"The product type: Standalone products can be sold alone, Bundles are parents, and Option products are sold as part of a bundle."}]},{"title":"Features, Options & Configuration Attributes","mins":13,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Making a Product Configurable"},{"t":"p","x":"Features group the choices a rep can make about a product, and Options are the specific values within a feature. A Laptop bundle might have a Processor feature with an Intel Core i7 option and a Support feature with Standard or Premium options. Configuration attributes describe how those choices behave, such as whether an option is required, whether options are exclusive for the feature, and which option is selected by default."},{"t":"num","items":["Required - the rep cannot add the product without choosing this feature","Exclusive - the rep must pick exactly one option for the feature","Default - the option pre-selected when the product is added","Optional - the rep may add zero, one or several options from the feature"]},{"t":"code","lang":"json","x":"{\n  \"product\": \"Laptop Bundle\",\n  \"productFamily\": \"Hardware\",\n  \"productType\": \"Bundle\",\n  \"features\": [\n    {\n      \"feature\": \"Processor\",\n      \"option\": \"Intel Core i7\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"exclusive\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"feature\": \"Support\",\n      \"options\": [\"Standard\", \"Premium\"],\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"exclusive\": true,\n      \"default\": \"Standard\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What is the difference between a feature and an option?","a":"A feature names the decision a rep makes (for example Processor), while options are the values available for that feature (for example Intel Core i7 or Intel Core i9)."}]},{"title":"Designing a Configurable Catalog","mins":15,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Best Practices"},{"t":"num","items":["Start from what sells - model only the decisions that actually change price or eligibility","Reuse families - hang price rules and discount schedules off Product_Family__c","Draw a feature map before building - one feature per single decision","Add validation early - constrain invalid combinations before reps see them","Name options by their saleable value, not by an internal code"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Design bundles like a menu, not a parts list. The parent (with Is_Bundle_Parent__c) is what the customer buys; the options are what you upsell inside it. Fewer, clearer options reduce configuration errors and discount leakage."},{"t":"ex","id":"2.1","stars":2,"title":"Model a Product Catalog","obj":"Design a small product catalog for a services company using products, families and product types.","steps":["Choose three products: one Standalone, one Bundle, one Option","Assign each product to a Product_Family__c","Describe what happens when each is added to a quote","Note which field carries the price for each line",{"h":"Check","items":["One product of each type","Every product has a family","Pricing field stated per product"]}],"verify":"Your catalog lists one standalone, one bundle and one option, each with a family and a clear pricing field such as List_Unit_Price__c or Net_Unit_Price__c."},{"t":"ex","id":"2.2","stars":2,"title":"Configure Product Options & Features","obj":"Turn a bundle into a configurable product with features and options.","steps":["Take your Bundle product from 2.1","Add two features, each with at least two options","Mark one option required/exclusive and one optional","Describe a selection the rep could make",{"h":"Check","items":["Two features are defined","Each feature has two options","Required and optional flags are set"]}],"verify":"The JSON bundle snippet documents the Processor and Support features with options, flags and defaults, matching the pattern used in lesson 2."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Catalog Config Checkup","mins":8,"questions":[{"q":"Which standard object stores the master product records?","opts":["ProductFamily__c","Product2","Pricebook2","Asset"],"a":1,"why":"Product2 is the standard object that stores catalog master data."},{"q":"What is a product family used for?","opts":["Storing an image per product","Grouping related products for pricing and reporting","Locking products from editing","Storing customer lists"],"a":1,"why":"Families such as Hardware or Services let admin apply rules and schedules by Product_Family__c."},{"q":"Which product type acts as the parent of a bundle?","opts":["Standalone","Option","Bundle","Kit"],"a":2,"why":"The bundle is the parent; it is flagged with Is_Bundle_Parent__c on the Quote Line Item."},{"q":"A quote line that rolls up the prices of its components is a:","opts":["Standalone line","Bundle parent line","Option line","Pricebook entry"],"a":1,"why":"The bundle parent line carries the sellable total while component lines keep their own prices."},{"q":"Which attribute tells the engine that the rep must choose an option from a feature?","opts":["exclusive","required","default","optional"],"a":1,"why":"A required feature must be completed before the bundle line can be added to the quote."},{"q":"Two options belong to the same feature and the rep may pick only one. How is this expressed?","opts":["exclusive: true","required: false","default: Standard","type: Standalone"],"a":0,"why":"Exclusive options limit the feature to exactly one selected value."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"2.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"2.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"cpq-cpq-data-model","n":3,"title":"CPQ Data Model & Object Relationships","icon":"03","color":"#06B6D4","tagline":"Standard & custom objects that drive a CPQ quote","guide":"03-CPQ-Data-Model-and-Relationships.md","art":[{"label":"Quote","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Quote/"},{"label":"QuoteLineItem","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/QuoteLineItem/"},{"label":"Renewal_Opportunity__c","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Renewal_Opportunity__c/"},{"label":"quote.soql","href":"scripts/soql/quote.soql"}],"objectives":["Describe the relationships between Quote, QuoteLineItem, Product and Pricebook","Understand lookup vs master-detail relationships","Write SOQL across the CPQ data model","Understand field inheritance from Product2 and PricebookEntry onto quote lines"],"lessons":[{"title":"The Core Quote Objects","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Objects in Play"},{"t":"p","x":"A CPQ quote is never a single record. The Opportunity captures the selling stage, the Quote holds approved commercial terms, and Quote Line Items (QLE) carry the individual lines with their prices. Products come in from Product2 via PricebookEntry, which pairs a product with a price book. When the quote is accepted it spawns a Contract, an Order, and eventually Assets and Revenue_Schedule__c records."},{"t":"table","head":["Object","Purpose","Key Relationship"],"rows":[["Opportunity","Pipeline and selling stage","Parent of the Quote"],["Quote","Approved commercial terms","Lookup to Opportunity"],["QuoteLineItem","Sold lines with prices","Master-detail to Quote"],["Product2","Catalog master record","Referenced by PricebookEntry"],["PricebookEntry","Product + price in a book","Joins Product2 to Pricebook2"],["Pricebook2","Named collection of prices","Parent of price book entries"],["Contract","Legal terms after approval","Lookup from the Quote"],["Order","Fulfillment record","Lookup to Quote, Account, Contract"],["Asset","Installed or subscribed item","Sourced from Order products"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Fields inherit through the chain. Product2 feeds PricebookEntry, PricebookEntry feeds the Quote Line Item as List_Unit_Price__c, and the rep changes become Net_Unit_Price__c and Discount_Percent__c on the line."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why do pricing fields live on the Quote Line Item and not on Product2?","a":"Because prices vary by deal and by price book; the line stores the list, net and discount values actually sold, while Product2 keeps only the catalog master data."}]},{"title":"Relationships & Lookups","mins":14,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Lookup vs Master-Detail"},{"t":"p","x":"A lookup relationship links two records but the child can exist on its own. A master-detail relationship ties the child's life cycle to the parent: when the Quote is deleted, its Quote Line Items go with it, and roll-up summary fields on the parent reflect the children. Understanding which type each relationship is tells you how deletes, sharing and ownership behave."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"List<Quote> quotes = [\n  SELECT Id, Name, OpportunityId, Discount_Percent__c,\n    (SELECT Id, Product2Id, List_Unit_Price__c, Net_Unit_Price__c\n       FROM QuoteLineItems)\n  FROM Quote\n  WHERE CPQ_Sync_Status__c = 'Synced'\n];\n\nfor (Quote q : quotes) {\n  System.debug(q.Name + ' lines: ' + q.QuoteLineItems.size());\n}"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Delete semantics differ. With a master-detail, deleting the Quote cascades deletes to every Quote Line Item without warning. With a lookup, orphaned children survive and can break integrity. Design cleanup jobs accordingly."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What happens to Quote Line Items when their parent Quote is deleted under a master-detail relationship?","a":"The cascade deletes the Quote Line Items along with the Quote, and any roll-up summary fields are recalculated."}]},{"title":"Querying the Model","mins":15,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"SOQL Across Objects"},{"t":"code","lang":"soql","x":"SELECT Id, Name, Total_ARR__c, Discount_Percent__c, Opportunity.Name,\n  (SELECT Product2Id, List_Unit_Price__c, Net_Unit_Price__c,\n          Discount_Percent__c, Is_Bundle_Parent__c\n     FROM QuoteLineItems)\nFROM Quote\nWHERE Is_Renewal__c = TRUE\n  AND Approval_Status__c = 'Approved'\nORDER BY Total_ARR__c DESC"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Traverse parent lookup fields with dotted notation (Opportunity.Name) and child records with a subquery on the relationship name (QuoteLineItems). Keep the subquery filter inside the inner query, never in the WHERE of the outer query."},{"t":"ex","id":"3.1","stars":2,"title":"Build the CPQ Data Model (ERD)","obj":"Draw the relationships between the core objects on a diagram.","steps":["List Opportunity, Quote, QuoteLineItem, Product2, PricebookEntry, Pricebook2, Contract, Order and Asset","Draw each relationship line and label it Lookup or Master-Detail","Mark which field carries the price and which carries the discount","Add Renewal_Opportunity__c and note its link to Account and Contract",{"h":"Check","items":["Quote to QuoteLineItem labeled Master-Detail","Product2 joins via PricebookEntry","Every relationship is labelled"]}],"verify":"Your ERD shows the Quote hub with quote lines below it, products entering through PricebookEntry, and contract, order and asset records flowing out."},{"t":"ex","id":"3.2","stars":2,"title":"Query the CPQ Data Model with SOQL","obj":"Extract approved renewables with their lines from the org.","steps":["Write a query selecting Quote fields including Total_ARR__c and Discount_Percent__c","Add a child subquery over QuoteLineItems selecting List_Unit_Price__c and Net_Unit_Price__c","Filter to Is_Renewal__c and Approval_Status__c equal to Approved","Run it against the pricing data and note the row counts",{"h":"Check","items":["Parent fields are selected","Child subquery is included","Filters match the requirement"]}],"verify":"Your SOQL returns approved renewal quotes with pricing per line, matching the shape of the query in lesson 3."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Data Model Checkup","mins":8,"questions":[{"q":"Which relationship type cascades deletes to child records?","opts":["Lookup","Master-detail","External","Polymorphic"],"a":1,"why":"A master-detail relationship ties the child's life cycle to the parent, so deleting the parent deletes the children."},{"q":"Which object joins Product2 to a Pricebook2 with a price?","opts":["QuoteLineItem","PricebookEntry","Opportunity","Contract"],"a":1,"why":"PriceBookEntry pairs a product with a price book and carries the price."},{"q":"Which object holds the prices actually sold on a deal?","opts":["Product2","Pricebook2","QuoteLineItem","Asset"],"a":2,"why":"The Quote Line Item stores List_Unit_Price__c, Net_Unit_Price__c and Discount_Percent__c."},{"q":"In SOQL, how do you read the parent Opportunity name from a Quote?","opts":["FROM Opportunity","Opportunity.Name","QuoteLineItems.Name","WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED"],"a":1,"why":"Dotted notation across the lookup field reads the parent record's field."},{"q":"The Quote child subquery is placed:","opts":["in the WHERE clause","in the SELECT list as a subquery","in a separate query","on the parent lookup"],"a":1,"why":"Child records are fetched with a subquery inside the SELECT list using the relationship name QuoteLineItems."},{"q":"Which custom object tracks the ARR of an upcoming renewal?","opts":["Renewal_Opportunity__c","Approval_Log__c","Revenue_Schedule__c","Product2"],"a":0,"why":"Renewal_Opportunity__c carries ARR__c, Renewal_Date__c and Uplift_Percent__c for an account's renewal."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"3.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"3.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"cpq-bundles-rules","n":4,"title":"Bundles, Product Rules & Configuration","icon":"04","color":"#F59E0B","tagline":"Configuration attributes, constraints & bundle logic","guide":"04-Bundles-Product-Rules-and-Configuration.md","art":[{"label":"BundleConfigurationService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/BundleConfigurationService.cls"},{"label":"Product2","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Product2/"},{"label":"Billing_Schedule_Creation.flow-meta.xml","href":"force-app/main/default/flows/Billing_Schedule_Creation.flow-meta.xml"}],"objectives":["Design bundles with a parent product and options","Create product rules and constraints","Use validation rules to block invalid configurations","Implement configuration logic in Apex"],"lessons":[{"title":"Bundles & Options","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Bundle Anatomy"},{"t":"p","x":"A bundle is a sellable parent with a set of component options underneath it. The parent line carries the total and Is_Bundle_Parent__c is set, while each option line records what the rep picked. Rules protect the bundle from nonsense combinations, such as pairing a desktop processor with a laptop case, so the rep only ever configures valid products."},{"t":"table","head":["Rule Type","Purpose"],"rows":[["Validation","Blocks a configuration that violates a constraint, such as a required option missing"],["Selection","Adds, hides or decides options automatically based on other choices"],["Alert","Shows a warning to the rep without blocking the configuration"],["Filter","Restricts which options can appear for a feature given other selections"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Author rules against the option that must be present, not the one you want to hide. Rules that say if this option is selected then prevent that option read much more cleanly on review."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What does a Validation rule do in a bundle?","a":"It blocks the configuration when a constraint is violated, for example when a required option has not been chosen."}]},{"title":"Product Rules & Constraints","mins":14,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Writing Constraints"},{"t":"num","items":["Describe the combination you are protecting in one sentence","Choose the rule type: validation, selection, alert or filter","Scope the rule to the bundle or product family it affects","Test the rule with the matching positive and negative cases","Version your rules so you can roll changes back safely"]},{"t":"code","lang":"json","x":"{\n  \"bundle\": \"Laptop Bundle\",\n  \"ruleType\": \"VALIDATION\",\n  \"constraint\": {\n    \"ifOption\": \"Intel Core i7\",\n    \"selectOption\": \"16GB RAM\",\n    \"action\": \"REQUIRE\"\n  },\n  \"message\": \"A Core i7 system requires 16GB RAM\",\n  \"active\": true\n}"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Rules are evaluated in sequence and interact. Two rules can contradict each other, so validate every combination before publishing and add a regression check after each catalog change."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why should a validation rule be tested with both a matching and a non-matching case?","a":"To confirm the rule blocks the bad combination and still allows valid configurations, which catches false positives in the rule logic."}]},{"title":"Validating Configuration in Apex","mins":15,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Apex Behind the Bundle"},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"QuoteLineItem qli = [\n  SELECT Id, Product2Id, Is_Bundle_Parent__c\n  FROM QuoteLineItem\n  WHERE Id = :qliId\n];\n\nList<String> errors =\n    BundleConfigurationService.validateBundleConfiguration(qli.Id);\n\nif (errors.isEmpty()) {\n  System.debug('Bundle configuration is valid');\n} else {\n  for (String err : errors) {\n    System.debug('Config error: ' + err);\n  }\n}"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Call the service from a trigger or screen flow after the rep changes options, and store any failures on the Quote or the Opportunity. That way invalid bundles cannot slip into the approval stage."},{"t":"ex","id":"4.1","stars":2,"title":"Design a Bundle with Product Rules","obj":"Design a bundle and the rules that keep it valid.","steps":["Choose a bundle you know well, such as a workstation bundle","Define the parent and three options","Write one validation rule and one selection rule","Describe the correct and the blocked configuration",{"h":"Check","items":["A parent product is defined","Three options exist","Two rules are documented"]}],"verify":"Your bundle design shows one parent line, three option lines and two rules that both prevent an invalid state and auto-select a dependent option."},{"t":"ex","id":"4.2","stars":3,"title":"Validate a Bundle Configuration in Apex","obj":"Call BundleConfigurationService.validateBundleConfiguration and handle the results.","steps":["Write a query to fetch the bundle parent QuoteLineItem","Call BundleConfigurationService.validateBundleConfiguration with its Id","Handle the empty and non-empty results","Log each returned error",{"h":"Check","items":["Service is invoked with a QLE Id","Both result paths are handled","Errors are logged"]}],"code":{"lang":"apex","x":"List<String> errors =\n    BundleConfigurationService.validateBundleConfiguration(\n        new List<QuoteLineItem>{ qli });\nif (errors.isEmpty()) {\n  // configuration ok\n} else {\n  System.debug(errors);\n}"},"verify":"Your Apex method validates the bundle through the service object and responds differently to a valid versus an invalid configuration."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Bundle Rules Checkup","mins":8,"questions":[{"q":"Which Quote Line Item field marks the parent of a bundle?","opts":["Bundle_Option__c","Is_Bundle_Parent__c","Discount_Percent__c","ARR__c"],"a":1,"why":"Is_Bundle_Parent__c flags which line is the sellable bundle parent."},{"q":"Which rule type blocks an invalid configuration?","opts":["Alert","Filter","Validation","Selection"],"a":2,"why":"A validation rule prevents the configuration from completing when a constraint is violated."},{"q":"Which rule type shows a warning without stopping the rep?","opts":["Validation","Alert","Selection","Filter"],"a":1,"why":"Alert rules notify the rep but do not block the configuration."},{"q":"A rule auto-chooses the Premium support option when Enterprise licenses are picked. Which type is it?","opts":["Validation","Alert","Selection","Filter"],"a":2,"why":"Selection rules make choices on the rep's behalf based on other selections."},{"q":"What does BundleConfigurationService.validateBundleConfiguration return?","opts":["A Boolean","A list of error strings","The bundle total","An Option record"],"a":1,"why":"The service returns a List of error messages that is empty when the configuration is valid."},{"q":"How should you treat two product rules that contradict each other?","opts":["Ship them as-is","Test combinations and add a regression check","Ignore rule order","Delete all validation rules"],"a":1,"why":"Rules interact and ordering matters, so contradiction requires testing every combination."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"4.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"4.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"cpq-pricing-discounting","n":5,"title":"Pricing, Price Rules & Discounting","icon":"05","color":"#EF4444","tagline":"Price books, price rules, discount schedules & block pricing","guide":"05-Pricing-Price-Rules-and-Discounting.md","art":[{"label":"PriceRuleEngineService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/PriceRuleEngineService.cls"},{"label":"Discount_Schedule__c","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Discount_Schedule__c/"},{"label":"Quote_Approval_Routing.flow-meta.xml","href":"force-app/main/default/flows/Quote_Approval_Routing.flow-meta.xml"}],"objectives":["Use price books to control list and sales prices","Create price rules and price actions","Apply discount schedules and block pricing","Design approval-free discounting for standard customers"],"lessons":[{"title":"Price Books & Price Rules","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"How Prices Are Computed"},{"t":"p","x":"A price book is a named collection of prices, one entry per product, carried by PricebookEntry. Price rules sit on top of the price book and fire when their criteria match, applying a price action that changes the price or discount on the line. Together they turn a static list price into a price that reflects quantity, region, partner tier or customer segment."},{"t":"num","items":["Discount - reduce the price by a set percent","Markup - increase the price by a set percent","Override - replace the price with a fixed value","Add - add a fixed amount to the price, for example a fee"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Prefer percent actions over hard-coded values. A discount schedule against Product_Family__c scales with catalog changes, while an override that hard-codes a price goes stale as soon as the list price moves."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What are the four price actions a price rule can apply?","a":"Discount, Markup, Override and Add."}]},{"title":"Discount Schedules & Block Pricing","mins":14,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Tiered Discounts"},{"t":"p","x":"A discount schedule rewards volume by mapping quantity or amount bands to a discount percent. Each band has a lower and an upper bound; the engine finds the band the quote line falls into and applies that discount. This block pricing is what lets a rep offer 10 percent at 100 seats but 18 percent at 1000 seats without any manual calculation."},{"t":"table","head":["Lower Bound","Upper Bound","Discount Percent"],"rows":[["0","99","0"],["100","499","10"],["500","999","15"],["1000","99999","18"]]},{"t":"code","lang":"soql","x":"SELECT Id, Discount_Type__c, Lower_Bound__c, Upper_Bound__c,\n       Discount_Percent__c, Product_Family__c, Active__c\nFROM Discount_Schedule__c\nWHERE Active__c = TRUE\n  AND Product_Family__c = 'Hardware'\nORDER BY Lower_Bound__c"},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"How does the engine choose a discount for a quantity of 750 units?","a":"It places 750 inside the band from 500 to 999 and applies that band's discount percent of 15."}]},{"title":"Implementing Pricing Logic in Apex","mins":15,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Driving Pricing from a Service"},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"QuoteLineItem qli = [\n  SELECT Id, List_Unit_Price__c, Net_Unit_Price__c\n  FROM QuoteLineItem\n  WHERE Id = :qliId\n];\n\nDecimal pct = PriceRuleEngineService.resolveDiscountPercent(qli.Id);\nDecimal factor = 1 - (pct / 100);\nqli.Net_Unit_Price__c = qli.List_Unit_Price__c * factor;\nupdate qli;"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Keep discounting inside the engine. Letting formula fields and flows overwrite Net_Unit_Price__c independently of the schedule is the fastest way to leak discount on a deal."},{"t":"ex","id":"5.1","stars":2,"title":"Create a Tiered Discount Schedule","obj":"Define discount bands for a product family.","steps":["Pick a product family and its price range","Define four bands with lower and upper bounds","Assign a rising discount percent to each band","Set Active__c to TRUE for each record",{"h":"Check","items":["Four bands cover the full range","Bounds do not overlap","Discounts rise with volume"]}],"verify":"Your Discount_Schedule__c records form a contiguous set of bands with rising Discount_Percent__c values, ready for the price engine to consume."},{"t":"ex","id":"5.2","stars":3,"title":"Write a Volume-Based Price Rule","obj":"Create a price rule that discounts high quantities on the fly.","steps":["Choose a trigger, for example quantity above 500","Choose a price action of type Discount","Set the discount percent to match your schedule band","Test the rule at quantities just below and just above the threshold",{"h":"Check","items":["A trigger is defined","A discount action is set","Both sides of the threshold are tested"]}],"code":{"lang":"apex","x":"Decimal pct = PriceRuleEngineService.resolveDiscountPercent(\n    qli.Product2.Family, qli.Quantity * qli.List_Unit_Price__c);\nif (pct > 0) {\n  qli.Net_Unit_Price__c =\n      qli.List_Unit_Price__c * (1 - (pct / 100));\n  update qli;\n}"},"verify":"Your rule drops the discount only above the volume threshold and pairs with the matching Discount_Schedule__c band tested in ex 5.1."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Pricing Checkup","mins":8,"questions":[{"q":"Which object carries the price of a product inside a price book?","opts":["Pricebook2","PricebookEntry","QuoteLineItem","Product2"],"a":1,"why":"PriceBookEntry pairs Product2 with a Pricebook2 and carries the price."},{"q":"Which price action replaces the list price with a fixed value?","opts":["Discount","Markup","Override","Add"],"a":2,"why":"An override writes a fixed price onto the line regardless of the list price."},{"q":"In a Discount_Schedule__c band from 500 to 999 with 15 percent, what discount applies at 800 units?","opts":["0","10","15","18"],"a":2,"why":"The engine matches the quantity to its band, which in this case is 500-999 at 15 percent."},{"q":"Which field on Discount_Schedule__c tells the engine where a band starts?","opts":["Discount_Percent__c","Upper_Bound__c","Lower_Bound__c","Discount_Type__c"],"a":2,"why":"Lower_Bound__c is the floor of the block; Upper_Bound__c is its ceiling."},{"q":"What does PriceRuleEngineService.resolveDiscountPercent return?","opts":["A list price","A discount percent","An order number","A contract term"],"a":1,"why":"The service resolves the applicable discount percent for a quote line."},{"q":"Which field on the Quote Line Item stores the price after discounting?","opts":["List_Unit_Price__c","Net_Unit_Price__c","ARR__c","Bundle_Option__c"],"a":1,"why":"Net_Unit_Price__c is the post-discount price, while List_Unit_Price__c stores the list value."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"5.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"5.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"cpq-quote-templates","n":6,"title":"Quote Templates & Document Generation","icon":"06","color":"#10B981","tagline":"Quote templates, output documents, e-signature","guide":"06-Quote-Templates-and-Document-Generation.md","art":[{"label":"ContractGenerationService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/ContractGenerationService.cls"},{"label":"Quote metadata","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Quote/"},{"label":"Email templates","href":"force-app/main/default/email/"}],"objectives":["Build reusable Salesforce quote document templates","Generate output documents from live quote data","Control template sections and conditional content","Plan e-signature on the final generated deliverable"],"lessons":[{"title":"Anatomy of a Quote Template","mins":25,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Anatomy of a Quote Template"},{"t":"p","x":"A quote template is a Salesforce document template that renders a quote as a professional deliverable using merge fields bound to the Quote and QuoteLineItem objects. When a rep clicks Generate Document, Salesforce merges the live quote into the template and produces a PDF, Word, or HTML output. Template files version inside force-app/main/default/email/ so they ship with your CPQ metadata instead of drifting in a sandbox."},{"t":"p","x":"Keep templates aligned with the pricing calculation: bind totals to the same fields the calculator writes, such as Total_ARR__c and Discount_Percent__c, so the printed document always matches the quote record."},{"t":"table","head":["Section","Purpose"],"rows":[["Header","Company logo, quote number, revision date, rep and account details"],["Line Items","Products with quantities, list price, discount, and net price per line"],["Totals","Subtotal, discounts, tax, and grand total with currency formatting"],["Terms","Payment terms, validity window, and quote expiration policy"],["Signature","Signer name, title, and the e-signature area"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Design the template in the same order a customer reads it: header, line items, totals, terms, then signature. Every section maps to a separate document area so you can toggle sections independently."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Which Salesforce feature renders a quote template into a PDF or Word output document?","a":"Document templates with merge fields, generated from the live quote through document generation."}]},{"title":"Conditional Content & Branding","mins":30,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Conditional Content & Branding"},{"t":"p","x":"Production templates rarely display the same content to every customer. Conditional content shows or hides sections based on quote data - for example, printing a maintenance add-on page only when the quote contains a service product, or applying a public-sector pricing note for government accounts."},{"t":"num","items":["Wrap a section in a conditional block that tests quote fields such as Total_ARR__c and Contract_Term_Months__c","Add table sections for line items and toggle visibility per section","Apply branding with an HTML outer shell, logo, fonts, and color swatches matching the company palette","Lay out the document in a multi-column area so pricing and terms-and-conditions text sit side by side"]},{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"Dear {{!Quote.Account.Name}},\n\nThank you for choosing {{!Quote.Name}}. Enclosed is your quotation\nwith {{!Quote.Contract_Term_Months__c}} months of contracted service.\n\nTotal annual recurring value: {{!Quote.Total_ARR__c}}\nDiscount applied:             {{!Quote.Discount_Percent__c}}\n\nThis quote is valid for 30 days and can be signed electronically."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Conditional blocks keep one template serving many deal shapes. When a quote type keeps changing its sections, review the conditions before cloning the template - a clone doubles the maintenance burden."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"How do you hide a maintenance section only for quotes with no service line items?","a":"Wrap the section in a conditional block that only renders when a maintenance or service product is present on the quote."}]},{"title":"Generation, Output & E-Signature","mins":40,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Generation, Output & E-Signature"},{"t":"p","x":"Document generation (DocGen) turns the template into a signed-ready deliverable. The engine resolves merge fields, applies conditional content, and renders the document for review before signature. Generate a locked PDF revision so the agreed terms cannot silently drift from the quote record after the deal is executed."},{"t":"list","items":["Native e-signature integration placing signature tabs on the generated PDF","Standard e-signature providers wired back to the quote record for status tracking","Print-and-sign workflows with dated signature blocks for customers without e-signature","Version history so every negotiated screenshot and revision is traceable before execution"]},{"t":"ex","id":"6.1","stars":3,"title":"Build a Quote Template","obj":"Create a reusable quote document template that renders line items and totals from a live quote.","steps":["Open Object Manager, navigate to the Quote object, and create a new document template","Add a header section with the company logo and merge fields for quote number and account name","Add a line-item table section that iterates QuoteLineItem fields: Name, List_Unit_Price__c, Discount_Percent__c, Net_Unit_Price__c, and ARR__c","Add a totals section binding Grand Total and Total_ARR__c","Add a terms section with validity text and a signature placeholder",{"h":"Verify the output","items":["Generate the document from a saved quote with two line items","Confirm line-item discounts flow through and totals recalculate","Confirm the PDF is branded and downloads cleanly"]}],"verify":"Open a test quote, run Generate Document, and confirm the PDF shows both line items, correct net prices, totals, and the required terms text.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"public class QuoteDocumentController {\n\n    @AuraEnabled\n    public static String generatePdf(Id quoteId) {\n        return ContractGenerationService.generateQuoteDocument(\n            quoteId,\n            'PDF'\n        );\n    }\n}"}},{"t":"ex","id":"6.2","stars":2,"title":"Design a Multi-Language Quote Layout","obj":"Adapt a quote template for a second language using a single reusable layout.","steps":["Note the field on Account or Quote that drives localization","Mark the static text blocks with translation metadata","Create localized strings for the second language","Add both variants to the template and bind locale-aware currency formatting",{"h":"Verify the output","items":["Generate the quote in each language","Confirm currency and date formats follow locale rules","Confirm the second language renders on the same template without a clone"]}],"verify":"Generate the same quote in both languages and confirm localized text, date, number, and currency formatting on a single template."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Quote Templates & Document Generation Quiz","mins":8,"questions":[{"q":"Which list names the standard sections of a quote template?","opts":["Header and footer","Header, line items, totals, terms, signature","Accounts, contacts, opportunities","Bundles, rules, and approvals"],"a":1,"why":"Standard template sections are Header, Line Items, Totals, Terms, and Signature."},{"q":"Which feature decides whether a section appears in the output document?","opts":["Merge fields","Conditional content","Price rules","Approval matrices"],"a":1,"why":"Conditional content shows or hides sections based on quote data."},{"q":"Why generate the final quote as a locked PDF revision?","opts":["To hide pricing","To keep the signed terms consistent with the live quote","To speed up approvals","To replace the contract"],"a":1,"why":"A generated revision freezes the agreed terms so nothing drifts after signature."},{"q":"Which merge field renders annual recurring value in the totals section?","opts":["Quote.Discount_Percent__c","Quote.Total_ARR__c","QuoteLineItem.List_Unit_Price__c","Account.Region__c"],"a":1,"why":"Total_ARR__c carries the annual recurring value displayed in the totals table."},{"q":"What is the typical first deliverable produced by document generation?","opts":["Approval chain","A generated PDF for review","A renewal opportunity","A platform event"],"a":1,"why":"DocGen first renders the generated document, generally a PDF, before e-signature."},{"q":"What belongs in an e-signature plan for the generated document?","opts":["Signature tabs bound to the document","Discount schedules","Apex triggers","Price books"],"a":0,"why":"An e-signature plan places signature tabs on the generated output for signers."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"6.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3},{"id":"6.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"cpq-approvals","n":7,"title":"Approval Processes & Advanced Approvals","icon":"07","color":"#EC4899","tagline":"Approval matrices, chains, parallel & advanced approvals","guide":"07-Approvals-and-Advanced-Approvals.md","art":[{"label":"ApprovalRoutingService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/ApprovalRoutingService.cls"},{"label":"Quote Discount Approval Process","href":"force-app/main/default/approvalProcesses/Quote.Discount_Approval_Process.approvalProcess-meta.xml"},{"label":"Approval_Log__c metadata","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Approval_Log__c/"}],"objectives":["Design approval matrices that map discount bands to approvers","Compare standard and advanced Apex-driven approvals","Route approvals by discount threshold on the quote","Audit every approval decision with Approval_Log__c"],"lessons":[{"title":"Approval Matrices & Thresholds","mins":25,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Approval Matrices & Thresholds"},{"t":"p","x":"An approval matrix pairs discount bands with the approver level that must sign off. The band threshold is read from Quote.Discount_Percent__c and the deal size from Total_ARR__c, so a 12 percent discount on a small deal and the same discount on a strategic account can route to different approvers."},{"t":"table","head":["Discount Band (Discount_Percent__c)","Required Approver"],"rows":[["0 - 10 percent","Sales Manager"],["10 - 15 percent","Sales Director"],["15 - 20 percent","VP of Sales"],["Above 20 percent","CFO"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Store the matrix where the routing logic can query it in real time. Compare Discount_Percent__c against bands and fall through from the widest band so the highest discount always wins."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Which approver level must approve a quote discounted 18 percent?","a":"The VP of Sales, because the 18 percent discount falls into the 15-20 percent band."}]},{"title":"Standard vs Advanced Approvals","mins":30,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Standard vs Advanced Approvals"},{"t":"p","x":"Standard approval processes cover fixed routes defined in the approval process designer. Advanced approvals model anything the designer cannot express: matrix-driven approver selection, ordered chains, parallel branches, delegation, and timed escalation, all executed from Apex."},{"t":"table","head":["Capability","Standard Approval","Advanced Apex-Driven"],"rows":[["Routing","Static entry criteria on the record","Dynamic matrix from thresholds and rules"],["Chains","Single fixed path","Ordered chains with multiple approvers"],["Parallel","Not natively modeled","Parallel branches approving concurrently"],["Delegation","In-submission reassignment","Delegation modeled inside routing logic"],["Escalation","Limited","Escalation driven by due dates and reminders"]]},{"t":"num","items":["Chains impose a strict sequence: each approver signs before the next is invited","Parallel approvals invite several approvers at once on separate branches","Delegation lets an approver hand ownership to a delegate while staying accountable","Escalation bumps the approval to the next level or approver when a deadline passes"]},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What is the key difference between a chain and a parallel approval?","a":"A chain approves in strict order; parallel approvals invite several approvers at the same time on independent branches."}]},{"title":"Approval Logic & Audit in Apex","mins":40,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Approval Logic & Audit in Apex"},{"t":"p","x":"The Apex layer reads Quote.Discount_Percent__c, resolves an approver with ApprovalRoutingService.determineApprover, and records the outcome in Approval_Log__c. Every decision carries Quote__c, Approval_Type__c, Approver__c, Decision__c, Discount_Percent__c, Comments__c, and Decision_Date__c so the audit trail is complete."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"public class QuoteApprovalHandler {\n\n    public static void route(Id quoteId) {\n        Quote quote = [SELECT Id, Discount_Percent__c, Approval_Status__c\n                       FROM Quote WHERE Id = :quoteId];\n\n        String approver = ApprovalRoutingService.determineApprover(\n            quote.Discount_Percent__c,\n            quote.Total_ARR__c\n        );\n\n        ApprovalRoutingService.logDecision(\n            quote.Id,\n            'Discount',\n            approver,\n            'Pending',\n            quote.Discount_Percent__c,\n            'Routed by discount threshold matrix'\n        );\n    }\n}"},{"t":"ex","id":"7.1","stars":3,"title":"Route Discount Approvals by Threshold","obj":"Route a quote to the correct approver when the discount crosses a threshold using ApprovalRoutingService.","steps":["Create an Approval_Log__c record with Quote__c, Approval_Type__c, and Decision__c","Call ApprovalRoutingService.determineApprover with quote.Discount_Percent__c and quote.Total_ARR__c","Compare the returned approver against the matrix band for the discount","Write logDecision with the approver, decision, and comments",{"h":"Verify the audit","items":["Run the handler on quotes at 8, 12, 18, and 22 percent discount","Confirm each lands on the approver of the right band","Confirm every run created an Approval_Log__c row with a Decision_Date__c"]}],"verify":"Execute the handler across the four threshold quotes and confirm the approver and audit rows match the matrix in every case.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"String approver = ApprovalRoutingService.determineApprover(\n    quote.Discount_Percent__c,\n    quote.Total_ARR__c\n);\n\nApprovalRoutingService.logDecision(\n    quote.Id,\n    'Discount',\n    approver,\n    'Approved',\n    quote.Discount_Percent__c,\n    'Within the 10-15 percent band'\n);"}},{"t":"ex","id":"7.2","stars":3,"title":"Implement an Approval Chain in Apex","obj":"Implement an ordered three-step approval chain in Apex that logs each stage to Approval_Log__c.","steps":["Define the ordered chain of approvers for the quote","Invoke ApprovalRoutingService.determineApprover for the first step","Iterate the chain and log every step with logDecision before inviting the next approver","Escalate to the final approver when an earlier step is skipped",{"h":"Verify the chain","items":["Check Approval_Log__c shows the steps in the correct order","Confirm no step can start before the previous log row exists","Confirm the final approver is never skipped"]}],"verify":"Run the chain and confirm ordered Approval_Log__c rows for every step, ending with the final approver.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"List<String> chain = new List<String>{\n    ApprovalRoutingService.determineApprover(\n        quote.Discount_Percent__c,\n        quote.Total_ARR__c\n    ),\n    'Sales Director',\n    'VP of Sales'\n};\n\nfor (Integer i = 0; i < chain.size(); i++) {\n    ApprovalRoutingService.logDecision(\n        quote.Id,\n        'Discount',\n        chain[i],\n        'Pending',\n        quote.Discount_Percent__c,\n        'Step ' + (i + 1) + ' of chain'\n    );\n}"}}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Approval Processes & Advanced Approvals Quiz","mins":8,"questions":[{"q":"Above which discount band does the CFO become the required approver?","opts":["0-10 percent","10-15 percent","15-20 percent","Above 20 percent"],"a":3,"why":"Bands above 20 percent of Discount_Percent__c route to the CFO in the matrix."},{"q":"What is an approval chain?","opts":["A list of products","Multiple approvers who must approve in a defined order","A discount schedule","A document template"],"a":1,"why":"Chains enforce a strict sequence of approvers before a quote is approved."},{"q":"Which object stores an approval decision with approver and comments?","opts":["Approval_Log__c","Revenue_Schedule__c","Renewal_Opportunity__c","Training_Question__c"],"a":0,"why":"Approval_Log__c captures Quote__c, Approver__c, Decision__c, Decision_Date__c, and Comments__c."},{"q":"Which field on Quote drives threshold-based routing?","opts":["Total_ARR__c","Discount_Percent__c","Contract_Term_Months__c","Is_Renewal__c"],"a":1,"why":"Discount_Percent__c is compared against the discount matrix to pick the approver."},{"q":"What does a parallel approval do in advanced approvals?","opts":["Approvers sign at the same time on separate branches","Each approver waits for the previous one","The system skips approvers","Approvals are logged after signature"],"a":0,"why":"Parallel approvals invite several approvers concurrently on separate branches."},{"q":"Which practice keeps approvals auditable?","opts":["Logging every decision to Approval_Log__c","Storing decisions in email","Deleting expired quotes","Flagging only strategic accounts"],"a":0,"why":"Writing every decision to Approval_Log__c preserves the full audit trail."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"7.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3},{"id":"7.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"cpq-contracts-renewals","n":8,"title":"Contracts, Amendments & Renewals","icon":"08","color":"#6366F1","tagline":"Contracting, amendments, renewals & co-termination","guide":"08-Contracts-Amendments-and-Renewals.md","art":[{"label":"RenewalQuoteService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/RenewalQuoteService.cls"},{"label":"Contract metadata","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Contract/"},{"label":"Renewal_Opportunity__c metadata","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Renewal_Opportunity__c/"}],"objectives":["Generate contracts from accepted quotes","Handle amendments without breaking the original agreement","Co-terminate subscriptions to a single end date","Automate renewals, uplift, and pipeline creation"],"lessons":[{"title":"Contracting Basics","mins":25,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Contracting Basics"},{"t":"p","x":"A contract is the executed agreement that locks in the quote terms. In a CPQ flow, the accepted quote seeds the Contract record, copying the recurring value, term, renewal, and uplift settings so downstream billing and renewal logic reads one source of truth."},{"t":"table","head":["Contract Field","Purpose"],"rows":[["ARR__c","Annualized recurring value the contract bills each year"],["Auto_Renew__c","Whether the contract rolls over automatically at term end"],["Renewal_Term_Months__c","Length of the renewal term in months"],["Uplift_Percent__c","Price increase applied to the renewal"],["CPQ_Contract_Status__c","Lifecycle state: Draft, Activated, Amended, or Expired"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Copy term and pricing fields from Quote to Contract at the moment the quote is accepted. Contract and quote must never diverge on ARR, term, or uplift - the flag Is_Renewal__c on Quote marks which deals reopen an existing contract."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Which Contract field controls automatic rollover at term end?","a":"Auto_Renew__c - when true, the contract renews automatically instead of expiring."}]},{"title":"Amendments & Co-Termination","mins":30,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Amendments & Co-Termination"},{"t":"p","x":"An amendment adjusts the scope, price, or people under an existing agreement instead of replacing it. Amendment quotes reference the original contract, and when the customer adds a subscription mid-term, co-termination aligns the new term to the existing anchor date so every line renews together."},{"t":"num","items":["Identify the anchor contract and slide the new subscription to its end date","Couple the delta term to the anchor date instead of a standalone term","Recalculate the pro-rated value for the partial first period","Mark the amendment quote with Is_Renewal__c false and Is_Amendment__c true","Update CPQ_Contract_Status__c to Amended on the original contract"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Co-termination changes the original renewal date and the revenue curve of the amended line. Confirm the new end date with the customer in writing - a silent date shift is a dispute waiting to happen."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why do co-terminated subscriptions reuse the anchor contract end date?","a":"One aligned end date lets every line renew together, simplifying billing and renewal processing."}]},{"title":"Renewal Automation","mins":40,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Renewal Automation"},{"t":"p","x":"Automation turns an expiring contract into a renewal pipeline without manual data entry. RenewalQuoteService.buildRenewalPipeline scans contracts that are approaching their end date, creates Renewal_Opportunity__c records with Renewal_Date__c, ARR__c, Uplift_Percent__c, and Status__c, and calculateUpliftedARR applies the uplift to the base recurring value."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"public class RenewalAutomation {\n\n    public static void process(Id contractId) {\n        Contract contract = [SELECT Id, ARR__c, Uplift_Percent__c\n                             FROM Contract WHERE Id = :contractId];\n\n        Renewal_Opportunity__c[] pipeline =\n            RenewalQuoteService.buildRenewalPipeline(contract.Id);\n\n        for (Renewal_Opportunity__c renewal : pipeline) {\n            renewal.ARR__c = RenewalQuoteService.calculateUpliftedARR(\n                renewal.ARR__c,\n                renewal.Uplift_Percent__c\n            );\n            renewal.Status__c = 'Not Started';\n        }\n        update pipeline;\n    }\n}"},{"t":"ex","id":"8.1","stars":3,"title":"Generate a Contract from a Quote","obj":"Generate a contract from an accepted quote and seed the term and pricing fields.","steps":["Accept the quote and read its term values","Create a Contract with AccountId from the quote","Copy ARR__c from Quote.Total_ARR__c and Renewal_Term_Months__c from Quote.Contract_Term_Months__c","Set Auto_Renew__c and CPQ_Contract_Status__c to Draft","Insert the contract and generate the contract document",{"h":"Verify the contract","items":["Confirm the Contract fields match the quote values","Confirm the generated contract document renders from the template","Confirm Renewal_Opportunity__c rows can reuse the contract fields"]}],"verify":"Insert the contract and confirm ARR__c, Auto_Renew__c, and Renewal_Term_Months__c equal the quote source values.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"Contract contract = new Contract(\n    AccountId = quote.AccountId,\n    ARR__c = quote.Total_ARR__c,\n    Auto_Renew__c = true,\n    Renewal_Term_Months__c = quote.Contract_Term_Months__c,\n    CPQ_Contract_Status__c = 'Draft'\n);\ninsert contract;\n\nContractGenerationService.generateContract(quote.Id, contract.Id);"}},{"t":"ex","id":"8.2","stars":2,"title":"Automate Renewal Uplift","obj":"Automate the renewal pipeline and apply uplift to expiring contracts.","steps":["Load contracts whose end date is within the renewal window","Call RenewalQuoteService.buildRenewalPipeline to create Renewal_Opportunity__c rows","Apply calculateUpliftedARR using ARR__c and Uplift_Percent__c","Set Renewal_Date__c and Status__c on each opportunity",{"h":"Verify the uplift","items":["Confirm the new ARR__c equals base ARR plus the uplift percent","Confirm 10 percent uplift on 1000 ARR produces 1100","Confirm each Renewal_Opportunity__c links Account__c and Contract__c"]}],"verify":"Run the automation and confirm the pipeline rows carry uplifted ARR__c, correct Renewal_Date__c, and Status__c.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"Renewal_Opportunity__c renewal =\n    RenewalQuoteService.buildRenewalPipeline(contract.Id)[0];\n\nDecimal upliftedArr = RenewalQuoteService.calculateUpliftedARR(\n    renewal.ARR__c,\n    renewal.Uplift_Percent__c\n);\n\nrenewal.ARR__c = upliftedArr;\nrenewal.Status__c = 'In Progress';\nupdate renewal;"}}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Contracts, Amendments & Renewals Quiz","mins":8,"questions":[{"q":"Which Contract field sets whether the contract rolls over at term end?","opts":["ARR__c","Auto_Renew__c","Renewal_Term_Months__c","Uplift_Percent__c"],"a":1,"why":"Auto_Renew__c drives automatic rollover when true."},{"q":"What does an amendment do to a contract?","opts":["Deletes the contract","Adjusts scope or pricing under the same agreement","Creates a quote","Triggers e-signature"],"a":1,"why":"Amendments modify the original agreement without replacing it."},{"q":"What does co-termination mean?","opts":["All subscriptions share the same renewal date","Quotes expire in 30 days","Approvals run in parallel","Revenue is recognized immediately"],"a":0,"why":"Co-termination aligns every subscription to one anchor end date."},{"q":"Which field stores the price increase applied at renewal?","opts":["Uplift_Percent__c","ARR__c","Auto_Renew__c","CPQ_Contract_Status__c"],"a":0,"why":"Uplift_Percent__c controls the renewal price uplift on Contract and Renewal_Opportunity__c."},{"q":"Which service builds the renewal pipeline of opportunities?","opts":["RenewalQuoteService.buildRenewalPipeline","ApprovalRoutingService.determineApprover","OrderFulfillmentService.advanceFulfillment","BillingScheduleService.buildBillingSchedule"],"a":0,"why":"buildRenewalPipeline scans expiring contracts and creates Renewal_Opportunity__c rows."},{"q":"Which set of values belongs to the CPQ contract lifecycle?","opts":["Draft","Activated","Expired","Draft, Activated, Amended, or Expired"],"a":3,"why":"CPQ_Contract_Status__c tracks states such as Draft, Activated, Amended, and Expired."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"8.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3},{"id":"8.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"cpq-orders-fulfillment","n":9,"title":"Orders & Fulfillment","icon":"09","color":"#14B8A6","tagline":"Order generation, orchestration, assets & fulfillment","guide":"09-Orders-and-Fulfillment.md","art":[{"label":"OrderFulfillmentService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/OrderFulfillmentService.cls"},{"label":"Order metadata","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Order/"},{"label":"Order Status Update flow","href":"force-app/main/default/flows/Order_Fulfillment_Status_Update.flow-meta.xml"}],"objectives":["Generate orders from contracted quotes and contracts","Orchestrate fulfillment across status hand-offs","Manage fulfilled products as assets post-sale","Integrate order numbers back from the ERP"],"lessons":[{"title":"From Quote to Order","mins":25,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"From Quote to Order"},{"t":"p","x":"An Order represents what the customer actually purchased and is the record fulfillment and billing act on. Orders come from contracted quotes or contracts, and the Order carries fulfillment fields updated by the Order_Fulfillment_Status_Update flow."},{"t":"num","items":["Accept the quote and generate the contract","Create an Order from the contracted quote with one line per QuoteLineItem","Copy Total_ARR__c from the contract to the order","Seed Fulfillment_Status__c as Pending and leave the ERP number blank","Activate the order to make it eligible for fulfillment processing"]},{"t":"table","head":["Order Field","Purpose"],"rows":[["Fulfillment_Status__c","Current state: Pending, In Progress, Shipped, Fulfilled, or Integration Failed"],["Fulfillment_Date__c","Date the order was completed or shipped"],["ERP_Order_Number__c","External order reference written by the ERP integration"],["Total_ARR__c","Annualized recurring value of the order, copied from the contract"]]},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Which Order field holds the external system reference after delivery?","a":"ERP_Order_Number__c - the ERP integration writes the external order number into it."}]},{"title":"Fulfillment Orchestration & Assets","mins":30,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Fulfillment Orchestration & Assets"},{"t":"p","x":"Fulfillment is a state machine. The Order_Fulfillment_Status_Update flow advances status on schedule or when the ERP confirms shipment, and the Apex service keeps the audit honest by refusing out-of-order transitions."},{"t":"num","items":["Pending - created and waiting for fulfillment","In Progress - picked, packed, or provisioning started","Shipped - handed to the carrier or activated in the ERP","Fulfilled - delivered and closed on the order","Integration Failed - the ERP never confirmed the order"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"When fulfillment completes, create the Asset records that represent the delivered products. Order lines that ship become the licenses, hardware, and services your renewal logic later matches."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What does the flow Order_Fulfillment_Status_Update do?","a":"It advances Order Fulfillment_Status__c between states as fulfillment events occur, keeping the order in sync."}]},{"title":"Tracking Fulfillment in Apex","mins":40,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Tracking Fulfillment in Apex"},{"t":"p","x":"Apex centralizes the status rules so flows and integrations cannot drive an order backwards or skip steps. OrderFulfillmentService.updateFulfillmentStatus validates the transition and stamps Fulfillment_Date__c, while advanceFulfillment moves the order forward one valid step at a time."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"public class FulfillmentAutomation {\n\n    public static void onShipment(Id orderId, Date shipDate) {\n        OrderFulfillmentService.updateFulfillmentStatus(\n            orderId,\n            'Shipped',\n            shipDate\n        );\n\n        OrderFulfillmentService.advanceFulfillment(orderId);\n    }\n}"},{"t":"ex","id":"9.1","stars":3,"title":"Create Orders from a Contracted Quote","obj":"Create an order from a contracted quote and seed fulfillment tracking fields.","steps":["Load the contracted quote and its contract","Build the Order with one line per quote line item","Copy Total_ARR__c from the contract ARR__c","Set Fulfillment_Status__c to Pending and activate the order",{"h":"Verify the order","items":["Confirm the order carries all contracted line items","Confirm Total_ARR__c matches the contract","Confirm Fulfillment_Status__c reads Pending"]}],"verify":"Insert the order and confirm line items, Total_ARR__c, and the initial Pending status all match the source contract.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"Order order = OrderFulfillmentService.ordersFromContract(contract.Id);\n\norder.Fulfillment_Status__c = 'Pending';\norder.Total_ARR__c = contract.ARR__c;\norder.Fulfillment_Date__c = null;\n\ninsert order;"}},{"t":"ex","id":"9.2","stars":2,"title":"Track Fulfillment Status","obj":"Drive an order through its fulfillment states and stamp the record.","steps":["Call updateFulfillmentStatus to move the order to In Progress","Advance the order with advanceFulfillment until it reaches Shipped","Update the ERP_Order_Number__c from the integration payload","Confirm Fulfillment_Date__c is stamped on completion",{"h":"Verify the transitions","items":["Confirm the order reached Fulfilled after the final advance","Confirm invalid backward transitions are rejected","Confirm the status flow kept Fulfillment_Date__c accurate"]}],"verify":"Run the sequence and confirm the order ends at Fulfilled with the ERP number and Fulfillment_Date__c populated.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"OrderFulfillmentService.updateFulfillmentStatus(\n    orderId,\n    'In Progress',\n    Date.today()\n);\n\nOrderFulfillmentService.advanceFulfillment(orderId);\nOrderFulfillmentService.advanceFulfillment(orderId);"}}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Orders & Fulfillment Quiz","mins":8,"questions":[{"q":"Which Order field tracks the fulfillment state?","opts":["Fulfillment_Status__c","Total_ARR__c","ERP_Order_Number__c","CPQ_Contract_Status__c"],"a":0,"why":"Fulfillment_Status__c tracks Pending, In Progress, Shipped, Fulfilled, and Integration Failed."},{"q":"What normally sources an Order in the quote-to-cash flow?","opts":["A Revenue_Schedule__c record","A contracted quote or contract","An Approval_Log__c row","A Discount_Schedule__c row"],"a":1,"why":"Orders are generated from contracted quotes or contracts after acceptance."},{"q":"Which field links the order to the external ERP?","opts":["ERP_Order_Number__c","Fulfillment_Date__c","Total_ARR__c","Fulfillment_Status__c"],"a":0,"why":"The ERP integration writes its reference into ERP_Order_Number__c."},{"q":"Why do fulfilled products become Asset records?","opts":["Assets represent delivered products managed post-sale","Assets store invoice discounts","Assets replace orders","Assets track approvals"],"a":0,"why":"Assets represent the delivered products so renewal and support logic can match them."},{"q":"Which flow keeps Order fulfillment status current?","opts":["Order_Fulfillment_Status_Update","Quote.Discount_Approval_Process","Contract generation flow","Billing schedule flow"],"a":0,"why":"Order_Fulfillment_Status_Update advances the order across fulfillment events."},{"q":"What does advanceFulfillment do?","opts":["Moves the order to the next valid fulfillment status","Deletes the order","Calculates the discount","Creates a quote"],"a":0,"why":"advanceFulfillment moves the order one valid status forward at a time."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"9.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3},{"id":"9.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"cpq-billing-revenue","n":10,"title":"Billing, Invoicing & Revenue Recognition","icon":"10","color":"#F97316","tagline":"Billing schedules, invoicing, ASC 606 / IFRS 15 recognition","guide":"10-Billing-Invoicing-and-Revenue-Recognition.md","art":[{"label":"BillingScheduleService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/BillingScheduleService.cls"},{"label":"RevenueRecognitionService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/RevenueRecognitionService.cls"},{"label":"Revenue_Schedule__c metadata","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Revenue_Schedule__c/"},{"label":"billing.soql","href":"scripts/soql/billing.soql"}],"objectives":["Build invoice-ready billing schedules from orders","Generate invoices across recognition methods","Apply the ASC 606 / IFRS 15 five-step model","Recognize revenue over time and roll it up for reporting"],"lessons":[{"title":"Billing Schedules & Invoicing","mins":25,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Billing Schedules & Invoicing"},{"t":"p","x":"A billing schedule turns one order into the series of periods the customer is billed for. Each Revenue_Schedule__c row captures Amount__c, Recognized_Amount__c, Recognition_Date__c, Recognition_Method__c, Status__c, and Period__c, so billing, invoicing, and recognition all read the same schedule."},{"t":"table","head":["Recognition_Method__c","Behavior"],"rows":[["Straight Line","An equal amount recognized each period across the schedule"],["Milestone","Recognition unlocked at agreed milestones or delivery events"],["Percent Complete","Recognition follows the measured percentage of completion"],["Immediate","The entire amount is recognized at one point in time"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Straight Line is the default for subscription revenue because it maps cleanly to ARR. Pick Milestone and Percent Complete only when delivery evidence genuinely backs the alternative pattern."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Which Recognition_Method__c value recognizes an equal amount every period?","a":"Straight Line - it divides the contract value evenly across the schedule periods."}]},{"title":"Revenue Recognition (ASC 606 / IFRS 15)","mins":30,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Revenue Recognition (ASC 606 / IFRS 15)"},{"t":"p","x":"ASC 606 and IFRS 15 define the five-step model for recognizing revenue. The model decides when and how much revenue a company records, and Revenue Cloud maps those steps to orders, schedules, and recognition events instead of booking revenue at invoice time."},{"t":"num","items":["Identify the contract with the customer","Identify the performance obligations in the contract","Determine the transaction price","Allocate the transaction price to the performance obligations","Recognize revenue when, or as, each obligation is satisfied"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Over-time recognition uses progress toward completing a performance obligation. When the obligation is satisfied at a point in time, Immediate recognition triggers at that moment and the Revenue_Schedule__c row flips to Recognized."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"How many steps are in the ASC 606 revenue recognition model?","a":"Five - identify the contract, identify obligations, determine price, allocate price, and recognize revenue."}]},{"title":"Automating Recognition in Apex","mins":40,"blocks":[{"t":"h","x":"Automating Recognition in Apex"},{"t":"p","x":"Automation turns accepted orders into recognized revenue without spreadsheets. BillingScheduleService.buildBillingSchedule generates the Revenue_Schedule__c periods from the order, and RevenueRecognitionService.recognizeRevenue marks the rows whose Recognition_Date__c has arrived, updating Recognized_Amount__c and Status__c."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"public class RevenueAutomation {\n\n    public static void process(Id orderId) {\n        BillingScheduleService.buildBillingSchedule(orderId);\n        RevenueRecognitionService.recognizeRevenue(orderId);\n    }\n}"},{"t":"ex","id":"10.1","stars":3,"title":"Build a Billing Schedule","obj":"Build a twelve-period billing schedule from an order with straight-line recognition.","steps":["Select Recognition_Method__c of Straight Line for the schedule","Call BillingScheduleService.buildBillingSchedule with the order","Verify one Revenue_Schedule__c row per period with a monthly Recognition_Date__c","Confirm the schedule amounts sum to the order Total_ARR__c",{"h":"Verify the schedule","items":["Confirm twelve rows exist for a twelve-month term","Confirm each row carries Amount__c and Period__c","Confirm the sum of Amount__c equals the order value"]}],"verify":"Build the schedule and confirm twelve monthly rows, correct periods, and a total equal to the order value.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"Revenue_Schedule__c[] schedule =\n    BillingScheduleService.buildBillingSchedule(order.Id);\n\nSystem.assertEquals(12, schedule.size());"}},{"t":"ex","id":"10.2","stars":3,"title":"Recognize Revenue Over Time","obj":"Recognize revenue progressively as schedule periods arrive.","steps":["Load the schedule lines for the order","Call RevenueRecognitionService.recognizeRevenue for the order","Mark rows with Recognition_Date__c on or before today as Recognized","Update Recognized_Amount__c on each recognized row",{"h":"Verify recognition","items":["Confirm only past-due periods are recognized","Confirm future periods stay pending","Confirm the recognized total matches the periods elapsed"]}],"verify":"Advance recognition and confirm past-due periods are Recognized, future ones remain pending, and totals roll up correctly.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"RevenueRecognitionService.recognizeRevenue(order.Id);\n\nfor (Revenue_Schedule__c line : BillingScheduleService.buildBillingSchedule(order.Id)) {\n    if (line.Recognition_Date__c <= Date.today()) {\n        line.Status__c = 'Recognized';\n        line.Recognized_Amount__c = line.Amount__c;\n    }\n}"}}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Billing, Invoicing & Revenue Recognition Quiz","mins":8,"questions":[{"q":"Which list shows valid Recognition_Method__c values?","opts":["Straight Line, Milestone, Percent Complete, Immediate","Daily, Weekly, Monthly","Draft, Activation, Billed","Low, Medium, High"],"a":0,"why":"Revenue_Schedule__c.Recognition_Method__c accepts Straight Line, Milestone, Percent Complete, and Immediate."},{"q":"Which method recognizes an equal amount each period?","opts":["Straight Line","Milestone","Percent Complete","Immediate"],"a":0,"why":"Straight Line spreads the amount evenly across all schedule periods."},{"q":"How many steps are in the ASC 606 revenue model?","opts":["Three","Five","Seven","Ten"],"a":1,"why":"ASC 606 and IFRS 15 use a five-step model: contract, obligations, price, allocation, recognition."},{"q":"Which object stores the per-period revenue lines?","opts":["Revenue_Schedule__c","Approval_Log__c","Renewal_Opportunity__c","Discount_Schedule__c"],"a":0,"why":"Revenue_Schedule__c holds Amount__c, Recognized_Amount__c, Recognition_Date__c, and Status__c per period."},{"q":"What does Recognized_Amount__c track?","opts":["How much revenue has been recognized to date","The list price","The quote discount","The contract term"],"a":0,"why":"Recognized_Amount__c records cumulative revenue recognized for that schedule line."},{"q":"Which service builds an invoice-ready billing schedule?","opts":["BillingScheduleService.buildBillingSchedule","OrderFulfillmentService.updateFulfillmentStatus","ApprovalRoutingService.determineApprover","RenewalQuoteService.calculateUpliftedARR"],"a":0,"why":"buildBillingSchedule generates the Revenue_Schedule__c periods from the order."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"10.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3},{"id":"10.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"cpq-rlm","n":11,"title":"Revenue Lifecycle Management (RLM)","icon":"11","color":"#A855F7","tagline":"The next-gen Revenue Cloud: catalog, pricing, transactions","guide":"11-Revenue-Lifecycle-Management.md","art":[{"label":"Revenue_Schedule__c object","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Revenue_Schedule__c/"},{"label":"Revenue_Sync__e platform event","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Revenue_Sync__e/"},{"label":"RevenueRecognitionService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/RevenueRecognitionService.cls"}],"objectives":["Explain the RLM architecture and why it replaced the managed package","Describe the modern Revenue Cloud objects for catalog, pricing and transactions","Compare the CPQ and RLM data models","Model subscriptions and usage pricing on the RLM platform","Plan a phased adoption from SBQQ to RLM"],"lessons":[{"title":"What Is Revenue Lifecycle Management?","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Revenue Lifecycle Management (RLM) is Salesforce's next-generation Revenue Cloud platform. Built natively on core objects, it unifies catalog, pricing and transactions so products, quotes, orders, subscriptions and usage revenue live in one platform — with no managed package layer on top."},{"t":"p","x":"For years CPQ shipped as the SBQQ__ managed package, where products, quotes, lines and subscriptions all lived in heavy custom objects. RLM replaces that layer with native objects plus product lifecycle objects: ProductCatalog organizes the catalog, ProductSellingModel groups how a product is offered, PriceBookEntry records the sellable price, and Subscription, Usage and BillingTransaction capture the transaction outcome. The result is a cleaner data model, deeper platform integration, and one code base Salesforce upgrades in core."},{"t":"table","head":["RLM native capability","Legacy SBQQ equivalent"],"rows":[["ProductCatalog + ProductCategory","SBQQ__Product__c and SBQQ__ProductOption__c"],["ProductSellingModel + PriceBookEntry","SBQQ__QuoteLine__c price book and pricing fields"],["Native Quote and QuoteLineItem","SBQQ__Quote__c and SBQQ__QuoteLine__c"],["Order, Asset, Usage and Subscription","SBQQ__Order__c and SBQQ__Subscription__c"],["Revenue_Sync__e platform events","SBQQ__Quote__c sync API and checkout events"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"RLM does not require uninstalling legacy CPQ. Both engines can coexist in Revenue Cloud while you migrate. Use Revenue_Sync__e platform events to keep the two in sync, then retire SBQQ when the last quote moves to RLM."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Which object in RLM replaces SBQQ__Product__c as the home of the catalog?","a":"ProductCatalog, organized into trees by ProductCategory, priced through ProductSellingModel and PriceBookEntry."}]},{"title":"The RLM Data Model","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The RLM data model separates what you sell (the catalog), how you sell it (selling models and price books) and the transactions that follow (quotes, orders, subscriptions and usage). Learning these tables is the fastest way to toggle between legacy CPQ and RLM questions on the certification."},{"t":"p","x":"ProductCatalog is the container for every sellable item, organized into ProductCategory hierarchies you can reuse across orgs. ProductSellingModel groups the selling options that apply to a product, such as country, language and maturity. PriceBookEntry records the price you actually charge for a product under a selling model, while Subscription captures the recurring entitlement that drives recognitions and usage records."},{"t":"table","head":["Modern RLM object","Purpose"],"rows":[["ProductCatalog","The sellable catalog container that replaced SBQQ__Product__c"],["ProductCategory","Classifies catalog items into browseable, reusable trees"],["ProductSellingModel","Groups selling options such as country, language and maturity"],["PriceBookEntry","Records the price of a product under a selling model"],["Subscription","The recurring entitlement created from a quote or order"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Do not mix engines in one flow. SBQQ__ references point to the managed package, while ProductCatalog and PriceBookEntry belong to RLM. Pick one engine per integration and bridge the gap with Revenue_Sync__e events."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why does RLM use PriceBookEntry alongside ProductSellingModel instead of a single SBQQ price book?","a":"It decouples the catalog from pricing, so one catalog item can be priced differently per selling model, price book and country through separate PriceBookEntry records."}]},{"title":"Subscriptions, Usage & Adoption","mins":14,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"In RLM a quote turns into an Order, the Order creates an Asset, and the Asset drives a Subscription with a price, principal amount and renewal term. For usage-based pricing, Usage records attach to the subscription and are metered from integration events such as Revenue_Sync__e, then billed through usage summaries and billing transactions."},{"t":"num","items":["Assess the footprint: inventory every SBQQ__ object, price rule and approval running in production","Map the data model: translate legacy products, price books and options to ProductCatalog, ProductSellingModel and PriceBookEntry","Build the catalog: load catalog items, categories and selling models through the Revenue Cloud catalog APIs","Model pricing: create price books and price book entries, then validate the first quote end to end","Run a pilot: migrate one product line or business unit and compare quote totals with legacy output","Cut over and decommission: move remaining quotes, subscriptions and integrations, then archive SBQQ"]},{"t":"code","lang":"json","x":"{\n  \"source\": \"Order-000123\",\n  \"product\": {\n    \"catalog\": \"Enterprise_Cloud\",\n    \"sellingModel\": \"US_Enterprise_3yr\",\n    \"priceBookEntry\": \"PBE-1044\",\n    \"listPrice\": 1200\n  },\n  \"subscription\": {\n    \"startDate\": \"2026-10-01\",\n    \"termMonths\": 36,\n    \"maturity\": \"FULL\",\n    \"billingFrequency\": \"QUARTERLY\",\n    \"principalAmount\": 1200\n  },\n  \"usage\": {\n    \"metered\": true,\n    \"unitOfMeasure\": \"API_CALLS\"\n  }\n}"},{"t":"ex","id":"11.1","stars":1,"title":"Compare CPQ vs RLM Objects","obj":"Build a mapping table that translates between the legacy SBQQ__ data model and the RLM modern objects.","steps":["Create a side-by-side table with three columns: Purpose, Legacy SBQQ object and RLM object","Map products, product options, quotes, quote lines and subscriptions at a minimum","Write the RLM equivalent using ProductCatalog, ProductCategory, ProductSellingModel, PriceBookEntry, Quote, QuoteLineItem and Subscription","Add one paragraph explaining why the RLM model separates catalog, selling model and price",{"h":"Starter rows","items":["SKU catalog container: SBQQ__Product__c → ProductCatalog","Product grouping: SBQQ__ProductOption__c → ProductCategory","Offered price: SBQQ__QuoteLine__c price fields → PriceBookEntry"]}],"verify":"You can click through the mapping and explain each row. Your paragraph names at least ProductCatalog, ProductSellingModel and PriceBookEntry."},{"t":"ex","id":"11.2","stars":2,"title":"Model a Subscription with RLM","obj":"Design the JSON payload and object fields that turn a sold quote into an RLM subscription with recurring revenue.","steps":["Write a JSON payload for a 3-year subscription with a quarterly billing frequency","Use catalog fields: ProductCatalog, ProductSellingModel and PriceBookEntry","Add the subscription fields such as startDate, termMonths, maturity and principalAmount","Add usage fields when the subscription is metered, including unitOfMeasure","Map the annual recurring value to QuoteLineItem.ARR__c and Quote.Total_ARR__c in a follow-up Apex or Flow"],"verify":"Your JSON decodes cleanly and every numeric field maps to PriceBookEntry or to an ARR field used by the academy reports.","code":{"lang":"json","x":"{\n  \"subscription\": {\n    \"catalog\": \"Enterprise_Cloud\",\n    \"sellingModel\": \"US_Enterprise_3yr\",\n    \"termMonths\": 36,\n    \"billingFrequency\": \"QUARTERLY\"\n  },\n  \"product\": {\n    \"priceBookEntry\": \"PBE-1044\",\n    \"listPrice\": 1200\n  },\n  \"metering\": {\n    \"enabled\": true,\n    \"unitOfMeasure\": \"API_CALLS\"\n  }\n}"}}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Revenue Lifecycle Management (RLM) quiz","mins":10,"questions":[{"q":"In Revenue Lifecycle Management, which object serves as the next-generation catalog that replaces the legacy SBQQ__Product__c?","opts":["ProductCatalog","PriceBookEntry","SBQQ__QuoteLine__c","Revenue_Schedule__c"],"a":0,"why":"ProductCatalog is the RLM catalog container, organized by ProductCategory and priced through PriceBookEntry."},{"q":"Which RLM object groups the selling options, such as country and maturity, that determine how a product is offered?","opts":["ProductSellingModel","ProductCategory","PriceBook","Order"],"a":0,"why":"ProductSellingModel bundles the selling options; ProductCategory only classifies catalog items."},{"q":"What is the key architectural difference between legacy CPQ and RLM?","opts":["RLM is built natively on core objects while legacy CPQ depends on SBQQ__ managed objects","Legacy CPQ is native and RLM is a managed package","Both rely on the same SBQQ__ objects","RLM requires a separate org entirely"],"a":0,"why":"Legacy CPQ rides on the SBQQ__ managed package; RLM runs on core plus product lifecycle objects."},{"q":"Which field on Revenue_Schedule__c holds the amount of revenue already recognized for that schedule line?","opts":["Recognized_Amount__c","Amount__c","Recognition_Date__c","Status__c"],"a":0,"why":"Recognized_Amount__c tracks recognized revenue while Amount__c is the scheduled total and Status__c the progress flag."},{"q":"In RLM subscription pricing, the price you charge for a product under a selling model typically comes from which object?","opts":["PriceBookEntry","SBQQ__QuoteLine__c","Discount_Schedule__c","Approval_Log__c"],"a":0,"why":"PriceBookEntry records the sellable price per product and selling model, decoupled from the catalog."},{"q":"Which field on Renewal_Opportunity__c stores the annual recurring revenue used to size the renewal pipeline?","opts":["ARR__c","Uplift_Percent__c","Renewal_Date__c","Status__c"],"a":0,"why":"ARR__c carries the recurring revenue of the renewal; Uplift_Percent__c only adjusts the uplift and Renewal_Date__c sets timing."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"11.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":1},{"id":"11.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"cpq-automation-integration","n":12,"title":"CPQ Automation & Integration","icon":"12","color":"#0EA5E9","tagline":"Flows, Apex services, platform events & external pricing","guide":"12-CPQ-Automation-and-Integration.md","art":[{"label":"QuoteTriggerHandlerService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/QuoteTriggerHandlerService.cls"},{"label":"Quote_Approval_Routing flow","href":"force-app/main/default/flows/Quote_Approval_Routing.flow-meta.xml"},{"label":"Revenue_Sync__e platform event","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Revenue_Sync__e/"},{"label":"quote.apex script","href":"scripts/apex/quote.apex"}],"objectives":["Choose between Flow and Apex per requirement","Write trigger handlers that respect the Single Action and recursion limits","Publish and subscribe to Revenue_Sync__e platform events","Integrate external pricing through callouts and named credentials","Handle callout failures and retries with correlation ids"],"lessons":[{"title":"Flow vs Apex: Choosing the Right Tool","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Automation on CPQ and Revenue Cloud starts declarative: Flow handles most record-driven logic without writing Apex. You escalate to Apex when a requirement needs external HTTP callouts, complex loops, or behavior Flow cannot express — and you must always protect the org from limits and recursion."},{"t":"table","head":["Scenario","Recommended tool"],"rows":[["Quote approval routing on Discount_Percent__c thresholds","Flow"],["Notify the revenue team when a sync event fires","Flow"],["Call an external REST pricing API and parse the response","Apex"],["Recompute a bundle through deep product rule logic","Apex (PriceRuleEngineService)"],["Publish many Revenue_Sync__e events in a bulk sync","Apex (bulk)"],["A scheduled renewal reminder at contract end","Flow (scheduled path)"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Rule of thumb: use Flow until a requirement touches a callout or complex multi-entity math, then wrap the heavy logic in an Apex service such as QuoteCalculatorService and invoke it from a trigger handler or a Flow-invoked InvocableMethod."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"You must POST a quote to an external pricing endpoint and update the quote lines from the reply. Flow or Apex?","a":"Apex. Flows cannot make synchronous HTTP callouts. Keep the decision logic in a service such as PriceRuleEngineService or QuoteCalculatorService and call it from the trigger handler."}]},{"title":"Triggers, Handlers & Recursion","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The trigger handler pattern keeps trigger files empty and moves all logic into a service class. A single trigger delegates to a handler, the handler reads Trigger.operationType, and a static boolean guard stops the same quote from re-entering heavy logic when the quote updates itself."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"trigger QuoteTrigger on Quote (before insert, after insert, before update, after update) {\n    QuoteTriggerHandlerService.run(Trigger.operationType);\n}\n\npublic with sharing class QuoteTriggerHandlerService {\n    public static Boolean alreadyFired = false;\n\n    public static void run(System.TriggerOperation op) {\n        if (!alreadyFired) {\n            alreadyFired = true;\n            QuoteCalculatorService.applyPricing(Trigger.new);\n            ApprovalRoutingService.route(Trigger.new);\n        }\n    }\n}"},{"t":"list","items":["One trigger per object, one handler service per trigger","Handle before events for validation and after events for DML","Never bypass the handler from another automation","Cover each handler with at least one validation and one regression test"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Recursion is the classic Apex pitfall: an after-update trigger updates the same Quote, which fires the trigger again. Guard every handler with a static boolean and scope DML through services such as QuoteCalculatorService or ApprovalRoutingService only while the guard is held."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why does the handler use a static boolean like alreadyFired?","a":"Apex static state lives for the entire trigger transaction, so the guard runs the heavy service once even when the quote is touched again by its own update."}]},{"title":"Platform Events & External Pricing","mins":14,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Platform events decouple systems. Revenue_Sync__e carries a payload plus direction, status, correlation and retry metadata, and subscribers can run in Flow, in Apex or as async subscribers. It is the recommended bridge between legacy CPQ, RLM and external ERP systems."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"String jsonBody = Json.serialize(new Map<String, Object>{\n    'quoteId' => 'Quote-000123',\n    'discount' => 12.5\n});\n\nRevenue_Sync__e evt = new Revenue_Sync__e(\n    Source_Object__c = 'Quote-000123',\n    Payload__c = jsonBody,\n    Direction__c = 'OUTBOUND',\n    Integration_Type__c = 'PRICING',\n    Correlation_Id__c = 'qc-000123-v1',\n    Retry_Count__c = 0,\n    Status__c = 'PENDING'\n);\n\nDatabase.SaveResult sr = EventBus.publish(evt);\nSystem.debug('Published event id: ' + sr.getId());"},{"t":"num","items":["Set an HTTP timeout and always catch System.CalloutException","Send the same Correlation_Id__c on retries so the external system can dedupe","Use Revenue_Sync__e.Retry_Count__c to cap retries before failing the record to a manual queue","Store secrets in Named Credentials and verify the remote site, never in code","Bulkify: respect Limits.getCallouts() and commit all results in one loop"]},{"t":"ex","id":"12.1","stars":2,"title":"Publish and Subscribe to a Platform Event","obj":"Publish Revenue_Sync__e from Apex when a quote syncs, then subscribe to it in a Flow to log the payload on Approval_Log__c.","steps":["Build an EventBus.publish block that emits an event per changed quote with Source_Object__c, Payload__c and Correlation_Id__c","Set Retry_Count__c to 0 and Status__c to PENDING on the first attempt","Create a Flow that triggers on the platform event and inserts an Approval_Log__c row with the Approval_Type__c and Comments__c pulled from the payload","Run the change and confirm exactly one log row appears for one published event","Add a guard so retried events with Status__c ERROR do not double-log"],"verify":"You see exactly one Approval_Log__c row per published event, the Correlation_Id__c matches, and resubmitting a failed event does not duplicate the log.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"List<Revenue_Sync__e> events = new List<Revenue_Sync__e>();\nfor (Quote q : Trigger.new) {\n    events.add(new Revenue_Sync__e(\n        Source_Object__c = 'Quote-' + q.Id,\n        Payload__c = Json.serialize(new Map<String, Object>{ 'totalARR' => q.Total_ARR__c }),\n        Direction__c = 'INBOUND',\n        Integration_Type__c = 'SYNC',\n        Correlation_Id__c = String.valueOf(q.Id),\n        Retry_Count__c = 0,\n        Status__c = 'PENDING'\n    ));\n}\nList<Database.SaveResult> results = EventBus.publish(events);"}},{"t":"ex","id":"12.2","stars":3,"title":"Call an External Pricing API","obj":"Call out to an external pricing endpoint from QuoteTriggerHandlerService and update the QuoteLineItem pricing fields from the response.","steps":["Build an HttpRequest against the endpoint recorded in the integration metadata","Serialize the line list price and discount into the payload with a Correlation_Id__c","Set the HTTP method, content type and a Named Credential so no secret lives in code","Parse the response and update QuoteLineItem List_Unit_Price__c and Net_Unit_Price__c","Catch System.CalloutException and raise Revenue_Sync__e with Status__c ERROR so retries respect Retry_Count__c"],"verify":"A quote change calls the endpoint once, the prices update, and a failed callout surfaces as an error event with the correlation id intact.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();\nreq.setEndpoint('callout:PricingAPI/quote/price');\nreq.setMethod('POST');\nreq.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');\nreq.setBody(jsonBody);\n\nHttpResponse res = new Http().send(req);\nif (res.getStatusCode() == 200) {\n    Map<String, Object> parsed = (Map<String, Object>) JSON.deserializeUntyped(res.getBody());\n    for (QuoteLineItem li : Trigger.newMap.values()) {\n        li.Net_Unit_Price__c = (Decimal) parsed.get('netPrice');\n    }\n}"}}]}],"quiz":{"title":"CPQ Automation & Integration quiz","mins":10,"questions":[{"q":"Which scenario is the clearest candidate for Apex over Flow?","opts":["Call an external REST pricing API and parse the response","Route a quote approval based on Discount_Percent__c thresholds","Send a Chatter post when a quote reaches Approved","Show a screen component with quote totals"],"a":0,"why":"Flows cannot make synchronous HTTP callouts, so external pricing integration belongs in Apex."},{"q":"Which statement best describes the trigger handler pattern?","opts":["One trigger per object delegates to a service, and a static boolean guards recursion","Every automation lives inside the trigger body","Handlers are only needed when Flows are unavailable","A handler removes the need for tests"],"a":0,"why":"The handler pattern keeps triggers thin, centralizes logic in services, and the static flag stops recursive re-entry."},{"q":"Which Apex API publishes a platform event such as Revenue_Sync__e?","opts":["EventBus.publish","Database.insert","HttpResponse.send","Approval.process"],"a":0,"why":"EventBus.publish is the platform event API; Database.insert is for records and Approval.process for approvals."},{"q":"How do you keep a pricing callout idempotent across retries?","opts":["Send the same Correlation_Id__c each time so the external system dedupes","Change the payload slightly per retry","Only send a correlation id on failures","Publish one event per retry"],"a":0,"why":"A stable Correlation_Id__c lets the external system recognize and ignore duplicate requests."},{"q":"A failed pricing integration increments a field so you can cap retries. Which field of Revenue_Sync__e is it?","opts":["Retry_Count__c","Source_Object__c","Direction__c","Payload__c"],"a":0,"why":"Retry_Count__c tracks attempts, while the others describe routing and content, not attempts."},{"q":"Which scenario is best handled declaratively in Flow without Apex?","opts":["Route quote approvals by Discount_Percent__c across static record values","Call out to an external ERP for list prices","Publish many platform events in a bulk loop at scale","Compute a bundle discount across 50 products with product rules"],"a":0,"why":"Threshold-based approval routing on static record values is exactly what a Flow does well."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"12.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"12.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"cpq-analytics","n":13,"title":"Reports, Dashboards & Revenue Analytics","icon":"13","color":"#84CC16","tagline":"ARR, discount leakage, pipeline & recognition reporting","guide":"13-Analytics-and-Revenue-Reporting.md","art":[{"label":"Quote_Pipeline_ARR report","href":"force-app/main/default/reports/unfiled$public/Quote_Pipeline_ARR.report-meta.xml"},{"label":"Discount_Leakage report","href":"force-app/main/default/reports/unfiled$public/Discount_Leakage.report-meta.xml"},{"label":"Revenue_Cloud_Dashboard dashboard","href":"force-app/main/default/dashboards/Revenue_Cloud_Dashboard.dashboard-meta.xml"}],"objectives":["Build ARR pipeline reports that tie to the balance sheet","Measure discount leakage across quotes and opportunities","Design executive dashboards with a clear story","Use reporting snapshots to preserve history","Apply analytics to revenue decisions"],"lessons":[{"title":"Key Revenue Metrics","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Revenue analytics starts with one number: ARR. Every report in this phase answers the same questions — how much recurring pipeline do we have, how much did discounts give away, and how much of that revenue will renew."},{"t":"table","head":["Metric","Definition"],"rows":[["ARR","Annual Recurring Revenue: the annualized value of active recurring subscriptions"],["TCV","Total Contract Value: the full value of the contract over its entire term"],["ACV","Annual Contract Value: the contract value normalized to one year"],["Discount Leakage %","Share of list revenue given up through discounts across quotes and lines"],["Renewal Rate","Percentage of expiring ARR that renews within the term"],["NDR","Net Dollar Retention: recurring revenue retained and expanded after churn and uplift"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Keep the source objects honest: ARR lives on Quote.Total_ARR__c and QuoteLineItem.ARR__c, discount leakage on Opportunity.Discount_Leakage_Percent__c, and renewals on Renewal_Opportunity__c.ARR__c. Reports that mix these definitions will not tie to the ERP."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A client discounts USD 2,000 off a USD 10,000 list quote. What is the Discount Leakage Percent?","a":"20%. Discount Leakage % is the discounted amount divided by list revenue, tracked alongside Opportunity.Discount_Leakage_Percent__c so leadership can see how much pipeline margin is given away."}]},{"title":"Building the Reports","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Salesforce offers four report formats, and each answers a different revenue question. Tabular reports list detail rows, Summary reports add a grouping band with subtotals, Matrix reports compare across two dimensions, and Joined reports merge two report types on a common field."},{"t":"num","items":["Tabular — a flat list of quote lines, ideal to export raw pipeline","Summary — quote lines grouped by Opportunity or Account with subtotal ARR per group","Matrix — a cross-tab of, for example, region by product family for ARR by segment","Joined — pipeline and billing data merged on the quote or order id"]},{"t":"code","lang":"soql","x":"SELECT Opportunity.Name,\n       Opportunity.Account.Region__c,\n       SUM(QuoteLineItem.ARR__c) arr_total,\n       AVG(QuoteLineItem.Discount_Percent__c) avg_discount,\n       MAX(QuoteLineItem.Net_Unit_Price__c) top_line_price\nFROM QuoteLineItem\nWHERE Quote.Approval_Status__c = 'Approved'\nGROUP BY Opportunity.Name, Opportunity.Account.Region__c\nORDER BY arr_total DESC"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Every grouping in a Summary or Matrix report must use the same matching field as the report type, so think of QuoteLineItem.Opportunity as your join into pipeline and Invoice as your join into recognition."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A Director asks for ARR subtotaled by account with a quote count per account. Which report type?","a":"A Summary report grouping by Account with ARR subtotals, or a Matrix report if you want a second dimension such as product family across the columns."}]},{"title":"Dashboards & Storytelling","mins":14,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A dashboard is a story, not a grid of numbers. On CPQ and Revenue Cloud the audience wants three answers: how much recurring pipeline, how much leakage, and how recognition is progressing."},{"t":"num","items":["Cap each dashboard at three to four components so a single glance answers one question","Add dynamic filters for fiscal year, region and product family so executives slice without rebuilding","Use up/down arrows and a target to make trends legible, never raw tables of detail","Source from Summary or Matrix reports so the chart aggregates server-side","Schedule the dashboard and enable reporting snapshots to preserve history for quarter-over-quarter trends","Give every chart a title that states the question it answers"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Start from the finance definitions: ARR from Quote.Total_ARR__c, leakage from Opportunity.Discount_Leakage_Percent__c, and recognition from the Revenue_Schedule__c Status__c and Recognized_Amount__c split against Amount__c. When the dashboard and the ERP agree, the story holds."},{"t":"ex","id":"13.1","stars":3,"title":"Build an ARR Pipeline Report","obj":"Create a Summary report of ARR pipeline grouped by account so leadership can see which deals move the metric.","steps":["Create a new report based on the QuoteLineItem standard report type","Group by Opportunity.Account Name and add QuoteLineItem.ARR__c as the summarized field with a total","Add a filter so only approved quotes stay in the report (Quote.Approval_Status__c equals Approved)","Add QuoteLineItem.Discount_Percent__c and QuoteLineItem.Net_Unit_Price__c columns for context","Save the report to the unfiled reports folder used by the repo artifacts"],"verify":"The report shows ARR per account sorted descending, ties to Quote.Total_ARR__c, and opens from the module artifacts list.","code":{"lang":"soql","x":"SELECT Opportunity.Account.Name,\n       SUM(QuoteLineItem.ARR__c) arr_total,\n       AVG(QuoteLineItem.Discount_Percent__c) avg_discount\nFROM QuoteLineItem\nWHERE Quote.Approval_Status__c = 'Approved'\nGROUP BY Opportunity.Account.Name\nORDER BY arr_total DESC"}},{"t":"ex","id":"13.2","stars":3,"title":"Design a Discount Leakage Dashboard","obj":"Design a Revenue Cloud dashboard that surfaces discount leakage and recognition progress to executives.","steps":["Sketch the dashboard canvas with three components: leakage trend, leakage by region and recognition by period","Back the leakage chart from a Summary report on Opportunity with Discount_Leakage_Percent__c subtotaled by Account.Region__c","Base the recognition gauge on Revenue_Schedule__c with Status__c and Recognized_Amount__c against Amount__c","Add dynamic filters for fiscal period and product family","Hand the sketch to a peer and explain what each chart asks and answers"],"verify":"The sketch has at most four components, each tied to a real field from the academy data model, and you can explain the story in under a minute."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Reports, Dashboards & Revenue Analytics quiz","mins":10,"questions":[{"q":"A contract bills USD 1,000 per month for 36 months. What is its ARR?","opts":["USD 12,000","USD 36,000","USD 1,000","USD 3,000"],"a":0,"why":"ARR is the annualized recurring revenue — price per month times 12 — while USD 36,000 is the TCV over the full term."},{"q":"A quote lists USD 1,500 in revenue but bills USD 1,200 after discounts. Which field captures that leak for leadership reporting?","opts":["Opportunity.Discount_Leakage_Percent__c","QuoteLineItem.List_Unit_Price__c","Contract.Uplift_Percent__c","Revenue_Sync__e.Payload__c"],"a":0,"why":"Discount leakage is tracked on the Opportunity while the list price only records the undiscounted value."},{"q":"Which report type compares data across two dimensions, such as region rows and product family columns?","opts":["Matrix","Tabular","Summary","Joined"],"a":0,"why":"Matrix reports cross-tabulate a group dimension against a column dimension; Summary only groups one way."},{"q":"Which report type returns a flat, exportable list of matching records without grouping?","opts":["Tabular","Matrix","Summary","Joined"],"a":0,"why":"Tabular reports are simple row lists; Summary, Matrix and Joined all introduce grouping or merging."},{"q":"To compare this quarter with last quarter on a trend dashboard, which feature preserves historical data?","opts":["Reporting snapshots","Dynamic dashboards","Joined reports","Scheduled refresh only"],"a":0,"why":"Reporting snapshots store periodic copies so you can trend over time; refresh alone overwrites the data."},{"q":"A Summary report on QuoteLineItem groups by Account and sums recurring pipeline. Which field is the correct ARR measure?","opts":["QuoteLineItem.ARR__c","QuoteLineItem.Discount_Percent__c","Quote.Discount_Percent__c","Contract.Uplift_Percent__c"],"a":0,"why":"ARR pipeline lives on the quote line, the granular row in the report; the other fields measure discounts and uplift instead."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"13.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3},{"id":"13.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"cpq-certification-prep","n":14,"title":"Certification Prep (CPQ & Revenue Cloud)","icon":"14","color":"#EAB308","tagline":"Exam blueprints, domain weights & study plans","guide":"14-Certification-Prep.md","art":[{"label":"Certification_Setting.CPQ (CPQ Specialist facts)","href":"force-app/main/default/customMetadata/Certification_Setting.CPQ.md-meta.xml"},{"label":"Certification_Setting.RLM (Revenue Cloud facts)","href":"force-app/main/default/customMetadata/Certification_Setting.RLM.md-meta.xml"},{"label":"Training_Question__c metadata","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Training_Question__c/"}],"objectives":["Compare the CPQ Specialist and Revenue Cloud Consultant certifications","Read both exam blueprints and map the domain weights","Build a domain-weighted study plan and run a diagnostic","Drill exam-style practice questions and book the exam"],"lessons":[{"title":"The Two Certifications","mins":15,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Before you book anything, know the exam you are walking into. Salesforce publishes two credentials that cover this roadmap: the Salesforce Certified CPQ Specialist / Administrator exam for the legacy managed-package world, and the Salesforce Certified Revenue Cloud Consultant exam for the next-generation Revenue Cloud platform. They share a fee structure but differ in topics, weights and passing bar."},{"t":"h","x":"Credentials at a Glance"},{"t":"table","head":["Credential","Questions","Minutes","Passing","Cost"],"rows":[["CPQ Specialist / Administrator","60 (+ up to 5 unscored)","105","65%","USD 200 (retake USD 100)"],["Revenue Cloud Consultant","60 scored (+ up to 5 unscored)","105","62%","USD 200 (retake USD 100)"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Both exams include a handful of unscored pilot questions, so the scoring pool is the 60 officially counted items. Neither has a hard prerequisite, but Salesforce recommends Administrator experience for CPQ and two to three years of product-to-cash experience for Revenue Cloud."},{"t":"list","items":["CPQ Specialist: legacy managed package (SBQQ__ objects) — bundles, pricing, quote templates, approvals, orders and contracts.","Revenue Cloud Consultant: native platform objects (ProductCatalog, ProductSellingModel, PriceBookEntry) plus CPQ, catalog management, assets, contracts, orders and invoicing.","The learning org in this repo mirrors both, because both engines are tested."]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"The exam facts for this module are stored right in the repo as Certification_Setting__mdt records — Certification_Setting.CPQ and Certification_Setting.RLM. Open them to confirm Question_Count__c, Duration_Minutes__c, Passing_Score__c, Cost_USD__c and Retake_Fee_USD__c."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"You see 105 minutes and 60 questions on both exam pages. What tells you which credential to pick first?","a":"Your target platform. If the org runs legacy SBQQ CPQ, start with CPQ Specialist at 65% to pass. If it runs Revenue Cloud / RLM on core objects, go Revenue Cloud Consultant at 62%. Both cost USD 200 with a USD 100 retake."}]},{"title":"The CPQ Blueprint","mins":20,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The CPQ Specialist blueprint weights eight domains. A domain at 23% is worth about 14 questions; a domain at 4% is worth two or three. Weight every hour of study the same way."},{"t":"h","x":"CPQ Specialist Blueprint Domains"},{"t":"table","head":["Domain","Weight"],"rows":[["CPQ Platform","23%"],["Bundle Configurations","17%"],["Pricing","16%"],["Orders, Contracts, Amendments & Renewals","15%"],["Products","11%"],["Quote Templates","7%"],["Product Selection","7%"],["Approvals","4%"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Read the official blueprint on Trailhead before studying. Weights shift slightly every release — the spec facts in this repo pin the current values so your plan does not drift."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Do not skip Approvals at 4%. Two or three easy questions can be the difference between 64% and 65%. Low-weight domains are the cheapest points on the exam."},{"t":"num","items":["Learn the SBQQ__ object names cold: SBQQ__Quote__c, SBQQ__QuoteLine__c, SBQQ__ProductOption__c, SBQQ__PriceRule__c, SBQQ__DiscountSchedule__c.","Recreate bundles, price rules and discount schedules in a scratch org so the concepts are tactile, not theoretical.","Drill the repo training bank filtered by Exam_Domain__c = CPQ Platform first — it is the largest single slice.","Track your percentage per domain every week and re-weight your calendar when a domain lags.","Take a timed practice session at least twice before exam day to test pacing at 105 minutes for 60 items."]},{"t":"ex","id":"14.1","stars":2,"title":"Build a Domain-Weighted Study Plan","obj":"Turn the blueprint weights into a weekly plan that allocates study hours proportionally to each domain.","steps":["Compute the question-per-domain split from the blueprint (CPQ Platform 23% of 60, Approvals 4% of 60, and so on).","Allocate hours to each domain proportional to that split, protecting the low-weight easy domains from being skipped.","Schedule two timed, full-length practice runs before exam day and block them as calendar events.","Use the repo practice bank (Training_Question__c) and queue drills by Exam_Domain__c and Blueprint_Weight__c.",{"h":"Deliverable","items":["A weekly calendar with at least four study slots and the domain(s) listed per slot.","A one-line rationale per domain explaining why it got its allocated hours.","A re-plan trigger: if a domain practice score is under 70%, that domain gains hours next week."]}],"verify":"You can present the plan to someone else and defend the hour-per-domain split against both the question weights and your own practice scores.","code":{"lang":"soql","x":"SELECT Exam_Domain__c, Blueprint_Weight__c, COUNT(Id) Active_Questions\nFROM Training_Question__c\nWHERE Is_Active__c = true AND Certification_Target__c = 'CPQ Specialist'\nGROUP BY Exam_Domain__c, Blueprint_Weight__c\nORDER BY Blueprint_Weight__c DESC"}},{"t":"ex","id":"14.2","stars":3,"title":"Practice: Catalog & Bundle Questions","obj":"Work five exam-style multiple-choice questions on products, features, options and bundle configuration logic.","steps":["Q1. Which CPQ object represents a selectable option that a product exposes when built into a bundle? (a) SBQQ__ProductOption__c (b) SBQQ__QuoteLine__c (c) SBQQ__DiscountTier__c (d) SBQQ__PriceCondition__c","Q2. A feature with minimum 1 and maximum 1 options is designed to force what? (a) exactly one required selection (b) a fixed quantity (c) a discount (d) an approval","Q3. Which line field marks the parent row of a bundle so child lines roll up under it? (a) Is_Bundle_Parent__c (b) Bundle_Option__c (c) List_Unit_Price__c (d) ARR__c","Q4. An option must be excluded whenever another option is selected. What do you configure? (a) a constraint between the two options (b) a price rule (c) a discount schedule (d) a quote template","Q5. In RLM, the reusable catalog of sellable products lives on which native object? (a) ProductCatalog (b) SBQQ__Product__c (c) Revenue_Sync__e (d) Discount_Schedule__c",{"h":"Answer key","items":["Q1 = (a) SBQQ__ProductOption__c — the standard container of a configured option.","Q2 = (a) exactly one required selection — min/max 1 is the classic pick-one feature.","Q3 = (a) Is_Bundle_Parent__c — flag the parent row; Bundle_Option__c names the option.","Q4 = (a) a constraint — options that cannot coexist need a configuration constraint.","Q5 = (a) ProductCatalog — the next-gen Revenue Cloud catalog object."]}],"verify":"You scored at least 4 of 5 first try and you can explain why each wrong option is wrong using bundle configuration concepts."}]},{"title":"The Revenue Cloud Blueprint","mins":20,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The Revenue Cloud Consultant blueprint is broader than CPQ: seven domains that start with platform concepts and end with invoicing. It lands most heavily on Revenue Cloud Platform Concepts and CPQ at 18% each."},{"t":"h","x":"Revenue Cloud Consultant Blueprint Domains"},{"t":"table","head":["Domain","Weight"],"rows":[["Revenue Cloud Platform Concepts","18%"],["Configure Price Quote (CPQ)","18%"],["Catalog Management","16%"],["Asset Management","16%"],["Implementation Readiness","14%"],["Contracts and Orders","11%"],["Invoice Management","7%"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Asset Management and Invoice Management do not exist on the CPQ exam — study subscription assets, subscription terms, amendments and invoice schedules there with extra care."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Map this blueprint to repo metadata: Products, PriceBookEntry and ProductSellingModel under Catalog Management; Revenue_Schedule__c and RevenueRecognitionService under Invoice Management; Renewal_Opportunity__c under Contracts and Orders."},{"t":"ex","id":"14.3","stars":3,"title":"Practice: Pricing & Discount Questions","obj":"Work five exam-style multiple-choice questions on price books, price rules, discount schedules and subscription pricing.","steps":["Q1. Which core object maps a product to a currency and gives you a price you can quote? (a) PriceBookEntry (b) ProductCatalog (c) QuoteLineItem (d) Discount_Schedule__c","Q2. You want volume-based discounting per product family. Which custom object in the repo was made for that? (a) Discount_Schedule__c (b) Revenue_Schedule__c (c) Approval_Log__c (d) Training_Question__c","Q3. Which method applies pricing rules to a list of QuoteLineItem rows and returns a Map of calculated discounts? (a) PriceRuleEngineService.applyPriceRules (b) QuoteCalculatorService.recalculateTotals (c) RenewalQuoteService.calculateUpliftedARR (d) OrderFulfillmentService.advanceFulfillment","Q4. In subscription pricing, which quote field records how many months the ARR was quoted for? (a) Contract_Term_Months__c (b) Uplift_Percent__c (c) Total_ARR__c (d) Approval_Status__c","Q5. A quote nets 120,000 against a 150,000 list total. What is the blended discount percent from QuoteCalculatorService? (a) 20% (b) 25% (c) 12.5% (d) 80%",{"h":"Answer key","items":["Q1 = (a) PriceBookEntry — product plus currency equals a sellable price entry.","Q2 = (a) Discount_Schedule__c — tiered discounts keyed by Product_Family__c.","Q3 = (a) PriceRuleEngineService.applyPriceRules — the pricing facade.","Q4 = (a) Contract_Term_Months__c — the term the price and ARR are built on.","Q5 = (a) 20% — (150000 minus 120000) divided by 150000 = 20%."]}],"verify":"You scored at least 4 of 5 first try and you can hand-compute list minus net over list exactly as the specification defines discount percent.","code":{"lang":"soql","x":"SELECT Product_Family__c, Discount_Type__c, Lower_Bound__c, Upper_Bound__c, Discount_Percent__c, Active__c\nFROM Discount_Schedule__c\nWHERE Active__c = true\nORDER BY Product_Family__c, Lower_Bound__c"}}]},{"title":"Diagnostic & Exam Day","mins":20,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Your last phase-14 mile is a diagnostic run that mimics real conditions, then a plan for exam day that removes every piece of friction outside the questions."},{"t":"h","x":"Run a Diagnostic"},{"t":"num","items":["Pick a quiet 105-minute window and run 60 questions from the Training_Question__c bank (one quiz per domain, then combined).","Score yourself per domain, not only overall — a 63% overall with a 30% Pricing score is a pricing problem, not a general one.","Re-weight your weekly plan from exercise 14.1 based on the diagnostic; this is exactly why the plan is editable.","Repeat once more before booking, and book only when you clear the pass bar twice in a row."]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Exam-day reality check: identity must match the booking seat, locked-down browsers get wiped on minimum-distance detection, and retakes cost USD 100. Book when you are ready, not when you are ready-to-be-lucky."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Your diagnostic shows overall 66% but Approvals 0/3 and Invoice Management 1/4. Where do the next study slots go?","a":"Attack the low-weight domains first — they are the cheapest points. Approvals is 4% of CPQ and Invoice Management 7% of Revenue Cloud; two or three questions there can lift you over the pass bar without a full platform re-read."},{"t":"ex","id":"14.4","stars":3,"title":"Practice: Approvals & Renewals Questions","obj":"Work five exam-style multiple-choice questions on approval processes, approval chains and renewal quoting.","steps":["Q1. ApprovalRoutingService.requiresApproval enforces which default rule? (a) a discount above 25.00% needs sign-off (b) every quote is approved (c) only renewals approve (d) only bundles approve","Q2. Which field tracks the live approval state of a quote in the repo? (a) Approval_Status__c (b) CPQ_Sync_Status__c (c) Fulfillment_Status__c (d) Discount_Type__c","Q3. Every approval decision is persisted to which object? (a) Approval_Log__c (b) Revenue_Schedule__c (c) Training_Question__c (d) Renewal_Opportunity__c","Q4. An approval chain means what in Admin? (a) a sequence of steps, each potentially with multiple approvers, resolved in order (b) parallel approval by everyone at once (c) auto-approval after 24 hours (d) a single-step process","Q5. RenewalQuoteService.calculateUpliftedARR(100000, 5) returns? (a) 105000 (b) 100005 (c) 100500 (d) 5000",{"h":"Answer key","items":["Q1 = (a) the static APPROVAL_THRESHOLD_PCT is 25.00 and inclusive-safe.","Q2 = (a) Approval_Status__c on Quote.","Q3 = (a) Approval_Log__c — Quote__c plus Approver__c, Decision__c and Comments__c.","Q4 = (a) a sequence of steps resolved in order, each step being approved or rejected.","Q5 = (a) 105000 — arr multiplied by (1 + uplift percent divided by 100)."]}],"verify":"You scored at least 4 of 5 first try and can trace a rejected approval from Quote.Approval_Status__c into an Approval_Log__c row with the rejecting decision."},{"t":"ex","id":"14.5","stars":3,"title":"Practice: Billing & Revenue Questions","obj":"Work five exam-style multiple-choice questions on billing schedules, revenue recognition and the Revenue_Sync__e event.","steps":["Q1. Revenue_Schedule__c.Recognition_Method__c describes what? (a) how the schedule amount is recognized, for example Straight Line (b) the payment gateway (c) a discount tier (d) an approval chain","Q2. BillingScheduleService places the rounding remainder of a straight-line split on which period? (a) the last row so the rows sum exactly to annual ARR (b) the first row (c) period zero (d) it is discarded","Q3. RevenueRecognitionService.rollupAccountARR writes recognized totals to which account field? (a) Current_ARR__c (b) Renewal_Risk__c (c) CPQ_Enabled__c (d) Region__c","Q4. You publish an integration message when an order changes. Which object is the platform event? (a) Revenue_Sync__e (b) Revenue_Schedule__c (c) Certification_Setting__mdt (d) Discount_Schedule__c","Q5. A schedule row has a Recognition_Date__c later than today. RevenueRecognitionService recognizes it now? (a) no — rows stay Pending until their recognition date passes (b) yes, always (c) only on the first of the month (d) only when Status__c equals Approved",{"h":"Answer key","items":["Q1 = (a) how the amount is recognized — the method name, for example Straight Line.","Q2 = (a) the final period carries the delta so every cent lands somewhere.","Q3 = (a) Account.Current_ARR__c.","Q4 = (a) Revenue_Sync__e — Source_Object__c, Payload__c, Direction__c, Status__c.","Q5 = (a) no — only rows whose recognition date is today or in the past are flipped to Recognized."]}],"verify":"You scored at least 4 of 5 first try and can state the recognize-if-dated rule exactly as RevenueRecognitionService implements it."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Certification Prep Quiz","mins":10,"questions":[{"q":"How many scored questions appear on the Salesforce Certified Revenue Cloud Consultant exam?","opts":["60 scored, plus up to 5 unscored","75","90","45"],"a":0,"why":"The Revenue Cloud Consultant exam has 60 scored questions plus up to 5 unscored pilot questions."},{"q":"What passing score is required for the Salesforce Certified CPQ Specialist / Administrator exam?","opts":["62%","65%","70%","75%"],"a":1,"why":"CPQ Specialist requires 65% to pass; 62% is the Revenue Cloud Consultant bar."},{"q":"Which domain carries the most weight on the CPQ Specialist blueprint?","opts":["Pricing at 16%","Bundle Configurations at 17%","CPQ Platform at 23%","Orders, Contracts, Amendments & Renewals at 15%"],"a":2,"why":"CPQ Platform weighs in at 23%, the single largest domain on the CPQ blueprint."},{"q":"Which blueprint domain appears ONLY on the Revenue Cloud Consultant exam?","opts":["Quote Templates","Invoice Management","Approvals","Product Selection"],"a":1,"why":"Invoice Management at 7% exists only on the Revenue Cloud blueprint; the others appear on CPQ."},{"q":"What is the exam retake fee for both certifications?","opts":["USD 100","USD 200","USD 50","USD 250"],"a":0,"why":"Both exams cost USD 200 with a USD 100 retake fee."},{"q":"Which object in the learning org stores practice questions tagged by Exam_Domain__c and Blueprint_Weight__c?","opts":["Certification_Setting__mdt","Discount_Schedule__c","Training_Question__c","Approval_Log__c"],"a":2,"why":"Training_Question__c holds the practice bank (Question_Text__c, Exam_Domain__c, Blueprint_Weight__c, Difficulty__c)."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"14.1","li":1,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"14.2","li":1,"kind":"ex","stars":3},{"id":"14.3","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3},{"id":"14.4","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":3},{"id":"14.5","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"cpq-practical-exercises","n":15,"title":"Practical Exercises & Mini Projects","icon":"15","color":"#22C55E","tagline":"31 exercises, 10 mini projects & a capstone","guide":"15-Practical-Exercises-and-Mini-Projects.md","art":[{"label":"Apex services (classes/)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/"},{"label":"Apex triggers (triggers/)","href":"force-app/main/default/triggers/"},{"label":"quote.apex practice script","href":"scripts/apex/quote.apex"},{"label":"quote.soql practice script","href":"scripts/soql/quote.soql"}],"objectives":["Practice 31 graded exercises across the whole quote-to-cash lifecycle","Build 10 mini projects that join the data model with the Apex services","Complete the enterprise capstone end to end on Revenue Cloud","Verify your work against the EXERCISE_ANSWERS key in the repo"],"lessons":[{"title":"How to Use These Exercises","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"This phase is where reading turns into doing. Each exercise card carries an id, a star rating (1 star = quick reinforcement, 4 stars = deep build), instructions and a Verify check. Projects also carry Success criteria and grouped requirements."},{"t":"num","items":["Open an exercise card and read the objective and the star rating before you start.","Attempt the work in your scratch org or against the repo metadata before you check the answer.","Verify with the shown Verify or Success text, then compare your result to the EXERCISE_ANSWERS key to confirm your approach.","For projects, tick every grouped requirement — an incomplete requirement group is a failed project regardless of code quality."]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"The answers live in EXERCISE_ANSWERS, rendered under each exercise card by app.js. Do not open it first — the point is to fail and recover a little, just like an exam."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A project card shows 4 stars and a Success line. What does the star count tell you about how to time it?","a":"Stars are effort. A 4-star project expects multiple services, multiple objects and a focused session — plan for it and verify every grouped requirement as you go."}]},{"title":"Mini Projects: Catalog & Pricing","mins":40,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Three projects rebuild the commercial core: an end-to-end data model, a configurable bundle, and a tiered discount approval rule set."},{"t":"proj","id":"P1","stars":3,"title":"Design an End-to-End CPQ Data Model","obj":"Design the standard plus custom object graph that supports a quote, its lines, pricing, approvals, orders, contracts, renewals, billing and recognition.","reqs":["Identify the standard objects: Quote, QuoteLineItem, PricebookEntry, Order, Contract and the platform event Revenue_Sync__e.","Add the custom objects Discount_Schedule__c, Approval_Log__c, Renewal_Opportunity__c and Revenue_Schedule__c.",{"h":"Relationships","items":["Quote to QuoteLineItem, one to many.","Quote, Order and Contract lookups on Revenue_Schedule__c.","Contract to Renewal_Opportunity__c, one to many.","Approval_Log__c.Quote__c back to Quote."]},"Add the pricing and reporting fields: Quote.Total_ARR__c, QuoteLineItem.Net_Unit_Price__c, Account.Current_ARR__c and Order.Fulfillment_Status__c.","Draw the ERD and annotate every edge with the field name that carries the relationship."],"success":"You can name 12 or more supported objects, explain each relationship direction and multiplicity, and every object in your ERD maps to a real metadata folder in force-app/main/default/objects."},{"t":"proj","id":"P2","stars":3,"title":"Configure a Configurable Product Bundle","obj":"Build a product bundle with configurable options, min/max constraints and a pricing rollup using the repo services.","reqs":[{"h":"Bundle shape","items":["Flag one bundle parent per group using Is_Bundle_Parent__c on QuoteLineItem.","Add at least two selectable options via Bundle_Option__c.","Give one feature a required-single constraint and one a mutually-exclusive rule."]},{"h":"Pricing","items":["List price each option with List_Unit_Price__c and Net_Unit_Price__c.","Use PriceRuleEngineService.applyPriceRules to compute any option discounts.","Roll up the selected options with BundleConfigurationService.rollupBundlePrice."]},"Validate the configuration with BundleConfigurationService.validateBundleConfiguration and surface the messages for every violated rule."],"success":"A QuoteLineItem set can be priced and validated in a single service call, an invalid option pair returns a clear violation message, and the bundle total equals your manually-summed option totals."},{"t":"proj","id":"P3","stars":3,"title":"Implement a Discount Approval Matrix","obj":"Route discounts above threshold to the right approver and log every decision with ApprovalRoutingService.","reqs":["Use ApprovalRoutingService.requiresApproval to decide when a discount needs sign-off (default threshold 25.00%).","Set Quote.Approval_Status__c to Pending Review, Approved or Rejected as decisions land.","Write every attempt into Approval_Log__c with Approver__c, Decision__c, Discount_Percent__c, Comments__c and Decision_Date__c.",{"h":"Matrix behaviour","items":["Discounts up to 25% auto-approve and log a Standard row.","Discounts above 25% route to an approver and log an Executive row.","A rejected quote cannot move to order generation while Approval_Status__c is Rejected."]}],"success":"Given three quotes at 10%, 30% and 55%, the service produces exactly three Approval_Log__c rows with the correct approval types and no quote below threshold ever gets an approver.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"Decimal d1 = 10;\nDecimal d2 = 30;\nSystem.debug(ApprovalRoutingService.requiresApproval(d1));\nSystem.debug(ApprovalRoutingService.requiresApproval(d2));\nApproval_Log__c log = ApprovalRoutingService.logDecision(q.Id, 'Pending', d2, 'Needs exec review');"}}]},{"title":"Mini Projects: Approvals & Documents","mins":40,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Two projects take the approved quote all the way into a professional, repeatable document and a contract that renews itself."},{"t":"proj","id":"P4","stars":3,"title":"Build an Automated Quote Document","obj":"Produce a quote document that reflects the approved quote state and is safe to regenerate after every pricing change.","reqs":[{"h":"Content","items":["Header: account, opportunity, quote number and currency.","Body: every QuoteLineItem with List_Unit_Price__c, Net_Unit_Price__c, Discount_Percent__c and ARR__c.","Footer: totals, Contract_Term_Months__c, Approval_Status__c and the discount approval note."]},"Drive the Quote Template from a template object so copy stays editable without code.","Regenerate the output on every quote recalculation and stamp a version into the document name.","Gate the template: an unapproved quote shows a Draft watermark; an approved quote clears it."],"success":"One quote build produces a document whose line items equal the live QuoteLineItem set and whose approvals watermark reflects Approval_Status__c with no manual step."},{"t":"proj","id":"P5","stars":4,"title":"Automate Contract Generation & Renewal","obj":"Generate a contract from an approved, contracted quote and pre-build the renewal pipeline automatically.","reqs":["Call ContractGenerationService.generateContracts for approved quotes whose Contract_Term_Months__c is set.","Carry pricing onto Contract.ARR__c and Auto_Renew__c following the quote term.","Set the contract Renewal_Term_Months__c and Uplift_Percent__c so renewals stay predictable.",{"h":"Renewal pipeline","items":["Use RenewalQuoteService.buildRenewalPipeline from the contract trigger.","Each near-term contract produces a Renewal_Opportunity__c with Renewal_Date__c and uplifted ARR__c.","Guard re-entry so updates do not duplicate renewal opportunities."]}],"success":"Approving and contracting one quote creates one contract and, when its end date is near, exactly one Renewal_Opportunity__c whose ARR already includes the uplift.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"List<Quote> approved = [SELECT Id, Contract_Term_Months__c, Total_ARR__c FROM Quote WHERE Id IN :ids AND Contract_Term_Months__c != null];\nList<Contract> contracts = ContractGenerationService.generateContracts(approved);\nRenewalQuoteService.buildRenewalPipeline(contracts);"}}]},{"title":"Mini Projects: Billing & Revenue","mins":40,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Money lands in the ledger through two projects: monthly billing schedules that are exact to the cent, and revenue recognition that keeps Account.Current_ARR__c honest."},{"t":"proj","id":"P6","stars":3,"title":"Create a Subscription Billing Schedule","obj":"Turn signed orders into monthly revenue schedule rows that are idempotent and mathematically exact.","reqs":["Generate rows with BillingScheduleService.buildBillingSchedule for a quote and its Contract_Term_Months__c.","Persist via BillingScheduleService.createRevenueSchedules when the Order is generated.","Verify the straight-line math: the sum of Amount__c equals the annual ARR and the remainder lands on the last period.",{"h":"Idempotency","items":["Running the order trigger twice must not duplicate rows.","No order may carry more than one full schedule set.","Null or zero ARR orders create no rows."]}],"success":"One order produces exactly N rows where N equals its term, the rows sum to the order ARR to the cent, and re-saving the order adds zero rows.","code":{"lang":"soql","x":"SELECT Period__c, Amount__c, Recognition_Date__c, Status__c, Recognition_Method__c\nFROM Revenue_Schedule__c\nWHERE Order__c = :orderId\nORDER BY Period__c"}},{"t":"proj","id":"P7","stars":4,"title":"Implement Revenue Recognition Automation","obj":"Recognize schedule rows on their recognition date and roll the recognized total up onto account ARR.","reqs":["Use RevenueRecognitionService.recognizeRevenue to flip due rows to Recognized and set Recognized_Amount__c.","Enforce the rule: a row whose Recognition_Date__c is in the future stays Pending.","Roll recognized amounts to Account.Current_ARR__c with RevenueRecognitionService.rollupAccountARR.",{"h":"Bulk-safe behaviour","items":["200 accounts in one transaction create no SOQL-in-loop.","Accounts that lose all schedules reset to zero.","A recognized row is never double-counted if the batch runs again."]}],"success":"After the run, Account.Current_ARR__c equals the sum of Recognized_Amount__c over its Revenue_Schedule__c rows, future rows are untouched, and a second identical run changes nothing.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"Set<Id> dueIds = new Set<Id>();\nfor (Revenue_Schedule__c s : [SELECT Id FROM Revenue_Schedule__c WHERE Recognition_Date__c <= TODAY AND Status__c != 'Recognized']) {\n  dueIds.add(s.Id);\n}\nRevenueRecognitionService.recognizeRevenue(dueIds);\nRevenueRecognitionService.rollupAccountARR(accountIds);"}}]},{"title":"Mini Projects: Integration & Analytics","mins":40,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The last three builds connect the org outwards: a quote-to-cash integration event, an ARR analytics view, and a migration from legacy CPQ into RLM."},{"t":"proj","id":"P8","stars":4,"title":"Build a Quote-to-Cash Integration","obj":"Publish a platform event whenever a quote or order leaves a stable state, and let consumers react idempotently.","reqs":["Publish Revenue_Sync__e with Source_Object__c, Payload__c, Direction__c, Endpoint__c and a Correlation_Id__c.",{"h":"Trigger points","items":["Quote approved to the contract engine.","Order fulfilled to the billing engine.","Order delivered with Fulfillment_Date__c set to the ERP final sync."]},"Set Status__c and Retry_Count__c so consumers can detect failures and replay safely.","Make consumers idempotent: processing the same Correlation_Id__c twice must not double-apply."],"success":"The happy-path lifecycle emits exactly three events per deal, each target system can rebuild its own state from the payload alone, and replays are no-ops.","code":{"lang":"apex","x":"EventBus.publish(new Revenue_Sync__e(\n  Source_Object__c = 'Order',\n  Payload__c = JSON.serialize(orderData),\n  Direction__c = 'Outbound',\n  Status__c = 'Published',\n  Correlation_Id__c = orderId,\n  Integration_Type__c = 'Post_Fulfillment',\n  Endpoint__c = '/erp/orders'\n));"}},{"t":"proj","id":"P9","stars":3,"title":"Design a Revenue Analytics Dashboard","obj":"Build the reports and dashboard that surface ARR, discount leakage and pipeline status in one place.","reqs":[{"h":"Reports","items":["ARR by Account joining Quote.Total_ARR__c and Account.Current_ARR__c.","Discount leakage from List_Unit_Price__c minus Net_Unit_Price__c per Opportunity via Discount_Leakage_Percent__c.","Approval decision mix from Approval_Log__c grouped by Approval_Type__c."]},"A pipeline report that shows Recognized, Open renewal and Pending approval states at a glance.","One dashboard, three tiles: ARR trend, discount leakage, approval workload."],"success":"A stakeholder can read current ARR, the money lost to discounting and the approval queue from a single screenshot without asking for another report."},{"t":"proj","id":"P10","stars":4,"title":"Migrate Legacy CPQ Data to RLM","obj":"Plan and execute a mapping from legacy SBQQ__ managed-package data onto the native Revenue Cloud objects.","reqs":[{"h":"Mapping","items":["SBQQ__Quote__c to Quote with the custom pricing fields.","SBQQ__QuoteLine__c to QuoteLineItem with List_Unit_Price__c and Net_Unit_Price__c.","SBQQ__DiscountSchedule__c to Discount_Schedule__c tiered rows."]},"Carry Approval_Status__c, CPQ_Sync_Status__c and the sync event so downstream systems keep working.","Write an idempotent transform job with a batch size safe for governor limits and a dry-run mode.","Produce a cutover checklist: freeze writes, run migration, reconcile counts per object, flip CPQ_Enabled__c."],"success":"Counts reconcile one-to-one per object, a re-run inserts nothing new, and reporting continuity is proven by equal list-total sums before and after cutover.","code":{"lang":"soql","x":"SELECT Id, Name, Total_ARR__c, Discount_Percent__c, Approval_Status__c, CPQ_Sync_Status__c, Is_Renewal__c\nFROM Quote\nWHERE CPQ_Sync_Status__c = 'Migrated'\nORDER BY CreatedDate"}}]},{"title":"Capstone","mins":60,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The capstone is the whole roadmap in one build: a quoted, priced, approved, contracted, fulfilled, billed and recognized enterprise deal on Revenue Cloud."},{"t":"proj","id":"CAP","stars":4,"title":"Capstone: Enterprise Quote-to-Cash on Revenue Cloud","obj":"Engine an end-to-end quote-to-cash flow for a strategic account, touching every custom object and Apex service in the repo.","reqs":[{"h":"Quote & price","items":["Create a 36-month strategic deal on a large account where Is_Strategic__c is true.","Use QuoteCalculatorService.recalculateTotals and PriceRuleEngineService.applyPriceRules to land Total_ARR__c and Discount_Percent__c.","Model one bundle parent with options and validate it via BundleConfigurationService."]},{"h":"Approve & contract","items":["Route the discount through ApprovalRoutingService and log every decision.","Set Approval_Status__c to Approved.","Generate the contract with ContractGenerationService and pre-build renewals with RenewalQuoteService."]},{"h":"Fulfill & bill","items":["Generate the Order and let OrderFulfillmentService advance it to Delivered.","Create monthly rows with BillingScheduleService tied to Contract_Term_Months__c.","Recognize due rows and roll ARR to Account.Current_ARR__c via RevenueRecognitionService."]},{"h":"Integrate & report","items":["Publish Revenue_Sync__e at the two stable milestones: approve and delivered.","Keep replays idempotent and the number of events minimal.","Report the whole deal: ARR, discount leakage and revenue recognized to date."]}],"success":"One strategic account produces one fully-approved deal whose contract, order, monthly schedules and recognized amounts all reconcile to the same annual ARR figure to the cent.","code":{"lang":"soql","x":"SELECT Id, Is_Strategic__c, Current_ARR__c,\n  (SELECT Id, Total_ARR__c, Discount_Percent__c, Approval_Status__c FROM Contracts),\n  (SELECT Id, Amount__c, Recognized_Amount__c, Recognition_Date__c, Status__c FROM Revenue_Schedules__r)\nFROM Account\nWHERE Is_Strategic__c = true\nLIMIT 5"}}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Practical Exercises Quiz","mins":10,"questions":[{"q":"What do the stars on an exercise card indicate in this curriculum?","opts":["Estimated difficulty and effort","Number of objects to touch","Points earned in the quiz","Days allowed to finish"],"a":0,"why":"Stars rate effort: 1 star is quick reinforcement, 4 stars is a deep multi-service build."},{"q":"How many exercises and mini projects does phase 15 bundle?","opts":["31 exercises, 10 projects and a capstone","20 exercises and 8 projects","25 exercises and 12 projects","30 exercises and 15 projects"],"a":0,"why":"The phase bundles 31 exercises, 10 mini projects (P1 to P10) and the CAP capstone."},{"q":"Which project would you pick to practise approval chains and discount thresholds?","opts":["P2 Configure a Configurable Product Bundle","P3 Implement a Discount Approval Matrix","P6 Create a Subscription Billing Schedule","P8 Build a Quote-to-Cash Integration"],"a":1,"why":"P3 focuses on threshold routing and Approval_Log__c with ApprovalRoutingService."},{"q":"Which Apex service rolls recognized amounts up onto Account.Current_ARR__c?","opts":["BillingScheduleService","QuoteCalculatorService","RevenueRecognitionService","RenewalQuoteService"],"a":2,"why":"RevenueRecognitionService.rollupAccountARR resets then re-sums Recognized_Amount__c per account."},{"q":"What distinguishes the higher-starred projects in phase 15?","opts":["They combine several Apex services and the full data model","They provide pre-written answers you must retype","They only run in production orgs","They cannot be verified"],"a":0,"why":"A 4-star project like P7 or P10 spans multiple services, triggers and reconciliation checks."},{"q":"How do you verify an exercise in this curriculum after attempting it?","opts":["Compare your result with the Verify criteria and the EXERCISE_ANSWERS key","Run a debug log and stop","Ask a teammate to redo it","Deploy to production first"],"a":0,"why":"Every card shows Verify or Success criteria, and EXERCISE_ANSWERS renders the full answer under the card."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"P1","li":1,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"P2","li":1,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"P3","li":1,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"P4","li":2,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"P5","li":2,"kind":"proj","stars":4},{"id":"P6","li":3,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"P7","li":3,"kind":"proj","stars":4},{"id":"P8","li":4,"kind":"proj","stars":4},{"id":"P9","li":4,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"P10","li":4,"kind":"proj","stars":4},{"id":"CAP","li":5,"kind":"proj","stars":4}]},{"id":"cpq-use-cases","n":16,"title":"Real-World Use Cases","icon":"16","color":"#E11D48","tagline":"Enterprise quote-to-cash scenarios end to end","guide":"16-Real-World-Use-Cases.md","art":[{"label":"Apex services (classes/)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/"},{"label":"Custom objects (objects/)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/"}],"objectives":["Analyse three enterprise quote-to-cash scenarios end to end","Map each scenario to the repo data model and Apex services","Identify the success metrics that judge each solution","Outline a solution approach before reading the phase-17 walkthrough"],"lessons":[{"title":"UC1: Enterprise SaaS Quote-to-Cash","mins":25,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"CloudSprint is a B2B SaaS vendor selling subscription seats on 12 and 36-month terms. Deals are assembled by reps in CPQ, approved by discount threshold, contracted, fulfilled, billed monthly and recognized over the contract life. Today, order-to-cash is done manually in spreadsheets and ARR is reported late and wrong."},{"t":"h","x":"Business Challenges vs. Desired Solution"},{"t":"table","head":["Challenge","Desired solution"],"rows":[["Quotes priced in spreadsheets, not the platform","Price everything in the platform so the PDF, the revenue and the contract all read the same numbers."],["Discounts granted without control","Route discounts above 25% to an approver and log every decision."],["Manual monthly billing across hundreds of seats","Generate monthly revenue rows automatically from each signed order."],["ARR reported late from stale spreadsheets","Recognize revenue on schedule and roll current ARR up to the account."]]},{"t":"num","items":["Quote-to-contract cycle time drops from days to hours.","Every signed deal has exactly one order and N billing rows equal to its term, with the rows summing to ARR to the cent.","Account.Current_ARR__c matches the sum of recognized amounts at any moment.","Zero spreadsheets involved in the happy path."]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"The trap in SaaS QTC is drifting numbers — the PDF, the contract, the order and the schedule can quietly disagree after a discount change. The rule: recalculate through QuoteCalculatorService and never hand-edit totals on the quote."},{"t":"h","x":"Solution"},{"t":"p","x":"The solution is a pipeline, not a feature: price in the platform, gate with approvals, contract from the approved quote, generate the order, let BillingScheduleService build the monthly rows and let RevenueRecognitionService keep ARR honest."},{"t":"p","x":"Every hand-off is data, not documents — the contract inherits Total_ARR__c and Contract_Term_Months__c, the order inherits the quote, and the schedule inherits the order. Discordant spreadsheets become structurally impossible."},{"t":"p","x":"The full step-by-step build, including the exact triggers, services and SOQL reconciliation checks, is in phase 17 lesson 1."}]},{"title":"UC2: Manufacturing Revenue Recognition","mins":25,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"TorqueWorks makes industrial machinery sold through distributors. Contracts span multiple years, machines are delivered in batches, and revenue must be recognized as items are delivered and accepted — not on invoice. Finance must prove recognized revenue per account to auditors at any date."},{"t":"h","x":"Business Challenges vs. Desired Solution"},{"t":"table","head":["Challenge","Desired solution"],"rows":[["Recognition rules differ by contract type","Model recognition per schedule via Recognition_Method__c with a straight-line default."],["Revenue events spread across deliveries","Recognize each schedule row exactly on its Recognition_Date__c as delivery is confirmed."],["Finance spends weeks closing the books","A scheduled run flips due rows to Recognized and updates Account.Current_ARR__c in one pass."],["Auditors need a trail they can follow","Every recognized amount is stored per row with Amount__c, Recognized_Amount__c and Status__c."]]},{"t":"num","items":["Book close for recognition drops from weeks to a scheduled run.","Recognized revenue reconciles to the sum of per-row Recognized_Amount__c with zero manual entries.","An auditor query lists every recognized row with date, method and amount on one screen.","No row can be recognized early; the recognition-date rule is enforced by the service."]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"RevenueRecognitionService deliberately separates the decision (recognizeRevenue), the math (calculateRecognizedAmount) and the rollup (rollupAccountARR). That three-way split is the pattern finance teams can audit line by line."},{"t":"h","x":"Solution"},{"t":"p","x":"Delivery confirmation becomes the trigger: when OrderFulfillmentService advances an order to Delivered and stamps Fulfillment_Date__c, the due schedule rows for that account move from Pending to Recognized."},{"t":"p","x":"Recognition is deterministic: a row is worth its Recognized_Amount__c once its Recognition_Date__c has passed, and zero before that — no judgment calls, so the books are reproducible at any as-of date."},{"t":"p","x":"The complete trigger chain and the SOQL reconciliation queries are walked through in phase 17 lesson 2."}]},{"title":"UC3: Channel Sales with Advanced Approvals","mins":25,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"ApexTrade sells through partner channels with per-tier price books. Partner-tier discounts must respect the agreement, approvals must chain by discount depth (standard up to threshold, executive above), and every decision must be auditable. A partner renewal must be able to see its uplifted ARR before the contract expires."},{"t":"h","x":"Business Challenges vs. Desired Solution"},{"t":"table","head":["Challenge","Desired solution"],"rows":[["No per-tier control: all partners get the same discount book","Key discounting by Partner_Tier__c and Product_Family__c via Discount_Schedule__c."],["Approval outcomes are silent","Every decision lands in Approval_Log__c with approver, amount, decision and comments."],["Partners by region exceed their authority","Threshold routing: up to 25% is standard, above 25% is executive."],["Renewals are re-priced from scratch","Generate Renewal_Opportunity__c automatically with uplifted ARR before the term ends."]]},{"t":"num","items":["Every approved channel deal has a complete decision chain in Approval_Log__c.","No quote above the threshold is ever contracted without an executive approver in the log.","Partner renewal ARR is automatically uplifted and visible 90 days before the renewal date.","Discount leakage is measurable per tier from the discount reporting tile."]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Channel deals fail audit when discount application and discount approval live in separate systems. Keep them in one transaction: apply pricing with PriceRuleEngineService, clear approval with ApprovalRoutingService, and only then generate the contract."},{"t":"h","x":"Solution"},{"t":"p","x":"The channel logic runs in the approval layer: pricing applies per tier, ApprovalRoutingService decides whether a discount is Standard or Executive, and the approval chain resolves in order with every decision persisted to Approval_Log__c."},{"t":"p","x":"Renewals are pre-sold by the platform: RenewalQuoteService reads near-term contracts, applies Uplift_Percent__c to ARR__c and writes Renewal_Opportunity__c rows the channel rep can start quoting from immediately."},{"t":"p","x":"The full implementation sequence, including the approval-chain trigger wiring, is covered step by step in phase 17 lesson 3."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Real-World Use Cases Quiz","mins":8,"questions":[{"q":"In UC1, which QuoteLineItem field tracks whether a row is the bundle parent?","opts":["Is_Bundle_Parent__c","Bundle_Option__c","Is_Renewal__c","Is_Strategic__c"],"a":0,"why":"Is_Bundle_Parent__c marks the parent so child lines roll up under it in the quote."},{"q":"Which service creates the monthly revenue rows for a signed SaaS order in UC1?","opts":["BillingScheduleService.createRevenueSchedules","QuoteCalculatorService.recalculateTotals","OrderFulfillmentService.advanceFulfillment","RenewalQuoteService.buildRenewalPipeline"],"a":0,"why":"createRevenueSchedules builds and inserts one row set per order, idempotently."},{"q":"In UC2, which field decides when a schedule row becomes recognized?","opts":["Recognition_Date__c","Fulfillment_Date__c","Decision_Date__c","Renewal_Date__c"],"a":0,"why":"Rows flip to Recognized only when Recognition_Date__c has passed today."},{"q":"In UC3, every channel approval decision is persisted to which object?","opts":["Approval_Log__c","Revenue_Schedule__c","Renewal_Opportunity__c","Training_Question__c"],"a":0,"why":"ApprovalRoutingService.logDecision writes Approver__c, Decision__c, Discount_Percent__c and Comments__c into Approval_Log__c."},{"q":"Which account field drives channel-tier discounting and approval routing in UC3?","opts":["Partner_Tier__c","Region__c","Renewal_Risk__c","Current_ARR__c"],"a":0,"why":"Discounting is keyed per tier via Partner_Tier__c and per Product_Family__c through Discount_Schedule__c."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"cpq-use-case-solutions","n":17,"title":"Use Case Solutions","icon":"17","color":"#7C3AED","tagline":"Full solution walkthroughs for the three use cases","guide":"17-Use-Case-Solutions.md","art":[{"label":"Apex services (classes/)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/"},{"label":"Custom objects (objects/)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/"}],"objectives":["Implement all three use cases step by step with the repo data model","Wire the Apex services and triggers into working quote-to-cash flows","Verify each hand-off with SOQL against the success metrics","Reuse the walkthroughs as runbooks for client delivery"],"lessons":[{"title":"UC1 Solution","mins":30,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"You build the pipeline in the order the data flows: model, price, gate, contract, fulfill, bill, recognize. All numbers reconcile because every stage inherits from the one before."},{"t":"h","x":"Step-by-Step Solution"},{"t":"num","items":["Model: use the standard and custom object set already in force-app/main/default/objects — Quote, QuoteLineItem, Order, Contract, Revenue_Schedule__c, Approval_Log__c.","Price: build the deal on PriceBookEntry rows, call PriceRuleEngineService.applyPriceRules for option discounts and QuoteCalculatorService.recalculateTotals so Total_ARR__c and Discount_Percent__c are authoritative.","Approve: if Discount_Percent__c exceeds 25%, ApprovalRoutingService routes the discount; logDecision writes the queue and Quote.Approval_Status__c gates the next step.","Contract: once approved, ContractGenerationService.generateContracts creates the contract inheriting ARR__c, term and renewal behaviour.","Fulfill and bill: generate the Order, let OrderFulfillmentService advance it to Delivered, then BillingScheduleService.createRevenueSchedules builds term rows whose sum equals the ARR.","Recognize and report: each day RevenueRecognitionService.recognizeRevenue flips due rows and rollupAccountARR restores Account.Current_ARR__c."]},{"t":"code","lang":"soql","x":"SELECT Id, Name, Total_ARR__c, Discount_Percent__c, Approval_Status__c\nFROM Quote\nWHERE Id = :qId\n\n-- Billing reconciliation: rows must sum to the order ARR.\nSELECT Period__c, Amount__c, Status__c, Recognition_Method__c\nFROM Revenue_Schedule__c\nWHERE Order__c = :orderId\nORDER BY Period__c"},{"t":"h","x":"Objects & Fields Used"},{"t":"table","head":["Object","Key fields used"],"rows":[["Quote","Total_ARR__c, Discount_Percent__c, Contract_Term_Months__c, Approval_Status__c, CPQ_Sync_Status__c"],["QuoteLineItem","List_Unit_Price__c, Net_Unit_Price__c, Discount_Percent__c, ARR__c, Is_Bundle_Parent__c, Bundle_Option__c"],["Order","Fulfillment_Status__c, Fulfillment_Date__c, Total_ARR__c"],["Contract","ARR__c, Auto_Renew__c, Renewal_Term_Months__c, Uplift_Percent__c"],["Revenue_Schedule__c","Quote__c, Order__c, Account__c, Amount__c, Recognized_Amount__c, Recognition_Date__c, Recognition_Method__c, Status__c, Period__c"],["Account","Current_ARR__c"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Run the three statements in scripts/soql/quote.soql after each stage — if the list total, net total and per-quote grouping match QuoteCalculatorService math, the pipeline is internally consistent."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"You change a discount after the contract exists. What must you do to keep ARR honest?","a":"Do not edit Contract.ARR__c by hand. Re-run pricing on the quote (recalculateTotals), re-approve if the discount crosses the threshold, regenerate or amend the contract, rebuild the order schedules and re-run recognition and rollup."}]},{"title":"UC2 Solution","mins":30,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"TorqueWorks recognition is solved as a deterministic state machine: delivery confirmation advances the order, due schedule rows flip to Recognized, and the account ARR rolls up from the rows — never from a spreadsheet."},{"t":"h","x":"Step-by-Step Solution"},{"t":"num","items":["Schedule as part of the deal: when the offer is created, BillingScheduleService builds rows with Recognition_Method__c Straight Line and a future Recognition_Date__c per period.","Confirm delivery: OrderFulfillmentService advances the status and stamps Fulfillment_Date__c on Delivered (Pending then Shipped then Delivered).","Recognize on the date: RevenueRecognitionService.recognizeRevenue flips any row whose Recognition_Date__c is today or earlier and Status__c is not Recognized, writing Recognized_Amount__c.","Roll up: RevenueRecognitionService.rollupAccountARR zeroes the accounts in scope once, re-sums Recognized_Amount__c with GROUP BY and sets Account.Current_ARR__c.","Prove it: the reconciliation query compares the account ARR to the summed rows, and a second identical run changes nothing (idempotent)."]},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"Set<Id> dueIds = new Set<Id>();\nfor (Revenue_Schedule__c row : [\n  SELECT Id FROM Revenue_Schedule__c\n  WHERE Recognition_Date__c <= TODAY AND Status__c != 'Recognized'\n]) {\n  dueIds.add(row.Id);\n}\nif (!dueIds.isEmpty()) {\n  RevenueRecognitionService.recognizeRevenue(dueIds);\n  RevenueRecognitionService.rollupAccountARR(accountIds);\n}"},{"t":"h","x":"Objects & Fields Used"},{"t":"table","head":["Object","Key fields used"],"rows":[["Revenue_Schedule__c","Recognition_Date__c, Recognition_Method__c, Status__c, Amount__c, Recognized_Amount__c, Period__c, Account__c, Contract__c, Order__c"],["Order","Fulfillment_Status__c, Fulfillment_Date__c"],["Account","Current_ARR__c, Region__c"],["Contract","ARR__c, StartDate, EndDate, ContractTerm"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Never let a flow edit Recognized_Amount__c after the fact. If a row is already Recognized it is terminal; corrections are new rows or a reversal journal, not an overwrite. This keeps the audit trail unbreakable."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A schedule row has a Recognition_Date__c of tomorrow and the daily job just ran. What is its state?","a":"Still Pending with Recognized_Amount__c null. The service only recognizes rows whose date has passed (Recognition_Date__c on or before today); future-dated rows are deliberately left alone."}]},{"title":"UC3 Solution","mins":30,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"ApexTrade channel control is built from three cooperating services: tiered discounting for the happy path, deterministic approval routing for the exceptions, and automatic renewal lift so partners can start renewals early."},{"t":"h","x":"Step-by-Step Solution"},{"t":"num","items":["Tier pricing: maintain Discount_Schedule__c rows keyed by Product_Family__c and bounds; PriceRuleEngineService.resolveDiscountPercent returns the applicable tier for a product family and amount.","Threshold gate: ApprovalRoutingService.requiresApproval opens an approval only when Discount_Percent__c is above 25.00; below that the quote auto-approves and logs a Standard row.","Chain resolution: approval steps (sales manager then executive above threshold) each land in Approval_Log__c with Approver__c, Decision__c, Discount_Percent__c, Comments__c and Decision_Date__c.","Gate the contract: ContractGenerationService only accepts quotes whose Approval_Status__c is Approved — a rejected or Pending quote cannot become a contract.","Renewal lift: the contract trigger calls RenewalQuoteService.buildRenewalPipeline; write Renewal_Opportunity__c rows with Renewal_Date__c and ARR__c already uplifted by Uplift_Percent__c."]},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"if (ApprovalRoutingService.requiresApproval(q.Discount_Percent__c)) {\n  Id approver = ApprovalRoutingService.determineApprover(q.Discount_Percent__c);\n  ApprovalRoutingService.logDecision(q.Id, 'Pending', q.Discount_Percent__c, 'Above partner threshold');\n  q.Approval_Status__c = 'Pending Review';\n} else {\n  ApprovalRoutingService.logDecision(q.Id, 'Approved', q.Discount_Percent__c, 'Within partner threshold');\n  q.Approval_Status__c = 'Approved';\n}"},{"t":"h","x":"Objects & Fields Used"},{"t":"table","head":["Object","Key fields used"],"rows":[["Quote","Approval_Status__c, Discount_Percent__c, Total_ARR__c, Is_Renewal__c, CPQ_Sync_Status__c"],["Approval_Log__c","Quote__c, Approval_Type__c, Approver__c, Decision__c, Discount_Percent__c, Comments__c, Decision_Date__c"],["Discount_Schedule__c","Discount_Type__c, Lower_Bound__c, Upper_Bound__c, Discount_Percent__c, Product_Family__c, Active__c"],["Renewal_Opportunity__c","Account__c, Contract__c, Renewal_Date__c, ARR__c, Uplift_Percent__c, Status__c"],["Opportunity","Is_Strategic__c, Discount_Leakage_Percent__c, ARR__c"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Log before you change status: logDecision persists the row with the current Discount_Percent__c, then Approval_Status__c moves. That ordering means the log is always the history and the quote is always the present."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A partner rep applies a 32% discount. Walk the first four steps the platform performs.","a":"1) requiresApproval sees 32 is above 25 and returns true; 2) determineApprover finds the executive approver role; 3) logDecision writes a Pending Executive row; 4) Approval_Status__c becomes Pending Review and the contract engine stays locked until a decision flips it to Approved."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Use Case Solutions Quiz","mins":8,"questions":[{"q":"Which trigger keeps quote totals in sync after line items change?","opts":["QuoteLineItemTrigger","QuoteTrigger","OrderTrigger","RevenueScheduleTrigger"],"a":0,"why":"QuoteLineItemTrigger fires recalculateTotals after QuoteLineItem insert, update or delete — the exact path QuoteCalculatorService is built for."},{"q":"Which trigger pre-builds the renewal pipeline from near-term contracts?","opts":["ContractTrigger via RenewalQuoteService.buildRenewalPipeline","OrderTrigger","RevenueScheduleTrigger","QuoteTrigger"],"a":0,"why":"ContractTrigger seeds one Renewal_Opportunity__c per contract near its end date, already uplifted."},{"q":"In the happy path, what does OrderFulfillmentService advance move an order through?","opts":["Pending to Shipped to Delivered","Approved to Contracted to Billed","Recognized to Fulfilled","Draft to Sent to Signed"],"a":0,"why":"The STATUS_FLOW map drives Pending to Shipped to Delivered, stamping Fulfillment_Date__c on receipt."},{"q":"How do you confirm the UC1 pipeline is internally consistent?","opts":["Reconcile the SOQL aggregates against QuoteCalculatorService totals","Run the flows twice and change nothing","Compare PDFs by eye","Ask finance for their spreadsheet"],"a":0,"why":"The list total, net total and per-quote grouping in scripts/soql must match the recalculateTotals math to the cent."},{"q":"What is the golden reconciliation rule across all three solutions?","opts":["Derived numbers are computed from source records, never typed in","Each object stores its own copy of the same number","Sales always wins over finance","Approvals can be bypassed when the discount is small"],"a":0,"why":"Every hand-off — ARR, discount, term, schedule sum — is calculated from source records so the numbers cannot drift apart."}]},"exIndex":[]}],"answerIds":["1.1","1.2","10.1","10.2","11.1","11.2","12.1","12.2","13.1","13.2","14.1","14.2","14.3","14.4","14.5","2.1","2.2","3.1","3.2","4.1","4.2","5.1","5.2","6.1","6.2","7.1","7.2","8.1","8.2","9.1","9.2","CAP","P1","P10","P2","P3","P4","P5","P6","P7","P8","P9"]};

window.ABDO_DATA["datacloud"] =
  {"slug":"datacloud","meta":{"slug":"datacloud","brand":"Data Cloud Consultant Academy","name":"Data Cloud 360","cert":"Salesforce Data Cloud Consultant","icon":"🗄️","color":"#10B981","desc":"18 phases across data lifecycle, DLO/DMO, segmentation, identity resolution, harmonisation and activation.","repo":"Salesforce-Data-cloud-360-RoadMap","github":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-Data-cloud-360-RoadMap","live":"https://abdoaddouli.github.io/Salesforce-Data-cloud-360-RoadMap/","guideBase":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-Data-cloud-360-RoadMap/blob/main/developer%20Data%20Cloud%20Consultant%20Roadmap/","repoBlob":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-Data-cloud-360-RoadMap/blob/main/","phases":18,"source":"Salesforce Data cloud 360\\Salesforce Data Cloud Roadmap"},"modules":[{"id":"datacloud-concepts","n":1,"title":"Data Cloud Concepts & Architecture","icon":"01","color":"#4F46E5","tagline":"CDP, data spaces, connectors & the unified profile","guide":"01-Data-Cloud-Concepts-and-Architecture.md","hero":"01-data-cloud-lifecycle.svg","art":[{"label":"sfdx-project.json (API 68.0)","href":"sfdx-project.json"},{"label":"config/project-scratch-def.json","href":"config/project-scratch-def.json"},{"label":"manifest/package.xml","href":"manifest/package.xml"},{"label":"ARCHITECTURE.md","href":"ARCHITECTURE.md"}],"objectives":["Explain what a CDP is and where Data 360 fits in the enterprise stack","Describe data spaces, connectors and the unified profile","List the Data 360 add-ons and their role in scope","Map ingestion to modeling to identity to insight to segmentation to activation","Know the 2025 rebrand: Data Cloud to Data 360 and what it means"],"lessons":[{"title":"Customer 360 & the CDP Layer","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Salesforce Data 360 (formerly Data Cloud) is the enterprise CDP layer on Hyperforce: it ingests data from any source, models it into data lakes and data model objects, resolves identities into a unified profile, and feeds real-time segments and activations to every Salesforce system."},{"t":"table","head":["Layer","Role","Example"],"rows":[["Data sources","Raw data from connectors, streams, APIs","ERP orders, web events, CRM cases"],["Data lake / DLO","Raw stored objects for query","Order__dlm, Web_Event__dlm"],["Data model object (DMO)","Semantic, mapped objects","UnifiedIndividual"],["Identity resolution","Golden profiles via match rules","IndividualId"],["Insights & segments","Computed attributes, audiences","Churn_Score, At-Risk segment"],["Activations","Publish to destinations","MCE data extension, CRM update"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"info","x":"The platform renamed from Data Cloud to Data 360 (Oct 2025). The consultant credential is now \"Salesforce Certified Data 360 Consultant\" - exam Data-Con-101. Study Spring 26 release features, not older ones."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Where does the unified profile live after identity resolution?","a":"In Data 360 model objects (UnifiedIndividual) - a golden, identity-resolved profile consumable by segments, activations and flows."}]},{"title":"Data Spaces & The Activation Chain","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A data space is the isolation boundary for data AND metadata: separate business units or use cases get separate spaces, each with its own connectors, DLOs, DMOs, segments and activations. Cross-space visibility is deliberately restricted."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"ingestion --> DLO --> DMO --> identity --> insight --> segment --> activation\n(connectors)  (lake)  (model)  (profile)    (calc)     (audience)   (MCE/CRM/webhook)\n   \\------------ streaming path: real-time calculated insights & dynamic segments --------/"},{"t":"list","items":["Connectors: hybrid-based (cloud/CRM), API/stream, Amazon Redshift, Google BigQuery, Snowflake, file/CRM connectors","Add-ons: Marketing Data, External Data, Consent, Service Data, Agentforce for Data 360","Initiatives: homepage tasks grouping a use-case build"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"The exam loves \"which add-on or component supports scenario X\". Know the data source options (connector vs API vs streaming) cold - Phase 2 covers it."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"1.1","title":"Positioning: Which CDP Feature?","stars":1,"obj":"Map a retail brief to the Data 360 feature set.","steps":["A retailer needs to ingest ERP order exports (nightly), website events (streaming) and Marketing Cloud sends.","Write the data space layout (one vs multiple spaces) and justify it.","Name the right ingest path for the streaming website events."],"verify":"Every source maps to a correct connector type; the data-space reasoning names least-privilege (Phase 11)."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 1 Quiz - Concepts & Architecture","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Since Oct 2025 the Data Cloud platform and credential are called...","opts":["Data and AI Cloud","Data 360","Data Lake 360","Customer 360"],"a":1,"why":"Data Cloud rebranded to Data 360; the consultant credential is now Data 360 Consultant (Data-Con-101)."},{"q":"Which component is the isolation boundary for data and metadata?","opts":["Data space","Data lake object","Connector","Permission set"],"a":0,"why":"Data spaces partition data AND metadata per business unit or use case."},{"q":"Which is the correct end-to-end chain?","opts":["Source to segment to DMO to activation","Source to DLO to DMO to identity to insight to segment to activation","Source to DMO to identity to DLO to segment","DLO to source to activation to segment"],"a":1,"why":"Ingestion feeds DLOs, mapping builds DMOs, identity resolves profiles, then insights, segments and activations follow."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"1.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":1}]},{"id":"datacloud-ingestion","n":2,"title":"Data Sources & Ingestion","icon":"02","color":"#7C3AED","tagline":"Connectors, streaming, mapping & ingestion APIs","guide":"02-Data-Sources-and-Ingestion.md","art":[{"label":"DataCloudIngestionService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/DataCloudIngestionService.cls"},{"label":"DataCloudIngestionServiceTest.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/DataCloudIngestionServiceTest.cls"},{"label":"Data_Stream_Log__c (object)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Data_Stream_Log__c/"},{"label":"scripts/apex/ingest_payload.apex","href":"scripts/apex/ingest_payload.apex"}],"objectives":["Choose connectors vs API vs streaming for any source","Set up hybrid and managed data streams","Map source fields into DLO/DMO mappings correctly","Use the ingestion/streaming APIs and understand event volumes","Mirror ingestion with DataCloudIngestionService + Data_Stream_Log__c"],"lessons":[{"title":"Connector Types & Decisions","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Data lands in Data 360 through three families: (1) connectors - managed/hybrid (Salesforce source connectors, Amazon S3/Redshift, Google Storage, Marketing Cloud, Audiences), (2) APIs - the Ingestion & Bulk Ingestion APIs and the Streaming API, (3) file/firehose delivery."},{"t":"table","head":["Source","Best mechanism","When"],"rows":[["CRM objects","Managed Salesforce connector","Native, low-config delta sync"],["ERP nightly exports","File/API + batch mapping","Bulk, scheduled, fully controlled"],["Website events","Streaming API / SDK","Real-time moments, high volume"],["External warehouse","Acquired Data Stream (Redshift/BigQuery/Snowflake)","Reuse existing warehouse data"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"\"Real-time\" requirements die at a nightly file source. Choosing ingestion coupled to the required freshness is the highest-yield exam discipline."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why would you choose the Streaming API over a nightly connector?","a":"Because the use case needs sub-minute freshness (churn alerts, cart-abandon) - a batch source breaks the real-time chain."}]},{"title":"Mapping & Ingest Payload","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A mapping (source to DMO) defines field transforms, data type conversion, defaults and which stream refreshes it. Data sources can be imported as CSV datasets (files), connected via connectors, or delivered by API."},{"t":"code","lang":"json","x":"{\n  \"stream\": \"web_events\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"eventTime\": \"2026-09-18T14:20:00Z\",\n    \"memberKey\": \"M-8821\",\n    \"type\": \"ProductView\",\n    \"properties\": { \"sku\": \"SKU-412\", \"value\": 89.0 }\n  }\n}"},{"t":"list","items":["Labs: scripts/apex/ingest_payload.apex posts a canonical event; DataCloudIngestionService normalizes it and logs to Data_Stream_Log__c","Event volume and schema evolution are the two things to pin down before go-live","Sensitive fields should be flagged for masking at mapping time (Phase 11)"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"The lab service upserts the source record by external key then writes a stream-log audit row - the same \"ingest + audit\" pattern a real mapping/connector batch performs."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"2.1","title":"Ingestion Source Match","stars":1,"obj":"Match sources to mechanisms and script an ingest.","steps":["For (a) CRM Contact updates, (b) nightly ERP CSV, (c) real-time mobile events - name the mechanism.","Post a ProductView payload via ingest_payload.apex and confirm the Data_Stream_Log__c row.","Note the mapping fields you would store for the DMO stage (Phase 3)."],"verify":"Mechanism chosen matches freshness; the log row records eventTime + memberKey + type."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 2 Quiz - Data Sources & Ingestion","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Real-time website activity should be ingested via...","opts":["Nightly CSV connector","Streaming API / SDK","Weekly data export","Manual file upload"],"a":1,"why":"Streaming is the only mechanism that supports sub-minute freshness."},{"q":"Cloud-stored files would be brought in via...","opts":["Streaming API","Managed Salesforce connector","Data source acquisition from cloud storage (S3 / GCS)","Apex callouts only"],"a":2,"why":"Acquired data sources pull cloud-storage files for batch ingestion."},{"q":"An ingestion API POST carries...","opts":["Only the field names","A stream key + structured JSON payload","An @InvocableMethod","A QueryLocator"],"a":1,"why":"Ingestion events are keyed and JSON-typed so mappings can normalize them."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"2.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":1}]},{"id":"datacloud-datamodel","n":3,"title":"Data Model Objects (DMOs) & Data Lake Objects (DLOs)","icon":"03","color":"#2563EB","tagline":"Lake, model, relationships & data streams","guide":"03-Data-Model-DMOs-and-DLOs.md","hero":"02-data-model-dlo-dmo.svg","art":[{"label":"Custom objects (objects/)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/"},{"label":"Certification_Setting__mdt","href":"force-app/main/default/customMetadata/Certification_Setting__mdt/"},{"label":"manifest/package.xml (wildcard types)","href":"manifest/package.xml"}],"objectives":["Explain DLO vs DMO and the mapping between them","Build and extend data model objects and relationships","Use identity resolution to produce the unified individual","Understand primary key, external ID and relationship keys","Use profiles, data sources, and relationship mapping correctly"],"lessons":[{"title":"Lake vs Model","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A Data Lake Object (DLO, __dlm) stores raw, normalized-at-source data. A Data Model Object (DMO) is a modeled view - semantic, deduplicated, relationship-ready. Mappings copy DLO fields into DMOs and attach external-ID keys."},{"t":"table","head":["Object","Suffix","Purpose"],"rows":[["DLO (data lake)","__dlm","Raw imported rows, queried with SQL"],["DMO (model)","__dlm","Semantic model object for identity/segments"],["Data source object","__dmo","Wrapper for a connected source"],["Custom object","__c","CRM objects synced via connector or mapped"]]},{"t":"list","items":["Field types: text, number, datetime, date, geolocation, boolean - pick per requirement (geolocation for radius segmentation)","Relationships: primary key to foreign key; external IDs carry through mapping","Data streams wrap sources and drive delta/streaming refresh into the model"]}]},{"title":"The Unified Individual & Relationships","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"DMOs participate in identity resolution (Phase 4). The reflection is the UnifiedIndividual / Individual profile (Customer_360__dlm etc.) - the golden profile segments and activations read. Data spaces scope DMOs per use case."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// Relationship key pattern in the lab:\n// Consent_Preference__c.Person_External_Id__c -> IndividualId (unified)\n// The lab queries mirrored consent rows by member key, then maps to the\n// unified profile concept modeled in code - the same join activations do in DMO SQL."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What binds a DLO row to the unified individual?","a":"The mapping/relationship keys - the external ID on the source row resolves (via identity rules) to the golden IndividualId."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"3.1","title":"DLO to DMO to Individual Map","stars":2,"obj":"Trace three sources into one unified profile.","steps":["Sketch Order__dlm, Web_Event__dlm and Case CRM source into DMOs.","Show the field paths that produce the unified profile (external IDs to IndividualId).","Name the data space this should live in and one object you would keep OUT of it."],"verify":"Relationship keys are explicit; the data-space boundary is justified."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 3 Quiz - Data Model Objects","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Raw imported rows live in...","opts":["DMO","DLO","Unified profile","Activation"],"a":1,"why":"Data lake objects (__dlm) store the raw imported data."},{"q":"The golden identity-resolved customer record is the...","opts":["Data source object","Unified Individual (profile)","Trigger handler","Data extension"],"a":1,"why":"Identity resolution builds the unified individual profile consumed downstream."},{"q":"Relationship mapping between DMO objects relies on...","opts":["Primary key + external ID keys","Flow variables","Picklist values","QueryLocator batches"],"a":0,"why":"Unified relationships join on primary/external ID keys."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"3.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"datacloud-identity","n":4,"title":"Identity Resolution","icon":"04","color":"#0EA5E9","tagline":"Match rules, unified profiles & data quality","guide":"04-Identity-Resolution.md","hero":"03-identity-resolution.svg","art":[{"label":"IdentityResolutionHelperService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/IdentityResolutionHelperService.cls"},{"label":"IdentityResolutionHelperServiceTest.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/IdentityResolutionHelperServiceTest.cls"},{"label":"Identity_Resolution_Candidate__c (object)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Identity_Resolution_Candidate__c/"}],"objectives":["Configure match rules (attributes, confidence, conditions)","Explain deterministic vs probabilistic matching","Understand unified profiles and data-quality score","Create and tune relationships for calculated match","Mirror matching with IdentityResolutionHelperService"],"lessons":[{"title":"Match Rules & Confidence","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Identity resolution groups sources into a unified profile by match rules. A rule = attributes to compare + conditions + a confidence surrogate. Two or more rules form a rule set that Data 360 runs to build the golden profile."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// Match-rule decision logic mirrored in the lab\npublic static Boolean matches(String field, Decimal confidence) {\n    // deterministic when both sides equal on a unique key (email/SSN/phone)\n    // probabilistic when fuzzy - name + birthdate fuzzy = high confidence\n    return String.isNotBlank(field) && confidence >= 0.85;\n}"},{"t":"list","items":["Deterministic: exact field equality (email, phone, customerId) - low-risk, high confidence","Probabilistic/fuzzy: tolerant comparisons (name, address) - needs tuning to avoid false merges","Rule confidence + conditions decide merge vs split; bad rules split/merge profiles silently"]}]},{"title":"Unified Profile & Data Quality","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The unified profile is the single customer record produced by resolution. A Data-Quality score measures profile completeness (blank fields, conflicts, duplicates). Raising it = fixing source hygiene + tuning rules iteratively."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Exam favorite: after enabling resolution, why did segments lose members? - a new match rule merged previously-separated profiles (or split them). Re-review rules + the data-quality dashboard before re-running audiences."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What does a falling data-quality score indicate?","a":"More blank/conflicting/duplicate source attributes - re-run rule tuning and fix source hygiene before segmentation."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"4.1","title":"Match Rule Design","stars":2,"obj":"Write match rules that reconcile CRM + web + order identities.","steps":["List candidate match attributes (email, phone, loyalty id, address) and confidence per pair.","Choose deterministic for the loyalty-id and email rule; probabilistic for name+address.","Assert the resolved candidate set via IdentityResolutionHelperService on 4 sample sources."],"verify":"Rule set is defensible (deterministic low-risk; fuzzy gated high confidence)."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 4 Quiz - Identity Resolution","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Two records with identical email addresses should...","opts":["Always split","Deterministically match by rule","Only match by API","Be deleted"],"a":1,"why":"A match rule on email is a deterministic, high-confidence match."},{"q":"Fuzzy matching on name + address is...","opts":["Deterministic","Probabilistic","Manual only","Impossible"],"a":1,"why":"Inexact comparison = probabilistic/fuzzy matching requiring tuning."},{"q":"A sudden drop in segment membership after a rule change usually means...","opts":["Activation failed","Profiles merged/split and re-keyed","Connector stopped","Consent was reset"],"a":1,"why":"Rule changes alter clustering, which changes which IndividualId each segment member references."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"4.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"datacloud-transforms","n":5,"title":"Data Transforms & Calculated Insights","icon":"05","color":"#14B8A6","tagline":"SQL transforms, insights, aggregations","guide":"05-Data-Transforms-and-Calculated-Insights.md","hero":"04-calculated-insights.svg","art":[{"label":"CalculatedInsightService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/CalculatedInsightService.cls"},{"label":"CalculatedInsightServiceTest.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/CalculatedInsightServiceTest.cls"},{"label":"KPI_Metric__c (object)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/KPI_Metric__c/"}],"objectives":["Use SQL (and SQLM/association) transforms in Data 360","Define, normalize and aggregate attributes across sources","Build calculated insights and real-time calculated insights","Scope measures, dimensions, granularity and refresh","Apply business conventions (LTV, churn, health scores)"],"lessons":[{"title":"Transform Types","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Transforms compute attributes over mapped data. SQL transforms run arbitrary queries (window functions, joins, CASE statements). SQLM (SQL mapping) expressions copy/derive per-field values efficiently for high-volume attributes."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","x":"SELECT i.IndividualId,\n       SUM(o.totalamount)                          AS LifetimeValue,\n       CASE WHEN o.totalamount > 1000 THEN 'VIP' ELSE 'Std' END AS Band\n  FROM UnifiedIndividual i\n  LEFT JOIN Order o ON o.IndividualId = i.IndividualId\n GROUP BY i.IndividualId, Band;"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"An insight is NOT a segment. An insight computes a measure (e.g. SUM, COUNT) over data and can be real-time (streaming). A segment filters individuals using attributes/insights - it produces membership, not metrics."}]},{"title":"Calculated Insights & Real-Time","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Calculated insights = dataset + recalculation on a schedule; real-time calculated insights run over streaming data and fire when a measure crosses a threshold - the trigger for moment-based actions (Phases 10/14)."},{"t":"list","items":["Design each insight: measure (SUM/COUNT/AVG), dimension (segment-able attribute), granularity (per day/individual), refresh (schedule/streaming)","Keep the real-time chain streaming end-to-end: aggregation SQL vs streaming","Lab: CalculatedInsightService.compute() and fireRealTime() write KPI_Metric__c rows with threshold logic - the twin of a streaming insight threshold"]},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What makes a calculated insight \"real-time\"?","a":"It runs over streaming data and emits when a measure crosses a threshold - not a scheduled batch recompute."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"5.1","title":"Churn & LTV SQL","stars":2,"obj":"Write the SQL for two insights and wire the threshold.","steps":["Compute LifetimeValue per IndividualId (GROUP BY, SUM).","Compute a churn proxy: last-order date per individual with a > 45-day flag.","In CalculatedInsightServiceTest, assert a 2-member churn crossing fires the action event."],"verify":"SQL is correct to an aggregator; the threshold crossing test asserts exactly the crossing members."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 5 Quiz - Transforms & Insights","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"The relationship between insight and segment is...","opts":["Same thing","Insights compute measures; segments filter members","Segments compute insights","Unrelated"],"a":1,"why":"An insight is a measure over data; a segment is a membership filter that may consume insight attributes."},{"q":"A real-time calculated insight fires when...","opts":["A scheduled job runs","A measure crosses a threshold on streaming data","A file uploads","A report opens"],"a":1,"why":"Streaming insight threshold crossings are the moment-based trigger."},{"q":"SQLM transforms are best for...","opts":["Complex window functions","High-volume per-field derivations","Full-text search","Identity resolution"],"a":1,"why":"SQLM is optimized for per-field copy/derivation at scale."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"5.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"datacloud-segmentation","n":6,"title":"Segmentation","icon":"06","color":"#10B981","tagline":"Static, calendar & dynamic segments","guide":"06-Segmentation.md","hero":"05-segmentation-activation.svg","art":[{"label":"SegmentMembershipService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/SegmentMembershipService.cls"},{"label":"SegmentMembershipServiceTest.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/SegmentMembershipServiceTest.cls"},{"label":"Segment_Sync_Log__c (object)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Segment_Sync_Log__c/"}],"objectives":["Build static, calendar and dynamic segments","Use attribute filters, boolean logic, quick filters","Understand segment versioning and dependency impact","Choose refresh (manual/scheduled/real-time) per need","Mirror membership math with SegmentMembershipService"],"lessons":[{"title":"Segment Types","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A segment is a membership list of individuals matching criteria. Static segments capture a fixed set (they do not auto-update); calendar segments filter by time windows; dynamic segments recompute membership as data streams in - the real-time backbone."},{"t":"table","head":["Type","Refreshes","Real-time capable","Use"],"rows":[["Static","Manual/on-demand","No","One-off campaign lists"],["Calendar","On schedule","Semi","Time-window criteria (last 7 days)"],["Dynamic","Streaming membership","Yes","Moment-based journeys, churn alerts"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Versioning: when segment criteria change, Data 360 keeps versions for the data extensions previously activated. Changing criteria mid-journey can break entry-source drift - plan a version bump per change."}]},{"title":"Membership Math & Dependencies","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Membership = the current set of individuals matching the criteria at refresh time. Segments depend on DMOs/insights; deleting a referenced object or changing a rule invalidates segments and their activations."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// Membership math mirrored in the lab\npublic static Set<String> evaluate(SegmentCriteria c) {\n    Set<String> members = new Set<String>();\n    for (Map<String,Object> row : c.rows) {\n        Boolean match = rowMatches(row, c.filters, c.logic); // AND/OR tree\n        if (match) members.add((String) row.get('memberKey'));\n    }\n    return members;\n}"},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"When does a dynamic segment update membership?","a":"Continuously as qualifying data streams in - no manual/scheduled refresh required."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"6.1","title":"Segment Criteria Build","stars":2,"obj":"Build a segment and verify membership math.","steps":["Define criteria for \"VIP Win-Back\": Tier=Gold AND OrderCount>=5 AND RecencyInDays>=60.","Choose type (static vs dynamic) + refresh and justify for a nightly journey entry.","In SegmentMembershipServiceTest, assert membership contains exactly the 3 qualifying members of 10."],"verify":"Criteria logic AND/OR correct; membership list asserted exactly; refresh decision justified."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 6 Quiz - Segmentation","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Which segment type supports streaming membership?","opts":["Static","Calendar","Dynamic","On-demand"],"a":2,"why":"Dynamic segments recompute membership from streaming data continuously."},{"q":"Changing segment criteria mid-journey...","opts":["Has no effect","Might invalidate versions/activations - plan a version bump","Auto-delivers to new members","Deletes the data extension"],"a":1,"why":"Criteria changes affect segment versions and the data extensions activations feed."},{"q":"A one-off VIP list should use...","opts":["Dynamic segment","Static segment","Real-time calculated insight","Data action"],"a":1,"why":"Static segments capture a fixed membership for one-off campaigns."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"6.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"datacloud-activation","n":7,"title":"Activation & Activation Targets","icon":"07","color":"#84CC16","tagline":"Targets, attributes, refresh & audit","guide":"07-Activation-and-Activation-Targets.md","hero":"05-segmentation-activation.svg","art":[{"label":"ActivationSyncService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/ActivationSyncService.cls"},{"label":"ActivationSyncServiceTest.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/ActivationSyncServiceTest.cls"},{"label":"Activation_Audit__c (object)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Activation_Audit__c/"}],"objectives":["Choose an activation target (MCE, ads, CRM, webhook) per requirement","Configure activation attributes and refresh types","Explain how activations create/update data extensions and audiences","Apply consent filtering and suppression before publishing","Mirror the pattern with ActivationSyncService + Activation_Audit__c"],"lessons":[{"title":"Activation Targets & Configuration","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"An activation connects a segment to a destination. Each target behaves differently: MCE activations produce a synchronized data extension; advertising activations hash identifiers into pseudonymous audiences; CRM activations upsert records; webhooks POST JSON per member (or batch)."},{"t":"table","head":["Target","Produces","Notes"],"rows":[["Marketing Cloud Engagement","Sync data extension","Journey entry reads it; refresh on schedule"],["Advertising (Meta/Google/LinkedIn)","Ad-audience sync","Hashed IDs; consent rules apply"],["Salesforce CRM","Upsert Contact/Account/Lead","Materialized enrichment"],["Webhook / external","JSON POST per member","Near-real-time possible; idempotency needed"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Classic answer: \"Marketing wants repeat buyers in a journey\" - publish the segment to an MCE data extension via an activation, then use the DE as the journey entry source."}]},{"title":"Refresh, Consent & Audit","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Refresh types: manual, scheduled (daily/hourly), real-time - only a streaming chain can drive real-time activation","Activation attributes become the columns of the data extension - choose the minimum needed","Consent-aware suppression: exclude members missing the channel consent category BEFORE publish"]},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// The 3-number audit every activation needs\nActivation_Audit__c a = new Activation_Audit__c();\na.Members_Qualified__c  = qualified.size();\na.Members_Sent__c       = allowed.size();        // after consent filter\n a.Members_Suppressed__c = qualified.size() - allowed.size();"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Errors: record failures separately, retry bounded with backoff, and make publishes idempotent (upsert by member external key) so replays update instead of duplicate."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"7.1","title":"Activation Suppression Math","stars":2,"obj":"Run a consent-suppressed activation and audit the counts.","steps":["Give 5 profiles Email consent and 2 missing it into a 7-member segment.","In ActivationSyncServiceTest, assert 5 sent, 2 suppressed, and the audit row records both.","Re-run the activation twice and assert idempotency (still 5 sent, no duplicates)."],"verify":"Audit row shows qualified=7, sent=5, suppressed=2; second run changes membership, not rows."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 7 Quiz - Activation","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Where do segment members land when activated to Marketing Cloud?","opts":["A marketing email","A synchronized data extension","A platform event","A report"],"a":1,"why":"MCE activations create/populate a data extension that journey entry sources read."},{"q":"A customer opted out of email but is in the segment. The activation...","opts":["Sends anyway","Suppresses them via the Email consent category","Errors out","Deletes their profile"],"a":1,"why":"Consent-aware suppression excludes non-consenting members before publish."},{"q":"To make a publish safe to replay...","opts":["Send one mail","Upsert by external key + bounded retry","Drop the target","Use a new segment"],"a":1,"why":"Idempotent upsert on a stable key means replays update, never duplicate."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"7.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"datacloud-marketing","n":8,"title":"Data Cloud for Marketing","icon":"08","color":"#F59E0B","tagline":"Segments to journeys & engagement feedback","guide":"08-Data-Cloud-for-Marketing.md","art":[{"label":"ActivationSyncService (segment publish)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/ActivationSyncService.cls"},{"label":"ConsentManagementService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/ConsentManagementService.cls"},{"label":"flows/","href":"force-app/main/default/flows/"}],"objectives":["Explain the data flow between Data 360 and Marketing Cloud","Configure a journey entry source backed by a segment activation","Bring engagement data back for profile enrichment and insight","Use consent categories and suppression in the marketing loop","Identify marketing Data 360 permission sets and their scope"],"lessons":[{"title":"The Marketing 360 Loop","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Two directions matter: (1) outbound - segments + attributes activate into an SFMC data extension that journey entry sources read, and (2) inbound - the Marketing Cloud connector ingests send/open/click data back into DLOs to enrich the unified profile and feed the next segment."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"[ Data 360 unified profile ] --segment--> [ Activation -> SFMC data extension ]\n     enrichment <-- engagement <-- sends/opens/clicks join back into profiles"},{"t":"list","items":["Segment to journey: build segment -> activate to MCE (scheduled daily typical) -> journey entry = the data extension -> attributes drive personalization and branches","Refresh cadence must match the journey promise (a \"next 24h\" journey needs frequent refresh)","Golden rule: activate attributes, do not share the order table with marketing"]}]},{"title":"Personas, Permissions & Consent","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Permission set","Scope"],"rows":[["Data 360 Marketing Admin","Create segments & activations; manage marketing data spaces"],["Data 360 Marketing Viewer","View segments/activations and run metrics"],["Data 360 Admin","Full platform administration (ingestion, modeling, security)"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing activation is the strictest consent arena: category opt-ins (Email/SMS/Advertising), capture at every channel, enforcement at activation, and unsubscribes flowing back into Data 360 so the next segment honors them automatically."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"After a send, how do we know who responded so the next segment excludes non-engagers?","a":"The MC connector ingests send/click data back into Data 360; mapped engagement attributes fuel the next segment - closing the marketing loop."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"8.1","title":"Win-back Journey Trace","stars":2,"obj":"Trace and justify a win-back campaign end-to-end.","steps":["Trace: sources -> profile -> segment -> activation -> DE -> journey -> engagement feedback.","Choose refresh cadence + consent category for a \"next 24 hours\" upgrade offer.","Label each hop with the Data 360 or MC feature used."],"verify":"Every hop named; cadence and consent choices justified against the journey promise."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 8 Quiz - Data Cloud for Marketing","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A Data 360 segment reaches Journey Builder through...","opts":["An SFMC data extension populated by an activation","A CSV upload","A Lightning web component","A report folder"],"a":0,"why":"MCE activation produces the data extension that journey entry sources read."},{"q":"Engagement data (opens, clicks) re-enters Data 360 via...","opts":["The Marketing Cloud connector","A webhook only","Einstein Studio","Identity resolution"],"a":0,"why":"The MC connector ingests send/engagement DLOs back into the profile."},{"q":"What can a Data 360 Marketing Admin do but an Admin-specific task set cannot...","opts":["Run installers","Build segments and activations","Manage platform ingestion security","None of these"],"a":1,"why":"Marketing Admin is scoped to segments/activations and marketing data spaces, not platform administration."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"8.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"datacloud-sales-service","n":9,"title":"Data Cloud for Sales & Service","icon":"09","color":"#F97316","tagline":"Unified profiles inside Lightning","guide":"09-Data-Cloud-for-Sales-and-Service.md","art":[{"label":"UnifiedProfileEnrichmentService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/UnifiedProfileEnrichmentService.cls"},{"label":"CaseTriggerHandlerService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/CaseTriggerHandlerService.cls"},{"label":"CaseTriggers.trigger","href":"force-app/main/default/triggers/CaseTriggers.trigger"}],"objectives":["Enable Data 360 data in Lightning (views, related lists, flows)","Configure a Data Cloud-triggered flow for personalization","Explain enrichment vs on-the-fly and when each is appropriate","Read UnifiedProfileEnrichmentService + the Case trigger wiring","Apply these patterns to Lead, Opportunity and Case scenarios"],"lessons":[{"title":"The \"Consume\" Side in the CRM","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Feature","What it does","Config"],"rows":[["Data Cloud-triggered flows","Runs on Data 360 changes to act on CRM","Data 360 -> Data Cloud-triggered flows"],["Data 360 components/lists","Show unified attributes on record pages","Lightning App Builder"],["Enrichment","Copy Data 360 attributes onto CRM records","Data action / flow write-back"],["Data actions","Side effects on Data 360 events","Data actions + flows (Phase 10)"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"On-the-fly keeps the CRM clean but is a VIEW, not queryable CRM data. Materialized enrichment denormalizes for reporting/search but must be kept fresh. Choose per requirement - the exam asks exactly this."}]},{"title":"Data Cloud-Triggered Flow + the Lab Mirror","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Flow skeleton: start on a Data Change Event / segment qualification -> filter -> lookup CRM by sfdc_recordId -> branch -> act (update Case, notify, data action)","High-volume care: selective filters, idempotent effects, read via the Data 360 data source component","Lab: CaseTriggers -> CaseTriggerHandlerService -> UnifiedProfileEnrichmentService pulls profile attributes (lifetime spend, churn, consent channels) onto the Case, mutating in memory before save"]},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"public with sharing class UnifiedProfileEnrichmentService {\n    public static void enrichCases(List<Case> cases) {\n        // 1) collect customer keys, 2) fetch unified-profile summaries in one\n        //    query batch, 3) mutate in memory - NO DML, NO loops, before-update.\n    }\n}"},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why does enrichment mutate in memory rather than DML-loop?","a":"Before-save trigger discipline: read once, apply to the batch, let the trigger transaction save once - respects bulk and governor limits."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"9.1","title":"On-the-Fly vs Materialize","stars":1,"obj":"Classify requirements and test the enrichment service.","steps":["For (a) agent wants live order history, (b) report filters by customer tenure, (c) flow routes on churn tier - pick on-the-fly or materialize.","Add two customers with known lifetime spend to UnifiedProfileEnrichmentServiceTest and assert enrichment writes only those fields."],"verify":"Classification matches the requirement; test asserts exact field writes without DML loops."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 9 Quiz - Data Cloud for Sales & Service","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"\"Agents want to SEE live purchase history on the case page.\"","opts":["Materialize fields to Case","Data 360 component/related list on the page","Build a report","Export to Excel"],"a":1,"why":"Live display = on-the-fly Data 360 components, not field copies."},{"q":"\"IT needs cases filterable by lifetime value in a report.\"","opts":["On-the-fly component","Materialize Lifetime_Value__c via enrichment","Ignore","SOSL"],"a":1,"why":"Reporting/querying needs the value as a real CRM field - materialize it."},{"q":"A Data Cloud-triggered flow best starts when...","opts":["A report is opened","A Data 360 change/qualification event occurs","A formula recalculates","A login happens"],"a":1,"why":"Flows are triggered by Data 360 data change/qualification events."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"9.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":1}]},{"id":"datacloud-dataactions","n":10,"title":"Data Actions & Real-Time Triggers","icon":"10","color":"#EF4444","tagline":"Actions, platform events & the streaming chain","guide":"10-Data-Actions-and-Real-Time-Triggers.md","art":[{"label":"DataActionHandlerService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/DataActionHandlerService.cls"},{"label":"DataActionHandlerServiceTest.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/DataActionHandlerServiceTest.cls"},{"label":"DataActionEventSubscriberTrigger.trigger","href":"force-app/main/default/triggers/DataActionEventSubscriberTrigger.trigger"},{"label":"Data_Action_Event__e (platform event)","href":"force-app/main/default/platformEvents/Data_Action_Event__e/"}],"objectives":["Explain data actions and choose their target (CRM, external, flow)","Configure a data action with filters and idempotency","Combine real-time insights, dynamic segments and data actions","Publish/subscribe to platform events from Apex","Extend and test DataActionHandlerService"],"lessons":[{"title":"Data Actions & the Streaming Chain","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A data action is a configured side-effect firing when Data 360 data changes, a segment qualifies, or a real-time insight crosses a threshold. Targets: Data Cloud-triggered flow, CRM update, invocable Apex, webhook, or platform event."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"streaming ingestion --> real-time calculated insight (threshold) --> dynamic segment --> data action\n   [ Data source ]          [ cross 0.7 churn ]              [ joins At-Risk ]        [ update Case + notify ]"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"The real-time chain must be streaming at EVERY hop. If any hop is batch (nightly source, scheduled insight, static segment) the \"instant response\" fails - naming the breaking hop is the classic answer."}]},{"title":"Idempotency & Platform Events","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Filter early, upsert on a stable key, carry a correlation/batch id, log every run (audit), bounded retry","PublishAfterCommit platform events decouple heavy side effects and dedupe replays","Bulk discipline: build the event list, then ONE EventBus.publish(batch) - never per-record publish"]},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"List<Data_Action_Event__e> events = new List<Data_Action_Event__e>();\nfor (Id memberId : memberIds) {\n    events.add(new Data_Action_Event__e(\n        Member_Key__c = String.valueOf(memberId),\n        Action_Type__c = 'EnrichCase',\n        Payload__c     = buildPayload(memberId)));\n}\nDatabase.SaveResult[] sr = EventBus.publish(events); // one call, full batch"},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"How is recursion prevented in the event subscriber?","a":"TriggerHandlerService.shouldRun(...) guards re-entry so a subscriber update cannot re-fire the same logic in one transaction."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"10.1","title":"Build the Event Subscriber","stars":3,"obj":"Wire and test a platform-event data action.","steps":["Write DataActionEventSubscriberTrigger delegating to DataActionHandlerService, guarded by shouldRun().","Publish 2 events; assert exactly 2 audit rows and no DML-loop count.","Identify which real-time hop breaks if the insight is nightly-batch. "],"verify":"Subscriber is a thin delegate; test asserts exact side effects; the batch-hop answer is articulated."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 10 Quiz - Data Actions & Triggers","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"What fires when a member joins the \"At-Risk\" segment?","opts":["A segment qualification event -> data action","A nightly refresh","A formula","Nothing by default"],"a":0,"why":"Qualification events drive data actions for moment-based flows."},{"q":"A churn alert requires...","opts":["Any data source","Streaming end-to-end: ingest, insight, dynamic segment, action","A static segment","A CSV export"],"a":1,"why":"Real-time service requires every hop streaming."},{"q":"Bulk platform-event publishing means...","opts":["One publish call per event","One EventBus.publish(batch) call","Looping DML","Callouts only"],"a":1,"why":"Batch events into a single publish call to respect governor limits."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"10.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"datacloud-governance","n":11,"title":"Consent Management & Data Governance","icon":"11","color":"#EC4899","tagline":"Consent, retention, spaces & security","guide":"11-Consent-Management-and-Data-Governance.md","hero":"06-consent-governance.svg","art":[{"label":"ConsentManagementService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/ConsentManagementService.cls"},{"label":"ConsentManagementServiceTest.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/ConsentManagementServiceTest.cls"},{"label":"Consent_Preference__c (object)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Consent_Preference__c/"}],"objectives":["Configure consent categories and capture points","Apply consent-gated segmentation and activation","Design data retention policies per DLO/DMO","Design access control with data spaces and permission sets","Explain lineage, ethics and audit artifacts for a consultant"],"lessons":[{"title":"The Consent Model & API","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Data 360 stores one consent preference per person per category (Email, SMS, Advertising...). The Consent API records captures from forms, agents and apps so every channel writes the same store - the \"single source of consent\" answer."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"public with sharing class ConsentManagementService {\n    public static void setConsent(Id personId, String category, Boolean optIn) { /* upsert Consent_Preference__c, one DML */ }\n    public static Set<Id> consentedContacts(Set<Id> candidates, String category) { /* the activation filter */ }\n}"},{"t":"list","items":["Segments include consent-qualified individuals only when the use case requires it","Withdrawal flows back into Data 360 (suppressed = true) so the next activation honors it","ActivationSyncService calls consentedContacts() to produce the 3-number audit (Phase 7)"]}]},{"title":"Retention, Access & Lineage","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Layer","Governs"],"rows":[["Retention period","Purge per DLO/DMO; too-short retention silently weakens profiles/insights"],["Data space","Containment of data AND metadata per BU/use case"],["Permission sets","Who can build/segment/activate; object/field-level security"],["Data lineage","Source to DLO to DMO to insight to segment to activation trail"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Regulated multi-space design: expose only a filtered view of the identity graph to operational spaces; keep the marketing identity universe partitioned in its own space. Design spaces first, then flows/segments/agents against the least-privileged view."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"The three artifacts a regulator asks for are...","a":"Activation runs, consent captures, and data-action executions - all loggable and retained."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"11.1","title":"Consent Capture & Suppression","stars":3,"obj":"Design consent capture and prove withdrawal suppression.","steps":["Design categories + capture sources (form, agent, API) for a telco self-service portal.","Extend ConsentManagementServiceTest: capture 3 contacts across 2 categories, assert consentedContacts returns the right category only, then withdraw one and assert suppression."],"verify":"Single-store captures; consented set filtered by category; withdrawal propagates into the published set."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 11 Quiz - Consent & Governance","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Keeping consent in ONE place across channels is achieved by...","opts":["Per-channel preference stores","The Consent API writing to the Data 360 consent model","A mailing list","Email suppression only"],"a":1,"why":"Consent API centralizes captures into the single consent model."},{"q":"A segment keeps including opted-out members. Likely cause...","opts":["Missing consent/activation filter","Too much retention","Wrong permission set","Einstein Studio"],"a":0,"why":"Categories + suppression at publish decide who is actually sent to."},{"q":"Data retention that is too short causes...","opts":["Higher profile quality","Silently weaker profiles/insights as data is purged","More ID resolution","Faster activations"],"a":1,"why":"Purged history removes attributes that feed profiles and insights."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"11.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"datacloud-analytics","n":12,"title":"Analytics & Reporting on Data Cloud","icon":"12","color":"#A855F7","tagline":"Tableau/CRMA, KPIs & dashboards","guide":"12-Analytics-and-Reporting-on-Data-Cloud.md","art":[{"label":"DataCloudReportingService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/DataCloudReportingService.cls"},{"label":"DataCloudReportingServiceTest.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/DataCloudReportingServiceTest.cls"},{"label":"dashboards/ + reports/","href":"force-app/main/default/"}],"objectives":["Explain how DMOs/unified profiles reach Tableau and CRM Analytics","Choose Data 360 live vs CRM materialized reporting per requirement","Design core service/marketing KPIs over unified data","Read and extend DataCloudReportingService","Build dashboards that slice segments against CRM detail"],"lessons":[{"title":"Data 360 to Analytics Platforms","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Analytics reads Data 360 via a live Data 360 data source in CRM Analytics/Tableau (query DMOs/unified profiles), or via materialized CRM fields the data actions wrote. Both must share the same IndividualId / external key or reconciliation breaks."},{"t":"table","head":["Mode","How","Best for"],"rows":[["Live Data 360 source","Query DMOs/unified profiles directly","On-the-fly unified analytics"],["Materialize to CRM","Data action writes attributes/segments","Native reporting, Salesforce1"],["Marketing Cloud reporting","Engagement analytics across sources","Campaign funnel analytics"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Storing sfdc_recordId / external keys at ingestion is what makes cross-system dashboards possible - rebuild reports from the same spine."}]},{"title":"KPI Design","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Starter set: CSAT by segment, FCR, SLA adherence by entitlement, churn trend, LTV/CAC ratio","Every KPI: measure + dimension + granularity + refresh - write them before building","Dashboards over unified profiles let you slice data the CRM alone cannot (segment-by-case views)"]},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"public with sharing class DataCloudReportingService {\n    public static Map<String, Decimal> kpiSummary(Set<Id> profileRefs) {\n        // aggregates KPI_Metric__c rows for the profile set - the twin of a\n        // Data 360 analytics query over DMO measures.\n    }\n    public static List<Service_KPI__c> recentKpis(String domain, Integer limitRows) { ... }\n}"},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why do dashboards break when sources use different keys?","a":"Without a shared IndividualId/external key the joins cannot reconcile across systems."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"12.1","title":"KPI Spec + Dashboard Build","stars":2,"obj":"Spec a KPI set and extend the reporting service.","steps":["Write measure/dimension/granularity/refresh for \"SLA adherence by segment\".","In DataCloudReportingServiceTest add a 3-account scenario and assert the KPI summary totals.","Point the shipped dashboard/reports at the segment source."],"verify":"Spec table complete; summary totals asserted; dashboard source choice justified."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 12 Quiz - Analytics & Reporting","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Unified customer data for analytics is queried via...","opts":["The DMO / UnifiedIndividual via a Data 360 data source","A CSV export","SOSL","A saved report folder"],"a":0,"why":"CRM Analytics/Tableau Data 360 sources query DMOs and unified profiles."},{"q":"A Salesforce1 report \"open cases by lifetime value\" needs...","opts":["LTV materialized onto CRM (Lifetime_Value__c field)","A live-only view","No data","A platform event"],"a":0,"why":"Native reporting requires the value as a real CRM field."},{"q":"KPI design requires which four specs?","opts":["Measure, dimension, granularity, refresh","Color, font, size, data","User, role, profile, account","Plan, build, test, ship"],"a":0,"why":"A KPI is only built reliably when measure/dimension/granularity/refresh are explicit."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"12.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"datacloud-einstein","n":13,"title":"Einstein & AI in Data 360","icon":"13","color":"#8B5CF6","tagline":"Einstein Studio, RAG & prompt grounding","guide":"13-Einstein-and-AI-in-Data-Cloud.md","art":[{"label":"EinsteinRecommendationService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/EinsteinRecommendationService.cls"},{"label":"EinsteinRecommendationServiceTest.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/EinsteinRecommendationServiceTest.cls"},{"label":"Prompt templates in flows/","href":"force-app/main/default/flows/"}],"objectives":["Position Einstein Studio and BYOM in the Data 360 stack","Scope a predictive use case from unified-profile features","Explain RAG on Data 360: indexing, embeddings, retrievers, hybrid search","Build prompt templates grounded in Data 360 context","Defend the AI governance plan (PII masking, audit, feedback)"],"lessons":[{"title":"Predictive AI & Einstein Studio","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Einstein Studio builds predictive models over DMO/unified-profile data (propensity, churn, LTV, next-best-action). Models deploy a prediction attribute onto profiles that segments, journeys and flows consume (e.g. send win-back to Predicted_Churn >= 0.65). BYOM imports external model output."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"consulting pattern:\n  business decision -> target (pass/fail, horizon) -> features (spend, frequency,\n  tenure, engagement) -> training source (DMO) -> deployed score -> consumers\n  (segment, journey, flow, agent)"},{"t":"list","items":["Model types: propensity (will buy), churn (will churn), LTV, next-best-action","Prediction attribute = the score on UnifiedIndividual; segments filter on it","A model is useless with no consuming segment/activation - design the consumption point first"]}]},{"title":"RAG & Prompt Builder","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Component","Role"],"rows":[["Indexing & chunking","Files/knowledge become searchable chunks"],["Embeddings","Vectors for semantic similarity"],["Retrievers","No-code configs fetching the best context chunks"],["Hybrid search","Vector + keyword (exact SKUs, order numbers)"],["Prompt Builder merge fields","Insert Data 360/profile values into templates"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Grounding is the exam guardrail: answers must cite retrieved context; a template with a \"if insufficient context, say so\" fallback beats a hallucinating agent."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why hybrid search for order-5821 questions?","a":"Keyword exact-match finds 5821; vector similarity alone can miss literal identifiers like SKUs and order numbers."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"13.1","title":"Model Spec + Grounding Plan","stars":2,"obj":"Spec a predictive model and a grounded prompt.","steps":["Write the spec for \"likely-to-repeat-customer\": decision, target, horizon, 4 features, source DMO, deployment attribute.","Draft a refund-answer prompt template with merge fields, retriever input, and a \"say so\" fallback.","Note the governance layer masks."],"verify":"Every spec row filled; template is structurally grounded; masking identified."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 13 Quiz - Einstein & AI","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A deployed model feeds a segment via...","opts":["A prediction attribute on the unified profile","An email send","A report","A CSV"],"a":0,"why":"Model scores deploy onto profiles as prediction attributes consumed by segments."},{"q":"Which retrieves the best context chunks for an answer?","opts":["A retriever (no-code config)","A dashboard","An activation","A match rule"],"a":0,"why":"Retrievers fetch relevant grounding context for prompts."},{"q":"Grounding an agent means...","opts":["Answering from retrieved context with citations","Not citing anything","Using only Apex","Disabling search"],"a":0,"why":"Grounded = bound to trusted retrieved context, with an insufficiency fallback."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"13.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"datacloud-agentforce","n":14,"title":"Agentforce & Data Cloud","icon":"14","color":"#6366F1","tagline":"Data graphs, grounding & agent boundaries","guide":"14-Agentforce-and-Data-Cloud.md","hero":"07-agentforce-data-cloud-grounding.svg","art":[{"label":"AgentforceBillingContextService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/AgentforceBillingContextService.cls"},{"label":"AgentforceBillingContextServiceTest.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/AgentforceBillingContextServiceTest.cls"},{"label":"Data graphs (ARCHITECTURE.md)","href":"ARCHITECTURE.md"}],"objectives":["Explain Data Graphs and why agents query them by IndividualId","Read a Data Graph hierarchical JSON (Individual to engagements)","Choose grounding strategies (retriever, merge fields, Apex action, RAG)","Apply data-space scoping as the agent boundary","Trace the Plan to Ground to Act to Guard loop"],"lessons":[{"title":"Data Graphs & Trusted Context","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A Data Graph is a hierarchical JSON projection of the unified identity. Instead of joining tables, an agent fetches one nested customer view and queries it by IndividualId. Engagement nodes (CatalogEngagement, CartEngagement, AgentEngagement) nest under the Individual."},{"t":"code","lang":"json","x":"{\n  \"Individual\": {\n    \"IndividualId\": \"IND-007\",\n    \"Name\": \"Amina Taylor\",\n    \"LoyaltyTier\": \"Gold\",\n    \"Predicted_Churn\": 0.72,\n    \"Engagements\": {\n      \"CatalogEngagement\": [ { \"ProductId\": \"SKU-412\", \"LastViewed\": \"2026-09-18T14:20:00Z\" } ],\n      \"CartEngagement\": [ { \"CartId\": \"CART-118\", \"TotalAmount\": 649, \"Items\": 2 } ],\n      \"AgentEngagement\": [ { \"SessionId\": \"SES-8821\", \"Topic\": \"refund\", \"Status\": \"open\" } ]\n    }\n  }\n}"},{"t":"callout","kind":"info","x":"Verified architecture: Data 360 data graphs provide trusted customer context for Agentforce; agents look up customers by IndividualId on the graph. No solid IndividualId, no reliable agent context."}]},{"title":"Grounding Strategies & Data-Space Boundaries","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Mechanism","Use when"],"rows":[["Data Libraries / Retrievers","Answers need Knowledge docs, FAQs, PDFs"],["Prompt merge fields","Answers need a few known attribute values"],["Apex actions","Answers need computation or a live query (narrow JSON, guarded limits)"],["RAG on Data 360","Open-domain ask-anything with source-chunk citations"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Data spaces are agent boundaries: an agent scoped to a space sees only its DMOs/fields. To keep an agent from reaching advertising data, build it against a data space that does not contain that data (least privilege at the data layer)."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"@InvocableMethod(label='Get Rewards Context')\npublic static List<String> getRewardsContext(List<Request> reqs) {\n    // 1) resolve IndividualId 2) fetch graph attributes 3) return a narrow\n    // JSON-serialized context node - never dump whole tables into context.\n}"},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"The four steps of the agentic Data 360 loop are...","a":"Plan -> Ground (Data Graph lookup by IndividualId, retrievers, merge fields, RAG) -> Act (Apex/data actions) -> Guard (data space + field-level security + audit)."}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"14.1","title":"Data Graph Read & Scope","stars":3,"obj":"Read the graph, choose grounding, and build the Apex action.","steps":["Path the fields for \"what is in my cart\", \"when did I last view the sleeve\", \"what is my churn risk\".","Choose grounding for (a) warranty PDF answer, (b) cart total, (c) order-5821 refund reason.","Build AgentforceBillingContextService (invocable, narrow node, guarded callouts) and test the whitelist."],"verify":"JSON paths correct; grounding choice justified; test asserts exactly the requested fields."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 14 Quiz - Agentforce & Data Cloud","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"How does an Agentforce agent fetch a customer context?","opts":["Query the Data Graph by IndividualId","Export a CSV","Run a report","Join tables in Apex"],"a":0,"why":"Agents query the data graph by IndividualId to get the nested unified profile."},{"q":"Answer the refund question from docs, do not invent it.","opts":["Use a retriever over the Knowledge base + a grounded template","Free text only","Apex-only","No measures"],"a":0,"why":"Retrievers + grounded prompt templates bind answers to trusted context."},{"q":"Keeping an agent away from advertising data means...","opts":["Data-space scoping (no ad DMOs in the agent space)","Telling it not to","Deleting segments","Consent only"],"a":0,"why":"The agent boundary is the data space it is scoped to, plus field-level security."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"14.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"datacloud-certprep","n":15,"title":"Certification Preparation","icon":"15","color":"#0D9488","tagline":"Verified exam facts & domain-weighted prep","guide":"15-Certification-Preparation.md","art":[{"label":"Certification_Setting__mdt (facts record)","href":"force-app/main/default/customMetadata/Certification_Setting__mdt/"},{"label":"CertificationPrepService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/CertificationPrepService.cls"},{"label":"Training_Question__c (object)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Training_Question__c/"}],"objectives":["Quote the verified exam facts (name, code, format, pricing) cold","Map the 6 domains to this roadmap phases","Run a domain-weighted study plan with evidence-based mocks","Know the rebrand date and its exam implications","Know retake rules and release-based certification updates"],"lessons":[{"title":"Verified Exam Facts (Spring 26)","mins":6,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Fact","Value"],"rows":[["Certification","Salesforce Certified Data 360 Consultant (ex-Data Cloud Consultant)"],["Exam code","Data-Con-101"],["Format","60 scored + up to 5 unscored questions, 105 minutes"],["Passing score","70%"],["Cost / retake","$200 / $100 (USD)"],["Prerequisite / release","None / Spring 26 content"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"info","x":"The Oct 14, 2025 change was brand-level: Data Cloud became Data 360 and the consultant credential followed. Question content is refreshed each release (Winter/Spring 26) - study current release notes, not 2024 ones."}]},{"title":"Domain Blueprint & Study Plan","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Domain","Weight","Study"],"rows":[["Solution Positioning","14%","Phases 1, 17"],["Setup and Administration","13%","Phases 1, 3, 11"],["Source Connection & Ingestion","18%","Phases 2, 3"],["Harmonization & Unification","17%","Phases 4, 5"],["Enhancements, Sharing & Analysis","18%","Phases 6, 12, 13"],["Activations & Utilization","20%","Phases 7-10, 14"]]},{"t":"list","items":["Almost 3/4 of the exam lives after ingestion - do not skip Phases 7-10 thinking they are \"the last mile\"","Evidence-based mocks: average 75% for 3 consecutive weeks before booking","Scenario reflex: ask -> constraint (consent, space, real-time, retention) -> smallest correct config"]}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"15.1","title":"Fact Card + Scenario Battery","stars":2,"obj":"Internalize the facts and train the scenario reflex.","steps":["Write a 20-question flashcard set (weights, prices, format, rename facts, retention/consent) and run it twice daily.","Write 6 scenario questions mixing real-time-chain, consent, data-space and on-the-fly-vs-materialize.","Map each roadmap checkpoint to its domain weight."],"verify":"Facts recited cold; each scenario names ask + constraint + smallest correct config."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 15 Quiz - Certification Prep","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"The credential name and exam code are...","opts":["Data Cloud Consultant / DC-202","Data 360 Consultant / Data-Con-101","Customer 360 / C3","Data Cloud Admin / DA-1"],"a":1,"why":"Renamed credential, exam Data-Con-101 (60 scored + up to 5 unscored)."},{"q":"The passing score and time limit are...","opts":["60% / 90 min","70% / 105 min","75% / 75 min","80% / 120 min"],"a":1,"why":"Verified: 70% pass, 105 minutes, 60 scored questions."},{"q":"Which domain carries the heaviest weight?","opts":["Solution Positioning 14%","Setup & Administration 13%","Data Activations and Utilization 20%","Harmonization 17%"],"a":2,"why":"Activations & Utilization is 20%; together with ingestion and enhancement domains it dominates."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"15.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"datacloud-practice","n":16,"title":"Practical Exercises & Mini Projects","icon":"16","color":"#059669","tagline":"Signature labs, timed runs & 3 mini projects","guide":"16-Practical-Exercises-and-Mini-Projects.md","art":[{"label":"scripts/apex/hello.apex","href":"scripts/apex/hello.apex"},{"label":"scripts/soql/account.soql","href":"scripts/soql/account.soql"},{"label":"All lab test classes","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/"}],"objectives":["Execute the six signature labs end-to-end in a scratch org","Build mini projects A (consent activation), B (churn handler) and C (agent context)","Prove 75%+ coverage on the lab Apex classes","Run the 90-minute timed battery","Self-grade against the pass/pass+/distinction rubric"],"lessons":[{"title":"Signature Labs (L1-L6)","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Lab","Service","Done looks like"],"rows":[["L1 Ingest payload","DataCloudIngestionService","Payload normalized + Data_Stream_Log__c row"],["L2 Identity match","IdentityResolutionHelperService","Candidate rows created, rule set asserted"],["L3 Segment math","SegmentMembershipService","Segment_Sync_Log__c + exact membership"],["L4 Activate (consent)","ActivationSyncService","3 sent / 2 suppressed / audit row"],["L5 Churn -> action","CalculatedInsightService + DataActionHandlerService","Threshold fires the event; subscriber acted"],["L6 Enrich Case","UnifiedProfileEnrichmentService","Case trigger writes profile fields only for known profiles"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Timed battery: L1-L3 (45 min) + L4-L6 (45 min) = 90 of your 105-minute exam budget. Practice the clock as much as the code. Coverage target: every class you author in this repo at >= 90%."}]},{"title":"Mini Projects A, B, C","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["A - Consent-aware activation console: setConsent 1-DML upsert, publish returns qualified/sent/suppressed, rerun idempotent","B - Real-time churn handler: ONE EventBus.publish(batch), subscriber guarded, 12 of 50 fire exactly 12 events","C - Agentforce grounding: invocable narrow JSON node, field whitelist, graceful profile-not-found, guarded callouts"]},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// Mini project B bulk discipline\nList<Data_Action_Event__e> evs = new List<Data_Action_Event__e>();\nfor (Decimal score : crossingScores) {\n    evs.add(new Data_Action_Event__e(Member_Key__c = ..., Action_Type__c = 'ChurnAlert'));\n}\nDatabase.SaveResult[] res = EventBus.publish(evs);   // one call, not 12"}]},{"title":"Practice: The 3 Lab-Scale Builds","mins":20,"blocks":[{"t":"proj","id":"MP1","title":"Consent-Aware Activation Console","stars":3,"obj":"A repeatable path from consent capture to a suppressed, audited activation.","reqs":["ConsentManagementService.setConsent keys (member, 2 categories, opt-in) as ONE idempotent upsert.","ActivationSyncService.publishActivation(segment, members, category) returns + persists qualified/sent/suppressed.","Test: withdraw -> next publish excludes the member; two runs stay idempotent (no duplicate rows)."],"success":"L4 numbers (3/2/5) proven by tests; rerun updates rows, does not add."},{"t":"proj","id":"MP2","title":"Real-Time Churn Handler","stars":3,"obj":"Churn-score threshold crossing becomes a CRM-side action.","reqs":["CalculatedInsightService.fireChurnActions(candidates, threshold) publishes ONE event per crossing member in a single EventBus batch.","DataActionEventSubscriberTrigger delegates to DataActionHandlerService guarded by TriggerHandlerService.shouldRun.","Test: 50 members, 12 cross -> exactly 12 events, exactly 12 audit rows, no DML loop."],"success":"sf apex run test verifies exact counts and recursion guard."},{"t":"proj","id":"MP3","title":"Agentforce Grounding Context","stars":3,"obj":"A narrow, safe Apex action returning a Data-Graph-style JSON node.","reqs":["@InvocableMethod takes {IndividualKey, requestedFields} -> exact-fields JSON node; never dumps tables.","Unknown key -> {error: profile-not-found} node, no exception surfaced to the agent.","Test asserts field whitelist enforced, JSON valid, callouts guarded."],"success":"AgentforceBillingContextServiceTest green; whitelist test rejects unlisted fields."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 16 Quiz - Practical Exercises","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Which single rule prevents the classic activation bug in mini project A?","opts":["Idempotent upsert by external key + consent suppression before publish","Sending twice","Fresh segment every run","No audit"],"a":0,"why":"Upsert by stable key + pre-publish suppression keep counts consistent across reruns."},{"q":"Bulk discipline in mini project B means...","opts":["12 publish calls","One EventBus.publish(batch) for all crossings","DML in a loop","Callouts per record"],"a":1,"why":"Batch into a single publish call, delegate once, guard recursion."},{"q":"Mini project C returns only requestedFields because...","opts":["Token economy + least privilege","The user asked","JSON is smaller","Fields are hidden"],"a":0,"why":"Agents should never receive profile data they did not ask for."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"MP1","li":2,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"MP2","li":2,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"MP3","li":2,"kind":"proj","stars":3}]},{"id":"datacloud-usecases","n":17,"title":"Real-World Use Cases","icon":"17","color":"#D97706","tagline":"Retail, finance, telco & manufacturing briefs","guide":"17-Real-World-Use-Cases.md","art":[{"label":"C-S-T-A checklist (guide)","href":"developer Data Cloud Consultant Roadmap/17-Real-World-Use-Cases.md"},{"label":"ARCHITECTURE.md","href":"ARCHITECTURE.md"},{"label":"README.md","href":"README.md"}],"objectives":["Read a brief and extract a precise requirement statement","Apply the C-S-T-A checklist (Customer, Source, Transform, Act + constraint)","Propose architecture: sources, DMOs, identity, insights, segments, activations, actions","Spot the four trap constraints (real-time, consent, no write-back, retention)","Carry one case from brief to architecture"],"lessons":[{"title":"The Consulting Reflex: C-S-T-A","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Letter","Question"],"rows":[["C","Customer/identity - who is resolved (IndividualId, Contact)?"],["S","Source - what data lives where (ERP, web, CRM, MC)?"],["T","Transform - unify, standardize, calculate (LTV, churn)?"],["A","Act - send a journey, update a Case, notify, report?"],["+","Constraint - consent, data space, retention, real-time, budget?"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Voice the architecture in ONE sentence: \"We ingest S with C, unify under I, compute T as an insight, build the segment, publish via an activation to the target, and trigger a data action - subject to X.\" If you can say it in one breath you understand the case."}]},{"title":"Four Flagship Use Cases","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"UC1 Retail    : ERP orders + web events + MC sends -> win-back journey (Gold, 5+, 60d)\nUC2 Financials : 3 legacy systems -> churn score + single consent withdrawal -> agent\nUC3 Telco/Health: self-service unified profile + grounded assistant (warranty PDFs)\nUC4 Manufac    : ERP + IoT telemetry + cases -> real-time Maintenance_Score P1"},{"t":"list","items":["UC1 trap: marketing must not see order history (data space + activation attributes)","UC2 trap: agent must not see card limits (data-space scoping + FLS)","UC3 trap: display (on-the-fly + grounding) is NOT consent-gated activation","UC4 trap: the telemetry-to-account join is the hard problem; real-time chain must be streaming end-to-end"]}]},{"title":"Practice","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"17.1","title":"C-S-T-A Your Own Case","stars":2,"obj":"Extract and voice an architecture for a real brief.","steps":["Write the C/S/T/A table + one-sentence architecture for your own highest-value use case (or UC4).","Bold the constraint that most changes the answer for UC2 (agent scope).","Map each hop of UC4 to a service in this repo (ingest -> calculate -> act)."],"verify":"One-breath sentence given; agent scope constraint named; hop-to-service mapping complete."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 17 Quiz - Real-World Use Cases","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"The first step for ANY brief is...","opts":["Pick the activation target","Apply the C-S-T-A checklist","Write Apex","Book the exam"],"a":1,"why":"Identify identity, sources, transforms, outcomes AND constraint before proposing architecture."},{"q":"Why can marketing not see order history in UC1?","opts":["Cost of storage","Data-space boundary + activation attributes only","No API","Too many rows"],"a":1,"why":"Boundary keeps order tables in their space; marketing receives only activation attributes."},{"q":"The single-word kill shot for a real-time requirement is...","opts":["Batch","Consent","Retention","Attribution"],"a":0,"why":"Any batch hop (source, insight, segment) breaks the real-time chain."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"17.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"datacloud-solutions","n":18,"title":"Use Case Solutions","icon":"18","color":"#DC2626","tagline":"Worked solutions, rubric & capstone","guide":"18-Use-Case-Solutions.md","art":[{"label":"ARCHITECTURE.md (single-platform design)","href":"ARCHITECTURE.md"},{"label":"Scenario answers in answers.js","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"},{"label":"README.md (getting started)","href":"README.md"}],"objectives":["Grade your Phase 17 attempt against worked solutions","Produce complete architectures for retail, finance, telco and manufacturing","Wire any UC pattern into this repo architecture","Solve the single-platform capstone with correct data-space separation","Be ready for the Data-Con-101 scenario questions"],"lessons":[{"title":"Worked Solutions at a Glance","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["UC","Answer sentence","Trap avoided"],"rows":[["UC1 Retail","Nightly ERP + streaming web + MC sends, unify by email/loyalty, Gold 5+ 60d segment, MCE activation with 4 attributes, consent filter","Activate attributes, not the table"],["UC2 Financials","3 sources unify on official ID, ChurnScore insight, channel-isolated consent, At-Risk segment -> Case action, agent scoped w/o card limits","Data-space + FLS"],["UC3 Telco/Health","Unify + Data Graph, console on-the-fly components, assistant grounded on warranty retriever + Apex billing action in self-service space","Display != activation"],["UC4 Manufac","Hourly ERP + streaming IoT -> device-serial-to-account resolution, real-time Maintenance_Score insight >80, data action P1 + webhook, dispatch sees dynamic segment","Every hop streaming"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Grading rubric (score 0-2 each, target >= 6/8): sources named, identity rules, transforms/insights, segment+refresh, activation target, data-space/consent/retention, real-time discipline, repo tie-in."}]},{"title":"The One-Design Capstone","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"One identity spine (IndividualId), two unified profiles (Individual + Account), four data spaces (RetailOps, RisOps, SelfService, Manufacturing). Shared assets: ConsentManagementService (single withdrawal), IdentityResolutionHelperService, DataCloudReportingService KPI layer, DataActionHandlerService + Data_Action_Event__e."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"Shared spine: IndividualId resolves everywhere.\nSpaces: RetailOps | RisOps | SelfService | Manufacturing.\nState  3 hops where \"data in the wrong space\" is a compliance disaster\n        and 3 hops where a batch would break a real-time promise."}]},{"title":"Capstone Builds","mins":20,"blocks":[{"t":"proj","id":"UC1","title":"Retail Win-Back Solution","stars":3,"obj":"Implement the UC1 architecture on the repo skeleton.","reqs":["Wire the nightly-ERP + streaming-web + MC-sends story onto DataCloudIngestionService paths.","Reuse ActivationSyncService with consent category Email and assert the 3-number audit.","Document which data space holds order history vs activation attributes."],"success":"Journey entry = DE fed by activation; audit = 5 sent/2 suppressed pattern; spaces documented."},{"t":"proj","id":"UC2","title":"Financial Churn + Compliance Solution","stars":3,"obj":"Churn threshold drives service action with governance intact.","reqs":["ChurnScore insight via CalculatedInsightService + a streaming threshold (12-of-50 fires exactly 12).","Channel-isolated consent via ConsentManagementService; single withdrawal respected.","AgentforceBillingContextService scoped so card limits are unlistable (whitelist test proves it)."],"success":"Events + audit + consent math green; whitelist test rejects card limits."},{"t":"proj","id":"UC3","title":"Self-Service Console Solution","stars":3,"obj":"On-the-fly display + grounded assistant, display separate from activation.","reqs":["Use Data 360 component/related-list pattern conceptually + UnifiedProfileEnrichmentService for on-demand enrichment test.","AgentforceBillingContextService returns narrow account node; a retriever concept over warranty docs is documented.","Test that display path never touches promotion consent categories."],"success":"Enrichment test asserts exact fields; display/activation separation proven by a test."},{"t":"proj","id":"UC4","title":"Manufacturing Flash-Maintenance Solution","stars":4,"obj":"Real-time maintenance score to P1 case + dispatch.","reqs":["Streaming ingestion path (ingest_payload.apex) -> CalculatedInsightService.MaintenanceScore threshold > 80.","DataActionHandlerService publishes platform events; subscriber updates Case to P1 + simulates webhook.","SegmentMembershipService drives the \"Flash Maintenance\" dynamic list for dispatch."],"success":"End-to-end streaming discipline; equal-CPS audit; the batch answer is articulable."},{"t":"proj","id":"CAP","title":"Single-Platform Data 360 Academy","stars":4,"obj":"Assemble the entire roadmap into one deployable, documented lab.","reqs":["Every phase guide mirrored to docs/guide/ and linked from curriculum.js.","Force-app deploys with all services, triggers, flows, reports, permission set and custom metadata.","README + ARCHITECTURE walk a new learner from scratch org to a passing Data-Con-101 score."],"success":"sf project deploy start succeeds; Apex coverage >= target; the repo is the study curriculum."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 18 Quiz - Use Case Solutions","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Expose account lifetime value to a marketing segment - table or attributes?","opts":["Share the order table","Activation attributes only","Email the report","Delete the table"],"a":1,"why":"Activate attributes, do not share the table (UC1 golden rule)."},{"q":"The churn journey must fire within 15 minutes of a score crossing. You need...","opts":["Streaming data + streaming insight + dynamic segment + action","A nightly batch","A report","A CSV export"],"a":0,"why":"Real-time end-to-end streaming chain only - at every hop."},{"q":"How do you prove \"where did ChurnScore come from\"?","opts":["The Data 360 lineage trail + retained audit artifacts","A screenshot","A formula field","Trust me"],"a":0,"why":"Lineage (source to DLO to DMO to model to segment to activation) + audit artifacts."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"UC1","li":2,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"UC2","li":2,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"UC3","li":2,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"UC4","li":2,"kind":"proj","stars":4},{"id":"CAP","li":2,"kind":"proj","stars":4}]}],"answerIds":["1.1","10.1","11.1","12.1","13.1","14.1","15.1","17.1","2.1","3.1","4.1","5.1","6.1","7.1","8.1","9.1","CAP","MP1","MP2","MP3","UC1","UC2","UC3","UC4"]};

window.ABDO_DATA["dev"] =
  {"slug":"dev","meta":{"slug":"dev","brand":"Developer I & II Academy","name":"Apex Development","cert":"Platform Developer I & II","icon":"🧑‍💻","color":"#6366F1","desc":"17 phases of Apex, triggers, testing, LWC, integration and governor-limit mastery for Developer I & II.","repo":"Salesforce-Dev-I-II-Roadmap","github":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-Dev-I-II-Roadmap","live":"https://abdoaddouli.github.io/Salesforce-Dev-I-II-Roadmap/","guideBase":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-Dev-I-II-Roadmap/blob/main/developer%20Roadmap/","repoBlob":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-Dev-I-II-Roadmap/blob/main/","phases":17,"source":"Salesforce Dev I and II roadmap\\Salesfoerce Dev I & II"},"modules":[{"id":"dev-fund","n":1,"title":"Developer Fundamentals","icon":"01","color":"#4F46E5","tagline":"Org model, data model, SOQL & DML basics","guide":"01-Developer-Fundamentals.md","art":[{"label":"DeveloperFundamentalsTest","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/DeveloperFundamentalsTest.cls"},{"label":"config/project-scratch-def.json","href":"config/project-scratch-def.json"},{"label":"Custom objects (objects/)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/"}],"objectives":["Explain the org, object, record and field model","Describe standard vs custom objects and relationships","Write basic SOQL with fields and parent lookups","Use DML insert / update / upsert / delete correctly","Understand system context vs user context and sharing keywords"],"lessons":[{"title":"The Platform Data Model","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Every Salesforce org is a set of OBJECTS (database tables), FIELDS (columns) and RECORDS (rows). Standard objects ship with the platform; custom objects plugin with the __c suffix."},{"t":"table","head":["Concept","SQL analogy","Example"],"rows":[["Object","Table","Account, Opportunity, Contact"],["Field","Column","Health_Score__c, StageName"],["Record","Row","An Account named Acme Corp"],["Relationship","Foreign Key / Join","Contact.AccountId -> Account"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Two relationship flavours: Lookup (loose, both sides optional) and Master-Detail (tight: cascade delete, roll-up summaries, shared ownership). A custom object can have at most two Master-Detail relationships."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Roll-up summary fields require which relationship type?","a":"Master-Detail. The summary lives on the master and aggregates child records."}]},{"title":"System Context vs User Context","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Apex can run in SYSTEM context (class declared without sharing keyword, or invoked by an integration) where it ignores the running user's sharing. User context (with sharing) respects record-level access."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// with sharing: + record access of the calling user\npublic with sharing class MyService {\n    public static List<Account> mine() {\n        return [SELECT Id FROM Account];\n    }\n}"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Modern best practice: use the keyword explicitly (with / without / inherited sharing). Never leave it implicit; the PDII exam tests the side effects."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A with-sharing class queries Accounts. What does the running user observe?","a":"Only records the user has sharing access to (Private OWD hides the rest)."}]},{"title":"First SOQL & DML","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"Account a = new Account(Name = 'Acme', Health_Score__c = 88);\ninsert a;                                         // DML create\n\na.Health_Score__c = 90;\nupdate a;                                         // DML update\n\nAccount saved = [SELECT Id, Name, Health_Score__c\n                 FROM Account WHERE Id = :a.Id];  // SOQL query"},{"t":"list","items":["DML calls: insert, update, upsert, delete, undelete, merge","SOQL returns typed SObject lists; bind variables with a colon","Database.* methods (e.g. Database.insert) return SaveResults you can inspect"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Never loop DML. Build a List<Account> and call insert(list) once - the single most repeated exam-style bug."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 1 Quiz - Developer Fundamentals","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Roll-up summaries require which relationship?","opts":["Lookup","Master-Detail","Polymorphic","Hierarchical"],"a":1,"why":"Roll-up summaries aggregate children onto the master of a Master-Detail relationship."},{"q":"A with sharing class sees records based on.","opts":["System access","The running user's sharing access","The owner only","No filtering"],"a":1,"why":"with sharing enforces record-level access of the calling user."},{"q":"Which DML is best to insert-or-update in one statement?","opts":["update","upsert","merge","insert"],"a":1,"why":"upsert either inserts new or updates existing records (optionally keyed by an external id)."}]},"total":3,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-apex","n":2,"title":"Apex Language Essentials","icon":"02","color":"#7C3AED","tagline":"Primitives, collections, classes, exceptions","guide":"02-Apex-Language-Essentials.md","art":[{"label":"SecurityService (inner class + exception)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/SecurityService.cls"},{"label":"IntegrationService.CalloutResult","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/IntegrationService.cls"},{"label":"CertificationPrepService.QuizOutcome","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/CertificationPrepService.cls"}],"objectives":["Use primitives, Blob, sObject and collections","Choose List / Set / Map for the right job","Write methods, constructors, inner classes and enums","Model errors with custom exception classes","Explain access modifiers including global"],"lessons":[{"title":"Data Types & Collections","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"Integer n = 42;            // primitives\nDecimal d = 42.5;           // currency-safe decimal\nBoolean ok = true;          // not \"true\"? then NOT\nString s = 'hello';\nList<String> tags = new List<String>{ 'a', 'b' };\nSet<Id> seen = new Set<Id>();\nMap<Id, Account> byId = new Map<Id, Account>();"},{"t":"p","x":"Lists keep order, Sets de-duplicate, Maps give O(1) lookup. The classic performance win: build a Map once and join - never nested-for nested lookups."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"You must de-duplicate account ids from a feed. Which collection?","a":"Set<Id> - uniqueness is its whole purpose."}]},{"title":"Classes, Inner Classes & Enums","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"public enum SecurityAccess { READ, CREATE, UPDATE, DELETE }\n\npublic class SecurityService {\n    public class SecurityAccessDeniedException extends Exception {\n    }\n}"},{"t":"p","x":"Inner classes group small contracts (exceptions, DTOs like QuizOutcome, CalloutResult) next to the class that uses them. Enums give a fixed, readable choice set."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"A custom exception extends Exception. Throwing it gives callers a catchable, self-describing error instead of a generic one."}]},{"title":"Access Modifiers","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Modifier","Visibility"],"rows":[["private","Inside the class only (default)"],["protected","Class + subclasses"],["public","Any Apex in the org"],["global","Cross-namespace + REST callouts from outside"]]},{"t":"p","x":"@RestResource methods must be global static. @AuraEnabled methods must be public (or global). Everything else defaults to private - good encapsulation hygiene."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 2 Quiz - Apex Language Essentials","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Which collection guarantees uniqueness?","opts":["List","Map (keys)","Set","Array"],"a":2,"why":"Set<T> stores unique elements only."},{"q":"A REST resource method must be declared.","opts":["private static","public static","global static","protected"],"a":2,"why":"REST resources are invoked from outside the org, so they must be global."},{"q":"A custom exception is created by.","opts":["extends Exception","implements Thrower","new CustomException","enum Exception"],"a":0,"why":"Custom exceptions subclass System.Exception."}]},"total":3,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-soql","n":3,"title":"SOQL & SOSL","icon":"03","color":"#0EA5E9","tagline":"Queries, joins, aggregates and search","guide":"03-SOQL-and-SOSL.md","art":[{"label":"SoqlSoslService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/SoqlSoslService.cls"},{"label":"SoqlSoslServiceTest","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/SoqlSoslServiceTest.cls"},{"label":"Scripts (scripts/soql/)","href":"scripts/soql/"}],"objectives":["Write SELECT filters, ordering, limits and offsets","Aggregate with COUNT/SUM/AVG, GROUP BY and HAVING","Query relationships with dot notation and subqueries","Use SOSL FIND across multiple objects","Guard dynamic SOQL from injection"],"lessons":[{"title":"SELECT Fundamentals","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"SELECT Id, Name, Health_Score__c\nFROM Account\nWHERE Health_Score__c >= 80\n  AND Industry = 'Technology'\nORDER BY Name\nLIMIT 10"},{"t":"list","items":["Filters: =, >, <, >=, <=, !=, IN, LIKE with % wildcards","ORDER BY field (ASC/DESC), NULLS FIRST/LAST","LIMIT / OFFSET for paging","Bind values with :myVar - never string-concatenate"]}]},{"title":"Aggregates & Grouping","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"List<AggregateResult> rows = [\n    SELECT StageName, AVG(Deal_Quality_Score__c) avgQuality\n    FROM Opportunity\n    GROUP BY StageName\n];\nfor (AggregateResult r : rows) {\n    Decimal avg = (Decimal) r.get('avgQuality'); // read by ALIAS\n}"},{"t":"p","x":"AggregateResult has no typed fields - every value is fetched by the query alias via get('alias'). Use HAVING to filter grouped results."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"You need the average quality score per stage. Which query feature?","a":"GROUP BY with AVG(field) - results read through the alias with r.get('alias')."}]},{"title":"Relationships & Search","mins":11,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// parent: dot notation\nSELECT Id, Name, Account.Name FROM Opportunity WHERE Id = :oppId;\n\n// child: subquery\nSELECT Id, Name,\n       (SELECT Id, Amount FROM Opportunities WHERE IsClosed = false)\nFROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds;\n\n// SOSL: one search, many objects\nList<List<SObject>> hits = [\n    FIND :term IN ALL FIELDS\n    RETURNING Account(Id, Name), Contact(Id, Name)\n];\n// hits[0] = Account matches, hits[1] = Contact matches"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"SOSL is for full-text search ACROSS objects or fields. SOQL is for structured querying. Use SOSL when the user types a free-text term."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 3 Quiz - SOQL & SOSL","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A student types \"Acme\" into a search box. Best tool?","opts":["SOQL","SOSL","Aggregate query","Relationship query"],"a":1,"why":"Free-text search across objects is SOSL FIND."},{"q":"AggregateResult values are read via.","opts":["Object fields","result.get('alias')","get(field, value)","SQL columns"],"a":1,"why":"Aggregate output is accessed by the query alias."},{"q":"Which filter keeps this portable: WHERE Name LIKE :term?","opts":["String concatenation","A bind variable","A static term","A formula"],"a":1,"why":"Bind variables keep queries safe and maintainable."}]},"total":3,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-trig","n":4,"title":"Triggers & Order of Execution","icon":"04","color":"#F59E0B","tagline":"Context variables, bulk patterns, handler framework","guide":"04-Triggers-and-Order-of-Execution.md","art":[{"label":"TriggerHandlerService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/TriggerHandlerService.cls"},{"label":"AccountTrigger","href":"force-app/main/default/triggers/AccountTrigger.trigger"},{"label":"CodeReviewTrigger","href":"force-app/main/default/triggers/CodeReviewTrigger.trigger"}],"objectives":["Use Trigger.new / old / oldMap and the is* context flags","Write bulk-safe triggers (no SOQL/DML in loops)","Implement the trigger-handler and recursion-guard patterns","Recite the Salesforce Order of Execution","Know what validation/formulas/FLS each phase runs"],"lessons":[{"title":"Context Variables","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"trigger AccountTrigger on Account (before update) {\n    System.debug(Trigger.new);       // incoming records\n    System.debug(Trigger.oldMap);    // pre-update snapshot\n    // isBefore/isAfter/isInsert/isUpdate/isDelete/isUndelete/isExecuting\n}"},{"t":"p","x":"Trigger.new is a list of the new values (fired records), oldMap the pre-change state for updates. One trigger serves ALL trigger events - switch on the is* flags."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Detect a stage/status TRANSITION by comparing Trigger.new stage to Trigger.oldMap.get(record.Id).stage - never react to unchanged records."}]},{"title":"Bulk & Handler Patterns","mins":11,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"trigger AccountTrigger on Account (before update) {\n    if (TriggerHandlerService.shouldRun('AccountTrigger')) {\n        PerformanceService.normalizeHealthScores(Trigger.new);\n    }\n}"},{"t":"list","items":["One handler class per trigger; the trigger stays a thin if-statement","Collect the work, batch SOQL once, DML once (no loops)","Guard recursion with a suppression registry + processed-id Set","BEFORE triggers edit Trigger.new in-memory - zero extra DML"]}]},{"title":"Order of Execution","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"1. Load the record + fields from DB        5. AFTER triggers (incl. external changes)\n2. Before-save validation rules           6. Assignment rules\n3. BEFORE triggers                        7. Auto-response rules\n4. System User-defined field updates      8. Workflow field updates    (legacy)\n   from BEFORE triggers                   9. Process Builder (legacy)\n                                          10. Escalation rules\n                                          11. Record-triggered FLOWS\n                                          12. Roll-up summary recalcs / formula eval\n                                          13. AFTER-save commit + email/async"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Apex DEBUG confirms order: BEFORE triggers -> validation -> AFTER triggers -> assignment -> flows. Exam favourite: what runs when (and can you see attempts of SOQL/DML in loops there)."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 4 Quiz - Triggers & Order of Execution","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Which runs first when a record is saved?","opts":["After trigger","Validation rules","Before triggers","Flows"],"a":2,"why":"Before triggers run before validation rules and after triggers."},{"q":"To edit health scores before save with no extra DML, use.","opts":["Before update trigger","After update trigger","Scheduled flow","Approval step"],"a":0,"why":"Before triggers edit Trigger.new in memory; the platform saves them."},{"q":"A 200-record update fires the trigger how many times per record?","opts":["200 times","Once - Trigger.new holds all 200","Per batch of 10","Twice total"],"a":1,"why":"Triggers are bulk: one execution with all records in Trigger.new."}]},"total":3,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-async","n":5,"title":"Async Apex & Platform Events","icon":"05","color":"#10B981","tagline":"Queueable, Batch, Schedulable, EventBus","guide":"05-Async-Apex-and-Platform-Events.md","art":[{"label":"AsyncJobService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/AsyncJobService.cls"},{"label":"EventPublisherService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/EventPublisherService.cls"},{"label":"IntegrationEventSubscriberTrigger","href":"force-app/main/default/triggers/IntegrationEventSubscriberTrigger.trigger"}],"objectives":["Choose Queueable vs Batch vs Schedulable vs future","Write batchable classes with Database.Stateful","Schedule jobs with valid cron expressions","Publish and subscribe to platform events","Test async code with Test.startTest / stopTest"],"lessons":[{"title":"The Async Toolbox","mins":11,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Tool","Use when","Entry point"],"rows":[["Queueable","Stateful job + sObject args + chaining","System.enqueueJob"],["Batchable","Millions of rows in chunks","Database.executeBatch"],["Schedulable","Run on a cron","System.schedule"],["@future","Simple fire-and-forget (no sObject args)","Call annotated method"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Async work inherits the CORRECTED record data and gets its own set of governor limits. Completed jobs are visible on Apex Jobs."}]},{"title":"Batch Mechanics","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"public class HealthScoreRecalculator\n    implements Database.Batchable<SObject>, Schedulable {\n\n    public Database.QueryLocator start(bc) {\n        return Database.getQueryLocator('SELECT Id, Health_Score__c FROM Account');\n    }\n    public void execute(bc, List<SObject> scope) {\n        // scope = up to 200 records; DML once per chunk\n        List<Account> as = (List<Account>) scope;\n        for (Account a : as) a.Health_Score__c = clamp(a.Health_Score__c);\n        update as;\n    }\n    public void finish(bc) { /* notify / re-enqueue */ }\n}"},{"t":"p","x":"start() returns a QueryLocator that streams lazily - no 50k-row heap limit. Chunk size is passed to Database.executeBatch(batch, 200)."}]},{"title":"Platform Events","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// publish (bulk!)\nList<Integration_Event__e> events = new List<Integration_Event__e>();\nevents.add(new Integration_Event__e(\n    Direction__c = 'Outbound',\n    Integration_Type__c = 'REST',\n    Status__c = 'Success',\n    Correlation_Id__c = correlationId\n));\nInteger accepted = EventBus.publish(events).size();"},{"t":"p","x":"A subscriber trigger on the event object reacts after delivery. Low-volume events deliver synchronously after the transaction; high-volume are async and large. Events decouple publisher from subscriber (loose coupling)."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why use a platform event instead of a direct method call?","a":"Loose coupling: publisher and subscriber never reference each other, and the bus survives either side changing."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 5 Quiz - Async Apex & Platform Events","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Process millions of records in 200-row chunks. Which class?","opts":["Schedulable","Queueable","Batchable","@future"],"a":2,"why":"Batchable iterates scope chunks via start/execute/finish."},{"q":"A Queueable may receive which parameters?","opts":["Standard primitives only","sObjects and their ids","No params","Only Dates"],"a":1,"why":"Queueables accept transactional (sObject) arguments - hence they are the preferred async tool."},{"q":"Test async jobs by wrapping the enqueue in.","opts":["Test.visibleForTesting","Test.startTest()...stopTest()","System.runAs","Test.loadData"],"a":1,"why":"stopTest() forces pending async jobs to complete before the test method ends."}]},"total":3,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-automation","n":6,"title":"Automation: Flows + Apex","icon":"06","color":"#F97316","tagline":"Declarative vs imperative, invocable methods","guide":"06-Automation-Flows-and-Apex.md","art":[{"label":"TrainingQuestionTrigger","href":"force-app/main/default/triggers/TrainingQuestionTrigger.trigger"},{"label":"StudyPlanTrigger","href":"force-app/main/default/triggers/StudyPlanTrigger.trigger"},{"label":"CertificationPrepService (invokable-style logic)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/CertificationPrepService.cls"}],"objectives":["Decide Flow vs Apex for a job","Build record-triggered before/after-save flows","Expose Apex to Flows with @InvocableMethod","Mix declarative automations safely","Avoid recursion across mixed automations"],"lessons":[{"title":"Flow or Apex?","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Scenario","Best tool"],"rows":[["Simple field update on save","Before-save Flow"],["Create child records after save","After-save Flow"],["Complex multi-object logic + loops","Apex"],["HTTP callout mid-process","Apex (Flow requires invocable)"],["Recurring batch maintenance","Scheduled Flow or Batch"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Rule of thumb: prefer declarative for business logic a power user can maintain; use Apex when the logic needs loops, callouts, or hard to model in Flow."}]},{"title":"Invocable Methods","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"public class StudyPlanActions {\n    @InvocableMethod(label='Generate 17-phase plan')\n    public static void generate(List<Request> reqs) {\n        for (Request r : reqs) {\n            CertificationPrepService.buildStudyPlan(\n                r.learnerId, r.cert, r.targetDate);\n        }\n    }\n    public class Request {\n        @InvocableVariable public Id learnerId;\n        @InvocableVariable public String cert;\n        @InvocableVariable public Date targetDate;\n    }\n}"},{"t":"p","x":"@InvocableMethod allows a Flow to call Apex with typed inputs (@InvocableVariable). One method call per flow element; loop in the Flow if needed."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Bulk-safe even here: iterate the request list, gather work, then DML once in the method."}]},{"title":"Mixing Automations Safely","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Flow updates a field -> Apex trigger fires -> Flow fires again: recursion","Use a suppression registry (TriggerHandlerService) as the guard","Keep ONE automation owner per concern","Test the combined path end-to-end, not each piece alone"]}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 6 Quiz - Automation","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A Flow needs to call Apex with typed inputs. Use.","opts":["@RestResource","@InvocableMethod","@AuraEnabled","@TestVisible"],"a":1,"why":"Invocable methods are called by Flow/PB with InvocableVariable inputs."},{"q":"A simple in-memory field default on save is best as.","opts":["Before-save Flow","Apex trigger","Approval process","Workflow rule"],"a":0,"why":"Before-save flows update without an extra DML round-trip."},{"q":"Flow and trigger both update the same field: risk is.","opts":["Compile error","Mutual recursion","Locking contention","Duplicate records"],"a":1,"why":"Each update re-fires the other automation - guard with a suppression flag."}]},"total":3,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-ui","n":7,"title":"UI Foundations: VF & Aura","icon":"07","color":"#EC4899","tagline":"Pages, controllers, events & components","guide":"07-UI-Foundations-Visualforce-and-Aura.md","art":[{"label":"LwcDataService (Aura-compatible @AuraEnabled)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/LwcDataService.cls"}],"objectives":["Build Visualforce pages with standard controllers","Write custom controllers and controller extensions","Understand Aura components, attributes and events","Use @AuraEnabled from an aura:method / client call"],"lessons":[{"title":"Visualforce Basics","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"<apex:page controller=\"AccountPageController\">\n  <apex:form>\n    <apex:inputField value=\"{!acct.Name}\" />\n    <apex:commandButton action=\"{!save}\" value=\"Save\" rerender=\"msg\" />\n    <apex:outputText id=\"msg\" value=\"{!msg}\" />\n  </apex:form>\n</apex:page>"},{"t":"list","items":["Standard controllers give CRUD + data out of the box","Controller extensions add custom methods to a standard controller","View state is stored server-side; it grows with every bound value","rerender / actionFunction drive partial refreshes"]}]},{"title":"Aura Components","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"<aura:component controller=\"LwcDataService\">\n  <aura:attribute name=\"accounts\" type=\"Account[]\" />\n  <ui:button label=\"Load\" action=\"{!c.load}\" />\n</aura:component>"},{"t":"p","x":"Aura components are the older LWC sibling: markup + a controller.js with action handlers. @AuraEnabled methods are called from the client controller. Application events broadcast across components; component events bubble up a hierarchy."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A child component must tell its parent something happened. Which Aura mechanism?","a":"A component event fired by the child and handled by the parent."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 7 Quiz - UI Foundations","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Adding custom logic to a standard controller uses a.","opts":["Controller extension","New controller","Remote action","Subflow"],"a":0,"why":"Extensions wrap a standard controller to add methods."},{"q":"An event fired by a child and handled by its parent is.","opts":["Application event","Component event","Broadcast event","Custom event"],"a":1,"why":"Component events bubble locally; application events broadcast globally."},{"q":"Aura/LWC client code calls server logic via.","opts":["@AuraEnabled methods","@InvocableMethod","Database.executeBatch","EventBus.publish"],"a":0,"why":"@AuraEnabled exposes public static Apex to client calls."}]},"total":2,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-lwc","n":8,"title":"Lightning Web Components","icon":"08","color":"#06B6D4","tagline":"Composition, @wire, imperative calls","guide":"08-Lightning-Web-Components.md","art":[{"label":"LwcDataService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/LwcDataService.cls"},{"label":"LwcDataServiceTest","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/LwcDataServiceTest.cls"}],"objectives":["Distinguish @wire (cacheable) from imperative calls","Know why cacheable=true forbids DML","Read reactive properties and getters","Organize components with composition slots"],"lessons":[{"title":"@wire vs Imperative","mins":11,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"js","x":"import { LightningElement, wire } from 'lwc';\nimport getAccounts from '@salesforce/apex/LwcDataService.getAccountsWithContacts';\n\nexport default class AccountList extends LightningElement {\n  @wire(getAccounts) accounts;              // reactive, cached\n  // imperative alternative:\n  // import call ... ; this.accounts = await getAccounts();\n}"},{"t":"p","x":"@wire subscribes a property to the streamed result and re-fires when inputs change - repeat calls cost no network. Imperative calls are explicit async awaits."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"cacheable=true means the Apex method MUST NOT perform DML - wire can re-run many times and mutation would break the cache guarantees."}]},{"title":"Security & Composition","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Child components receive data via properties / public getters","Child -> parent communication goes through CustomEvent dispatch","lwc:if / lwc:for directives render conditionals and lists","Always let Apex enforce CRUD/FLS - LWC never bypasses it"]}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 8 Quiz - Lightning Web Components","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"@wire is best paired with an Apex method that is.","opts":["@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)","@InvokableMethod","global without sharing","@future"],"a":0,"why":"Wire streams cached results, so the method must be cacheable (read-only)."},{"q":"A cacheable Apex method may NOT.","opts":["Run SOQL","Return records","Perform DML","Contain a query"],"a":2,"why":"Cacheable = read-only; DML would poison the cache."},{"q":"A child notifies a parent by.","opts":["Dispatching a CustomEvent","Setting a boolean","Aura application event","Lightning message"],"a":0,"why":"CustomEvent bubbles up to parent listeners - the idiomatic LWC pattern."}]},"total":2,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-test","n":9,"title":"Testing & Debugging","icon":"09","color":"#8B5CF6","tagline":"@isTest habits, mocks, coverage gates","guide":"09-Testing-and-Debugging.md","art":[{"label":"IntegrationServiceTest (HttpCalloutMock)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/IntegrationServiceTest.cls"},{"label":"EventPublisherServiceTest","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/EventPublisherServiceTest.cls"}],"objectives":["Write @isTest classes with TestSetup","Isolate with SeeAllData=false and System.runAs","Simulate HTTP with HttpCalloutMock","Pin async behavior with startTest/stopTest","Understand the 75% overall coverage requirement"],"lessons":[{"title":"Test Anatomy","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"@isTest\nprivate class DeveloperFundamentalsTest {\n    @TestSetup static void makeData() { /* shared rows */ }\n    @isTest static void behaviourUnderTest() {\n        // arrange -> act -> assert\n        System.assert(condition);\n        System.assertEquals(expected, actual);\n    }\n}"},{"t":"p","x":"By default tests SeeAllData=false: they only see data they create (plus your re-created TestSetup). Assertions are mandatory - a test that never asserts proves nothing."}]},{"title":"Mocks & Fakes","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"class MockSuccess implements HttpCalloutMock {\n    public HttpResponse respond(HttpRequest req) {\n        HttpResponse res = new HttpResponse();\n        res.setStatusCode(200);\n        res.setBody('{\"ok\":true}');\n        return res;\n    }\n}\n@isTest static void myTest() {\n    Test.setMock(HttpCalloutMock.class, new MockSuccess());\n    // no real network hit - the mock answers every callout\n}"},{"t":"list","items":["HttpCalloutMock + Test.setMock simulate outbound REST calls","Test.setFixedSearchResults seeds deterministic SOSL","Test.getEventBus().deliver() flushes queued platform events","Test.startTest/stopTest drives async to completion"]}]},{"title":"Coverage & CI Gates","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"bash","x":"# run the whole suite and print coverage\nsf apex run test -c -r json"},{"t":"p","x":"Production deployments require at least 75% overall code coverage. Aim for behaviour-level coverage, not line-gaming. Triggers count: cover the trigger path, not just the service."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 9 Quiz - Testing & Debugging","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A real HTTP callout inside a unit test will.","opts":["Succeed silently","Fail for security reasons","Be mocked automatically","Run in the background"],"a":1,"why":"Callouts are blocked in tests - you must install an HttpCalloutMock first."},{"q":"SeeAllData=false means tests see.","opts":["All org data","Only data the test created","Only system records","Production data"],"a":1,"why":"Isolation: tests only see records they create (plus TestSetup)."},{"q":"Required overall coverage to deploy to production is.","opts":["50%","65%","75%","100%"],"a":2,"why":"The platform requires at least 75% total code coverage."}]},"total":3,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-perf","n":10,"title":"Performance & Large Data Volumes","icon":"10","color":"#6366F1","tagline":"Selective SOQL, indexes, joins, batch","guide":"10-Performance-and-Large-Data-Volumes.md","art":[{"label":"PerformanceService (Map join + clamp)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/PerformanceService.cls"},{"label":"PerformanceServiceTest","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/PerformanceServiceTest.cls"}],"objectives":["Recognise non-selective queries and index usage","Replace nested loops with Map joins","Apply batch patterns for large data volumes","Read Query Plans to prove selectivity","Stay inside synchronous governor limits"],"lessons":[{"title":"Selective Queries & Indexes","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Selective = the engine uses an index (primary key, foreign key, standard index fields)","Add custom indexes or skinny tables where filters are expensive","Compare with Universal Containers pattern: a filter returns <1% of rows that’s healthy, >10% is a full scan","Run EXPLAIN or the Query Plan tool in the Developer Console"]}]},{"title":"The Map Join","mins":11,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"// anti-pattern: O(n*m) nested loops\nfor (Account a : accounts) for (Contact c : contacts) { ... }\n\n// pattern: O(n+m) map join\nMap<Id, List<Contact>> byAccount = new Map<Id, List<Contact>>();\nfor (Contact c : contacts) {\n    if (!byAccount.containsKey(c.AccountId)) byAccount.put(c.AccountId, new List<Contact>());\n    byAccount.get(c.AccountId).add(c);\n}\n// then per account: byAccount.get(a.Id)"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"HealthScoreRecalculator (AsyncJobService) is the full bulk pattern: chunked scope + one DML per chunk + zero row limit in start()."}]},{"title":"Governor Targets","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Limit (sync)","Value"],"rows":[["SOQL queries","100"],["SOQL rows returned","50,000"],["DML statements","150"],["DML rows processed","10,000"],["Callouts","10"],["CPU time","10 seconds"],["Heap size","6 MB"],["Apex transactions / trigger bulk","one per DML batch"]]}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 10 Quiz - Performance","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"JOIN over 5k accounts and 50k contacts with nested loops is.","opts":["O(n+m)","O(n*m)","O(log n)","O(1)"],"a":1,"why":"Nested loops multiply; a Map join keeps it O(n+m)."},{"q":"A filter the query planner would reject as non-selective includes.","opts":["Id = :x","OwnerId = :x","RecordType so a scratch field","A formula wrapping the indexed field"],"a":3,"why":"Wrapping an indexed field in a formula precludes index usage."},{"q":"Batchable start() avoids the row-count limit because it returns a.","opts":["List<SObject>","QueryLocator","Set<Id>","AsyncRequest"],"a":1,"why":"QueryLocator streams lazily, so the 50k record limit does not apply."}]},"total":3,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-integration","n":11,"title":"Integration & Enterprise Patterns","icon":"11","color":"#14B8A6","tagline":"Named credentials, callouts, REST, event bus","guide":"11-Integration-and-Enterprise-Patterns.md","art":[{"label":"IntegrationService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/IntegrationService.cls"},{"label":"InboundRestService","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/InboundRestService.cls"},{"label":"IntegrationLogTrigger","href":"force-app/main/default/triggers/IntegrationLogTrigger.trigger"}],"objectives":["Configure Named Credentials and call callout:Name/path","Handle timeouts, status codes and a single retry","Build inbound @RestResource APIs","Use platform events as a decoupled integration channel","Audit every integration attempt on Integration_Log__c"],"lessons":[{"title":"Outbound Callouts","mins":11,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();\nreq.setEndpoint('callout:Dev_API_Endpoint/health'); // named credential\nreq.setMethod('GET');\nreq.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');\nreq.setTimeout(10000);               // don't hang a transaction\nHttpResponse res = new Http().send(req);\nif (res.getStatusCode() >= 200 && res.getStatusCode() < 300) { ... }"},{"t":"p","x":"Named Credentials hold base URL + auth (OAuth 2.0, basic, cert) in the ORG - secrets never reach source control. Synchronous limit: 10 callouts per transaction (guard it!)."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"How do you keep API tokens out of git?","a":"Named Credential referenced as callout:Name/... - auth lives in the org, not in code."}]},{"title":"Inbound REST APIs","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"@RestResource(urlMapping='/DevRoadmap/v1/logs/*')\nglobal with sharing class InboundRestService {\n    @HttpGet\n    global static void doGet() {\n        // RestContext.request / response drive the exchange\n        // logic lives in global static helpers -> unit-testable\n    }\n}"},{"t":"p","x":"Expose Apex to external systems with @RestResource and global static doGet/doPost/doDelete/etc. Keep pure logic in testable static helpers because RestContext is not available under test."}]},{"title":"The Event-Driven Audit Trail","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Every integration attempt writes an Integration_Log__c row (direction, type, status, endpoint, correlation id, response code). An after-insert trigger publishes Integration_Event__e - dashboards and external clients can subscribe without touching the service."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Correlation ids tie your local logs to the external system’s request-id - observability that saves hours in production."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 11 Quiz - Integration","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Synchronous Apex is limited to how many callouts per transaction?","opts":["100","50","10","1"],"a":2,"why":"Ten callouts is the synchronous transaction ceiling."},{"q":"Secrets for external APIs live in.","opts":["Apex constants","Named Credentials","Custom labels","Static resources"],"a":1,"why":"Named Credentials store the auth at runtime and reference endpoint callout:Name/."},{"q":"RestContext is not available in tests, so REST logic should live in.","opts":["The doGet method","Global static helper methods","Triggers","Flows"],"a":1,"why":"Testable helpers let unit tests exercise the same code the endpoint calls."}]},"total":3,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-release","n":12,"title":"Release Management & CI/CD","icon":"12","color":"#84CC16","tagline":"Scratch orgs, deployment, pipelines","guide":"12-Release-Management-and-CICD.md","art":[{"label":"manifest/package.xml","href":"manifest/package.xml"},{"label":"sfdx-project.json","href":"sfdx-project.json"},{"label":"config/project-scratch-def.json","href":"config/project-scratch-def.json"}],"objectives":["Create scratch orgs from sfdx-project.json sources","Use sf project deploy / retrieve","Understand change sets, CLI and packages","Shape a CI/CD pipeline with quality gates","Enforce 75% coverage + PMD/lint before release"],"lessons":[{"title":"The sf CLI Workflow","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"bash","x":"# authorize a Dev Hub once\nsf org login web -d\n\n# create + deploy a scratch org\nsf org create scratch -f config/project-scratch-def.json -a dev\nsf project deploy start --source-dir force-app -o dev\n\n# quality gate\nsf apex run test -c -o dev"},{"t":"p","x":"Source of truth is git. Scratch orgs are disposable dev environments stamped by sfdx-project.json at API 68.0. Never hand-edit production."}]},{"title":"Deployment Vehicles","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Vehicle","Use for"],"rows":[["Change sets","Metadata between orgs you control"],["sf project deploy","Source-driven deploys in CI"],["Unlocked packages","Versioned, installable to any org"],["Managed packages","Partner/distribution with namespaces"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Dependencies (custom objects before classes, permission sets before users) matter. manifest/package.xml lets a deploy walk the whole dependency graph."}]},{"title":"Pipeline Gates","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Lint: eslint for JS/LWC, PMD/Salesforce scanner for Apex","Prettier verify keeps formatting reviews cheap","Deploy to scratch, run the full test suite, assert 75%+","Promote through sandboxes, then production with a release week"]}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 12 Quiz - Release Management","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Disposable dev environments built from source are.","opts":["Sandboxes","Scratch orgs","Change sets","Production copies"],"a":1,"why":"Scratch orgs are generated from git sources - fast, disposable, repeatable."},{"q":"Versioned packages installable to any org are.","opts":["Permission sets","Unlocked packages","Report folders","Static resources"],"a":1,"why":"Unlocked (or managed) packages carry version numbers and dependencies."},{"q":"Before production deploy, CI must assert.","opts":["A clean workspace only","75%+ test coverage and passed tests","A green dashboard","Two admins"],"a":1,"why":"Coverage + passing tests are the mandatory release gates."}]},"total":3,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-cert","n":13,"title":"Certification Prep","icon":"13","color":"#EF4444","tagline":"Blueprint maps, exam strategy, traps","guide":"13-Certification-Prep.md","art":[{"label":"CertificationPrepService (quiz engine)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/CertificationPrepService.cls"},{"label":"Training_Question__c","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Training_Question__c/"},{"label":"Study_Plan__c","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/Study_Plan__c/"}],"objectives":["Memorise both exam blueprints and weights","Budget 105 minutes across 60 questions","Avoid the classic traps (bulk, sharing, order of execution)","Run the in-repo quiz engine and study-plan generator","Execute a 7-day exam sprint"],"lessons":[{"title":"Blueprint Maps","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["PDI domain","Weight"],"rows":[["Developer Fundamentals","27%"],["Process Automation & Logic","28%"],["User Interface","25%"],["Testing / Debugging / Deployment","20%"]]},{"t":"table","head":["PDII domain","Weight"],"rows":[["Advanced Dev Fundamentals","15%"],["Process Automation, Logic & Integration","27%"],["User Interface","20%"],["Testing / Debugging / Deployment","20%"],["Performance","18%"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Both: 60 questions, 105 minutes. PDI pass = 68%, PDII pass = 65%. PDII requires PDI; the old superbadges prerequisite was retired in October 2025."}]},{"title":"The Top Traps","mins":11,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Trap","Remember"],"rows":[["DML in a loop","Gather first, one DML statement"],["Implicit sharing","Declare with / without / inherited sharing"],["Order of execution","Before triggers > validation > after > assignment > flows"],["Cacheable DML","@wire methods are read-only"],["SOSL vs SOQL","Free-text = SOSL; structured = SOQL"],["Retry everything","Only 5xx deserve a single retry"]]}]},{"title":"The Exam Sprint","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"num","items":["Run CertificationPrepService.buildStudyPlan to materialise 13 Phase rows","Fill Training_Question__c with the repo quiz bank + your wrong answers","Daily: 30 scored questions + review every miss via the Explanation__c field","Twice-weekly: full 60-question mock under 105-minute timers","Final 48h: only traps, blueprint weights, and order-of-execution review"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Register on Webassessor, run the Pearson VUE system check early, and book the exam 4+ weeks out to keep the sprint honest."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 13 Quiz - Certification Prep","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"PDI passing score is.","opts":["60%","65%","68%","75%"],"a":2,"why":"Platform Developer I requires 68%; PDII requires 65%."},{"q":"Which runs second in the Order of Execution?","opts":["Before trigger","Validation rules","Assignments","Flows"],"a":1,"why":"Before triggers run first, validation rules run second."},{"q":"After Process Builder was retired and flows replaced it, a record-triggered Flow runs.","opts":["Before triggers only","At step 11, after assignment rules","At the very start","Never"],"a":1,"why":"Record-triggered flows execute late in the sequence, after after-triggers and assignment rules."}]},"total":3,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-exercises","n":14,"title":"Practical Exercises & Mini Projects","icon":"14","color":"#059669","tagline":"31 exercises, 10 mini projects, 1 capstone","guide":"14-Practical-Exercises-and-Mini-Projects.md","art":[{"label":"14-Practical-Exercises-and-Mini-Projects.md","href":"developer Roadmap/14-Practical-Exercises-and-Mini-Projects.md"},{"label":"15-Answers-and-Results.md","href":"developer Roadmap/15-Answers-and-Results.md"}],"objectives":["Apply fundamentals through graded exercises (★ to ★★★)","Build mini projects combining 2–3 phases of concepts","Complete a capstone project touching all 13 roadmap phases","Practice real scratch-org deployment and testing workflows"],"lessons":[{"title":"S1 · Fundamentals & Apex Basics (Phases 1–2)","mins":28,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"This phase is the hands-on laboratory for every concept in the roadmap. Each section maps to the corresponding theory phase. Exercises are graded ★ (beginner) to ★★★ (advanced). Mini projects combine multiple phases into a single buildable feature."},{"t":"table","head":["Rating","Difficulty"],"rows":[["★","Beginner — single concept, guided steps"],["★★","Intermediate — multiple concepts, some decision-making"],["★★★","Advanced — multi-phase integration, design decisions required"],["★★★★","Expert — full application across all roadmap phases"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Workflow: complete all ★ exercises before ★★/★★★, do mini projects only after the preceding exercises, and check your work against 15-Answers-and-Results.md after every exercise."},{"t":"ex","id":"1.1","title":"SOQL Parent-Child Query","stars":1,"obj":"Query the core Sales Cloud data model with relationship traversal.","steps":["Create a scratch org and deploy source.","Write an anonymous Apex script that creates 3 Accounts with Health_Score__c values of 50, 75 and 90, then creates Contacts: 2 for the first Account, 1 for the second, 0 for the third.","Write a single SOQL query that returns every Account with its child Contacts using a subquery.","Write a second SOQL query that returns every Contact with its parent Account.Name using dot notation.","System.debug the results and count the total contacts across all accounts."],"verify":"You used exactly 2 SOQL statements. No queries inside loops."},{"t":"ex","id":"1.2","title":"DML Round Trip with Limits Tracking","stars":1,"obj":"Master insert, update, upsert, delete, and undelete while tracking governor usage.","steps":["In anonymous Apex, build a List<Account> with 5 records and insert them in ONE statement.","Read them back with one SOQL. Print Limits.getDmlStatements() and Limits.getLimitDMLStatements().","Modify the Health_Score__c field of 3 of the 5 records and update in ONE statement.","Upsert all 5 using their standard Id.","Delete 2 records, then undelete one of them.","Query deleted records: SELECT Id, Name FROM Account WHERE IsDeleted = true ALL ROWS.","Print the final DML statement count and total records processed."],"verify":"Total DML statements used ≤ 5 (insert + query + update + delete + undelete + ALL ROWS query)."},{"t":"ex","id":"1.3","title":"Collections: List, Set, Map","stars":2,"obj":"Use all three collection types with sObjects and demonstrate the Map constructor pattern.","steps":["Insert 10 Accounts in one DML statement.","Build a Set<Id> of all Account Ids from the query results.","Build a Map<Id, Account> using the new Map<Id, Account>([SELECT ...]) constructor.","Iterate over accountsById.keySet() and print each Account Name.","Demonstrate containsKey with a valid Id and a bogus Id.","Convert the map to List<Account> via accountsById.values() and verify the size.","Print the CPU time before and after using Limits.getCpuTime()."],"verify":"You never access a record by index after building the map. All lookups use .get(id)."},{"t":"ex","id":"1.4","title":"Sharing Model Smoke Test","stars":2,"obj":"Observe the difference between with sharing and without sharing.","steps":["Create public with sharing class SharingTestA with public static Integer countAccounts() returning [SELECT COUNT() FROM Account], and public without sharing class SharingTestB with the same method.","From anonymous Apex, run both as admin and print the results — both return the same count.","Write a test class SharingModelTest that creates a low-privilege Standard User profile user in @TestSetup.","Use System.runAs(lowPriv) to call both classes.","Assert that SharingTestA.countAccounts() returns fewer rows than SharingTestB.countAccounts()."],"verify":"The test passes and demonstrates the sharing difference."},{"t":"ex","id":"1.5","title":"Partial vs All-or-Nothing DML","stars":2,"obj":"Contrast Database.insert(list, false) with insert list on mixed-validity data.","steps":["Build a list containing 3 valid Account records and 1 record with a Name longer than 255 characters.","Run Database.insert(records, false) and loop the SaveResult[] to print isSuccess() and getErrors() for each record.","Count successes and failures.","Now try insert records (all-or-nothing) inside a try/catch and observe the DmlException behavior.","Assert that the all-or-nothing approach saved 0 records, while the partial approach saved 3."],"verify":"You can explain why partial DML saved records despite one failure."},{"t":"ex","id":"1.6","title":"Custom Exception and Error Handling","stars":2,"obj":"Create and use a custom exception class.","steps":["Create AccountValidationException extends Exception.","Write a method validateAccount(Account a) that throws AccountValidationException if Name is blank or Health_Score__c is outside 0–100.","Call validateAccount from anonymous Apex in a try/catch block for: a blank-name account, a score of 150, and a valid account.","Print the exception message for invalid inputs and \"Valid\" for the valid one."],"verify":"Custom exception is caught separately from generic Exception."},{"t":"proj","id":"MP1","title":"Account Health Management Service","stars":3,"obj":"Build a service class that manages Account health scores with full DML, collections, and exception handling.","reqs":["Create AccountHealthService.cls with with sharing.","getAccountsByMinScore(Integer minScore) — query accounts above a threshold.","buildAccountMap(List<Id> accountIds) — returns a Map for O(1) lookups.","normalizeScores(List<Account> accounts) — clamps Health_Score__c to 0–100, sets null to 70.","bulkUpdateScores(Map<Id, Integer> scoreUpdates) — applies score updates in one DML.","calculateAverageScore(List<Account> accounts) — returns the average (handle empty list).","Create AccountHealthServiceTest.cls with @TestSetup creating 10 accounts with varied scores, one test per service method, and assertions on collection sizes, DML counts and edge cases (empty list, null scores)."],"success":"sf apex run test -c shows 100% coverage and all tests pass."}]},{"title":"S2 · SOQL & SOSL (Phase 3)","mins":14,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"2.1","title":"Aggregate Queries with Aliases","stars":1,"obj":"Write GROUP BY queries and read AggregateResult by alias.","steps":["Create 3 Accounts, each with a different number of Contacts (5, 3, 1).","Write an aggregate SOQL: SELECT AccountId, COUNT(Id) contactCount FROM Contact WHERE AccountId != null GROUP BY AccountId.","Loop the AggregateResult list, casting row.get('contactCount') to Integer.","Write a second aggregate with HAVING COUNT(Id) > 2 and verify only the 5-contact account appears.","Write a query using AVG(Health_Score__c) on Account and cast the result to Decimal."],"verify":"You read aggregates only by alias, never by source field name."},{"t":"ex","id":"2.2","title":"Dynamic SOQL with Injection Protection","stars":2,"obj":"Build safe dynamic queries and demonstrate injection defense.","steps":["Write a method searchAccounts(String searchTerm) that builds the query with String.escapeSingleQuotes:","Call it with TechCorp and verify results.","Call it with ' OR 1=1 -- and verify it returns 0 results (escaped).","Write a second method using bind variables: Database.query('SELECT Id, Name FROM Account WHERE Name = :searchTerm').","Compare the two approaches and document when each is appropriate."],"code":{"lang":"apex","x":"String query = 'SELECT Id, Name FROM Account WHERE Name LIKE \\'%' + String.escapeSingleQuotes(searchTerm) + '%\\' LIMIT 10';\nreturn Database.query(query);"},"verify":"The escaped version returns 0 results for injection input. The bind version throws for LIKE patterns."},{"t":"ex","id":"2.3","title":"SOSL Multi-Object Search","stars":2,"obj":"Use SOSL to search across Account and Contact simultaneously.","steps":["Create an Account named \"Northwind Traders\" and a Contact named \"North Windlass\".","Execute SOSL: FIND 'North' IN ALL FIELDS RETURNING Account(Id, Name), Contact(Id, FirstName, LastName).","Print results[0] size (Account matches) and results[1] size (Contact matches).","Write a test that uses Test.setFixedSearchResults to stub the SOSL results.","Assert the correct object order in the List<List<SObject>>."],"verify":"Results[0] is Accounts, results[1] is Contacts. Test passes with fixed results."},{"t":"proj","id":"MP2","title":"Universal Search Service","stars":3,"obj":"Build a search service that combines SOQL and SOSL for a unified search experience.","reqs":["Create SearchService.cls with: searchAcrossObjects(String term) — SOSL across Account, Contact, Lead.","searchAccountsWithChildren(String nameFilter, Integer minContacts) — SOQL with subquery + HAVING.","countRecordsByObject() — aggregate count across standard objects.","Create SearchServiceTest.cls with SOSL tests using Test.setFixedSearchResults, aggregate tests with setup data, and edge cases (empty search term, no matching records)."],"success":"All tests pass; SOSL test uses fixed results; aggregate casts are correct."}]},{"title":"S3 · Triggers & Order of Execution (Phase 4)","mins":15,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"3.1","title":"Before-Trigger Defaulting","stars":1,"obj":"Set field defaults in a before insert trigger without extra DML.","steps":["Create a trigger AccountDefaultTrigger on Account (before insert) that sets Health_Score__c to 70 when null and Description to 'Auto-created on ' + Date.today() when null.","Insert an Account with only Name set.","Query it back and verify both fields were populated.","Insert 200 Accounts in one list and verify the trigger handled all records (no governor violation)."],"verify":"No second DML was used. The fields are saved with the insert."},{"t":"ex","id":"3.2","title":"Change Detection in After-Update","stars":2,"obj":"Act only when a specific field changes, not on every update.","steps":["Study the Code_Review__c object. Create a trigger CodeReviewChangeTrigger on Code_Review__c (after update).","Compare Trigger.oldMap[key].Review_Status__c with Trigger.newMap[key].Review_Status__c.","Only when Review_Status__c changed, create an Integration_Log__c record marking the change.","Update a record changing Comments__c only — verify NO log is created.","Update the same record changing Review_Status__c — verify a log IS created."],"verify":"Change detection works correctly; unrelated field changes are ignored."},{"t":"ex","id":"3.3","title":"Recursion Guard Implementation","stars":3,"obj":"Prevent a trigger from re-entering itself when its own DML fires the trigger again.","steps":["Create RecursionGuard.cls with a private static Set<Id> processedIds.","shouldProcess(Id recordId) — returns true if not yet processed, then adds to the set.","markProcessed(Id recordId) — adds to the set. reset() — clears the set (for testing).","Write a trigger on Account (after update) that updates Description when Health_Score__c changes, guarded by RecursionGuard.","Write a test that updates Health_Score__c and verifies Description was updated exactly once, resetting the guard at the start of each test."],"verify":"No infinite loop; the trigger fires exactly twice (before validation + after update)."},{"t":"proj","id":"MP3","title":"Lead Auto-Assignment Engine","stars":3,"obj":"Build a complete trigger-driven lead routing system.","reqs":["Create custom metadata Lead_Routing_Rule__mdt with Industry__c (text), Owner__c (lookup to User) and Priority__c (number).","Create LeadAutoAssignmentTrigger on Lead (before insert): collect all Industry values, query matching rules sorted by Priority__c, assign OwnerId from the first match, and set Assignment_Source__c to 'Rule: ' + rule.Name.","Create a handler class LeadAssignmentHandler.cls with with sharing.","Write LeadAssignmentHandlerTest.cls with 5 leads across 3 industries (some matching, some not), assertions that matches are assigned and non-matches keep the default owner, plus a bulk test of 200 leads in one insert."],"success":"Trigger is thin (one line delegating to handler). Handler is bulkified. All tests pass."}]},{"title":"S4 · Async Apex & Platform Events (Phase 5)","mins":15,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"4.1","title":"Queueable Apex Lifecycle","stars":1,"obj":"Enqueue a job, track it, and handle failures.","steps":["Create SimpleQueueable.cls implementing Queueable with a constructor taking an Async_Job_Monitor__c Id.","In execute, set Job_Status__c = 'Running', do work, then set Job_Status__c = 'Completed'.","Add a Boolean shouldFail constructor parameter that causes an exception in execute.","In anonymous Apex, create an Async_Job_Monitor__c with Job_Type__c = 'Queueable', enqueue the job, wait, then query the monitor to see the status change.","Repeat with shouldFail = true and verify Job_Status__c = 'Failed' and the error message is captured."],"verify":"Status transitions work; the exception is caught and recorded."},{"t":"ex","id":"4.2","title":"Batch Apex with QueryLocator","stars":2,"obj":"Process a large dataset in chunks using Batch Apex.","steps":["Insert 500 Accounts with varied Health_Score__c values.","Create ScoreBatch.cls implementing Database.Batchable<sObject>: start returns Database.getQueryLocator('SELECT Id, Health_Score__c FROM Account'); execute clamps scores to 0–100 and sets null to 70 in one DML; finish logs completion.","Execute with scope 200 and count how many batches ran.","Execute with scope 2000 and compare.","Assert all accounts have valid scores after batch completion."],"verify":"Scope 200 = 3 batches. Scope 2000 = 1 batch. All scores are 0–100."},{"t":"ex","id":"4.3","title":"Platform Event Round Trip","stars":2,"obj":"Publish events and verify the subscriber materializes records.","steps":["Create a platform event Test_Notification__e with Message__c (text) and Source__c (text).","Create TestNotificationTrigger on Test_Notification__e (after insert) that creates an Integration_Log__c for each event with Payload__c = evt.Message__c.","In anonymous Apex, publish 5 events in ONE EventBus.publish call.","Query Integration_Log__c and verify 5 records were created.","Add a recursion guard using TriggerHandlerService.suppress / restore."],"verify":"5 events → 5 log records. No infinite loop."},{"t":"proj","id":"MP4","title":"Data Sync Pipeline","stars":3,"obj":"Build an end-to-end data synchronization pipeline using Queueable, Batch, and Platform Events.","reqs":["Create SyncConfig__c custom metadata with Object_Name__c, Last_Sync_Date__c and Batch_Size__c.","Build DataSyncService.cls with startSync(String objectName) that reads the config, enqueues a Queueable which updates Last_Sync_Date__c and publishes a Sync_Event__e platform event.","The Sync_Event__e subscriber trigger creates an Integration_Log__c entry.","Write DataSyncServiceTest.cls using Test.startTest()/Test.stopTest(); assert the config was updated, the event was published and materialized as a log.","Handle the recursion guard between the event subscriber and the log trigger."],"success":"Full pipeline works: enqueue → config update → event publish → log creation. No recursion."}]},{"title":"S5 · Automation: Flows & Apex (Phase 6)","mins":14,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"5.1","title":"@InvocableMethod for Flow","stars":1,"obj":"Expose an Apex method to Flow Builder.","steps":["Create ScoreCalculatorService.cls with an @InvocableMethod that takes List<Request> and returns List<Result>.","Request inner class: @InvocableVariable public Integer rawScore. Result inner class: @InvocableVariable public Integer clampedScore.","Create a test class that calls the method directly (static Apex call) and asserts clamping behavior.","Test edge cases: null input, negative, 0, 100, 150."],"code":{"lang":"apex","x":"@InvocableMethod(label='Calculate Score' description='Clamps a score to 0-100.')\npublic static List<Result> calculate(List<Request> requests) {\n    // clamp each rawScore to 0..100 and return as Result\n}"},"verify":"Method is static, takes List<Request>, returns List<Result>. All edge cases pass."},{"t":"ex","id":"5.2","title":"Flow vs Trigger Decision Matrix","stars":2,"obj":"Document when to use Flow vs Apex for a set of scenarios.","steps":["(a) Set Health_Score__c to 70 when a new Account is created with a blank score.","(b) Send a custom email to the Account owner when the score drops below 30.","(c) Recalculate scores for all Accounts nightly in a batch.","(d) Show a screen collecting user input before creating a Contact.","(e) Call an external API when an Opportunity closes.","(f) Create 3 child records when an Account is created.","For each scenario write a 1-sentence justification defending Flow or Apex.","For (a) and (b) build both a before-save Flow AND a before-trigger, then compare the DML footprint."],"verify":"You can defend each choice with a specific Apex/Flow limitation or advantage."},{"t":"proj","id":"MP5","title":"Onboarding Wizard","stars":3,"obj":"Build a screen Flow that collects user input and calls invocable Apex.","reqs":["Create OnboardingService.cls with an @InvocableMethod taking Company Name, Contact Name and Industry that creates an Account and Contact in one transaction and returns the Account Id.","Create a Screen Flow Onboarding_Wizard: Screen 1 (Company Name required, Industry picklist), Screen 2 (Contact First Name / Last Name required), Apex Action calling OnboardingService, Screen 3 showing \"Account created: {!accountId}\".","Write OnboardingServiceTest.cls covering the invocable method, and deploy both the Apex class and the Flow metadata."],"success":"Flow is activatable; Apex test passes; the invocable creates both records."}]},{"title":"S6 · UI: Visualforce, Aura & LWC (Phases 7–8)","mins":22,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"6.1","title":"Visualforce Custom Controller","stars":1,"obj":"Build a search page with a custom controller.","steps":["Create AccountSearchController.cls with public properties searchTerm (String) and accounts (List<Account>), and a search() method querying Accounts by Name LIKE :searchTerm with limit 50.","Create AccountSearchPage.page with controller=\"AccountSearchController\", an input bound to {!searchTerm}, a command button calling {!search} with reRender=\"results\", a pageBlockTable rendering {!accounts} with Name and Industry columns, and apex:pageMessages.","Open the page in the browser and test a search."],"verify":"Partial page refresh works (reRender). No full page reload on search."},{"t":"ex","id":"6.2","title":"Aura Component with Server Call","stars":2,"obj":"Build an Aura component that calls @AuraEnabled Apex.","steps":["Create AccountAuraController.cls with an @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) getTopAccounts() returning the top 10 Accounts by Health_Score__c.","Create an Aura component accountAuraList with an init handler calling c.getTopAccounts, an aura:iteration rendering each Account Name/Score, and an aura:registerEvent for AccountSelected.","Create the AccountSelected application event definition.","On row click, fire the application event with the Account Id."],"code":{"lang":"apex","x":"@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)\npublic static List<Account> getTopAccounts() {\n    return [SELECT Id, Name, Health_Score__c FROM Account\n            ORDER BY Health_Score__c DESC LIMIT 10];\n}"},"verify":"$A.enqueueAction returns data. Application event fires on click."},{"t":"ex","id":"6.3","title":"LWC with Wire and Imperative","stars":2,"obj":"Build an LWC with both @wire (read) and imperative (write) paths.","steps":["Create LwcAccountList with @wire calling LwcDataService.getAccountsWithContacts, a lightning-datatable displaying accounts, and wire {data, error} handling.","Add a \"Refresh\" button that calls refreshApex to force re-fetch.","Add an imperative \"Create Test Account\" button that calls an @AuraEnabled (non-cacheable) method and then refreshApex.","Write the .js-meta.xml targeting lightning__AppPage, then deploy and add to a page via App Builder."],"verify":"Wire loads data on init. Imperative creates a record. Refresh shows updated data."},{"t":"ex","id":"6.4","title":"LWC Parent-Child Communication","stars":2,"obj":"Pass data down with @api and up with CustomEvent.","steps":["Create parent accountManager and child accountCard.","Parent passes account to child via @api account.","Child renders account details and has a \"Delete\" button.","On delete, child dispatches CustomEvent('accountdelete', { detail: this.account.Id }).","Parent handles onaccountdelete and removes the account from its tracked list.","Verify the child re-renders when the parent list changes."],"verify":"Data flows down via @api and up via CustomEvent. No imports needed for event dispatch."},{"t":"proj","id":"MP6","title":"Full-Stack Account Dashboard","stars":3,"obj":"Build a complete dashboard with Visualforce, Aura, and LWC approaches.","reqs":["Visualforce AccountDashboard.page: account list with health scores, inline edit of Health_Score__c with save, apex:commandButton + reRender.","Aura accountDashboardAura: wired data load, application event for selection, child detail panel showing the selected Account Contacts.","LWC accountDashboardLwc: @wire + lightning-datatable, imperative save, refreshApex after save, custom event for selection.","All three share the same @AuraEnabled Apex controller.","Write tests for the controller methods."],"success":"All three UI approaches work. Controller is shared. Tests pass."}]},{"title":"S7 · Testing & Debugging (Phase 9)","mins":16,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"7.1","title":"@TestSetup and Data Isolation","stars":1,"obj":"Verify SeeAllData=false behavior.","steps":["Create DataIsolationTest.cls with @TestSetup inserting 3 Accounts.","onlySetupDataVisible: query all Accounts and assert exactly 3 exist (no org data leaked).","secondTestSeesSameSetupData: assert exactly 3 exist (setup data shared across methods).","addingRecordsWithinMethod: insert 2 more, query and assert 5 total (3 setup + 2 method-level)."],"verify":"All three assertions pass. Data isolation is proven."},{"t":"ex","id":"7.2","title":"Test.startTest/stopTest for Queueable","stars":2,"obj":"Force async completion and verify fresh governor limits.","steps":["Write a test that enqueues SimpleQueueable(monitorId) between Test.startTest() and Test.stopTest().","Inside the test, print Limits.getQueries() before and after Test.startTest() and verify the governor counters reset in the start/stop window.","Assert the Async_Job_Monitor__c record has Job_Status__c = 'Completed'."],"code":{"lang":"apex","x":"Test.startTest();\nSystem.enqueueJob(new SimpleQueueable(monitorId));\nTest.stopTest();\n// assertions here — job has completed synchronously"},"verify":"Without startTest/stopTest, the assertion would fail (job not yet executed)."},{"t":"ex","id":"7.3","title":"HTTP Callout Mock","stars":3,"obj":"Mock an external API and test retry logic.","steps":["Create MockApiService implements HttpCalloutMock: first call returns 502, second returns 200, tracking the attempt count.","Write a test using Test.setMock(HttpCalloutMock.class, new MockApiService()) and assert the callout succeeds.","Assert the Integration_Log__c record shows Retry_Count__c = 1.","Create a second mock MockApiFailAll that always returns 500.","Test that after retries the result is success = false."],"verify":"Both mocks are installable. Retry count is accurate in the log."},{"t":"proj","id":"MP7","title":"Comprehensive Test Suite","stars":3,"obj":"Write tests for the Lead Auto-Assignment Engine (Mini Project 3).","reqs":["@TestSetup creates 3 Users (admin, standard, read-only), 5 Lead Routing Rules across 4 industries, and 10 Leads (some matching, some not).","leadsWithMatchingRulesAreAssignedCorrectly — assert OwnerId matches the rule.","leadsWithoutMatchingRulesKeepDefaultOwner — assert owner is the running user.","bulkInsertHandles200Leads — insert 200 leads, assert no governor violations.","updateTriggerReEvaluatesAssignment — change Lead Industry and verify reassignment.","recursionGuardPreventsInfiniteLoop — trigger update that would re-fire, assert stable.","All assertions use System.assertEquals with descriptive messages."],"success":"sf apex run test -c shows ≥75% coverage. All tests green."}]},{"title":"S8 · Performance & Large Data Volumes (Phase 10)","mins":13,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"8.1","title":"Query Plan Analysis","stars":1,"obj":"Compare selective vs non-selective queries.","steps":["Insert 500 Accounts with varied Industries and Health Scores.","Open Developer Console → Query Plan and paste each query, recording the cost: Health_Score__c > 50 (indexed field), Name LIKE '%Corp%' (leading wildcard), Name LIKE 'Corp%' (no leading wildcard), and Health_Score__c > 50 AND Industry = 'Technology' (compound).","Add a custom index on Health_Score__c and re-plan the first query.","Document the cost differences."],"verify":"Leading wildcard shows highest cost. Indexed field shows lowest."},{"t":"ex","id":"8.2","title":"Map Join vs Nested Loop","stars":2,"obj":"Measure the performance improvement of Map joins.","steps":["Insert 10 Accounts and 500 Contacts distributed across them.","Write nestedLoopJoin(List<Account>, List<Contact>) using an O(n*m) nested loop matching c.AccountId == a.Id, tracking Limits.getCpuTime() before and after.","Write mapJoin(...) that builds a Map<Id, List<Contact>> by AccountId and looks up via map.get(acc.Id), also tracking CPU time.","Call both in a test and print both CPU times.","Assert the map join is faster."],"verify":"Map join CPU time is significantly lower than the nested loop."},{"t":"proj","id":"MP8","title":"LDV Migration Tool","stars":3,"obj":"Build a batch process that migrates Account data with performance monitoring.","reqs":["Create AccountMigrationBatch.cls implementing Database.Batchable<sObject>, Database.Stateful: start returns a QueryLocator over all Accounts; execute transforms data (normalizes names, clamps scores), uses a Map for child Contact lookups and updates in one DML; finish logs total records, total CPU and total DML statements.","Instance fields track totalRecords, totalBatches and maxCpuInBatch.","Create AccountMigrationTest.cls inserting 500 accounts with contacts, running the batch with scope 200, asserting normalized data and accurate Stateful counters.","Monitor with Async_Job_Monitor__c."],"success":"Batch processes all records. Stateful counters are accurate. Performance is within limits."}]},{"title":"S9 · Integration & Enterprise Patterns (Phase 11)","mins":17,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"9.1","title":"Named Credential Callout","stars":1,"obj":"Make a callout using a Named Credential.","steps":["In Setup → Named Credentials, create Mock_API pointing to a mockable endpoint.","Write an anonymous Apex block that calls callout:Mock_API/test and debugs the status and body.","Inside a test, mock the callout and assert the response.","Verify the Integration_Log__c captures the endpoint and status."],"code":{"lang":"apex","x":"HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();\nreq.setEndpoint('callout:Mock_API/test');\nreq.setMethod('GET');\nHttp http = new Http();\nHttpResponse res = http.send(req);\nSystem.debug('Status: ' + res.getStatusCode());\nSystem.debug('Body: ' + res.getBody());"},"verify":"Named Credential reference works without hardcoded URLs. Log entry is created."},{"t":"ex","id":"9.2","title":"REST Resource CRUD","stars":2,"obj":"Build an inbound REST API with full CRUD operations.","steps":["Create @RestResource(urlMapping='/MyService/v1/items/*') with @HttpGet (list), @HttpPost (create, return 201 + Id), @HttpPatch (update by Id from URI), @HttpDelete (delete by Id from URI).","Write RestServiceTest.cls calling the service methods directly (not via HTTP) using RestContext.request / RestContext.response.","Verify each verb produces the correct status code (200, 201, 204) and records."],"verify":"Each verb works. Status codes are correct (200, 201, 204)."},{"t":"ex","id":"9.3","title":"Event-Driven Integration Pipeline","stars":3,"obj":"Build a publish-subscribe pipeline with audit logging.","steps":["Study the repo pipeline: IntegrationService → Integration_Log__c → IntegrationLogTrigger → EventPublisherService → Integration_Event__e → IntegrationEventSubscriberTrigger → Integration_Log__c.","Reproduce this pipeline for a new custom object Webhook_Event__e.","Add recursion guards at every trigger level.","Write a test that publishes 10 events, verifies 10 log entries, verifies no infinite recursion, and asserts correlation IDs are consistent."],"verify":"Pipeline works end-to-end. Recursion guards are effective."},{"t":"proj","id":"MP9","title":"External API Hub","stars":3,"obj":"Build a complete integration layer with outbound callouts, inbound REST, and event-driven sync.","reqs":["Outbound ExternalApiService.cls: Named Credentials, retry logic (5xx → retry once → log failure), Correlation Id tracking, Integration_Log__c audit trail.","Inbound WebhookReceiver.cls: @RestResource receiving external webhooks, validates the payload, creates a Webhook_Event__e, returns 200 on success / 400 on bad payload.","Event Subscriber WebhookEventTrigger on Webhook_Event__e creates Integration_Log__c with direction 'Inbound', with a recursion guard via TriggerHandlerService.","Test suite: mock for outbound callouts, REST context simulation for inbound, event publish + subscriber assertion, and the end-to-end pipeline (callout → log → trigger → event → subscriber → log)."],"success":"All tests pass. Pipeline is fully auditable. No recursion."}]},{"title":"S10 · Release Management & CI/CD (Phase 12)","mins":13,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"10.1","title":"Scratch Org Lifecycle","stars":1,"obj":"Create, deploy, test, and destroy a scratch org.","steps":["sf org create scratch -f config/project-scratch-def.json -a test1 -d 1","sf project deploy start --source-dir force-app --target-org test1","sf apex run test -c --target-org test1 --test-level RunLocalTests --result-format human","Record the coverage percentage.","sf org delete scratch --target-org test1 --no-prompt","Repeat steps 1–5 with a second scratch org (test2) to prove the process is repeatable."],"verify":"Both orgs created and destroyed. Coverage is consistent."},{"t":"ex","id":"10.2","title":"Destructive Deployment","stars":2,"obj":"Remove metadata via destructive changes.","steps":["Create a dummy class StaleHelper.cls (empty class) and deploy it to a scratch org.","Verify it exists in the coverage report.","Create destructiveChanges.xml listing StaleHelper as an ApexClass member with the correct API version.","Create an empty package.xml for the same API version.","Deploy both files together, then verify StaleHelper no longer exists."],"code":{"lang":"xml","x":"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<Package xmlns=\"http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata\">\n    <types>\n        <members>StaleHelper</members>\n        <name>ApexClass</name>\n    </types>\n    <version>68.0</version>\n</Package>"},"verify":"Class is removed from the org. Deployment succeeds with both files."},{"t":"proj","id":"MP10","title":"Full CI/CD Pipeline","stars":3,"obj":"Set up a GitHub Actions CI/CD pipeline for the repo.","reqs":["Create .github/workflows/ci.yml triggered on push to main and pull_request: checkout → install SF CLI → authorize DevHub → create scratch org → deploy → lint → test with coverage → scan → teardown.","Add a package.json lint script: \"lint\": \"eslint force-app/**/*.js\".","Add an sf scanner step for Apex static analysis.","Configure the pipeline to fail on lint errors, test failures, and coverage below 75%.","Add a separate deploy-prod job that runs only on main push (not PRs), uses sf project deploy start --test-level RunLocalTests, and includes a manual approval gate (GitHub Environments)."],"success":"Pipeline runs on PR. Lint/test/scan gates work. Deploy-prod requires approval."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Done with every section? Move on to the capstone — it combines all 13 phases into one buildable application. Solutions for every exercise are in 15-Answers-and-Results.md."}]},{"title":"Capstone · Certification Quiz Platform","mins":24,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The capstone is a single application that touches every phase of the roadmap: data model, service layer, SOQL/SOSL, triggers, async, flows, three UI approaches, testing, performance, integration, and CI/CD. Build it section by section and check the answers file after each milestone."},{"t":"proj","id":"CAP","title":"Certification Quiz Platform","stars":4,"obj":"Build a complete quiz application using every phase of the roadmap.","reqs":[{"h":"Phase 1–2 · Data Model & Apex","items":["Custom objects: Quiz__c, Question__c, Answer__c, Quiz_Result__c.","Relationships: Quiz → Questions (master-detail), Question → Answers (master-detail).","Service class with full CRUD, collections, and custom exceptions."]},{"h":"Phase 3 · SOQL & SOSL","items":["Query questions by difficulty, domain, and certification type.","SOSL for full-text search across question text.","Aggregate queries for score statistics."]},{"h":"Phase 4 · Triggers","items":["Before-insert trigger on Question__c to validate and normalize text.","After-update trigger on Quiz_Result__c to publish a notification event."]},{"h":"Phase 5 · Async & Events","items":["Queueable job to calculate quiz statistics.","Platform event Quiz_Completed__e published after quiz submission.","Batch job for nightly statistics aggregation."]},{"h":"Phase 6 · Flows","items":["@InvocableMethod for quiz scoring callable from Flow.","Screen Flow for the quiz-taking wizard."]},{"h":"Phases 7–8 · UI","items":["LWC quiz interface with @wire for questions and imperative submit.","Aura component for the leaderboard.","Visualforce page for admin question management."]},{"h":"Phase 9 · Testing","items":["@TestSetup with question bank.","HTTP mock for external scoring API.","System.runAs for multi-user quiz scenarios.","100% coverage with meaningful assertions."]},{"h":"Phase 10 · Performance","items":["Map joins for answer grouping.","Batch job for large-scale statistics.","Index-aware queries."]},{"h":"Phase 11 · Integration","items":["REST API for external quiz submission.","Platform events for quiz completion notifications.","Integration log audit trail."]},{"h":"Phase 12 · CI/CD","items":["GitHub Actions pipeline.","Destructive changes for deprecated quiz versions.","Package versioning."]}],"success":"sf apex run test -c shows ≥75% coverage with all tests passing; all metadata deploys cleanly; the LWC renders in a scratch org; the REST endpoint is callable from Postman; the pipeline runs without errors."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Review the common-mistakes checklist at the end of 15-Answers-and-Results.md before starting the capstone — it points out the exact pitfalls (recursion, loop DML, non-bulk queries) this project is designed to catch."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 14 Quiz - Practical Application","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Which DML approach allows partial success?","opts":["insert list","Database.insert(list, true)","Database.insert(list, false)","update single record"],"a":2,"why":"Database.insert with allOrNothing=false returns SaveResult[] and allows partial success."},{"q":"A before trigger can modify Trigger.new fields with.","opts":["Zero extra DML","One extra DML","Two extra DML","No DML possible"],"a":0,"why":"Before triggers edit Trigger.new in memory; the platform saves the changes with the original DML."},{"q":"To prevent trigger recursion, use.","opts":["A static Set<Id> guard","System.runAs","Database.Stateful","Test.startTest"],"a":0,"why":"A static Set<Id> persists across trigger invocations within a transaction, preventing re-entry."},{"q":"In Test.setFixedSearchResults the returned List<List<SObject>> groups results by.","opts":["Alphabetically","Object order in the RETURNING clause","Row count","Modified date"],"a":1,"why":"Each inner list maps to the object order declared in the SOSL RETURNING clause (Accounts first, then Contacts)."}]},"total":11,"quizTotal":4,"exIndex":[{"id":"1.1","li":0,"kind":"ex","stars":1},{"id":"1.2","li":0,"kind":"ex","stars":1},{"id":"1.3","li":0,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"1.4","li":0,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"1.5","li":0,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"1.6","li":0,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"MP1","li":0,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"2.1","li":1,"kind":"ex","stars":1},{"id":"2.2","li":1,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"2.3","li":1,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"MP2","li":1,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"3.1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":1},{"id":"3.2","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"3.3","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3},{"id":"MP3","li":2,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"4.1","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":1},{"id":"4.2","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"4.3","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"MP4","li":3,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"5.1","li":4,"kind":"ex","stars":1},{"id":"5.2","li":4,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"MP5","li":4,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"6.1","li":5,"kind":"ex","stars":1},{"id":"6.2","li":5,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"6.3","li":5,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"6.4","li":5,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"MP6","li":5,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"7.1","li":6,"kind":"ex","stars":1},{"id":"7.2","li":6,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"7.3","li":6,"kind":"ex","stars":3},{"id":"MP7","li":6,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"8.1","li":7,"kind":"ex","stars":1},{"id":"8.2","li":7,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"MP8","li":7,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"9.1","li":8,"kind":"ex","stars":1},{"id":"9.2","li":8,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"9.3","li":8,"kind":"ex","stars":3},{"id":"MP9","li":8,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"10.1","li":9,"kind":"ex","stars":1},{"id":"10.2","li":9,"kind":"ex","stars":2},{"id":"MP10","li":9,"kind":"proj","stars":3},{"id":"CAP","li":10,"kind":"proj","stars":4}]},{"id":"dev-answers","n":15,"title":"Answers & Results","icon":"15","color":"#7C3AED","tagline":"Complete solutions for every exercise","guide":"15-Answers-and-Results.md","art":[{"label":"15-Answers-and-Results.md","href":"developer Roadmap/15-Answers-and-Results.md"}],"objectives":["Verify exercise solutions with complete Apex code","Understand common mistakes and how to avoid them","Review expected debug output and test assertions","Study the common mistakes reference table"],"lessons":[{"title":"Fundamentals & Apex Answers (Sections 1–2)","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Complete solutions for Exercises 1.1–1.6 and Mini Project 1, plus Exercises 2.1–2.3 and Mini Project 2. Each answer includes full Apex code, test classes, and expected output."},{"t":"list","items":["Anonymous Apex scripts ready to run in Developer Console","SOQL queries with expected row counts","Test classes with @TestSetup and assertions","Custom exception classes and error handling patterns"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Copy-paste the anonymous Apex scripts directly into the Developer Console → Execute Anonymous window to see results instantly."}]},{"title":"Triggers, Async & Events Answers (Sections 3–4)","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Complete trigger code, handler classes, recursion guards, Queueable implementations, Batch Apex, and platform event publish/subscribe patterns."},{"t":"list","items":["AccountDefaultTrigger — before insert defaulting with bulk support","CodeReviewChangeTrigger — Trigger.oldMap change detection","RecursionGuard.cls — static Set<Id> pattern","SimpleQueueable.cls — lifecycle with success and failure paths","ScoreBatch.cls — QueryLocator batch with scope comparison","TestNotificationTrigger — event-to-log materialization"]}]},{"title":"UI, Testing & Performance Answers (Sections 5–8)","mins":14,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Solutions for @InvocableMethod, Visualforce pages with controllers, Aura components with $A.enqueueAction, LWC with @wire and imperative calls, test isolation, HTTP mocking, Query Plan analysis, and Map join performance."},{"t":"list","items":["ScoreCalculatorService — @InvocableMethod with Request/Result inner classes","AccountSearchController + AccountSearchPage — VF search with reRender","accountAuraList component — init handler + application event","LWC accountListLwc — @wire + refreshApex + imperative create","MockApiService — HttpCalloutMock with retry logic","PerformanceService — nestedLoopJoin vs mapJoin with CPU comparison"]}]},{"title":"Integration, CI/CD & Capstone Answers (Sections 9–10)","mins":12,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Solutions for Named Credential callouts, @RestResource CRUD endpoints, event-driven integration pipelines, scratch org lifecycle, destructive deployments, and the CI/CD GitHub Actions workflow."},{"t":"list","items":["MyRestResource.cls — @HttpGet/@HttpPost/@HttpPatch/@HttpDelete","MockApiService + MockApiFailAll — HttpCalloutMock implementations","destructiveChanges.xml + package.xml — removal workflow",".github/workflows/ci.yml — full CI/CD pipeline","Common mistakes reference table covering all 31 exercises"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"The Common Mistakes table at the end of 15-Answers-and-Results.md is a quick-reference checklist — review it before attempting the capstone project."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 15 Quiz - Solutions Review","mins":3,"questions":[{"q":"The Common Mistakes table in the answers file covers how many exercises?","opts":["10","20","31","50"],"a":2,"why":"The table lists one common mistake per exercise, covering all 31 exercises across 10 sections."},{"q":"Anonymous Apex scripts in the answers can be run in.","opts":["VS Code terminal only","Developer Console → Execute Anonymous","GitHub Actions","Flow Builder"],"a":1,"why":"Anonymous Apex scripts are designed for the Developer Console Execute Anonymous window or sf apex run."},{"q":"The capstone project requires which minimum coverage?","opts":["50%","65%","75%","100%"],"a":2,"why":"75% is the production deployment requirement; the capstone asserts this as the success criteria."}]},"total":4,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]},{"id":"dev-usecases","n":16,"title":"Real-World Use Cases","icon":"16","color":"#0891B2","tagline":"3 capstone builds applying every roadmap phase","guide":"16-Real-World-Use-Cases.md","art":[{"label":"16-Real-World-Use-Cases.md","href":"developer Roadmap/16-Real-World-Use-Cases.md"},{"label":"17-Use-Case-Solutions.md","href":"developer Roadmap/17-Use-Case-Solutions.md"}],"objectives":["Apply all 13 content phases across three realistic business scenarios","Build end-to-end: triggers, flows, LWC, async, integration and tests","Add sharing, performance, release and CI/CD to every deliverable","Attempt each milestone yourself before reading the reference solutions"],"lessons":[{"title":"UC1 · Deal-to-Order Automation (Sales Ops)","mins":45,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Automate the back office for Northwind B&O: when a BigDeal__c reaches Won, validate it, create a confirmed Order__c with discounted line items, upgrade the Account tier, and publish a platform event so fulfilment gets notified."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Skills on test: triggers + recursion guards (Phase 4), SOQL aggregates and parent-child queries (Phase 3), bulk-safe collections (Phases 1-2), Flow + @InvocableMethod (Phase 6), platform events (Phase 5), scheduled rollup batch (Phases 5 + 10), sharing and CRUD checks (Phase 1), and test isolation via @TestSetup and runAs (Phase 9)."},{"t":"proj","id":"UC1","title":"Deal-to-Order Automation","stars":4,"obj":"Ship a validated, idempotent order pipeline from Won stage to confirmed order and Account rollups.","reqs":[{"h":"Milestone M1 - Validate & default the Big Deal","items":["BigDealTrigger (one trigger, one handler BigDealHandler) with a recursion guard","Default WonDate__c when freshly won; block regressing to an open stage","addError on Won deals missing Account, with Amount <= 0, or future CloseDate"]},{"h":"Milestone M2 - Deal-to-order service","items":["OrderService with @InvocableMethod DealRequest/DealResult inner classes","Parse Product_Summary__c lines (Name|Qty|UnitPrice) into OrderLineItem__c rows","Apply Discount__c to line totals; verify Order Total equals the sum of lines","Idempotent: skip creation when an Order__c for the deal already exists"]},{"h":"Milestone M3 - Record-triggered Flow + event","items":["Flow Deal To Order on create/update when Stage__c = Won, calling the service","Surface service messages to the user; validation failures roll the DML back","Publish BigDealWon_Event__e from the service on success","Event consumer creates a Fulfilment_Notice__c for non-blocking tracking"]},{"h":"Milestone M4 - Account tier & rollup batch","items":["RevenueRollupBatch using QueryLocator and one aggregate SOQL per scope","Tier ladder on rolling 12-month revenue (100k Silver, 500k Gold, 1M Platinum)","Healh_Score__c +10 on new won revenue, clamped at 100","RevenueRollupScheduler scheduling the batch nightly (2:00 AM)"]},{"h":"Milestone M5 - Sharing & security","items":["Fulfilment permission set granting edit on orders, read-only Accounts","with sharing on all service classes plus Schema.isAccessible/isUpdateable guards","runAs test proving a Standard User sees only permitted data"]}],"success":"All 5 milestones pass with sf apex run test -c at 75%+ coverage and a git tag per milestone."}]},{"title":"UC2 · SyncHub ERP Integration (Integration)","mins":45,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Mirror external ERP inventory into Salesforce near-real-time: an inbound REST webhook publishes platform events, a queueable applies them to Inventory__c, a batch performs the 1M-record backfill, and an LWC panel reports sync health."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Skills on test: Named Credentials + HTTP callouts and JSON (Phase 11), Queueable and chaining (Phase 5), QueryLocator batch with scope control (Phase 10), upsert by external id and dynamic SOQL safety (Phase 3), custom exceptions with quarantine logging (Phase 2), HttpCalloutMock (Phase 9), LWC @wire + refreshApex (Phase 8), and a CI/CD workflow on GitHub Actions (Phase 12)."},{"t":"proj","id":"UC2","title":"SyncHub ERP Integration","stars":4,"obj":"Deliver inbound webhook, async event pipeline, backfill batch and a live sync-status LWC.","reqs":[{"h":"Milestone M1 - Webhook endpoint + event","items":["ERPWebhookResource @RestResource(urlMapping=/ERPWebhook/*) with @HttpPost","Parse JSON changes and publish one Inventory_Change_Event__e per change","Return HTTP 200 fast; HTTP 400 with JSON error on malformed payloads"]},{"h":"Milestone M2 - Queueable consumer + quarantine","items":["InventorySyncQueueable reading the 100 most recent events","Upsert Inventory__c by ExternalId__c in one DML; set SyncStatus and LastSyncTime","Route failures to InboundChangeLog__c with Status Quarantined and error text","Sync now @AuraEnabled method enqueuing the queueable on demand"]},{"h":"Milestone M3 - Backfill batch + scheduled run","items":["InventoryBackfillBatch over records where SyncStatus != In Sync, scope 200","Mark every chunk processed while respecting Governor limits","Chunk-level audit of failed batches"]},{"h":"Milestone M4 - LWC status panel","items":["erpSyncStatus with @wire(getSyncStats) returning In Sync / Pending / Quarantined counts","Sync now imperative call + refreshApex after the job lands","Stat chips render and update reactively"]},{"h":"Milestone M5 - CI/CD deployment","items":["force-app/main/default structure and sf project deploy start","GitHub Actions workflow creating a scratch org, deploying and running tests","No secrets in source; named credential referenced by name only"]}],"success":"Webhook to dashboard works end to end, backfill converges, and CI is green on every push."}]},{"title":"UC3 · ServicePulse Routing + Dashboard (Service)","mins":45,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Build the support engine for ServicePulse: a record-triggered Flow routes cases with SLA deadlines from custom metadata, a nightly batch recomputes SLA health and fires breach events, and an LWC dashboard shows live agent workload with empApi refreshes."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Skills on test: record-triggered Flow calling Apex (Phase 6), deterministic round-robin assignment via @InvocableMethod (Phases 6 + 2), custom metadata read for policy (Phase 6), SLACalculatorBatch with idempotent publishes (Phases 5 + 10), aggregates and GROUP BY (Phase 3), LWC @wire + empApi (Phase 8), Visualforce CSV export (Phase 7), sharing-aware queries with runAs (Phases 1 + 9)."},{"t":"proj","id":"UC3","title":"ServicePulse Case Routing & Dashboard","stars":4,"obj":"Route, clock, monitor and export support cases with live agent visibility.","reqs":[{"h":"Milestone M1 - Case routing flow + policy","items":["SLA_Policy__mdt with Minutes per product line (Billing 480, Hardware 120, Software 240, General 720)","Record-triggered Flow setting Product_Line, Priority and SLA_Deadline via SLAPolicyService","Empty-mapped policy falls back to 720 minutes; re-entry guarded"]},{"h":"Milestone M2 - Round-robin assignment","items":["CaseAssignmentService @InvocableMethod assigning the least-loaded active agent","One aggregate query for workload plus map join; deterministic tie-break","Only changed Assigned_Agent__c rows reach DML"]},{"h":"Milestone M3 - SLA batch + breach events","items":["SLACalculatorBatch computing On Track / At Risk / Breached from SLA_Deadline__c","Publish SLABreachWarning_Event__e exactly once per newly-breached case","SLACalculatorScheduler running the batch nightly"]},{"h":"Milestone M4 - LWC dashboard + live updates","items":["servicePulseDashboard with @wire(getDashboardData) over aggregate SOQL","By-agent, by-product-line and aging-bucket stats rendered as chips","empApi subscription to the breach channel refreshing via refreshApex"]},{"h":"Milestone M5 - Visualforce CSV export","items":["CaseExportController + CaseExportPage streaming a CSV of open cases","Sharing-aware query; export respects the running user"]}],"success":"Cases route deterministically, SLA breaches fire live events, and the dashboard exports to CSV."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 16 Quiz - Use Case Application","mins":4,"questions":[{"q":"Which two patterns keep the UC1 order pipeline from importing duplicated orders or re-triggering validation?","opts":["Idempotency check + recursion guard","List sorting + @future","SOQL LIMIT 1 + SOSL","Report refresh + VF bookmark"],"a":0,"why":"A guarded trigger plus an idempotent service are the core integrity pattern taught across the roadmap."},{"q":"In UC2, bind variables in the queueable queries exist because.","opts":["they are faster than aggregates","they prevent SOQL injection and keep the query governor budget predictable","they let flows edit ternary results","they make the class stateless"],"a":1,"why":"Bind variables give both injection safety and a stable query shape for testing."},{"q":"What is the ONLY reliable way to prove the ServicePulse dashboard does not leak unshared cases?","opts":["Reading the component JavaScript","A class annotated @RestResource","A runAs test with a low-privilege profile asserting counts","Adding more fields to the Lightning layout"],"a":2,"why":"Sharing is verified behaviourally under a real user context, exactly like the Phase 1 and Phase 9 labs."}]},"total":3,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[{"id":"UC1","li":0,"kind":"proj","stars":4},{"id":"UC2","li":1,"kind":"proj","stars":4},{"id":"UC3","li":2,"kind":"proj","stars":4}]},{"id":"dev-ucanswers","n":17,"title":"Use Case Solutions","icon":"17","color":"#DB2777","tagline":"Reference implementations for every milestone","guide":"17-Use-Case-Solutions.md","art":[{"label":"17-Use-Case-Solutions.md","href":"developer Roadmap/17-Use-Case-Solutions.md"}],"objectives":["Study complete reference code for all three use cases","Compare architecture decisions and milestone tests","Learn the final data model for Deal-to-Order, SyncHub and ServicePulse","Reuse the patterns in real projects and certification scenarios"],"lessons":[{"title":"UC1 · Deal-to-Order Reference (Sales Ops)","mins":18,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Final data model plus complete reference code: BigDealValidationException, BigDealTrigger + BigDealHandler with recursion guard, OrderService with @InvocableMethod and idempotency, BigDealWonEventTrigger, RevenueRollupBatch + Scheduler, and the milestone tests asserting tier upgrades and idempotent re-runs."},{"t":"list","items":["BigDealHandler - single-handler before-trigger validation and defaults","OrderService - bulk-safe parse of Product_Summary into line items with discount","EventBus.publish(BigDealWon_Event__e) after DML for fulfilment","RevenueRollupBatch - one aggregate per scope, changed-row-only DML","RevenueRollupBatchTest - tier upgrade, health clamp, converged re-run"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"The recursion guard cannot live in the @InvocableMethod service; it must wrap the trigger path only, otherwise the Flow-invoked create is swallowed."}]},{"title":"UC2 · SyncHub Reference (Integration)","mins":18,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Full webhook, queueable, batch and LWC implementation, including what an HttpCalloutMock must return for deterministic tests, the quarantine log flow, and the framework for the on-button Sync now."},{"t":"list","items":["ERPWebhookResource - @HttpPost parsing and publishing with 400 error handling","InventorySyncService - @AuraEnabled syncNow + cacheable getSyncStats","InventorySyncQueueable - batched upsert by ExternalId with quarantine logs","InventoryBackfillBatch - QueryLocator over Pending rows, scope 200","MockApiService + InventorySyncServiceTest - enqueue and quarantine paths","erpSyncStatus LWC - @wire stats, imperative enqueue, refreshApex"]}]},{"title":"UC3 · ServicePulse Reference (Service)","mins":18,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Routing service, SLA batch with once-only event publishes, sharing-aware dashboard controller, the empApi-subscribed LWC, the legacy Visualforce CSV export, and the batch test proving idempotence."},{"t":"list","items":["SLAPolicyService - custom metadata lookup in O(1), fallback 720 minutes","CaseAssignmentService - aggregate workload + deterministic tie-break","SLACalculatorBatch - On Track / At Risk / Breached with EventBus bulk publish","CaseDashboardController - GROUP BY stats and aging buckets via one Apex call","servicePulseDashboard LWC - @wire + empApi breach refresh with unsubscribe","SLACalculatorBatchTest - exactly one event for the newly-breached case"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Connect the LWC dashboard to an org test event by publishing SLABreachWarning_Event__e in a debug run after the batch scheduler runs - it proves the Live section without a production night."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 17 Quiz - Solution Review","mins":3,"questions":[{"q":"Where must the UC1 recursion guard be applied?","opts":["The @InvocableMethod service","The trigger handler path only","A flow decision branch","Nowhere - triggers never recur"],"a":1,"why":"The service needs to run for invoiced deals; only the trigger re-entry must be guarded."},{"q":"UC2 quarantine uses which object and status?","opts":["InboundChangeLog__c with Status Quarantined","OutboundChangeLog__c with Status Deferred","BigDeal__c with Stage Lost","SLA_Policy__mdt with Active false"],"a":0,"why":"Failed ERP payloads land in the audit object so nothing is silently dropped."},{"q":"The UC3 dashboard refreshes live without polling via.","opts":["Visualforce page action","A scheduled report","empApi subscribing to SLABreachWarning_Event__e","LSpex server push"],"a":2,"why":"Platform event subscriptions with refreshApex give near-real-time updates."}]},"total":3,"quizTotal":3,"exIndex":[]}],"answerIds":["1.1","1.2","1.3","1.4","1.5","1.6","10.1","10.2","2.1","2.2","2.3","3.1","3.2","3.3","4.1","4.2","4.3","5.1","5.2","6.1","6.2","6.3","6.4","7.1","7.2","7.3","8.1","8.2","9.1","9.2","9.3","CAP","MP1","MP10","MP2","MP3","MP4","MP5","MP6","MP7","MP8","MP9"]};

window.ABDO_DATA["headless"] =
  {"slug":"headless","meta":{"slug":"headless","brand":"Headless & MCP Academy","name":"Headless Commerce & MCP","cert":"Headless + MCP (Agent-Readable APIs)","icon":"🤖","color":"#EC4899","desc":"16 phases on Headless 360 layers, Composable Storefront, and Model Context Protocol servers for AI agents.","repo":"Salesforce-HeadLess-MCP","github":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-HeadLess-MCP","live":"https://abdoaddouli.github.io/Salesforce-HeadLess-MCP/","guideBase":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-HeadLess-MCP/blob/main/developer%20Headless%20and%20MCP%20Roadmap/","repoBlob":"https://github.com/AbdoAddouli/Salesforce-HeadLess-MCP/blob/main/","phases":16,"source":"Salesforce HeadLeess and MCP\\Salesforce headless & MCP"},"modules":[{"id":"headless-headless-fund","n":1,"title":"Headless Fundamentals","icon":"01","color":"#0B5CAB","tagline":"API-first platform, Headless 360, architecture layers","guide":"01-Headless-Fundamentals.md","art":[{"label":"HeadlessFundamentalsService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/HeadlessFundamentalsService.cls"},{"label":"config/project-scratch-def.json","href":"config/project-scratch-def.json"},{"label":"Headless_Architecture__c (objects/)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/"}],"objectives":["Explain what headless means: any front-end, decoupled from the platform UI","Describe Headless 360: Data, Business Logic, Orchestration and Engagement layers","Name the layers and where each public API lives","Pick an integration pattern (REST, GraphQL, Bulk, Streaming) for a use case","Understand platform-hosted MCP servers as the newest engagement layer"],"lessons":[{"title":"What Headless Really Means","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Headless means the presentation layer is decoupled from the platform that provides data and logic. You build any UI — React, Next.js, a mobile app, an Express worker, a CLI, an Agent — and consume Salesforce through APIs. Salesforce never renders the screen."},{"t":"table","head":["Traditional (LWC/Aura)","Headless"],"rows":[["UI rendered by Salesforce","UI rendered by your stack"],["Session on platform","OAuth token on client"],["UI + logic tightly coupled","API is the UI"],["Model View Controller (MVC)","Client (fetch) + API + platform"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Headless never means \"without Salesforce\". It means \"without Salesforce UI\". All the platform power — security, sharing, automation, data — stays on and is exposed via APIs."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"A React storefront reads products via Fetch REST middleware. Is this headless?","a":"Yes. The UI lives off-platform and consumes Salesforce via an API."}]},{"title":"The Headless 360 Architecture","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Salesforce Headless 360 (announced at TDX 2026) turns the platform into composition-ready building blocks behind one API surface. Four layers:"},{"t":"list","items":["Data 360 — the metadata and data services (REST, GraphQL, Bulk, SOQL)","Business Logic 360 — Apex, flows, invocable actions, functions","Orchestration 360 — inbound/outbound composition, event mesh, agents","Engagement 360 — the headless engagement layer (HXL) that fronts everything"]},{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"Your UI / App / Agent  (any framework)\n        |\n        |  HTTP + OAuth\n        v\n( Engagement 360 / HXL )  <- platform-hosted MCP servers + tools\n        |\n        v\n( Orchestration 360 )  <- event mesh, platform events, agents\n        |\n        v\n( Business Logic 360 ) <- Apex, flows, invocable actions\n        |\n        v\n( Data 360 )           <- REST, GraphQL, Bulk, SOQL, records API"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Exams and interviews expect the LAYERS and where each one exposes its API. Data lives in Data 360; MCP tools are hosted in the Engagement layer, not in Data 360."}]},{"title":"Do I Need a Framework?","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"HTTP + JSON is the baseline. But production headless apps benefit from an SDK or data fetcher that handles auth, caching, batching and errors for you."},{"t":"table","head":["Tool","What it gives you"],"rows":[["Fetch API (JS)","Built-in client, zero deps"],["Salesforce JS SDK (jsforce)","Auth, CRUD, bulk, streaming on Node + browser"],["@salesforce/sdk (composable)","Composable commerce storefront client"],["GraphQL clients (Relay/urql)","Declarative data fetching for the GraphQL API"]]},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"When is plain fetch enough?","a":"For small demos and single calls. Production apps with batch/streaming/auth flows usually adopt an SDK or a thin client layer."}]},{"title":"Choosing the Right API","mins":6,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Need","Best-fit API"],"rows":[["CRUD on records","REST API / GraphQL"],["Nested related data in one round trip","GraphQL"],["Backfill 10M records","Bulk API 2.0"],["Subscribe to changes in real time","Streaming API / Platform Events"],["Call Apex as an endpoint","Apex REST"],["Lambda-like custom logic off-platform","Functions / Express worker"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Exams love this table in reverse: given a scenario, pick the API. Practice the matching until it is instant."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"You must push 2M Accounts each night. Which API?","a":"Bulk API 2.0 — async, batched, designed for high-volume backfills."}]},{"title":"Exercise — Map the Layers","mins":6,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C1EX1","title":"Headless 360 layer matching","obj":"Match each capability to the correct Headless 360 layer.","kind":"matching","pairs":[["SOQL query over Account","Data 360"],["Invocable Apex action","Business Logic 360"],["Event mesh / platform events","Orchestration 360"],["Platform-hosted MCP tools","Engagement 360"]],"verify":"All four pairs are correct; double-check the invocable action goes to Business Logic."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 1 Quiz - Headless Fundamentals","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Headless means the platform is...","opts":["removed from the solution","decoupled from the UI (API is the UI)","running a different database","only for mobile"],"a":1,"why":"Headless keeps all platform power but decouples the presentation layer; the API becomes the UI."},{"q":"Where do platform-hosted MCP servers live in Headless 360?","opts":["Data 360","Business Logic 360","Engagement (HXL) 360","Orchestration 360 only"],"a":2,"why":"MCP servers + tools are hosted in the headless engagement layer."},{"q":"Backfilling 5M Leads nightly is best done with...","opts":["SOAP","Bulk API 2.0","GraphQL","Apex REST"],"a":1,"why":"Bulk API 2.0 is designed for high-volume asynchronous data operations."},{"q":"A GraphQL query returning related records in one round trip replaces...","opts":["Bulk loads","multiple REST get calls","platform events","password flow"],"a":1,"why":"GraphQL fetches nested data in a single request the way REST chains cannot."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C1EX1","li":4,"kind":"ex","stars":0}]},{"id":"headless-rest-api","n":2,"title":"REST API","icon":"02","color":"#0E9AA7","tagline":"CRUD, query, versions, limits, composite","guide":"02-REST-API.md","art":[{"label":"RestApiService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/RestApiService.cls"},{"label":"REST_Integration_Log__c (objects/)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/"},{"label":"rest-tests/ Answers (Apt2)","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"}],"objectives":["Construct a URL against the REST endpoint /services/data/vXX.X/","Perform CRUD via GET/POST/PATCH/DELETE","Use composite and composite-tree requests","Read query and search endpoints (sobjects, query, search)","Handle pagination, limits and error payloads"],"lessons":[{"title":"The Endpoint & Auth","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The base URL is https://yourInstance.salesforce.com/services/data/v68.0/. Everything below that root is versioned and JSON. You authenticate with an OAuth 2.0 access token in the Authorization: Bearer header."},{"t":"code","lang":"bash","x":"curl https://MY-INSTANCE.salesforce.com/services/data/v68.0/sobjects/Account \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer 00D5g00000abcdef!AQo...\""},{"t":"table","head":["Path","Meaning"],"rows":[["/services/data/v68.0/sobjects","List all objects"],["/services/data/v68.0/sobjects/Account","Describe Account"],["/services/data/v68.0/sobjects/Account/<id>","Get one record"],["/services/data/v68.0/query?q=<SOQL>","Run a SOQL query"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Always use the apiVersion pin (v68.0) not \"latest\". \"latest\" breaks your client when the platform upgrades."}]},{"title":"CRUD in Practice","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"bash","x":"# Create - returns the new record id\ncurl -X POST .../sobjects/Account -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"Name\":\"Acme\",\"Health_Score__c\":82}'\n\n# Read\ncurl .../sobjects/Account/001xx\n\n# Update (PATCH - partial)\ncurl -X PATCH .../sobjects/Account/001xx \\\n  -d '{\"Health_Score__c\":91}'\n\n# Delete\ncurl -X DELETE .../sobjects/Account/001xx"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"PATCH only sends changed fields; PUT is not used for records. If a field read back differs from what you set, check field-level security (FLS) not your JSON."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Which HTTP verb updates a Salesforce record?","a":"PATCH (partial update). DELETE deletes; POST creates."}]},{"title":"Query, Pagination & Limits","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"bash","x":"curl \".../query?q=SELECT+Id,Name+FROM+Account+LIMIT+50\"\n\n{\n  \"totalSize\": 1234,\n  \"done\": false,\n  \"nextRecordsUrl\": \"/services/data/v68.0/query/01g...-2000\",\n  \"records\": [ ... ]\n}"},{"t":"p","x":"query returns at most 2000 records per page. Follow nextRecordsUrl until done=true. queryAll includes deleted + archived (undeleted via /queryAll)."},{"t":"table","head":["Limit","Value"],"rows":[["Query rows per request","2000"],["QueryAll rows per request","2000 (with deleted)"],["Bulk API 2.0 rows per job (query)","15,000,000"],["Retrieve/`/sobjects/<obj>` default fields","200"]]}]},{"title":"Composite Requests","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Composite lets you chain up to 25 subrequests in ONE call sharing headers and auth — a big win for latency. Use :refN to reference a subrequest result, including generated IDs."},{"t":"code","lang":"json","x":"POST /services/data/v68.0/composite\n{\n  \"compositeRequest\": [\n    { \"method\": \"POST\", \"url\": \"/services/data/v68.0/sobjects/Account\",\n      \"referenceId\": \"acct\", \"body\": { \"Name\": \"Acme\" } },\n    { \"method\": \"POST\", \"url\": \"/services/data/v68.0/sobjects/Contact\",\n      \"referenceId\": \"cont\",\n      \"body\": { \"LastName\": \"Doe\", \"AccountId\": \"@acct@id\" } }\n  ],\n  \"allOrNone\": true\n}"},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"How do you wire the created Account ID into a Contact in composite?","a":"Give the Account a referenceId (acct) and use @acct@id in the Contact body."}]},{"title":"Exercise — Build a REST Client","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C2EX1","title":"REST CRUD + composite","obj":"Use your favorite HTTP client to create an Account, update it, then read all fields.","reqs":["Create Account {\"Name\":\"Acme\"} via POST /sobjects/Account","PATCH Health_Score__c to 88","GET the record back and assert the score","Optional: refactor to a composite request with a Contact"],"verify":"200 status + an Id on create; PATCH returns 204; GET returns Health_Score__c = 88.","stars":3,"code":"POST /services/data/v68.0/sobjects/Account"}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 2 Quiz - REST API","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"The REST base URL must include...","opts":["your instance + /services/data/v68.0/","your org id only","a SOQL string","the session id"],"a":0,"why":"The base is <instance>/services/data/vXX.X/ with the version pinned."},{"q":"To update a single field you use...","opts":["POST","PATCH","PUT","DELETE"],"a":1,"why":"PATCH performs partial updates on a record."},{"q":"A query with 6000 matches returns...","opts":["all in one payload","pages of 2000 until done=true","only 200","an error"],"a":1,"why":"GET query returns 2000 rows per page with a nextRecordsUrl."},{"q":"Composite lets you...","opts":["run 25 subrequests in one call","stream events","bulk load files","use SOAP"],"a":0,"why":"Composite batches up to 25 subrequests sharing auth and headers."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C2EX1","li":4,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"headless-apex-rest","n":3,"title":"Apex REST & Custom APIs","icon":"03","color":"#7C3AED","tagline":"@RestResource, @HttpPost, JSON, custom endpoints","guide":"03-Apex-REST-and-Custom-APIs.md","art":[{"label":"CustomRestEndpoint.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/CustomRestEndpoint.cls"},{"label":"CustomRestEndpointTest.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/CustomRestEndpointTest.cls"},{"label":"OpenAPI / Postman artifact","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"}],"objectives":["Expose Apex with @RestResource(urlMapping=...)","Handle JSON in and out with System.JSON","Return proper HTTP status codes","Secure your endpoint (with sharing, whitelisting, CORS)","Write tests that cover happy + error paths"],"lessons":[{"title":"Defining Your Endpoint","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"@RestResource(urlMapping='/acme/health/v1/*')\nglobal with sharing class CustomRestEndpoint {\n\n  @HttpPost\n  global static HealthResponse upsertHealth(HealthRequest req) {\n    if (req == null) throw new RestRequestException('Missing body');\n    ...\n    return new HealthResponse(ok, message);\n  }\n}"},{"t":"p","x":"urlMapping is relative to /services/apexrest/ so this class is reachable at /services/apexrest/acme/health/v1/*. Annotations: @HttpGet, @HttpPost, @HttpPut, @HttpPatch, @HttpDelete."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Method names are arbitrary — the HTTP verb comes from the annotation pairing. Map one method per verb per URL."}]},{"title":"Reading & Writing JSON","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"global class HealthRequest {\n  global String accountId;\n  global Integer score;\n}\n\n// from incoming body\nHealthRequest req = (HealthRequest) JSON.deserialize(\n  RestContext.request.requestBody.toString(), HealthRequest.class);\n\n// build outgoing JSON\nString body = JSON.serialize(new HealthResponse(true, 'Ok'));"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Version your payloads. Public REST APIs should put a version in the URL (/v1) and never break existing consumers on a breaking field change."}]},{"title":"Status Codes & Errors","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Case","Code"],"rows":[["Success","200 / 201 Created"],["Created in POST","201"],["Wrong input","400 Bad Request"],["Not authenticated","401 Unauthorized"],["Forbidden","403 Forbidden"],["Not found","404 Not Found"],["Server error","500"]]},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"global static HttpResponse myGet() {\n  RestResponse res = RestContext.response;\n  res.statusCode = 404;\n  res.responseBody = Blob.valueOf('Not found');\n  return res;\n}"}]},{"title":"Exercise — Apex Health Endpoint","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C3EX1","title":"Health score upsert API","obj":"Expose an endpoint that upserts an Account by external key and returns the new score.","reqs":["@RestResource(urlMapping='/acme/health/v1/*')","@HttpPost accepting {externalId, score}","Upsert on Id or External_Key__c","Return JSON {id, score} with 201 on create, 200 on update","with sharing + a test covering both paths"],"verify":"POST returns created Id (201) the first time grave 200 afterwards; test passes with coverage.","stars":3,"code":"@RestResource(urlMapping='/acme/health/v1/*')"}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 3 Quiz - Apex REST","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A class serving POSTs declares...","opts":["@HttpPost method","static void","AuraEnabled","ContactPointType"],"a":0,"why":"The HTTP verb is chosen by the annotation on the global method."},{"q":"The full URL for urlMapping='/acme/health/v1/*' is...","opts":["/services/apexrest/acme/health/v1/*","/services/data/v68.0/acme","/acme/health","/apex/acme"],"a":0,"why":"Apex REST mounts under /services/apexrest/ + the mapping."},{"q":"To forbid a caller you return...","opts":["403","200","204","301"],"a":0,"why":"403 Forbidden signals the caller is authenticated but not allowed."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C3EX1","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"headless-graphql","n":4,"title":"GraphQL API","icon":"04","color":"#0EA5E9","tagline":"Query, mutate, fragments, one round trip","guide":"04-GraphQL-API.md","art":[{"label":"GraphQLService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/GraphQLService.cls"},{"label":"GraphQL exercise (Avp4)","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"}],"objectives":["POST a GraphQL query to /services/data/v68.0/graphql","Read nested related records without client joins","Use fragments and variables for reusable queries","Run mutations for writes","Handle validation and partial errors"],"lessons":[{"title":"The GraphQL Endpoint","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"graphql","x":"POST /services/data/v68.0/graphql\nAuthorization: Bearer <token>\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  \"query\": \"query { uiapi { query { Account(first: 5) {\n              edges { node { Id Name { value } } } } } } }\"\n}"},{"t":"p","x":"Salesforce GraphQL API models records as nodes/edges. Access is behind the uiapi namespace so respect field-level security — you only see fields your user can."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"GraphQL shines for dashboards and mobile lists: define exactly the fields you need (no over-fetching) and traverse relationships in one round trip."}]},{"title":"Fragments & Variables","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"graphql","x":"query GetAccounts($n: Int!){\n  uiapi {\n    query {\n      Account(first: $n) {\n        edges { node { Id  name: Name { value } } }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}"},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why put a $variable for the limit?","a":"Reusable, cache-friendly, and avoids building the query string per request. Validation catches type errors server-side."}]},{"title":"Exercise — Fetch Nested Data","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C4EX1","title":"Accounts with contacts","obj":"One GraphQL query returning the first 3 Accounts with each related Contact.","reqs":["POST to /graphql","Query Account(first:3) including Id, Name","Traverse contacts relationship in the same query","Include a fragment for reuse"],"verify":"The response nests contacts under each account with zero extra REST calls.","stars":3}]},{"title":"Mutations & Errors","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Mutations change data. The signature returns the affected record so the client stays in sync. Errors come back in the errors array with partial results — never assume all-or-nothing."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Unlike composite allOrNone, GraphQL returns partial success. Always check the errors array per field before trusting the data."}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 4 Quiz - GraphQL","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"GraphQL records are exposed as...","opts":["nodes / edges","XML elements","blobs","reports"],"a":0,"why":"Salesforce GraphQL uses the cursor/edge nodes model."},{"q":"Fetching Accounts AND their Contacts in one query is possible because...","opts":["GraphQL traverses relationships server-side","of composite","of Bulk","of SOAP"],"a":0,"why":"You traverse related records in a single round trip."},{"q":"A failed field returns...","opts":["an errors array with partial data","an HTTP 500 only","a silent skip","a session reset"],"a":0,"why":"GraphQL returns partial results plus an errors array."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C4EX1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"headless-soap-bulk","n":5,"title":"SOAP, Bulk 2.0 & Streaming","icon":"05","color":"#D97706","tagline":"Bulk 2.0 jobs, SOAP WSDLs, long-polling","guide":"05-SOAP-Bulk-and-Streaming-APIs.md","art":[{"label":"BulkService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/BulkService.cls"},{"label":"BulkServiceTest.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/BulkServiceTest.cls"}],"objectives":["Create a Bulk API 2.0 job, add batches, wait for results","Choose delete/query/ingest and serial vs parallel batches","Interpret Bulk 2.0 limits (15M query rows, 150M total)","Know when SOAP is still the right tool","Understand long-polling for streaming"],"lessons":[{"title":"Bulk API 2.0","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"bash","x":"# 1. Create the job\nPOST /services/data/v68.0/jobs/ingest\n{ \"object\": \"Account\", \"operation\": \"insert\",\n  \"contentType\": \"CSV\", \"lineEnding\": \"LF\" }\n\n# 2. Upload CSV batches (max 10k rows / 10MB)\nPUT  /services/data/v68.0/jobs/ingest/<jobId>/batches\n\n# 3. Close the job -> platform starts processing\nPATCH /services/data/v68.0/jobs/ingest/<jobId>  { \"state\": \"UploadComplete\" }"},{"t":"p","x":"Bulk 2.0 is async and thus free of the 10k DML-row limit per transaction. Jobs = object + operation; batches = CSV chunks; states: Open → UploadComplete → InProgress → JobComplete."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Watch limits: 15,000,000 query rows / job, 150,000,000 total, 10,000 rows per batch, 5 concurrent jobs. DML is not subject to the sync 10k limit."}]},{"title":"Bulk Variants","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Operation","Endpoint (/jobs/...)","Use"],"rows":[["ingest","/jobs/ingest","Insert/update/upsert/delete bulk"],["query","/jobs/query","Export big query results"],["bulk v1 (legacy)","/services/async/38.0/","Legacy, avoid"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Use parallel batches for ingest throughput, serial when you must preserve order (e.g., parent before child in upserts)."}]},{"title":"SOAP API Today","mins":6,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"SOAP remains relevant for SSO/single-message operations and enterprise middleware that still speaks WSDL. Partner vs Enterprise WSDL: Partner uses dynamic fields (harder), Enterprise is static + object-specific."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Your Java middleware is WSDL-only. Which API do you use for one-off account creates?","a":"SOAP (Partner or Enterprise WSDL) — mature WSDL tooling without JSON."}]},{"title":"Exercise — Bulk 2.0 Load","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C5EX1","title":"Bulk load 10k Accounts","obj":"Load a 10k-row CSV into Account with Bulk 2.0 and confirm results.","reqs":["Create an ingest job (insert, CSV)","Upload two batches of 5k rows","Close the job and poll state to JobComplete","Fetch the successfulResults URL and verify 10k Ids"],"verify":"JobComplete with NumberRecordsProcessed = 10000 and 0 failed.","stars":3}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 5 Quiz - Bulk & Streaming","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Bulk API 2.0 is best for...","opts":["high-volume async loads","realtime UI","OAuth refresh","metadata deploy"],"a":0,"why":"Designed for large asynchronous data processing."},{"q":"A job with 25k rows and 10k row batches needs...","opts":["3 batches","25 batches","1 batch","5 batches"],"a":0,"why":"25k / 10k per batch = 3 batches (ceil)."},{"q":"The correct final state to trigger processing is...","opts":["UploadComplete","Open","InProgress","Closed"],"a":0,"why":"Closing with UploadComplete signals the platform to process uploaded batches."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C5EX1","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"headless-events-cdc","n":6,"title":"Platform Events & CDC","icon":"06","color":"#DB2777","tagline":"Publish, subscribe, replay, Change Data Capture","guide":"06-Platform-Events-and-CDC.md","art":[{"label":"PlatformEventService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/PlatformEventService.cls"},{"label":"health_changed__e (objects/)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/"},{"label":"CDC__Change_Evaluation__e (objects/)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/"}],"objectives":["Model a platform event and publish with EventBus.publish","Subscribe from Apex triggers, flows, or via streaming","Explain replay IDs and the replay buffer","Use Change Data Capture to react to record changes","Compare push (events) vs pull (query/polling)"],"lessons":[{"title":"Publishing Events","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A platform event is a lightweight, transient record (custom_event__e suffix). Publish by creating an instance and calling EventBus.publish(). It is atomic with the transaction."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"health_changed__e evt = new health_changed__e(\n  External_Key__c = 'ACCT-001',\n  New_Score__c   = 91\n);\nDatabase.SaveResult rs = EventBus.publish(evt);\nSystem.debug('published? ' + rs.isSuccess());"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"EventBus.publish returns SaveResult; check isSuccess() so you catch limits exceptions instead of silently dropping events."}]},{"title":"Subscribing","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Four ways to consume: Apex trigger on the event, flow (after-save on an event), connections via Streaming API, and CometD/EMP client. Triggers fire asynchronously after the publishing transaction commits."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"trigger HealthChangedHandler on health_changed__e (after insert) {\n  for (health_changed__e e : Trigger.New) {\n    IntegrationLog__c log = new IntegrationLog__c(\n      External_Key__c = e.External_Key__c,\n      Event_Type__c   = 'health_changed'\n    );\n    inserts.add(log);\n  }\n  insert inserts;\n}"},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"When does a platform-event trigger run?","a":"Asynchronously, after the publishing transaction commits (fire-and-forget by design)."}]},{"title":"Replay & Change Data Capture","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Event streams persist in a replay buffer so late consumers can replay missed events. CometD clients pass a replay ID (-1 = replay everything). CDC captures creates/updates/deletes/undeletes on standard or custom objects as change event records."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"// Change event record for an Account change\nChangeEventHeader { changeType: \"UPDATE\", entityName: \"Account\",\n  recordIds: [\"001xxx\"], changedFields: [\"Health_Score__c\"] }"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Replay is not infinite — events age out of the buffer. Size your buffer or your replay ID will 400 (ReplayIdOutOfBounds)."}]},{"title":"Exercise — Publish & Log","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C6EX1","title":"Event-driven health alerts","obj":"Publish a health_changed event on every Account score change and log it.","reqs":["Add a trigger on Account (after update) that publishes only when score changed","health_changed__e includes AccountId, New_Score__c, Old_Score__c","Subscribe with an after-insert trigger that writes a REST_Integration_Log__c","Verify with an anonymous script"],"verify":"Updating a score leads to exactly one log record per change.","stars":3}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 6 Quiz - Platform Events","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Publishing an event is done with...","opts":["EventBus.publish()","Database.insert()","Callout","SOQL"],"a":0,"why":"EventBus.publish is the dedicated publisher and is atomic with the transaction."},{"q":"Subscriber triggers run...","opts":["after the publisher commits","inline in the publisher","only in batch","only via REST"],"a":0,"why":"Delivery is asynchronous — fire-and-forget after commit."},{"q":"Replaying past events uses...","opts":["replay IDs against the buffer","bulk queries","SOAP binding","report filters"],"a":0,"why":"CometD clients pass a replay ID to resume from a checkpoint."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C6EX1","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"headless-connect","n":7,"title":"Salesforce Connect & OData","icon":"07","color":"#059669","tagline":"External objects, OData, virtual data","guide":"07-Salesforce-Connect-and-OData.md","art":[{"label":"ConnectService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/ConnectService.cls"},{"label":"External_Account__x (objects/)","href":"force-app/main/default/objects/"}],"objectives":["Explain external data sources and external objects","Connect OData v4 endpoints without copying data","Use external lookups / indirect lookups for joins","Adhere to policy (read/read-write) and column limits","Know when Salesforce Connect beats data sync"],"lessons":[{"title":"External Data Sources","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Salesforce Connect exposes external data as if it were native — no data copy (virtually). An External Data Source (type OData 2.0/4.0, custom adapter, or Apex) feeds External Objects (suffix __x)."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"External objects cannot be the master in Master-Detail; they do not support roll-up summaries; and they bypass Apex sharing but respect the external data source policy."}]},{"title":"Joining Worlds","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"External Lookup lets an external record point to a local record; Indirect Lookup joins external records by a common key field. You can mix local + external in SOQL for unified views."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"SELECT Id, Local_Name__c, ext.Erp_Id__c\nFROM Account a\nLEFT JOIN External_Account__x ext   -- via indirect lookup on Erp_Id__c\nON a.Erp_Ref__c = ext.Erp_Id__c"}]},{"title":"When to Use Connect vs Sync","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Situation","Choice"],"rows":[["Realtime external source, small reads","Salesforce Connect (virtual)"],["Need advanced reporting/rollups locally","Data sync / replication"],["Large volume always-on joins","Connect + policy tuning"],["Offline or sandbox of external data","Sync"]]},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"You need local roll-up summaries over external data. What do you choose?","a":"Sync a copy — external objects do not support roll-up summaries."}]},{"title":"Exercise — Expose a Recipe API","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C7EX1","title":"OData external objects","obj":"Expose a JSON/OData endpoint and consume it as external data.","reqs":["Stand up a local OData v4 endpoint (or use a public sample)","Create an External Data Source of type OData 4.0","Generate an external object + indirect lookup to Account","Query with a mixed SOQL"],"verify":"A SOQL query returns local + external fields together.","stars":3}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 7 Quiz - Salesforce Connect","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Salesforce Connect reads external data...","opts":["live, without copying","by nightly replication","via Bulk","via spreadsheets"],"a":0,"why":"It federates data in real time — no copy."},{"q":"External objects typically use the suffix...","opts":["__x","__c","__e","__mdt"],"a":0,"why":"External object API names end in __x."},{"q":"Joining an external record to a local record uses...","opts":["external/indirect lookups","roll-up summaries","triggers on the external object","bulk jobs"],"a":0,"why":"External or indirect lookups link the two worlds."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C7EX1","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"headless-oauth","n":8,"title":"OAuth 2.0 & Security","icon":"08","color":"#9333EA","tagline":"Flows, tokens, scopes, cipher, PKCE","guide":"08-OAuth-and-Secure-Integrations.md","art":[{"label":"AuthService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/AuthService.cls"},{"label":"Named Credential (Apex integration)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/IntegrationService.cls"}],"objectives":["Pick web-server, user-agent, JWT and client-credentials flows","Use connected apps + scopes (api, refresh_token, full)","Refresh access tokens and store them safely","Choose Named Credentials over hardcoded endpoints","Apply TLS, cipher, headers and rate checks"],"lessons":[{"title":"OAuth 2.0 Flows","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Flow","Who","Use"],"rows":[["Web server","Server apps","Secure backend, refresh tokens"],["User agent","SPAs / client-side","No server secret, PKCE"],["JWT bearer","Server-to-server","Named-credential friendly, no user"],["Client credentials","Server-to-server","System-to-system, no user"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Refresh tokens are the long-lived keys; access tokens are the short-lived currency. Never ship a refresh token to a browser (SPA) — use PKCE + user-agent."}]},{"title":"Tokens, Scopes & Secrets","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Scopes gate what a token can do: api (data API), full (everything incl. metadata), refresh_token (get new access tokens), web (id/name). Store secrets in Named Credentials, not in code or custom settings."},{"t":"code","lang":"apex","x":"System.HttpRequest req = new System.HttpRequest();\nreq.setEndpoint('callout:External_ERP/customers');\nreq.setMethod('GET');\nreq.setHeader('Accept', 'application/json');\nHttpResponse res = new Http().send(req);\nif (res.getStatusCode() == 200) { ... }"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Never hardcode secrets. Use Named Credentials so the platform mints + refreshes the token. Rotate client secrets and set expiry on connected apps."}]},{"title":"Exercise — Secure Callout","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C8EX1","title":"Named-credential integration","obj":"Replace a hardcoded endpoint with a Named Credential and handle 401 refresh.","reqs":["Create a Connected App with api + refresh_token","Configure a Named Credential (principal) with the auth URL","Call it from IntegrationService with setEndpoint('callout:External_ERP/...')","Handle 401 by refreshing and retrying once"],"verify":"One Named Credential drives auth; logs show no hardcoded URLs in Apex.","stars":3}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 8 Quiz - OAuth & Security","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A SPA without a server secret should use...","opts":["user-agent + PKCE","client credentials","username-password","JWT only"],"a":0,"why":"SPAs must not hold client secrets; PKCE secures the code exchange."},{"q":"The scope that grants API access is...","opts":["api","full","web","offline_access"],"a":0,"why":"api scope authorizes REST/SOAP/Bulk usage."},{"q":"Hardcoded secrets are replaced by...","opts":["Named Credentials","custom labels","Apex constants","IP ranges"],"a":0,"why":"Named Credentials centralize endpoints + tokens and handle refresh."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C8EX1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"headless-commerce","n":9,"title":"Headless Commerce & Composable","icon":"09","color":"#F97316","tagline":"B2B/B2C headless, CBAPI, storefront","guide":"09-Headless-Commerce-and-Composable.md","art":[{"label":"CommerceService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/CommerceService.cls"},{"label":"Commerce Storefront exercise (Avp9)","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"},{"label":"Shopping Cart / Checkout artifacts","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"}],"objectives":["Differentiate B2B vs B2C Commerce Cloud headless","Call Commerce Cloud Business Manager APIs (hosted/CC API)","Explain cart, catalog, pricebooks, promotions headlessly","Build a minimal storefront client on the Composable APIs","Handle cart session + checkout orchestration"],"lessons":[{"title":"Commerce in Headless 360","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Commerce Cloud exposes B2B and B2C commerce via standard product/cart/checkout resources. Headless storefronts call those APIs instead of the Storefront Reference Architecture (SFRA) server-rendered pages."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Composable means assemble commerce from APIs — catalog, cart, pricing, promotions as separate services you compose. No monolithic page stack."}]},{"title":"Key Resources","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Area","API/Resource","Notes"],"rows":[["Catalog / products","GET /products, /categories","Paged, filtered"],["Cart","/*/carts operations","Session-scoped"],["Checkout","/*/checkout","Orchestrates payment"],["Promotions","promotions list","Apply at cart"],["Shopper","shopper resources","AuthNZ for the shopper"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Know the difference between hosted checkout vs headless checkout: hosted keeps the payment page on Commerce Cloud; headless calls payment service provider APIs yourself (more control, more PCI scope)."}]},{"title":"Exercise — Minimal Storefront","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C9EX1","title":"List & cart in React","obj":"A tiny React client that lists products and adds one to a cart via the Commerce APIs.","reqs":["GET /products to seed the list","POST to create a cart","Add an item with a pricebook entry","Render cart total from the response"],"verify":"Cart total reflects the added item; APIs called from the client with Bearer token.","stars":3}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 9 Quiz - Headless Commerce","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Headless checkout means...","opts":["you call payment/checkout APIs yourself","no checkout exists","checkout is hidden","only B2C"],"a":0,"why":"Headless checkout orchestrates payment via APIs; hosted keeps pages on-platform."},{"q":"A cart in Commerce APIs is...","opts":["session-scoped CRUD","a static file","a report","an Aura app"],"a":0,"why":"Cart resources maintain a shopper session across add/update/checkout calls."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C9EX1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"headless-serverless","n":10,"title":"Serverless & App Platform","icon":"10","color":"#0D9488","tagline":"Functions, Express workers, Node runtime","guide":"10-Serverless-and-App-Platform.md","art":[{"label":"FunctionsService / serverless example","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"},{"label":"app_worker.mjs (serverless/ folder)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/ServerlessWorker.cls"}],"objectives":["Describe Salesforce Functions (hosted Node runtime)","Compare Functions, Heroku, and Express workers","Build a tiny Express API that calls Salesforce CRUD","Use environment variables + secrets for tokens","Handle cold starts and scale concepts"],"lessons":[{"title":"Functions & the Runtime","mins":6,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Salesforce Functions run Node.js/TypeScript close to the platform with @salesforce/core to auth. They are ideal for CPU-heavy or processing jobs that do not fit in Apex limits."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Apex is the co-selling default; compute constraints (heap, CPU, 10k DML) are when you reach for Functions or a worker."}]},{"title":"Express Worker","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"js","x":"import express from 'express';\nimport jsforce from 'jsforce';\nconst app = express();\napp.use(express.json());\n\napp.post('/api/health', async (req, res) => {\n  const conn = new jsforce.Connection({\n    instanceUrl: process.env.SF_INSTANCE,\n    accessToken: process.env.SF_TOKEN\n  });\n  const r = await conn.sobject('Account').create({ Name: req.body.name });\n  res.json({ id: r.id });\n});\n\napp.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000);"},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why environment variables for tokens?","a":"Secrets should never be in source. Env vars + secret manager keep tokens out of Git and rotateable."}]},{"title":"Exercise — Worker + Trigger","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C10EX1","title":"Score worker","obj":"An Express endpoint that recomputes an Account Health field and writes back.","reqs":["POST /api/score {accountId, data}","Compute a score in JS","PATCH the Account via jsforce","Fire a platform event on change (optional)","Config-driven via env vars"],"verify":"Calling the endpoint updates the record and (optionally) publishes health_changed.","stars":3}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 10 Quiz - Serverless","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Functions run on...","opts":["Node.js/TS near the platform","Aura","REST only","the browser"],"a":0,"why":"Functions are a hosted Node/TypeScript runtime integrated with Salesforce."},{"q":"The worker stores tokens in...","opts":["environment vars / secrets","source code","custom labels","URL query"],"a":0,"why":"Secrets belong in env vars/secrets managers, not Git."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C10EX1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"headless-mcp-fund","n":11,"title":"MCP Fundamentals","icon":"11","color":"#2563EB","tagline":"Model Context Protocol, clients, servers, tools","guide":"11-MCP-Fundamentals.md","art":[{"label":"McpFundamentalsService.cls","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/McpFundamentalsService.cls"},{"label":"MCP architecture diagram","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"},{"label":"MCP facts (mdt)","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"}],"objectives":["Explain what the Model Context Protocol is and why it exists","Name MCP primitives: tools, resources, prompts","Describe how an AI client connects to an MCP server","Place MCP inside Headless 360 (Engagement layer)","List Salesforce platform-hosted MCP servers and tools"],"lessons":[{"title":"What is MCP?","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The Model Context Protocol (MCP) standardizes how AI applications talk to tools and data. A host (Agentforce, Claude Desktop, your app) connects to an MCP server that exposes capabilities as tools, resources and prompts. It is to AI tools what USB is to peripherals: one standard plug, many devices."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Memorize the three primitives: TOOLS (callable actions), RESOURCES (readable data), PROMPTS (reusable instructions). Tool calls are how AI actually acts on your org."}]},{"title":"MCP Architecture","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"text","x":"AI Host (Agentforce / your app / Claude)\n     |  (MCP protocol: initialize, list tools, call)\n     v\nMCP Server (platform-hosted or yours)\n     |  (tools -> mediated Salesforce commands)\n     v\nSalesforce (Data/BL/Orchestration via mediated APIs)"},{"t":"p","x":"Platform-hosted MCP servers ship with an ever-growing set of tools (60+ at TDX 2026) and coding skills (30+) that perform mediated, safe, permission-checked Salesforce operations — CRUD and process invocation."},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"What does \"mediated\" mean in platform MCP tools?","a":"The tool call goes through the platform enforcing permissions, limits and audit — never a raw SQL-like backdoor."}]},{"title":"MCP Inside Headless 360","mins":6,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"MCP servers live in the Engagement layer. They are another headless surface: any AI client — including agents — gets typed, permissioned, chat-native access to Salesforce data and processes without building bespoke tools."},{"t":"list","items":["Usage types: \"Salesforce Record Operation (CRUD)\" and \"Salesforce Process Invocation\"","Tool catalog: query, create, patch records; run flows; invoke invocable actions","Safety: sharing checks, field-level security, audit trails, rate limits"]}]},{"title":"Exercise — Diagram It","mins":6,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C11EX1","title":"MCP surface map","obj":"Draw (or pseudo-collapse into text) how an Agent calls Salesforce via an MCP server.","reqs":["Label the host, the MCP server, and the Salesforce layers","Name at least three tools it would expose","Mark where permission checks happen"],"verify":"The diagram clearly shows mediation at the platform before any data access.","stars":1}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 11 Quiz - MCP Fundamentals","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"MCP standardizes how...","opts":["AI apps use tools/data","payments work","records replicate","UI renders"],"a":0,"why":"MCP is the standard interface between AI and tools/data."},{"q":"The three MCP primitives are...","opts":["tools, resources, prompts","events, jobs, batches","flows, forms, tabs","soql, dml, sosl"],"a":0,"why":"Tools, resources and prompts are the primitives."},{"q":"Platform MCP tools execute Salesforce operations...","opts":["through mediated, permission-checked calls","as raw SQL","via guest access","bypassing audit"],"a":0,"why":"Mediation enforces sharing, FLS and audit."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C11EX1","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":1}]},{"id":"headless-mcp-build","n":12,"title":"Build MCP Servers","icon":"12","color":"#7C3AED","tagline":"TypeScript SDK, registration, Agentforce tools","guide":"12-Building-MCP-Servers-for-Salesforce.md","art":[{"label":"McpServerBuilder class (SDK sample)","href":"force-app/main/default/classes/McpServerService.cls"},{"label":"MCP server folder (mcpserver/)","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"},{"label":"MCP tool manifest example","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"}],"objectives":["Stand up an MCP server with the TypeScript SDK","Define typed tools backed by Salesforce APIs","Register a server + tools with Agentforce","Secure your server: auth, scopes, allowlists","Test tool calls end-to-end from an MCP client"],"lessons":[{"title":"A Minimal MCP Server","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"ts","x":"import { McpServer } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js';\nimport { StdioServerTransport } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js';\n\nconst server = new McpServer({ name: 'sf-health', version: '1.0.0' });\n\nserver.tool('get_account_health', { accountId: z.string() },\n  async ({ accountId }) => ({\n    content: [{ type: 'text', text: await fetchHealth(accountId) }]\n  }));\n\nconst transport = new StdioServerTransport();\nawait server.connect(transport);"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Tools declare input schemas (zod). Type-safe inputs = fewer hallucinated args and clearer failures for the model."}]},{"title":"Backing Tools with Salesforce","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Your tools typically call Salesforce via jsforce or a mediated gateway. Prefer platform-hosted MCP servers when they cover the need; build your own when you need custom, domain-specific multi-step operations."},{"t":"list","items":["Use Instance + token from env vars injected on the server","Validate the caller scope before acting","Return structured results the model can reason over (not \"done\")"]},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Why return structured results from a tool?","a":"Models reason from data they receive; rich structured output reduces guesswork and re-queries."}]},{"title":"Register with Agentforce","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Registering connects your server to an agent: agent gets the tool list (name, description, schema) and can invoke tools during a conversation. Scopes+auth ensure the agent only calls what it may."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Test from a real MCP client, not just curl. Verify tool discovery, argument validation and error surfacing — the failure modes an agent hits are API failures, not UI ones."}]},{"title":"Exercise — Health Tool","mins":10,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C12EX1","title":"Build the MCP health tool","obj":"A server exposing an MCP tool that returns an Account health summary.","reqs":["Scaffold with @modelcontextprotocol/sdk + zod","tool: get_account_health(accountId)","Back it with a jsforce read of Account + Health fields","Wire env vars for instance/token/scope","Connect via StdioTransport and call it from a client"],"verify":"The client lists and calls the tool, returning the account health JSON.","stars":3}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 12 Quiz - Build MCP Servers","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"A minimal MCP server starts with...","opts":["McpServer + transport connect","an Aura bundle","a Bulk job","a report folder"],"a":0,"why":"McpServer + a transport (stdio) is the SDK entry point."},{"q":"Tool input schemas are declared with...","opts":["zod / JSON Schema","SOQL","Apex triggers","flexipages"],"a":0,"why":"Tools declare zod/JSON Schema input — validated before the model calls."},{"q":"To have an agent invoke your tool you...","opts":["register the server + declare tools","publish a dashboard","write a test class","deploy a tab"],"a":0,"why":"Registration exposes the tool catalog to the agent."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C12EX1","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":3}]},{"id":"headless-exercises","n":13,"title":"Practical Exercises","icon":"13","color":"#0891B2","tagline":"End-to-end mini projects you build","guide":"13-Practical-Exercises.md","art":[{"label":"Isolation/Prefix patterns (scripts)","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"},{"label":"MiniProjectService / flows","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"}],"objectives":["Consolidate all phases into buildable mini projects","Exercise isolation, naming, and prefix conventions","Deploy patterns that respect limits and sharing","Verify each project with tests and logs"],"lessons":[{"title":"Mini Projects Overview","mins":6,"blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Project","Skills"],"rows":[["MP1 Health REST client","REST + composite"],["MP2 Bulk load pipeline","Bulk 2.0 + polling"],["MP3 Event-driven alerts","Platform events + CDC"],["MP4 OData external read","Salesforce Connect"],["MP5 MCP health tool","TypeScript SDK + tool"],["MP6 Headless storefront","Composable APIs"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Build them in order — each reuses the previous tools. Full answers and expected results live in docs/assets/answers.js."}]},{"title":"Working With the Repo Metadata","mins":6,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"force-app/ carries real, deployable patterns: services, triggers, objects, flows bespoke for Headless & MCP. Use them as skeletons; never paste secrets in."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"Prefix everything consistently (e.g., Headless or MCP) so cross-references and permission sets stay coherent — the repo does this so you can copy the habit."}]},{"title":"Exercise — Pick Your First","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C13EX1","title":"Mini project kickoff","obj":"Choose and scaffold your first mini project (MP1 recommended).","steps":["Fork/clone this roadmap and open the MP folder","Scaffold a scratch org from config/project-scratch-def.json","Run the reference service + test as your baseline","Extend it with one new behavior"],"verify":"Baseline tests pass and your extension is covered by a new test.","stars":4}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 13 Quiz - Exercises","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"Mini projects are ordered so that...","opts":["each builds on the prior tools","they are independent","only MP5 matters","they need no tests"],"a":0,"why":"Progressive reuse keeps each project reviewable."},{"q":"You should paste secrets...","opts":["never","into classes","into triggers","into dashboards"],"a":0,"why":"Secrets belong in Named Credentials/env env, never metadata."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C13EX1","li":2,"kind":"ex","stars":4}]},{"id":"headless-answers","n":14,"title":"Answers & Results","icon":"14","color":"#4D7C0F","tagline":"Everything a guide says, verified","guide":"14-Answers-and-Results.md","art":[{"label":"answers.js (all solutions)","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"}],"objectives":["Get the canonical solution for every exercise","Understand why each answer works (not just what)","Avoid the most common mistakes","Use tests as documentation of expected behavior"],"lessons":[{"title":"How to Use This Phase","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Before you peek: attempt the exercise, then compare. The answers file is indexed per exercise id (C1EX1 … C13EX1, MP…) with code, expected resultston and common-mistake notes."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"The answers are teaching artifacts — they show the WHY via expected-result and mistake sections, so model your own reasoning, not just your code."}]},{"title":"Self-Verification Habits","mins":6,"blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Run the associated test class after every class you write","Check debug logs for the expected assertion messages","Re-explain the solution aloud to a rubber duck","Contrast your attempt vs the answer: find the deltas"]}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 14 Quiz - Answers","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"The best way to study the answers is to...","opts":["attempt first then compare","read them fast","PDF print everything","skip guides"],"a":0,"why":"Attempt-then-compare makes the delta meaningful."},{"q":"Tests in force-app serve as...","opts":["documentation of expected behavior","random files","build blockers","UI themes"],"a":0,"why":"Maintained tests encode exactly what success means."}]},"exIndex":[]},{"id":"headless-usecases","n":15,"title":"Real-World Use Cases","icon":"15","color":"#334155","tagline":"Field-facing scenarios, decision logic","guide":"15-Real-World-Use-Cases.md","art":[{"label":"Scenario → decision tables","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"}],"objectives":["Map business scenarios to headless architecture","Justify API + security + data choices from requirements","Spot architecture smells (over-fetching, chatty, sync loops)","Write the \"why\" an architect gives in a review"],"lessons":[{"title":"Scenario: Realtime Approval Pull","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"A field app must show pending approvals and let managers approve instantly, offline-tolerant."},{"t":"list","items":["API: REST (low volume, per-manager) or GraphQL for the dashboard tile","Pub/sub: subscribe to approval process updates if they must be instant","Offline: cache POJOs + replay on reconnect"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","x":"Avoid polling every 5s for approvals — that is chatty. Prefer streaming or acceptable-delay refresh with cache."}]},{"title":"Scenario: 10M-row Sync","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Nightly sync of 10M external accounts into Salesforce, order-guaranteed with a durable log."},{"t":"list","items":["API: Bulk API 2.0 (async, order via serial batches)","CDC/replay for deltas after the full load","Monitoring: job state polling + failure resume via replay"]},{"t":"selfcheck","q":"Sync 10M nightly — which API?","a":"Bulk API 2.0 with serial batches to preserve parent/child order."}]},{"title":"Scenario: Agent Answering Users","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"An AI agent must answer \"what is my account health?\" from Salesforce data safely."},{"t":"list","items":["Expose a platform-hosted or custom MCP tool (get_account_health)","Mediate through the platform: sharing + FLS + audit","Return structured JSON so the agent can reason + cite"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"For agent surfaces, prefer MCP tools over raw API keys: the platform mediates permissions and audit."}]},{"title":"Exercise — Decide & Justify","mins":9,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C15EX1","title":"Use-case architecture brief","obj":"Choose an API + security approach for one real scenario and write the justification.","steps":["Pick: realtime inflight-view, 10M sync, or agent Q&A","Justify API, transport, security, scale decisions","Note the top trade-off and the smell you avoided"],"verify":"Your brief names trade-offs and defends the API choice with requirements — the way a solution-architecture review expects.","stars":2}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 15 Quiz - Use Cases","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"For instant approval updates in a field app you prefer...","opts":["streaming/pub-sub","polling every 5s","monthly reports","mass mail"],"a":0,"why":"Streaming delivers changes without chatty polling."},{"q":"For an AI agent answering data questions you...","opts":["expose MCP tools mediated by the platform","give the agent the admin password","export to CSV","build VF pages"],"a":0,"why":"Mediated MCP tools keep permissions and audit in place."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C15EX1","li":3,"kind":"ex","stars":2}]},{"id":"headless-solutions","n":16,"title":"Use Case Solutions","icon":"16","color":"#166534","tagline":"Canonical answers for every use case","guide":"16-Use-Case-Solutions.md","art":[{"label":"CaseC1 / CaseC2 / CaseC3 solutions","href":"docs/assets/answers.js"}],"objectives":["Audit your solution against the canonical one","Understand the acceptance criteria each case checks","Grab reusable snippets for interviews","See how trade-offs map to requirements"],"lessons":[{"title":"Reading a Solution","mins":7,"blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Each use-case solution lists the deliverable, the reasoning, and the acceptance checklist. Compare to your brief and note only the meaningful deltas."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","x":"When a review scenario is asked in an interview, walking through the use case → trade-off → decision structure scores far higher than naming an API."}]},{"title":"Exercise — Self-audit","mins":8,"blocks":[{"t":"ex","id":"C16EX1","title":"Grade your own brief","obj":"Compare your Phase 15 brief to the canonical solution and list one improvement.","steps":["Open the matching solution in answers.js","List where your brief disagrees (if any)","Rewrite the justification incorporating the feedback"],"verify":"Your revised brief is stricter on trade-offs and security than the first draft.","stars":1}]}],"quiz":{"title":"Phase 16 Quiz - Solutions","mins":5,"questions":[{"q":"The value of reading a canonical solution is...","opts":["finding meaningful deltas in your reasoning","memorizing code","auto-passing","skipping tests"],"a":0,"why":"Compare-and-correct sharpens architect reasoning."},{"q":"In an interview, a scenario answer should...","opts":["walk use case → trade-off → decision","name one tool","say \"it depends\"","show code only"],"a":0,"why":"Structured trade-off reasoning is what interviewers reward."}]},"exIndex":[{"id":"C16EX1","li":1,"kind":"ex","stars":1}]}],"answerIds":["C10EX1","C11EX1","C12EX1","C13EX1","C15EX1","C16EX1","C1EX1","C2EX1","C3EX1","C4EX1","C5EX1","C6EX1","C7EX1","C8EX1","C9EX1"]};

